window.G550={"guides":{"aircraft-general-and-performance":{"t":"Aircraft General / Performance Study Guide","sub":"Recurrent Training Memory-Item Notes · Consolidated from handwritten \"G550 Recurrent\" study notes (15 pages) · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (AFM Chapter 01, Rev 44)","html":"<h1 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten \"G550 Recurrent\" personal study notes (15 pages, scanned, image-only) into a searchable, color-coded reference. Unlike the Limitations Study Guide (which mirrors the FAA-approved AFM Chapter 01 word-for-word), these notes are informal recurrent-training memory items: quick facts, gotchas, and things worth remembering across systems, general dimensions/weights, and performance-planning notes. They are NOT a substitute for detailed performance charts and tables — those live in the Performance Handbook and are not reproduced here.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number in one place (dimensions, weights, speeds, altitudes, system limits).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Topic: storage practices, dimensions/weights, avionics quirks, lighting, electrical, engines, hydraulics, fire protection, flight controls, bleed air/pneumatics, air conditioning/pressurization, ice and rain protection, and a worked weight-and-balance example from the notes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cross-check against the Limitations Study Guide:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Where a figure in these notes matches the AFM-sourced Limitations guide, it is marked \"confirmed consistent\" - examples include max landing weight 75,300 lb, max zero-fuel weight 54,500 lb, usable fuel 41,300 lb / 6,118 US gal, max tire ground speed 195 kt, max demonstrated crosswind 28 kt, min APU start battery voltage 22 VDC, weather radar clearance (49 ft from people / 300 ft from fueling), bleeds-off takeoff minimum altitude 1,500 ft AGL, single-pack max altitude 48,000 ft, and the -10C generator cold-start rule.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Where these notes have unique content not in the Limitations guide (general dimensions, storage/cold-soak practices, avionics/lighting gotchas, worked examples), it is kept as-is and clearly marked as supplementary, not an AFM limitation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>One internal conflict was found and is flagged where it occurs: page 2 of the source notes lists wingspan as 97'3\", while page 11 separately lists 'WS. 93'6\"'. The G550's published wingspan is 93 ft 6 in (28.50 m); the 93'6\" figure is treated as authoritative, and the 97'3\" entry is flagged as a probable notation error (or possibly referring to a different, unlabeled dimension) in the original notes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A weight figure on page 15 (\"Max Take-off 70,400# / 599.4 moment ARM\") is NOT the aircraft MTOW (which is 91,000 lb per the Limitations guide) - it is treated here as a specific worked weight-and-balance practice problem from the notes, not a general aircraft limitation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--dimensions-weights\">Dimensions &amp; Weights</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wingspan (per page 11, \"WS.\")</td>\n<td>93 ft 6 in - matches published G550 spec; treated as authoritative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wingspan (per page 2, \"Span\") - FLAGGED</td>\n<td>97 ft 3 in - conflicts with page 11 and published spec; likely a notation error</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing thermal growth</td>\n<td>2 ft 4 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max landing weight</td>\n<td>75,300 lb - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide (MLW)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max zero-fuel weight</td>\n<td>54,500 lb - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide (MZFW, FAA)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max fuel</td>\n<td>41,300 lb (~6,118 US gal) - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide (usable fuel total)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max aft CG on 48,300 lb</td>\n<td>45 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eye-to-wheel height</td>\n<td>See Limitations guide 01-03-90: 15.6 ft (4.76 m), unloaded main gear, landing attitude</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#aircraft-general-and-performance--dimensions-weights-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Dimensions &amp; Weights</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--quick-numeric-limitations-gotchas\">Quick Numeric Limitations / Gotchas</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Approach category</td>\n<td>Category C (~75,300 lb)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vmca - sea level</td>\n<td>107 KCAS - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide (Vmc-Ground, SL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oxygen bottles</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Taxi light</td>\n<td>Automatically off on gear retraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Landing light</td>\n<td>Off at FL180 (no automatic time limit once selected)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air start speed limit</td>\n<td>Below 250 kt - generates \"ASSISTED START\" CAS message</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vortex generators</td>\n<td>120 installed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Battery charge minimum</td>\n<td>Min 7 VDC to charge; below 7 VDC needs deep-cycle charge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IAS/ALT trend vector</td>\n<td>Projects 6 seconds ahead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min voltage to start APU</td>\n<td>22 VDC - confirmed consistent with Limitations/APU guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max tire ground speed</td>\n<td>195 kt - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max demonstrated crosswind (dry)</td>\n<td>28 kt - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crosswind &gt;20 kt after shutdown</td>\n<td>Use SLAM acceleration technique (+600 ft to required runway)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#aircraft-general-and-performance--engines-br710\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: see relevant topic sections below</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--bleed-air-pneumatics-air-conditioning\">Bleed Air / Pneumatics &amp; Air Conditioning</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleeds-off takeoff window</td>\n<td>Ground to 1,500 ft AGL - confirmed consistent with APU/Limitations guides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single-pack max altitude</td>\n<td>FL480 - confirmed consistent with Limitations guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single-pack max altitude if dispatched pre-takeoff</td>\n<td>FL410</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air cart caution</td>\n<td>Do not plug in air cart with aircraft power OFF - it will pressurize the cabin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cabin temp AUTO range</td>\n<td>60-90 F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressurization landing mode lock-in</td>\n<td>Below 25,000 ft, stays in LANDING mode even if level off &gt; 3 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MCDU destination required</td>\n<td>Pressurization will not switch to FLIGHT mode if no destination is entered</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#aircraft-general-and-performance--bleed-air-pneumatics\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Bleed Air / Pneumatics and Air Conditioning / Pressurization</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--ice-rain-emergency-descent\">Ice &amp; Rain / Emergency Descent</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto anti-ice range</td>\n<td>1,500 ft AGL to 35,000 ft MSL (climb); 35,000 ft to ground (descent)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency descent - autopilot</td>\n<td>ON, at or above 40,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency descent trigger</td>\n<td>High cabin altitude CAS message</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#aircraft-general-and-performance--ice-and-rain-protection\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Ice and Rain Protection</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--part-2---full-detail-by-topic\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY TOPIC</h1>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--intro-storage-practices\">Intro / Storage Practices</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency batteries (E-BATTS): over 5 days of storage (overnight parking), pull CB1.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--dimensions-weights-1\">Dimensions &amp; Weights</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wingspan (\"WS.\", page 11)</td>\n<td>93 ft 6 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wingspan (\"Span\", page 2) - FLAGGED</td>\n<td>97 ft 3 in (see note below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing thermal growth</td>\n<td>2 ft 4 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max landing weight</td>\n<td>75,300 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max zero-fuel weight</td>\n<td>54,500 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max fuel</td>\n<td>41,300 lb (~6,118 US gal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max aft CG on 48,300 lb</td>\n<td>45 in</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> CONFLICT FLAGGED: page 2 of the source notes lists \"Span 97'3\"\" directly under the DIMENSIONS heading, while page 11 separately lists \"WS. 93'6\"\". The G550 manufacturer-published wingspan is 93 ft 6 in (28.50 m), so 93'6\" is treated as the authoritative figure here; the 97'3\" entry may be a transcription error or may refer to a different, unlabeled dimension (e.g., an early derivative spec) - it does not match any published G550 dimension and could not be reconciled from the notes alone.</em></p>\n<p>Max landing weight, max zero-fuel weight, and max fuel all cross-check exactly against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (MLW 75,300 lb, MZFW-FAA 54,500 lb, usable fuel 41,300 lb / 6,118 US gal) - confirmed consistent, no corrections needed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--quick-numeric-limitations-gotchas-1\">Quick Numeric Limitations / Gotchas</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Approach category: Category C (~75,300 lb).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Vmca (sea level): 107 KCAS - matches the Limitations guide Vmc-Ground figure at sea level.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2 oxygen bottles installed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Taxi light: automatically extinguishes when the gear is retracted.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Landing light: switched off at FL180; no automatic time limit once selected on.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Air start below 250 kt generates an \"ASSISTED START\" CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>120 vortex generators are installed on the aircraft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum 7 VDC required to charge the battery; below 7 VDC the battery needs a deep-cycle charge instead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IAS and altitude trend vectors on the PFD project 6 seconds into the future.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum voltage to start the APU: 22 VDC - matches the APU Study Guide and Limitations guide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum tire ground speed: 195 kt - matches the Limitations guide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum demonstrated dry-runway crosswind: 28 kt - matches the Limitations guide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After shutdown, if crosswind exceeds 20 kt, use SLAM acceleration technique for the next start/taxi; this adds about 600 ft to the required runway length.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--cabin-crew-doors\">Cabin Crew / Doors</h2>\n<p><strong>Cabin crew training (partially legible in source notes):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>10+ passenger operations require an Evac Specialist trained cabin crewmember (consistent with the additional-crewmember rule in the Limitations guide, 01-01-20).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>12-month recurrent: DVD and quiz.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>24-month recurrent: DVD, quiz, and physically removing one emergency exit.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Doors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Aft baggage compartment door: above 40,000 ft, only 5 minutes of access time - matches the Limitations guide (01-21-20), which specifies a 5-minute limit above 40,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--avionics\">Avionics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Charts: save the serial number and access code for the electronic charts application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FPA (Flight Path Angle) line on the VSD (Vertical Situation Display): shown as a dashed magenta line.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Press 2/3-way switch for 3 seconds to unlatch a PFD stuck on the MFD.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SFD (Standby Flight Display): gets heading from the magnetometer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Loss of LPV guidance on one side only: continue to LPV minimums using a single navigation source, provided both sides use the same source.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Backup instruments fallback: \"mini cabinet\" - ground-based instruments only; COMM operated from the right seat with the audio panel selected to EMER.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Weather radar clearance: 49 ft from people, 300 ft from fueling operations - confirmed consistent with the Limitations guide (01-34-20: 15 m / 92 m). Standby mode still transmits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RVDT (Rotary Variable Differential Transducer) failure: related to autopilot; shows an amber X on the affected control surface indication.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--exterior-cockpit-lighting\">Exterior / Cockpit Lighting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>EBDI (Electronic Backup/Standby Display) brightness: press MENU, then use the up/down arrows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Beacon: powered by the ground service bus in flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Landing light: off at FL180 (see Quick Numeric Limitations).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--electrical-ac-power\">Electrical / AC Power</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>AC components: RED circuit breakers = Essential bus; BLACK circuit breakers = Main bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cold weather (-10C or lower): select generators OFF for engine start to allow IDG oil to warm up - confirmed consistent with the Limitations guide cold-weather action-point table.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Engine failure leads to a bus power transfer (BPT).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Losing a generator in flight de-powers the galley and triggers a load shed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>HMG (Hydraulic Motor-driven Generator): powers standby AC using the AUX TRU; powered by the Left or PTU hydraulic system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff with a single generator is prohibited.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When starting one engine on the external AC power cart: watch/wait for the IDG to come online before disconnecting the cord - once the IDG is online, it will not allow a bus power transfer to occur.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--engines-br710\">Engines (BR710)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engine model: Rolls-Royce BR710.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fan blades contain transition strips.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Keep-out zone: 66% to 80% LP (low-pressure spool) during engine run.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>START MASTER or CRANK MASTER selection turns off the right air conditioning pack.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The starter can be re-engaged up to 42% HP.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Start switch configuration: if both START MASTER and CRANK MASTER are selected ON simultaneously, the engines will not start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The aircraft cannot be dispatched with an engine in ALT CONTROL.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Engine oil quantity check: valid within 5 to 30 minutes after shutdown (same window noted for the APU in the APU Study Guide).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU exhaust diagram notes: provides case airflow vent, and includes the surge control valve (see APU Study Guide for full altitude triggers).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>BAAV (Bleed Air Augmentation Valve): introduces hot air to prevent thermal shock; adds a 15-second delay for APU starts above 35,000 ft - consistent with the APU Study Guide (BAAV opens at/above 35,000 ft, 16-second delay figure there vs. 15 sec noted here; treat the APU guide's AFM-sourced 16-second figure as authoritative for that specific delay).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU MASTER switch sequence: performs BIT test, opens fuel shutoff valve, opens air inlet door.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum battery voltage to start APU: 22 VDC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RH cowl open light illuminates: APU will not start (starter inhibited with right engine cowl open, ground only) - consistent with the APU Study Guide.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--hydraulics\">Hydraulics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Do not over-service the hydraulic system - fluid returns on emergency gear extension.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Reference checklist page EB-10 for RH engine failure combined with LH hydraulic system failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PTU (Power Transfer Unit) auto-activation conditions: LH system pressure below 1,500 psi, RH system temperature below 104.4C, and at least 1 gallon of fluid trapped downstream of the check valve.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Auxiliary pump auto-activation (armed): more than 10 degrees of brake pedal deflection with both LH and PTU pressure below 1,500 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Reservoir max quantities and accumulator pre-charge: see Limitations Study Guide (01-29) - Left reservoir 4.8 gal, Right 1.6 gal; accumulator pre-charge 1,200 psi at 70F/21C.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--fire-protection\">Fire Protection</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Loop alert: reach for the TEST function to determine whether there is an actual fire or a bad loop.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A fire alert WILL be generated with a loop disabled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU fire detection: uses a helium gas pressure micro-switch; gas leaks cause a detect fail.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Aft equipment hot: considered a worse emergency than fire by test pilots.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Smoke evacuation (below 40,000 ft): deflate the baggage compartment seal with the internal baggage door open.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--flight-controls\">Flight Controls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>RVDT failure is related to autopilot; shows an amber X on the affected control surface.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Landing configuration warning with autopilot: flaps 20 with VREF + 10 kt; elevator or rudder hops at flaps 39.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Trim indicator: no green band shown under certain fault conditions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pressurization (\"W\" - possibly pressurization related note): press or fix.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Several entries on pages 12-14 of the source notes are fragmentary or only partially legible (brief scratch notes rather than full sentences). They are transcribed here as faithfully as possible; verify against current training material before relying on them operationally.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--bleed-air-pneumatics\">Bleed Air / Pneumatics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Do NOT plug in an external air cart with aircraft power OFF - it will pressurize the cabin.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Bleeds-off takeoff: from the ground up to 1,500 ft AGL - confirmed consistent with the bleed-off takeoff altitude noted in the APU Study Guide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single air conditioning pack: max altitude FL480 - confirmed consistent with the Limitations guide (48,000 ft single-pack limit).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If dispatching with a single pack before takeoff: max altitude is FL410 (a more restrictive pre-takeoff dispatch limit than the FL480 in-flight limit).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--air-conditioning-pressurization\">Air Conditioning / Pressurization</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>With the temperature switch in AUTO, the display shows the desired cabin temperature, adjustable 60-90 F.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Below 25,000 ft, the pressurization system stays in LANDING mode even if the aircraft levels off for more than 3 minutes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If no destination is entered on the MCDU, pressurization will not switch to FLIGHT mode.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency descent conditions: at or above 40,000 ft, with autopilot ON, triggered by a high cabin altitude CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--ice-and-rain-protection\">Ice and Rain Protection</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Auto anti-ice logic: active from 1,500 ft AGL up to 35,000 ft MSL during climb, and from 35,000 ft down to the ground during descent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Above 35,000 ft (both climb and cruise), auto anti-ice does not operate automatically - must be manually selected if required (matches the Limitations guide note that automatic anti-ice is inhibited above 35,000 ft).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--weight-balance---worked-example\">Weight &amp; Balance - Worked Example</h2>\n<p>Page 15 of the source notes contains a specific weight-and-balance practice calculation, not a general aircraft limitation:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Max Take-off (example scenario)</td>\n<td>70,400 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Moment / Arm</td>\n<td>599.4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This 70,400 lb figure is NOT the aircraft maximum takeoff weight - the G550 MTOW is 91,000 lb per the Limitations Study Guide. This appears to be a specific worked practice problem (e.g., a restricted-runway or high-elevation performance-limited weight, or a training scenario), retained here for reference to the original notes but should not be treated as a general limitation.</em></p>\n<p>Also noted on this page (fragmentary): suction feed available to 20,000 ft; rotor bow guidance around 20 minutes / 5 hours; fuel/hydraulic/air fire handle sequencing; crank/start off with right pack; and a start-up note that \"NO CAS -&gt; ESS AC FAIL, check the E-inverter.\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"aircraft-general-and-performance--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Recurrent.pdf - handwritten personal study notes (15 pages, scanned, image-only), original author Luciano Rocha.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - cross-checked and confirmed consistent for weights, fuel, speeds, crosswind, tire speed, APU start voltage, radar clearance distances, bleed-off takeoff altitude, single-pack altitude, and the -10C generator cold-start rule.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 APU Study Guide (this folder) - cross-checked for APU-related items (min start voltage, BAAV, RH cowl open light, engine oil check window).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Detailed performance charts, tables, and calculations are NOT reproduced in this guide - refer to the current G550 Performance Handbook / AFM Section 5.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are informal recurrent-training memory items, not a substitute for the AFM/AOM/Performance Handbook. One figure conflict (wingspan) and one out-of-context weight figure (worked W&amp;B example) were identified during cross-check and are flagged in Part 2. Always refer to the current approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","aircraft-general-and-performance--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","aircraft-general-and-performance--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Dimensions & Weights","aircraft-general-and-performance--dimensions-weights"],["h2","Quick Numeric Limitations / Gotchas","aircraft-general-and-performance--quick-numeric-limitations-gotchas"],["h2","Bleed Air / Pneumatics & Air Conditioning","aircraft-general-and-performance--bleed-air-pneumatics-air-conditioning"],["h2","Ice & Rain / Emergency Descent","aircraft-general-and-performance--ice-rain-emergency-descent"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY TOPIC","aircraft-general-and-performance--part-2---full-detail-by-topic"],["h2","Intro / Storage Practices","aircraft-general-and-performance--intro-storage-practices"],["h2","Dimensions & Weights","aircraft-general-and-performance--dimensions-weights-1"],["h2","Quick Numeric Limitations / Gotchas","aircraft-general-and-performance--quick-numeric-limitations-gotchas-1"],["h2","Cabin Crew / Doors","aircraft-general-and-performance--cabin-crew-doors"],["h2","Avionics","aircraft-general-and-performance--avionics"],["h2","Exterior / Cockpit Lighting","aircraft-general-and-performance--exterior-cockpit-lighting"],["h2","Electrical / AC Power","aircraft-general-and-performance--electrical-ac-power"],["h2","Engines (BR710)","aircraft-general-and-performance--engines-br710"],["h2","Hydraulics","aircraft-general-and-performance--hydraulics"],["h2","Fire Protection","aircraft-general-and-performance--fire-protection"],["h2","Flight Controls","aircraft-general-and-performance--flight-controls"],["h2","Bleed Air / Pneumatics","aircraft-general-and-performance--bleed-air-pneumatics"],["h2","Air Conditioning / Pressurization","aircraft-general-and-performance--air-conditioning-pressurization"],["h2","Ice and Rain Protection","aircraft-general-and-performance--ice-and-rain-protection"],["h2","Weight & Balance - Worked Example","aircraft-general-and-performance--weight-balance---worked-example"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","aircraft-general-and-performance--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"landing-gear-and-brakes":{"t":"Landing Gear & Brakes Study Guide","sub":"Tricycle Retractable Gear - Brake-by-Wire with Anti-Skid · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Landing Gear & Brakes system · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide and AFM limitations (","html":"<h1 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Landing Gear &amp; Brakes study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by system rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the relevant limitations (01-32, and gear/speed-brake interlocks in 01-27) already documented in the G550 Limitations Study Guide in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (tire pressures/speeds, Vlo/Vle, hydraulic pressures, nosewheel steering angles, anti-skid thresholds, brake accumulator figures) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by System: gear overview, gear system (selector/dump valve, hydraulics, PTU, nitrogen alternate extension), tires &amp; wheels, normal/alternate extension-retraction procedures, gear warnings, nosewheel steering, the WOW switch system, brakes (normal/alternate/emergency), parking brake, anti-skid protection, and safety pins.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All limitation figures found in these notes - Vlo normal 225 KCAS/0.70 Mt, Vlo alternate 175 KCAS, Vle 250 KCAS/0.70 Mt, maximum tire speed 195 kt (ground speed), and maximum altitude to operate the gear or fly with the gear extended (20,000 ft MSL) - were cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide and found to match exactly, with no corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Limitations guide additionally documents that automatic ground spoiler takeoff with anti-skid inoperative is permitted by MEL only if the runway is dry, ground spoilers are operative, 20 flaps are used, and cowl/wing anti-ice is not in use (01-32-10) - this dispatch-relevant condition is noted here since these handwritten notes do not cover MEL dispatch conditions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: the landing gear selector/dump valve (\"brains\" of the system), normal vs. alternate (nitrogen bottle) gear extension, the WOW switch logic (MLG switches \"make\" on-ground / NLG switch \"breaks\" on-ground, combined WOW logic), anti-skid protection modes (touchdown, locked-wheel, wheel spin-down), and the brake and parking-brake hydraulic architecture.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--gear-tire-core-data\">Gear &amp; Tire Core Data</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gear type</td>\n<td>Fully retractable, tricycle-type; each gear has an oleo-pneumatic shock strut with dual wheels/tires</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shock strut fluid / gas</td>\n<td>MIL-H-5606 hydraulic fluid, pressurized with dry nitrogen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strut extension (nose gear)</td>\n<td>2 - 9 in, even with other side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nosewheel tires</td>\n<td>One (1) set of two (2); rated 225 mph; pressure 136 psi; weight 20 lb each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main wheel tires</td>\n<td>Two (2) sets of two (2) each; rated 225 mph; pressure 199-207 psi (min/max); weight 82 lb each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheel fusible plugs</td>\n<td>Four (4) per wheel; melt at 390F to release tire pressure if wheel overheats</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheel safety plug</td>\n<td>One (1) per wheel; deflates tire if internal pressure exceeds 412.5 +/- 37.5 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Braking</td>\n<td>Each of the four (4) main wheels has individual brake-by-wire braking with anti-skid protection down to 10 kt</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#landing-gear-and-brakes--tires-wheels\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Tires &amp; Wheels</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--limitations\">Limitations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude to operate gear or fly with gear extended</td>\n<td>20,000 ft MSL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vlo - normal (raise/lower)</td>\n<td>225 KCAS / 0.70 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vlo - alternate (lower only, one-time use)</td>\n<td>175 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vle (gear extended, doors open or closed)</td>\n<td>250 KCAS / 0.70 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum tire speed</td>\n<td>195 kt (ground speed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed brakes + gear down in flight</td>\n<td>Prohibited (speed brakes not approved for extension with gear extended, in flight)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> All figures cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (01-03-40 speed schedule and 01-32) - confirmed consistent, no corrections needed.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#landing-gear-and-brakes--limitations-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Limitations</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--nosewheel-steering\">Nosewheel Steering</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Steering range (NWS handwheel input)</td>\n<td>80 (+/- 2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NWS overtravel indicator</td>\n<td>&gt;= 84 - should not be visible/raised in normal range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder pedal steering - normal</td>\n<td>Left 7 / Right 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder pedal steering - NWS failure</td>\n<td>Left 16 / Right 16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NWS switch</td>\n<td>Red guarded switch; a \"clunk\" indicates the NWS valve opening</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#landing-gear-and-brakes--nosewheel-steering-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Nosewheel Steering System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--brakes-parking-brake\">Brakes &amp; Parking Brake</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Brake type</td>\n<td>Air-cooled, multiple carbon-fiber discs; brake-by-wire with anti-skid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal hydraulic pressure</td>\n<td>3,000 psi (L Hyd Sys)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alternate sources (L sys pressure/fluid lost)</td>\n<td>AUX pump, or PTU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency source (no L sys pressure/fluid at all)</td>\n<td>Parking/Emergency accumulator - 5 to 6 brake applications, NO anti-skid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AUX pump auto-latch</td>\n<td>Brake pedals &gt; 10 and L Hyd Sys pressure &lt; 1,500 psi -&gt; AUX pump (if armed) commanded ON automatically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parking brake accumulator</td>\n<td>Pre-charged 1,200 psi nitrogen; hydraulically charged to 3,000 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brake wear indicators</td>\n<td>Two (2) front, two (2) in rear; parking brake must be set before checking \"life remaining\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#landing-gear-and-brakes--brake-system-normal-alternate-emergency\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Brake System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#landing-gear-and-brakes--parking-brake-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Parking Brake System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--anti-skid-protection\">Anti-Skid Protection</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Threshold</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum speed for anti-skid protection</td>\n<td>10 kt (below this, wheels can be locked for tight turns while taxiing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheel velocity control band</td>\n<td>Within 2% of IRU-sensed ground speed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Touchdown protection (brakes available)</td>\n<td>WOW Ground + 5 seconds, OR wheel speed &gt; 50 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locked-wheel protection</td>\n<td>Paired wheels (L/R, inboard/outboard) kept within 30% of each other; releases brake pressure if exceeded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheel spin-down</td>\n<td>Decelerates main wheels before gear retraction with a 3-second brake application; failure triggers \"WHEEL DESPIN FAIL\" CAS message</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#landing-gear-and-brakes--anti-skid-protection-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Anti-Skid Protection</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--part-2---full-detail-by-system\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--overview-general-description\">Overview / General Description</h2>\n<p>The G550 has a fully retractable, tricycle-type landing gear: one nose gear and two main gear, each incorporating a conventional oleo-pneumatic shock strut with dual wheels and tires.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Shock struts are filled with MIL-H-5606 hydraulic fluid and pressurized with dry nitrogen.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Strut extension should be 2 to 9 in and even with the opposite strut on the same gear.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--tires-wheels\">Tires &amp; Wheels</h2>\n<p><strong>Nosewheel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>One (1) set of two (2) nosewheel tires, rated at 225 mph.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tire pressure: 136 psi. Tire weight: 20 lb.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Main wheels:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Two (2) sets of two (2) main tires each, rated at 225 mph.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tire pressure: 199 - 207 psi (min/max). Tire weight: 82 lb.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wheel overheat/overpressure protection:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Four (4) fusible plugs per wheel melt at 390F to release tire pressure if the wheel overheats.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>One (1) safety plug per wheel deflates the tire if internal pressure exceeds 412.5 +/- 37.5 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each of the four (4) main gear wheels has individual braking via a brake-by-wire system, with anti-skid protection down to 10 kt.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--gear-system---selector-hydraulics-ptu-alternate-extension\">Gear System - Selector, Hydraulics, PTU, Alternate Extension</h2>\n<p>The Landing Gear Selector/Dump Valve is the \"brains\" of the system, located in the gear wheel well area in the center post. The gear handle is an electrical switch that commands the selector/dump valve (OPEN/CLOSE for doors, EXTEND/RETRACT for gear); hydraulic pressure (the \"muscles\") then mechanically actuates the downlocks, uplocks, doors, and struts, driving position indication.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension and retraction requires:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Electrical power to operate: L ESS DC and R MAIN DC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hydraulic power to actuate: normally the L Hydraulic System (engine-driven pump).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Power Transfer Unit (PTU):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If the engine-driven hydraulic pump fails, the gear can be extended or retracted using the PTU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The PTU also helps retract the landing gear following a left engine failure after V1 (regulatory purpose).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> If the L Hydraulic System suffers a total failure (reservoir, engine-driven pump, and PTU all unavailable), the landing gear can be extended via two (2) nitrogen bottles located in the nose gear wheel well: nominally 3,100 +/- 50 psi at 70F, 150 in3 and 412 in3 bottles. This alternate extension system ports high-pressure nitrogen to reposition the nose and main gear dump valves to the dump position, extending the gear - a one-time-use system.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--gear-extension-retraction-procedures\">Gear Extension / Retraction Procedures</h2>\n<p><strong>Landing Gear Extension (Normal):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. L ESS DC and R MAIN DC available, and L Hyd Sys or PTU available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. At or below Vlo (225 KCAS / 0.70 Mt).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Gear handle (electrical switch) selected DOWN (illuminates red).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Gear doors open fully.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>5. Landing gear extends and locks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>6. Three green (down and locked).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>7. Landing gear doors close.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>8. Gear handle light extinguishes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Landing Gear Retraction:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. L ESS DC and R MAIN DC available, and L Hyd Sys or PTU available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. At or below Vlo (225 KCAS / 0.70 Mt).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Gear handle selected UP (illuminates red).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Gear doors open fully.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>5. Landing gear retracts into the uplocks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>6. Landing gear doors close.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>7. Gear handle light extinguishes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Landing Gear Extension (Alternate) - one-time use:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. At or below Vlo (175 KCAS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Gear handle selected DOWN (illuminates red).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Pull EMER Landing Gear T-handle.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Gear doors open fully and remain open.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>5. Landing gear extends and locks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>6. Three green (down and locked).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>7. Gear handle light extinguishes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>8. Landing gear doors remain open (nitrogen extension does not close the doors).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--landing-gear-warnings\">Landing Gear Warnings</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt; 500 ft AGL and &lt; 190 KCAS, gear handle not DOWN</td>\n<td>\"TOO LOW, GEAR\" aural voice warning; GPWS/GND PROX OVRD switch silences the aural warning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;= 345 ft AGL and PLA &lt;= 5, gear not down and locked</td>\n<td>Gear unsafe warning horn (klaxon tone) sounds; HORN SILENCE switch silences the warning horn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps &gt; 22, gear not down and locked</td>\n<td>Gear unsafe warning horn (klaxon tone) sounds; HORN SILENCE switch will NOT silence the warning horn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WOW switches compressed, ADS &lt; 60 kt, gear handle in RETRACT</td>\n<td>Horn silence has no effect (aircraft is on ground / at low speed with gear retract selected)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--limitations-1\">Limitations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum altitude to operate the gear, or to fly with the gear extended</td>\n<td>20,000 ft MSL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vlo - normal operation (raise or lower)</td>\n<td>225 KCAS / 0.70 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vlo - alternate extension (lower only)</td>\n<td>175 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vle - gear extended, doors open or closed</td>\n<td>250 KCAS / 0.70 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum tire speed</td>\n<td>195 kt (ground speed)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Speed brakes and landing gear extended together, in flight, is prohibited. Confirmed identical to the G550 Limitations Study Guide (01-03-40 and 01-27-20). The Limitations guide also documents an MEL dispatch condition not covered in these handwritten notes: takeoff with automatic ground spoilers inoperative requires anti-skid operative, dry runway, 20 flaps, and cowl/wing anti-ice off; conversely, takeoff with anti-skid inoperative (01-32-10) requires a dry runway, operative ground spoilers, 20 flaps, and cowl/wing anti-ice off.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--nosewheel-steering-system\">Nosewheel Steering System</h2>\n<p>The nosewheel steering (NWS) system is electrically-controlled and hydraulically-driven by a steer-by-wire system.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NWS handwheel range: 80 (+/- 2) either direction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NWS overtravel indicator: extends/raises at or above 84 - should not be visible in normal operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rudder pedal steering (NWS operative): Left 7 / Right 7.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rudder pedal steering (NWS failure): Left 16 / Right 16.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rudder pedal input plus NWS handwheel input together determine total nosewheel deflection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NWS is a red guarded switch; a \"clunk\" sound indicates the NWS valve opening when NWS power is turned ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--weight-on-wheels-wow-switch-system\">Weight-on-Wheels (WOW) Switch System</h2>\n<p>The Weight-on-Wheels (WOW) switch system provides In-Air and On-Ground sensing for aircraft components that operate only in specific flight regimes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Require an On-Ground WOW signal: engine thrust reversers, ground spoilers, nosewheel steering.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Require an In-Air WOW signal: weather radar, TCAS, cabin window heat, landing gear.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Three (3) WOW switches, one on each gear, provide sensing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>MLG WOW switches (L and R): use a roller riding on a cam. The cam rotates to a low point after takeoff, which causes the switch to extend. The MLG WOW switches \"make\" On-Ground (they physically make contact). During the exterior pre-flight inspection, the crew must confirm there is NO gap between the roller and the cam.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NLG WOW switch: a microswitch that is either compressed (Ground mode) or not (Air mode). The NLG WOW switch \"breaks\" On-Ground - it does NOT make contact on ground. During the exterior pre-flight inspection, the crew must confirm there IS a gap at the NLG WOW switch.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dedicated WOW signal outputs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NWS WOW switch -&gt; NWS AUX pump.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L MLG WOW switch -&gt; Left Bus Power Control Unit (BPCU).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>R MLG WOW switch -&gt; Ground Service Bus (GSB).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L and R MLG WOW switches together -&gt; on-side brake control/indication, on-side thrust reverser, ground spoilers, and Combined WOW (through MAU #1 and #2).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Combined WOW signal logic: Combined WOW = Air mode only if BOTH L and R MLG WOW switches read Air mode; Combined WOW = Ground mode if EITHER (or both) L/R MLG WOW switch reads Ground mode.</p>\n<p><em><strong>WOW Fault After Takeoff:</strong> Combined WOW does NOT have anything to do with the ground spoilers and does NOT prevent the ground spoilers from deploying in the event of a WOW FAULT. It simply advises the crew there is a problem. Therefore: if a WOW FAULT CAS message appears after takeoff, disarm the ground spoilers before retarding the thrust levers to idle - otherwise the ground spoilers (three panels per wing) will deploy, destroy all lift, and can be catastrophic.</em></p>\n<p><strong>WOW Fault Warnings:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L and/or R MLG WOW switch = Air mode, and speed &lt; 50 kt -&gt; \"WOW FAULT\" CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L and/or R MLG WOW switch = Ground mode, and radar altitude &gt; 147.5 ft -&gt; \"WOW FAULT\" CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--brake-system-normal-alternate-emergency\">Brake System (Normal / Alternate / Emergency)</h2>\n<p>The main landing gear brakes are air-cooled, multiple carbon-fiber discs with anti-skid protection. Overheat protection for tires and wheels is provided by fusible plugs in the wheels that melt and release tire pressure if high temperature thresholds are exceeded. A Brake Temperature Monitoring System (BTMS) monitors and displays brake temperatures.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydraulic pressure (3,000 psi) is provided by, in priority order:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Normal source: L Hydraulic System.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. If L Sys reservoir/engine-driven pump unavailable: AUX pump, or PTU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. If L Sys entirely unavailable (reservoir, engine-driven pump, and PTU all failed): AUX pump only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AUX pump auto-latch: if the brake pedals are applied (&gt; 10) and Left Hydraulic System pressure is &lt; 1,500 psi, the AUX pump auto-latch feature will command the AUX pump, if armed, to come ON automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Emergency source - if NO hydraulic pressure/fluid is available from the L Hydraulic System:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Accumulator pressure from the Parking/Emergency brake system will provide five (5) to six (6) brake applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Braking is applied simultaneously to all four (4) wheel brakes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NO anti-skid protection capability in this mode.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--parking-brake-system\">Parking Brake System</h2>\n<p>The parking brake system has an accumulator pre-charged to 1,200 psi with nitrogen and hydraulically charged (by the AUX pump, via the L Hydraulic System) to 3,000 psi.</p>\n<p>Parking brake must be set prior to checking the brake wear indicators (\"life remaining\") - two (2) in the front and two (2) in the rear.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Chocks Required:</strong> Parking brake accumulator pressure will decrease continuously over a short time. Always chock the aircraft until ready for engine start - otherwise it may roll away.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--anti-skid-protection-1\">Anti-Skid Protection</h2>\n<p>Anti-skid protection for the main landing gear brakes is provided down to 10 kt. Below this speed, the main wheel brakes can be locked to allow tight turns while taxiing.</p>\n<p>The Anti-Skid Electronic Control Unit (ECU) is the \"brains\" of the system, located in the LEER (Left Electronic Equipment Rack). It receives wheel speed data from the Wheelspeed Monitoring Unit (WMU, x4) and IRU-sensed ground speed (IRU #1: outboard wheels, IRU #2: inboard wheels), and commands the Anti-Skid Control Valves at each main wheel.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Protection Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Description</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal anti-skid control</td>\n<td>ECU endeavors to keep wheel rotation velocity within 2% of IRU-sensed ground speed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Touchdown protection</td>\n<td>Prevents landing with brakes on. Brakes become available at WOW Ground + 5 seconds, or wheel speed &gt; 50 kt (whichever occurs).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locked-wheel protection</td>\n<td>Compares paired wheel speeds (left vs. right, inboard vs. outboard). If a wheel is &gt; 30% slower than its paired wheel, brake pressure is released on that wheel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheel spin-down</td>\n<td>Decelerates the main gear wheels prior to entering the wheel wells, with a three (3) second brake application. Failure triggers a \"WHEEL DESPIN FAIL\" CAS message.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--safety-pins\">Safety Pins</h2>\n<p><strong>Six (6) safety pins in total, made more visible by a \"Remove Before Flight\" red flag:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Three (3) gear pins (nose gear + two main gear downlocks) are installed during the post-flight inspection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>One (1) nose gear door pin and two (2) main gear door pins are installed before opening the gear doors (the AUX pump is used to open the gear doors on the ground).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>All six pins are removed during the exterior pre-flight inspection, in preparation for departure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One pilot removes/stores the pins; the other pilot confirms that ALL pins have been removed and announces it. Failure to remove the pins will prevent the landing gear from retracting after takeoff.</p>\n<p><em><strong>QRH Reference:</strong> The QRH addresses landing gear pins mistakenly left in - refer to \"Attempted Landing Gear Retraction with Safety Pins Installed.\"</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"landing-gear-and-brakes--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_landing_gear_and_brakes_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Landing Gear &amp; Brakes study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - speed schedule (01-03-40), altitude limits, tire speed limit, and 01-32 anti-skid MEL condition - cross-checked and confirmed consistent for all limitation figures (Vlo/Vle, max tire speed, max altitude gear extended).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","landing-gear-and-brakes--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","landing-gear-and-brakes--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Gear & Tire Core Data","landing-gear-and-brakes--gear-tire-core-data"],["h2","Limitations","landing-gear-and-brakes--limitations"],["h2","Nosewheel Steering","landing-gear-and-brakes--nosewheel-steering"],["h2","Brakes & Parking Brake","landing-gear-and-brakes--brakes-parking-brake"],["h2","Anti-Skid Protection","landing-gear-and-brakes--anti-skid-protection"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM","landing-gear-and-brakes--part-2---full-detail-by-system"],["h2","Overview / General Description","landing-gear-and-brakes--overview-general-description"],["h2","Tires & Wheels","landing-gear-and-brakes--tires-wheels"],["h2","Gear System - Selector, Hydraulics, PTU, Alternate Extension","landing-gear-and-brakes--gear-system---selector-hydraulics-ptu-alternate-extension"],["h2","Gear Extension / Retraction Procedures","landing-gear-and-brakes--gear-extension-retraction-procedures"],["h2","Landing Gear Warnings","landing-gear-and-brakes--landing-gear-warnings"],["h2","Limitations","landing-gear-and-brakes--limitations-1"],["h2","Nosewheel Steering System","landing-gear-and-brakes--nosewheel-steering-system"],["h2","Weight-on-Wheels (WOW) Switch System","landing-gear-and-brakes--weight-on-wheels-wow-switch-system"],["h2","Brake System (Normal / Alternate / Emergency)","landing-gear-and-brakes--brake-system-normal-alternate-emergency"],["h2","Parking Brake System","landing-gear-and-brakes--parking-brake-system"],["h2","Anti-Skid Protection","landing-gear-and-brakes--anti-skid-protection-1"],["h2","Safety Pins","landing-gear-and-brakes--safety-pins"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","landing-gear-and-brakes--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"pneumatic":{"t":"Pneumatic System Study Guide","sub":"Engine Bleed Air, Manifolds, Isolation, Precoolers, Overheat/Leak Detection · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Pneumatic System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (Chapter 01-21","html":"<h1 id=\"pneumatic--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Pneumatic System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by system rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (Chapter 01-21, Air Conditioning and Bleed Air) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (pressures, temperatures, set points, CAS message thresholds) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by System: overview and sub-components, the Bleed Air Controllers (BACs) and their inputs/outputs, valve types (PRSOV/HPSOV/fan air valves), the isolation valve, main engine bleed air (mid-stage and high-stage), APU bleed air, external ground air, the precooler heat exchanger, wing and cowl anti-ice bleed sources, system set points, and pressure/temperature sensors with associated CAS messages.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified / found:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The G550 Limitations Study Guide (01-21, Air Conditioning and Bleed Air) does not list any pneumatic-source-specific pressure or temperature limitation figures — its 01-21 content covers cabin pressure differential (10.48 psi max; 0.3 psi max for taxi/takeoff/landing) and the internal baggage door, which are ECS/pressurization limits rather than bleed-air-source limits. No conflicts found; nothing to correct.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The APU altitude/EGT/bleed-air figures (35,000 ft BAAV, 25,000 ft BR710C4-11 assisted start limit, 1,500 ft AGL bleed-off takeoff, etc.) already live in the G550 APU Study Guide and are cross-checked there — this guide only briefly cross-references the APU bleed connection (Load Control Valve into the right manifold, isolation valve auto-opens) rather than repeating that detail. See the APU Bleed Air section.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>All pressure/temperature set points (40 psi / 400-500F normal operation; 14 psi ground idle / 24 psi flight idle on 8th stage; 35 psi / 500F single-pack) and CAS threshold values (Bleed Pressure High &gt; 75 psi, Bleed Pressure Low &lt; 5 psi for &gt; 10 sec, Bleed Air Hot &gt; 765F inlet / &gt; 553F outlet) are transcribed directly from the source notes and were not independently found elsewhere for cross-check - treat as reference only per the original author's disclaimer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"pneumatic--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--pneumatic-system-overview\">Pneumatic System Overview</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>Generation of high pressure/temperature air from four sources: L Engine, R Engine, APU, External (Ground) Air</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Used by</td>\n<td>Engine starting, cowl/wing anti-ice, air conditioning/pressurization, potable water, TAT probe airflow (ground only), other systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulated by</td>\n<td>Two (2) identical, interchangeable Bleed Air Controllers (L BAC, R BAC), located in the AEER</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manifolds</td>\n<td>Two (2) separate, independent pneumatic manifolds (L and R); normally isolated, can be connected via the isolation valve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design intent</td>\n<td>Prevents total loss of pneumatic air if a leak occurs in one manifold</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pneumatic--overview-purpose-sub-components\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Purpose / Sub-Components</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--set-points-pressure-temperature\">Set Points (Pressure / Temperature)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Set Point</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground idle</td>\n<td>8th stage</td>\n<td>14 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flight idle</td>\n<td>8th stage</td>\n<td>24 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrust &gt; idle</td>\n<td>5th stage</td>\n<td>40 psi (400F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-ice (2 valves), idle</td>\n<td>8th stage (precooler 630F)</td>\n<td>40 psi (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-ice (2 valves), &gt; idle</td>\n<td>5th stage (precooler 630F)</td>\n<td>40 psi (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-ice (1 valve), &gt; idle</td>\n<td>5th stage (precooler 630F)</td>\n<td>40 psi (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single pack (other pack OFF/inop)</td>\n<td>8th stage</td>\n<td>35 psi (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Low power settings run 14-24 psi on 8th stage; single-pack operation runs 35 psi. If mid-stage (5th) pressure/temperature is insufficient, the BACs command the high-stage (8th) valves to modulate open, up to 40 psi.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pneumatic--set-points\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Set Points</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--sensors-cas-messages\">Sensors / CAS Messages</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>CAS Message</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Trigger</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R BLEED PRESSURE HIGH</td>\n<td>Manifold pressure &gt; 75 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R BLEED PRESSURE LOW</td>\n<td>Manifold pressure &lt; 5 psi for &gt; 10 seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R BLEED AIR HOT</td>\n<td>Precooler inlet temperature &gt; 765F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R BLEED AIR HOT</td>\n<td>Precooler outlet temperature &gt; 553F</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pneumatic--sensors-cas-messages-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Sensors / CAS Messages</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--bleed-air-sources-at-a-glance\">Bleed Air Sources at a Glance</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Manifold Connection</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R Engine (5th stage, mid)</td>\n<td>Own side manifold</td>\n<td>Primary bleed source; feeds through check valve and PRSOV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R Engine (8th stage, high)</td>\n<td>Own side manifold</td>\n<td>Used when 5th stage insufficient in pressure/temperature; HPSOV modulates open, fail closed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APU (via Load Control Valve)</td>\n<td>Directly to R manifold; opens isolation valve to reach L manifold</td>\n<td>See Overview / Purpose - APU Bleed Air; full detail in APU Study Guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External (Ground) Air</td>\n<td>Directly to R manifold</td>\n<td>Requires electrical power ON before connecting (placard)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pneumatic--main-engine-bleed-air\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Main Engine Bleed Air</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"pneumatic--part-2---full-detail-by-system\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--overview-purpose-sub-components\">Overview / Purpose / Sub-Components</h2>\n<p><strong>The Pneumatic System is about the generation of high pressure/temperature air from:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Left Engine (5th and 8th stage bleed)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Right Engine (5th and 8th stage bleed)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU (via the Load Control Valve)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>External (Ground) Air</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>That high pressure/temperature air is utilized by:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engine starting system</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cowl / wing anti-ice systems</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Air conditioning / pressurization systems</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Potable water system</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TAT probe airflow (ground only)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Other systems</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sub-components of the pneumatic system:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Bleed Air Controllers (BACs) - L BAC and R BAC, the \"brains\" of the system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Isolation Valve - normally keeps the L and R manifolds separate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pressure Regulating/Shutoff Valves (PRSOV) - one per engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>High-Pressure Shutoff Valves (HPSOV) - one per engine, on the 8th stage line.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fan Air Valves - modulate cooling air through the precoolers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pressure / temperature sensors throughout the manifolds and precoolers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Check valves (one-way flow) protecting the APU, external air connection, and 5th-stage ports.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The pneumatic manifolds can be connected but are normally operated in isolation via the isolation valve. This design prevents total loss of pneumatic air in the event of a leak in one of the manifolds.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--bleed-air-controllers-bacs\">Bleed Air Controllers (BACs)</h2>\n<p>The pneumatic system is regulated by two (2) identical and interchangeable microprocessors: L BAC and R BAC, located in the Aft Electronic Equipment Rack (AEER). The BACs are the brains of the system and control most pneumatic functions via two (2) separate and independent manifolds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L BAC is powered by the L ESS DC bus; R BAC is powered by the R ESS DC bus.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Data input (the BACs receive data from):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Aircraft altitude</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Static air temperature (SAT)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Engine LP RPM</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Precooler inlet temperature</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Precooler outlet temperature</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Bleed manifold pressure</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Wing anti-ice temperature</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cockpit input (the BACs also receive cockpit switch positions):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>BLEED AIR panel: L ENG / APU / R ENG / ISOLATION switches</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L WING / R WING anti-ice switches (OFF / AUTO / ON)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ENGINE START switches (L ENG, R ENG)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on data and cockpit input, the BACs command the HPSOVs and fan air valves to modulate as required, in order to produce proper air based on specific system requirements:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Parameter</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Produced Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressure</td>\n<td>Up to 40 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature</td>\n<td>400F normal; 500F for single-bleed or single-wing-anti-ice operation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--prsov-hpsov-fan-air-valves\">PRSOV / HPSOV / Fan Air Valves</h2>\n<p><strong>Pressure Regulating/Shutoff Valves (PRSOV)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Located on each engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Function as ON/OFF valves to the pneumatic manifold.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Controlled by the L and R BLEED AIR switches.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Electro-pneumatic valves: require electrical power to operate, and pneumatic pressure to close.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Modulate as needed in order to maintain up to 40 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Without L/R ESS DC bus power, the PRSOVs will not operate. Bleed air switches can remain ON for engine start.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>High-Pressure Shutoff Valves (HPSOV)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Located on each engine, on the 8th-stage (high-stage) line.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Commanded to modulate as needed by the BACs when mid-stage (5th) bleed air is insufficient.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Spring-loaded and fail CLOSED.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fan Air Valves</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Located at each precooler; modulate cooling (fan/LP) air flow through the precooler heat exchanger.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Commanded by the BACs based on precooler inlet and outlet temperature sensors.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> High-Pressure Stage 5 (HPS) Air Non-Return Valve: when the HPSOV is open, higher pressure from the 8th-stage port closes the 5th-stage check valve, preventing 8th-stage air from flowing backward into the 5th-stage line.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--isolation-valve\">Isolation Valve</h2>\n<p>Located in the tail compartment, between the L and R pneumatic manifolds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Powered by the L ESS DC bus; without L ESS DC bus power, the isolation valve will not operate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Electro-pneumatic valve: requires electrical power to operate, and pneumatic pressure to open.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fails frozen (i.e., remains in its last position).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The isolation valve opens when:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. START MASTER is selected ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. CRANK MASTER is selected ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. APU bleed is selected ON (APU stabilized at 100% RPM / 60 seconds; one-minute delay if EGT &lt; 149C) and weight-on-wheels (WOW) - Ground.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. The ISOLATION switchlight is manually selected OPEN.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Selection of APU bleed air automatically opens the isolation valve, allowing APU bleed air to reach the L manifold in addition to the R manifold it feeds directly.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--main-engine-bleed-air\">Main Engine Bleed Air</h2>\n<p><strong>Each engine supplies bleed air from two HP compressor stages:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Behavior</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 - Mid-stage bleed</td>\n<td>5th stage of HP compressor</td>\n<td>Primary source; if insufficient in pressure and temperature, the system uses high-stage bleed air instead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 - High-stage bleed</td>\n<td>8th stage of HP compressor</td>\n<td>Backup source, modulated open by the HPSOV (commanded by the BACs) when 5th stage is insufficient</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>5th-stage flow path: 5th-stage port -&gt; 5th-stage check valve (prevents higher-pressure 8th-stage air from entering the 5th-stage port) -&gt; joins the 8th-stage line at a junction -&gt; pressure regulating shutoff valve (PRSOV) -&gt; manifold. The cowl anti-ice valve taps 5th-stage bleed air upstream of this junction.</p>\n<p>8th-stage flow path: 8th-stage port -&gt; 8th-stage (high-pressure) check valve -&gt; high-pressure shutoff valve (HPSOV) -&gt; joins the same junction as the 5th-stage line -&gt; PRSOV -&gt; manifold.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Engine bleed air is extremely hot and would melt aluminum if not cooled — see the Precooler Heat Exchanger section for how fan air brings it down to a safe manifold temperature before use.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--apu-bleed-air\">APU Bleed Air</h2>\n<p>The APU's Load Control Valve (LCV) allows high pressure/temperature air into the pneumatic manifolds. APU bleed air plumbing connects directly to the R manifold; selecting APU bleed air automatically opens the isolation valve to also allow bleed air into the L manifold.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Sequence to bring APU bleed online: weight-on-wheels (WOW) - Ground, APU stabilized at 100% RPM, then a one-minute delay if EGT is below 149C (delay is removed if EGT is already warm).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU bleed air is available immediately to restart an engine in flight (WOW - Air).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A flapper-type check valve opens whenever APU or external air pressure is greater than manifold pressure, allowing the APU or external air source to pressurize the manifold; the same check valve protects the APU from reverse flow originating from the engines.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Cross-Reference:</strong> This guide keeps APU bleed air brief by design. For the full APU bleed-air detail (Load Control Valve internals, EGT/RPM start-sequencing, altitude limits for assisted air starts, Bleed Air Augmentation Valve, surge control valve, bleed-off takeoff procedure) see the G550 APU Study Guide, Bleed Air System section — already built and cross-checked in this Study Guides folder.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--external-ground-air\">External (Ground) Air</h2>\n<p>Provides air for main engine start when the APU is unavailable. Connects to the right (R) bleed air manifold, in parallel with the APU connection.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Placard on the access panel: \"DO NOT CONNECT WITHOUT ELECTRICAL POWER ON.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>DC power is required to open the isolation valve and the L/R PACK valves.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This prevents damage to the packs due to unregulated (uncontrolled pressure/temperature) external air.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Check valves (one-way flow) at both the APU and external air connections ensure air only flows from APU/external air toward the manifold, never backward into the source.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--precooler-heat-exchanger\">Precooler Heat Exchanger</h2>\n<p>Engine bleed air is extremely hot and would melt aluminum if it were not cooled. The precooler uses fan air (LP) and a heat exchanger to cool engine bleed air down before it enters the manifold. The precooler heat exchanger is located in the engine pylon.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fan air enters through the fan air valve, is modulated as needed, and exhausts under the pylon (precooler exhaust).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The BACs monitor precooler inlet and outlet temperature and modulate the opening of the fan air valve as necessary.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Precooler inlet temperature reflects whatever mid-stage (5th) or high-stage (8th) bleed is producing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Precooler outlet temperature is normally 400F; it rises to 500F when hotter air is needed to travel the longer distance through the crossover duct, due to wing anti-ice being ON with only a single bleed source available.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--wing-anti-ice-bleed-source\">Wing Anti-Ice Bleed Source</h2>\n<p>Wing anti-ice valves are electro-pneumatic: they require electrical power to operate, and pneumatic pressure to close. They use hot engine bleed air — either mid-stage (5th) or high-stage (8th), selected as needed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Controlled by the L WING and R WING anti-ice switches (OFF / AUTO / ON).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A check valve prevents mixing between 5th-stage and 8th-stage bleed air at the wing anti-ice valve inlet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Wing anti-ice valves fail OPEN.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This section covers only the bleed-air source side of wing anti-ice (which pneumatic stage feeds it, and the associated set points/precooler temperatures). Full wing anti-ice system operation, annunciations, and icing procedures belong in the Ice Protection study guide.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--cowl-anti-ice-bleed-source\">Cowl Anti-Ice Bleed Source</h2>\n<p>Cowl anti-ice valves are electro-pneumatic: they require electrical power to operate, and pneumatic pressure to close. Unlike wing anti-ice, the cowl anti-ice system uses hot engine bleed air from mid-stage (5th) ONLY, tapped at the 5th-stage port on each engine, upstream of the 5th-stage check valve.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Controlled by the L-R COWL ANTI-ICE ON switch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powered by the L/R ESS DC bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cowl anti-ice valves fail OPEN.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--set-points\">Set Points</h2>\n<p>Set points ensure adequate pneumatic pressure and temperature at the various power settings and bleed requirements. Low power settings run 14-24 psi; single pack operation runs 35 psi. If mid-stage (5th) bleed is not enough, the BACs command the high-stage (8th) valves to modulate open in order to provide up to 40 psi.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Set Point (psi)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground idle</td>\n<td>8th</td>\n<td>14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flight idle</td>\n<td>8th</td>\n<td>24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrust &gt; idle</td>\n<td>5th</td>\n<td>40 (400F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-ice (2), idle</td>\n<td>8th (precooler 630F)</td>\n<td>40 (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-ice (2), &gt; idle</td>\n<td>5th (precooler 630F)</td>\n<td>40 (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-ice (1), &gt; idle</td>\n<td>5th (precooler 630F)</td>\n<td>40 (500F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single pack</td>\n<td>8th</td>\n<td>35 (500F) *inop pack off</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pneumatic--sensors-cas-messages-1\">Sensors / CAS Messages</h2>\n<p><strong>Bleed Air Pressure Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L/R BLEED PRESSURE HIGH: manifold pressure exceeds 75 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L/R BLEED PRESSURE LOW: manifold pressure drops below 5 psi for more than 10 seconds.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bleed Air Temperature Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L/R BLEED AIR HOT: precooler inlet temperature exceeds 765F.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L/R BLEED AIR HOT: precooler outlet temperature exceeds 553F.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These pressure and temperature thresholds are the overheat/leak-detection logic for the pneumatic system - they annunciate abnormal manifold pressure (possible leak or valve malfunction) or abnormal precooler temperature (possible precooler/fan-air-valve malfunction).</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"pneumatic--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_pneumatic_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Pneumatic System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Air Conditioning and Bleed Air section (01-21), reviewed for pneumatic-source limitation figures; none found beyond cabin pressure differential (ECS-related, not a bleed-source limit) - no conflicts to correct.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 APU Study Guide (this folder) - cross-referenced for APU bleed air detail rather than duplicating it here.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","pneumatic--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","pneumatic--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Pneumatic System Overview","pneumatic--pneumatic-system-overview"],["h2","Set Points (Pressure / Temperature)","pneumatic--set-points-pressure-temperature"],["h2","Sensors / CAS Messages","pneumatic--sensors-cas-messages"],["h2","Bleed Air Sources at a Glance","pneumatic--bleed-air-sources-at-a-glance"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM","pneumatic--part-2---full-detail-by-system"],["h2","Overview / Purpose / Sub-Components","pneumatic--overview-purpose-sub-components"],["h2","Bleed Air Controllers (BACs)","pneumatic--bleed-air-controllers-bacs"],["h2","PRSOV / HPSOV / Fan Air Valves","pneumatic--prsov-hpsov-fan-air-valves"],["h2","Isolation Valve","pneumatic--isolation-valve"],["h2","Main Engine Bleed Air","pneumatic--main-engine-bleed-air"],["h2","APU Bleed Air","pneumatic--apu-bleed-air"],["h2","External (Ground) Air","pneumatic--external-ground-air"],["h2","Precooler Heat Exchanger","pneumatic--precooler-heat-exchanger"],["h2","Wing Anti-Ice Bleed Source","pneumatic--wing-anti-ice-bleed-source"],["h2","Cowl Anti-Ice Bleed Source","pneumatic--cowl-anti-ice-bleed-source"],["h2","Set Points","pneumatic--set-points"],["h2","Sensors / CAS Messages","pneumatic--sensors-cas-messages-1"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","pneumatic--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"air-conditioning":{"t":"Air Conditioning System Study Guide","sub":"Packs, Air Conditioning Controllers (ACCs), Zone Temperature, Ram Air & Equipment Cooling · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Air Conditioning System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study","html":"<h1 id=\"air-conditioning--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Air Conditioning System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Air Conditioning and Bleed Air section (01-21) of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (built from the FAA-approved AFM) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (zone temperature ranges, altitude thresholds, pack limits, cooling fan logic) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: pneumatic air source, the Air Conditioning Controllers (ACCs), L/R packs and mix valves, temperature/zone control, engine start airflow logic, ram air, the equipment cooling sub-system, single-pack operation, and the interface with the pressurization system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Single ECS pack limitations cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (01-21) and found consistent: maximum altitude on a single air conditioning pack is 48,000 ft; internal baggage compartment door access on a single pack is limited to 45,000 ft and below - no corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>These source notes cover the Air Conditioning System (packs, temperature control, ram air, equipment cooling) with only a brief one-page overview of the Pressurization System (the Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve / TROV) shown as the downstream destination for conditioned air. Detailed pressurization control logic (cabin altitude schedule, differential pressure limits, outflow valve modes) is a separate G550 system and, per the Limitations Study Guide, the maximum cabin pressure differential is 10.48 psi (0.3 psi max for taxi/takeoff/landing) - retained here only as a cross-reference, not as full subsystem detail.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"air-conditioning--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--air-conditioning-system---core-facts\">Air Conditioning System - Core Facts</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Air source</td>\n<td>High pressure, high temperature bleed air from the pneumatic system (bleed air manifold)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cabin air replacement</td>\n<td>All cabin air replaced with fresh air every two (2) minutes; no air recirculation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Three main functions</td>\n<td>(1) Airflow control for the pressurization system, (2) cabin/cockpit temperature control, (3) equipment cooling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controllers</td>\n<td>Two identical, interchangeable microprocessors: L ACC and R ACC (Air Conditioning Controllers), located in the AEER next to the Bleed Air Controllers (BACs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Packs</td>\n<td>L Pack and R Pack, each fed by its own pack valve; common cold air manifold cools all three cabin zones</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#air-conditioning--overview-three-main-functions\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Three Main Functions</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--pack-air-parameters\">Pack Air Parameters</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleed air supply to packs</td>\n<td>Up to 40 psi and 400F, high pressure/temperature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common cold air manifold</td>\n<td>35F (cold air supplied to all three zones)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trim air temperature</td>\n<td>400F (mixed with cold air to reach zone setpoint)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ozone converter</td>\n<td>One per pack, immediately downstream of the pack valve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleed air manifold set point</td>\n<td>35 +/- 3 psi</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#air-conditioning--packs-mix-valves-air-manifolds\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Packs / Mix Valves / Air Manifolds</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--temperature-zone-control\">Temperature / Zone Control</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Range</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>AUTO</td>\n<td>60F to 90F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAN (Manual)</td>\n<td>35F to 230F</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Three independently controlled zones: Cockpit, Forward Cabin, Aft Cabin - each with its own Cold/Hot knob and temperature readout on the Air Conditioning Control Panel.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#air-conditioning--temperature-zone-control-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Temperature / Zone Control</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--single-ecs-pack---altitude-limits\">Single ECS Pack - Altitude Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Restriction</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>48,000 ft</td>\n<td>Maximum altitude for operation on a single air conditioning pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>45,000 ft and below</td>\n<td>Internal baggage compartment door may be opened only when operating on a single pack at or below this altitude</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (01-21) and confirmed consistent.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#air-conditioning--single-ecs-pack-operation-limits\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Single ECS Pack Operation / Limits</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--equipment-cooling-fans---altitude-logic\">Equipment Cooling Fans - Altitude Logic</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Fan Speed</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Above 35,000 ft</td>\n<td>LEER/PDB (left side): low speed | REER/PDB (right side): high speed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At/below 35,000 ft</td>\n<td>LEER/PDB (left side): high speed | REER/PDB (right side): low speed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Two-speed fans switched by a 35,000 ft relay. Cooling fans supply LEER, REER, PDBs, cockpit center pedestal, AEER, and PSUs.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#air-conditioning--equipment-cooling-sub-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Equipment Cooling Sub-System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"air-conditioning--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--overview-three-main-functions\">Overview / Three Main Functions</h2>\n<p>The Air Conditioning System takes high pressure, high temperature bleed air from the pneumatic system and conditions it into cool, conditioned, dehumidified air for the cabin and cockpit.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All cabin air is replaced with fresh air every two (2) minutes - there is no air recirculation on the G550.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Air is exhausted overboard; the cabin operates as a continuous flow-through system, not a recirculating one.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The Air Conditioning System has three (3) main functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Airflow control for use by the pressurization system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Cabin and cockpit temperature control (three independently controlled zones).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Equipment cooling (avionics and electronics bays).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--pneumatic-system-bleed-air-source\">Pneumatic System (Bleed Air Source)</h2>\n<p>The pneumatic system is the source of air for the Air Conditioning System. Each engine bleed air source connects through the pneumatic manifold (with an isolation valve between L and R sides) and provides high pressure, high temperature air into the bleed air manifold.</p>\n<p>Pneumatic System -&gt; provides high pressure/temperature air (bleed air manifold) -&gt; Air Conditioning System.</p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--air-conditioning-controllers-accs\">Air Conditioning Controllers (ACCs)</h2>\n<p>The Air Conditioning System is regulated by two (2) identical and interchangeable microprocessors: L ACC and R ACC.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Brains of the Air Conditioning System - make all the logical decisions associated with the system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the AEER, next to the Bleed Air Controllers (L BAC and R BAC).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Receive inputs from Mix Augmentation Units (MAU #1, #2, #3) and the Air Conditioning Control Panel.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Command the L Pack and R Pack, which produce cool, conditioned, dehumidified air and control cockpit/cabin temperature.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The Air Conditioning Control Panel displays cockpit/FWD cabin/AFT cabin temperatures, has AUTO/MAN mode selection, Cold/Hot knobs per zone, and 1 Pack / 2 Pack fan-air selectors.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--packs-mix-valves-air-manifolds\">Packs / Mix Valves / Air Manifolds</h2>\n<p>The L Pack and R Pack produce cool, conditioned, dehumidified air. Each pack is fed high pressure/temperature bleed air (up to 40 psi and 400F) through its respective pack valve.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Pack Valve / R Pack Valve: control admission of hot, high-pressure bleed air into each pack.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ozone Converter: one per pack, immediately downstream of the pack valve, upstream of the pack itself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each pack discharges cold air (35F) into the Common Cold Air Manifold, which feeds all three cabin/cockpit zones.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Trim Air (400F, tapped from the Hot Air Manifold) is mixed into the cold air stream just before each zone outlet to reach the selected zone temperature.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Bleed air manifold set point: 35 +/- 3 psi.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cold Air Manifold (35F) + Trim Air (400F) = Zone Temperature.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--temperature-zone-control-1\">Temperature / Zone Control</h2>\n<p>Three (3) independently controlled zones: Cockpit, Forward (FWD) Cabin, and Aft Cabin. Each zone has its own temperature readout and a Cold/Hot selector knob on the Air Conditioning Control Panel.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Range</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>AUTO</td>\n<td>60F to 90F - ACCs automatically blend cold manifold air and trim air to hold the selected temperature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAN (Manual)</td>\n<td>35F to 230F - crew directly commands the mix valve position via the Cold/Hot knob</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--engine-start-airflow-logic\">Engine Start Airflow Logic</h2>\n<p>During engine start, the Air Conditioning System automatically reconfigures airflow to maximize air flow to the Air Turbine Starter:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Switch State</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Pack Configuration</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>START MASTER ON or CRANK MASTER ON</td>\n<td>R Pack OFF (L Pack and Ram Air unaffected)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L Eng Start ON and R Eng Start ON</td>\n<td>L Pack OFF and R Pack OFF (both packs shut off)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R Eng Start complete, AND (Start Master OFF or Crank Master OFF)</td>\n<td>Both packs return to normal (L Pack and R Pack back on)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Shutting off pack air during starter operation maximizes bleed air flow available to the Air Turbine Starter, reducing start time and thermal stress.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--ram-air-system\">Ram Air System</h2>\n<p>Ram Air intake is located on top of the fuselage, in front of the tail (dorsal fin air intake).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Outside air is forced into the dorsal fin air intake in flight and delivered via a dual-ducted plenum to the primary and secondary heat exchangers on the L Pack and R Pack.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Air enters through a Ram Air check valve, which opens only when Ram Air pressure exceeds cabin air pressure (differential pressure high-to-low).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When Ram Air is selected ON: both packs (L Pack and R Pack) are shut off, and cabin air is supplied directly from the Ram Air source.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ram Air is used for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Overpressurization due to loss of system control.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Aircraft interior smoke removal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ditching.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--equipment-cooling-sub-system\">Equipment Cooling Sub-System</h2>\n<p>The ECS packs provide airflow to equipment cooling fans for various sections of the aircraft that build up heat. The L ACC and R ACC control the cooling fans, which provide cooling to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>LEER (Left Electronic Equipment Rack) and its Power Distribution Box (PDB)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>REER (Right Electronic Equipment Rack) and its Power Distribution Box (PDB)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cockpit Center Pedestal</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AEER (Aft Electronic Equipment Rack)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PSUs (Personal Service Units)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two-speed fans, switched by a 35,000 ft relay:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>LEER / PDB (Left)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>REER / PDB (Right)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Above 35,000 ft</td>\n<td>Low speed</td>\n<td>High speed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At/below 35,000 ft</td>\n<td>High speed</td>\n<td>Low speed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--single-ecs-pack-operation-limits\">Single ECS Pack Operation / Limits</h2>\n<p>If one pack is lost or selected off, the remaining pack (L or R) alone can supply the aircraft, subject to the following limits:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>#</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Maximum altitude, single pack operation</td>\n<td>48,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Internal baggage door opening</td>\n<td>Permitted only at/below 45,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Bleed air manifold set point</td>\n<td>35 +/- 3 psi (unchanged, single or dual pack)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (01-21) Air Conditioning and Bleed Air section - both figures (48,000 ft single-pack max altitude, 45,000 ft baggage door limit) match exactly. No corrections needed.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"air-conditioning--pressurization-interface-trov\">Pressurization Interface (TROV)</h2>\n<p>The Pressurization System is a separate G550 system, but it is the downstream destination for all air conditioned by the Air Conditioning System - these notes include only a one-page overview of the interface, reproduced here for context.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cabin air exits the pressurized vessel via the Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve (TROV), located on the aft lower fuselage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The TROV modulates to achieve and hold optimum cabin pressure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Full pressurization control logic (cabin altitude schedule, outflow valve control modes, differential pressure limits) is documented in the dedicated G550 Pressurization System source material, not in this Air Conditioning source document. Per the G550 Limitations Study Guide (01-21): maximum cabin pressure differential is 10.48 psi; maximum cabin pressure differential for taxi, takeoff, or landing is 0.3 psi.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"air-conditioning--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_air_conditioning_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Air Conditioning System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Air Conditioning and Bleed Air section (01-21), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for all limitation figures (single-pack max altitude 48,000 ft, baggage door access 45,000 ft, cabin pressure differential 10.48 psi / 0.3 psi taxi-takeoff-landing).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","air-conditioning--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","air-conditioning--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Air Conditioning System - Core Facts","air-conditioning--air-conditioning-system---core-facts"],["h2","Pack Air Parameters","air-conditioning--pack-air-parameters"],["h2","Temperature / Zone Control","air-conditioning--temperature-zone-control"],["h2","Single ECS Pack - Altitude Limits","air-conditioning--single-ecs-pack---altitude-limits"],["h2","Equipment Cooling Fans - Altitude Logic","air-conditioning--equipment-cooling-fans---altitude-logic"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","air-conditioning--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / Three Main Functions","air-conditioning--overview-three-main-functions"],["h2","Pneumatic System (Bleed Air Source)","air-conditioning--pneumatic-system-bleed-air-source"],["h2","Air Conditioning Controllers (ACCs)","air-conditioning--air-conditioning-controllers-accs"],["h2","Packs / Mix Valves / Air Manifolds","air-conditioning--packs-mix-valves-air-manifolds"],["h2","Temperature / Zone Control","air-conditioning--temperature-zone-control-1"],["h2","Engine Start Airflow Logic","air-conditioning--engine-start-airflow-logic"],["h2","Ram Air System","air-conditioning--ram-air-system"],["h2","Equipment Cooling Sub-System","air-conditioning--equipment-cooling-sub-system"],["h2","Single ECS Pack Operation / Limits","air-conditioning--single-ecs-pack-operation-limits"],["h2","Pressurization Interface (TROV)","air-conditioning--pressurization-interface-trov"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","air-conditioning--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"ice-and-rain-protection":{"t":"Ice And Rain Protection System Study Guide","sub":"Wing / Cowl Anti-Ice, Windshield & Window Heat, Probe Heat, Ice Detection · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Ice and Rain Protection System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (0","html":"<h1 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Ice and Rain Protection System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Ice and Rain Protection section (01-30) of the G550 Limitations Study Guide already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Relationship to the Cold Weather Operations Guide:</strong></p>\n<p>You already have a \"G550 Cold Weather Operations Guide\" covering OPERATIONAL cold-weather procedures — deicing/anti-icing fluid holdover times, the RCAM contaminated-runway tables, ground-ops action points, and a general system review of cowl/wing anti-ice. This guide is deliberately different: it covers the ICE AND RAIN PROTECTION SYSTEM ITSELF — the valves, sensors, bleed-air routing, temperature targets, electrical sources, and CAS logic behind wing/cowl anti-ice, windshield/cabin/EVS window heat, pitot-static/AOA/TAT probe heat, and the ice detection system. Where the two guides overlap (e.g., icing-conditions definition, flap restrictions in icing), the figures were verified to match; ground deicing fluid procedures are NOT repeated here — see the Cold Weather Operations Guide for those.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (temperatures, pressures, altitudes, timers) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: icing conditions definition, ice detection, cowl anti-ice (CAI), wing anti-ice (WAI), automatic activation logic, crew procedures in icing conditions, pitot-static/AOA/TAT probe heat, windshield heat, cabin window heat, EVS window heat, and a consolidated CAS message table.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Icing-conditions definition cross-checked against the Limitations guide (01-30): the Limitations guide states SAT &lt;= +10C with visible moisture; these system notes give the fuller picture used operationally - SAT between +10C and -40C with visible moisture (rain, snow, clouds, fog &lt; 1 SM visibility, sleet, ice crystals) in flight or on the ground, OR SAT +10C or below on the ground/for takeoff with surface snow, ice, standing water, or slush on ramps, taxiways, or runways. No contradiction found - the two describe the same rule at different levels of detail.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic WAI/CAI activation altitude envelope (1,500 ft AGL to 35,000 ft MSL, inhibited above 35,000 ft) is new detail not present in the Limitations guide and does not conflict with the \"automatic anti-ice inhibited above 35,000 ft\" limitation already documented there - confirmed consistent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flap-use restriction in icing conditions (takeoff/approach/landing only; holding at flaps 0/UP) matches the Limitations guide (holding limited to flaps UP, minimum 160 KCAS) - confirmed consistent; this source does not independently state the 160 KCAS figure, so that number should still be taken from the Limitations guide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in either the Limitations guide or the Cold Weather Operations Guide: ice detector probe operating frequency and ice-accumulation trip threshold, cowl anti-ice normal/abnormal pressure bands, wing anti-ice 130F target temperature and Low/Overheat CAS thresholds, pitot/AOA/TAT probe heat setpoints, windshield and cabin window heat temperature bands, and EVS window heat cyclic duty logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--system-scope\">System Scope</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Protected Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Method</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine cowl inlets</td>\n<td>Hot engine bleed air (mid-stage, 5th stage) - Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing leading edges</td>\n<td>Hot engine bleed air (mid or high-stage, 5th or 8th stage HP) - Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pitot-static / AOA vanes</td>\n<td>Electrical heat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total Air Temperature (TAT) probes</td>\n<td>Electrical heat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Windshield / cabin windows</td>\n<td>Electrical heat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EVS window</td>\n<td>Electrical heat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine spinner</td>\n<td>Passive - rubber tip distorts and sheds ice, no heating required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--overview-scope\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Scope</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--icing-conditions-definition\">Icing Conditions Definition</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Criteria</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground or in-flight</td>\n<td>SAT between +10C and -40C AND visible moisture present (rain, snow, clouds, fog with visibility &lt; 1 SM, sleet, ice crystals)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground / for takeoff</td>\n<td>SAT +10C or below, operating on ramps/taxiways/runways covered with surface snow, ice, standing water, or slush</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Contaminants could be ingested or freeze on engines, nacelles, or engine probes.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--icing-conditions-definition-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Icing Conditions Definition</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--ice-detection-system\">Ice Detection System</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Probes</td>\n<td>Two (2), below pilot and copilot windows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probe vibration frequency</td>\n<td>40,000 Hz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ice accumulation trip threshold</td>\n<td>0.020 inches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequency decrease at trip</td>\n<td>~130 Hz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L-R Ice Detected extinguishes</td>\n<td>After 1 minute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cowl A/I valves close / message extinguishes</td>\n<td>After 3 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing A/I valves close / message extinguishes</td>\n<td>After 5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power</td>\n<td>Left probe = L Main AC; Right probe = R Main AC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--ice-detection-system-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Ice Detection System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--cowl-anti-ice-cai-pressure-indications\">Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) Pressure Indications</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Indication</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal pressure range</td>\n<td>1.6 - 33 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abnormal pressure</td>\n<td>&lt; 1.6 psi or &gt; 33 psi -&gt; \"L/R Cowl Anti-Ice Hi/Lo\" type message</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R miscompare threshold</td>\n<td>7.0 +/- 1 psi difference -&gt; \"Cowl Anti-Ice Miscompare\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valve type</td>\n<td>Electropneumatic, electrically-controlled / pneumatically-operated, open or closed only (not modulating)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valve power</td>\n<td>28 VDC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valve failure mode</td>\n<td>Fails OPEN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleed air source</td>\n<td>Mid-stage only (5th stage)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--cowl-anti-ice-cai-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--wing-anti-ice-wai-temperature-indications\">Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) Temperature Indications</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Indication</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Target operating temperature</td>\n<td>130F (valves modulate to maintain this)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal ON range (no message)</td>\n<td>&gt;= 100F and &lt; 180F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"L/R Wing Temperature Low\" message</td>\n<td>WAI selected ON, temperature &lt; 100F for &gt; 2 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"L/R Wing Overheat\" message</td>\n<td>WAI selected ON, temperature &gt;= 180F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleed air source</td>\n<td>Mid or high-stage (5th or 8th stage HP compressor); high-stage used at low power (e.g., descent)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valve type</td>\n<td>Spring-loaded closed, pneumatically actuated, variable pressure regulator and shutoff (butterfly-type), modulating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Location</td>\n<td>Tail compartment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum bleed requirement</td>\n<td>At least one (1) engine bleed air switch must be ON for WAI operation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--wing-anti-ice-wai-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--automatic-activation-altitude-envelope\">Automatic Activation Altitude Envelope</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Behavior</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>35,000 ft MSL</td>\n<td>Automatic WAI/CAI activation inhibited above this altitude; if already ON, remains ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,500 ft AGL to 35,000 ft MSL</td>\n<td>Automatic activation range - WAI/CAI activate automatically when ice is detected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any altitude</td>\n<td>Manual activation by the crew has no altitude restriction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> On the ground, the \"L-R Ice Detected\" CAS message is inhibited because Amber CAS messages are considered \"No-Go\" messages.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--automatic-activation-logic\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Automatic Activation Logic</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--probe-windshield-window-heat-setpoints\">Probe / Windshield / Window Heat Setpoints</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Air data probes below 60 kts</td>\n<td>Heated to 150C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air data probes above 60 kts</td>\n<td>Heated to 300C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TAT probes</td>\n<td>Heated only above 60 kts, or when both thrust levers advanced beyond 30 degrees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Windshield heat target</td>\n<td>104F - 114F (cycles ON/OFF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cabin window heat</td>\n<td>Electric heat to 14 cabin windows; prevents condensation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cabin window manual override (ground only)</td>\n<td>10-minute limit - irreparable damage can occur from lack of airflow beyond this</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EVS window heat target</td>\n<td>12C - 14C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EVS window heat - manual mode</td>\n<td>2 minutes ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EVS window heat - automatic, gear UP</td>\n<td>1 minute ON / 7 minutes OFF (cyclic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EVS window heat - automatic, gear DOWN</td>\n<td>Continuously ON</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#ice-and-rain-protection--pitot-static-aoa-tat-probe-heat\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Probe Heat, Windshield Heat, Cabin Window Heat, EVS Window Heat</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--overview-scope\">Overview / Scope</h2>\n<p><strong>The Ice and Rain Protection System is about the prevention or removal of ice formation on:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Engine cowl inlets - through the use of bleed air heat from the pneumatic system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Wing leading edges - through the use of bleed air heat from the pneumatic system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Pitot-static / AOA vanes - through the use of power from the electrical power system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Total Air Temperature (TAT) probes - through the use of power from the electrical power system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>5. Windshield / cabin windows - through the use of power from the electrical power system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>6. EVS window - through the use of power from the electrical power system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two power sources feed the whole system: hot engine bleed air (cowl and wing leading edges) and electrical power (probes, windshield, cabin windows, EVS window).</p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--icing-conditions-definition-1\">Icing Conditions Definition</h2>\n<p><strong>Icing conditions exist when the Static Air Temperature (SAT) on the ground or in flight is between +10C and -40C AND visible moisture is present in any form, such as:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Rain</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Snow</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Clouds</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fog with visibility &lt; 1 SM</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Sleet</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ice crystals</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Icing conditions also exist when the SAT on the ground and for takeoff is +10C or below, when operating on ramps, taxiways, or runways covered with:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Surface snow</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ice</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Standing water</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Slush</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These contaminants could be ingested or freeze on the engines, nacelles, or engine probes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--ice-detection-system-1\">Ice Detection System</h2>\n<p>The Ice Detection System consists of two (2) exterior probes located on both sides of the fuselage, below the pilot and copilot windows.</p>\n<p>Ice detector probes vibrate at a frequency of 40,000 Hz. Ice thickness affects the resonant properties of the probes. Activation of the system occurs when probes accumulate 0.020 inches of ice formation, which decreases the probe frequency by approximately 130 Hz. When this happens the crew is notified of the presence of ice via a CAS message: \"L-R Ice Detected.\"</p>\n<p><strong>The crew is then notified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>\"L-R Cowl Anti-Ice ON\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>\"L-R Wing Anti-Ice ON\"</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The probes are then heated to melt the ice and allow their vibration frequency to return to normal speed - ready to continue detecting more icing. The process continues until there is no more icing.</p>\n<p><strong>When icing is no longer detected by the probes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. \"L-R Ice Detected\" extinguishes after one (1) minute.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Cowl anti-ice valves close after three (3) minutes and \"L-R Cowl Anti-Ice ON\" extinguishes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Wing anti-ice valves close after five (5) minutes and \"L-R Wing Anti-Ice ON\" extinguishes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Power: Left probe = L Main AC. Right probe = R Main AC.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--cowl-anti-ice-cai-system\">Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) System</h2>\n<p>The CAI system protects the engine cowl inlets from ice accumulation. It consists of two (2) separate and independent systems - one (1) for each engine. It uses HOT engine bleed air (mid-stage only, 5th stage).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>CAI valves are electropneumatic: electrically-controlled (open or closed) and pneumatically-operated. They require 28 VDC power and pneumatic pressure to close.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>CAI valves fail in the OPEN position (e.g., \"R Cowl Valve Fail Open\").</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cowl anti-ice valve power: L Ess DC (left engine circuit shown in source notes).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CAI status is indicated in PRESSURE (PSI) instead of temperature, because engine bleed air is not modulated by the CAI system - pressure simply varies with engine power.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Indication</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal pressure</td>\n<td>1.6 - 33 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abnormal pressure</td>\n<td>&lt; 1.6 psi or &gt; 33 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L/R miscompare</td>\n<td>7.0 +/- 1 psi difference between L and R pressures -&gt; \"Cowl Anti-Ice Miscompare\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>After passing through the CAI supply duct and heating the cowl leading edges, the bleed air is exhausted overboard via vents located at the bottom of the engine cowls.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Engine Spinner:</strong> The engine spinner has a passive de-icing design and does NOT require heating. The rubber tip distorts in flight and this sheds any ice.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--wing-anti-ice-wai-system\">Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) System</h2>\n<p>The WAI system protects the wing leading edges against ice accumulation. It consists of two (2) separate and independent systems - one (1) for each wing - but joined by a crossover duct. It uses HOT engine bleed air (mid OR high-stage, 5th or 8th stage HP compressor). A check valve prevents mixing between 5th and 8th stage bleed air.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>At low power settings, such as on descent, engine bleed air is extracted from the high-stage bleed air (8th stage of the HP compressor) instead of mid-stage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Wing anti-ice valves are spring-loaded closed, pneumatically actuated, variable pressure regulator and shutoff valves. Located in the tail compartment. Butterfly-type.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Wing anti-ice valves fail CLOSED.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>At least one (1) engine bleed air switch must be ON for WAI operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The wing anti-ice valves MODULATE (unlike the on/off cowl valves) in order to maintain a 130F temperature target.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The hot engine bleed air then passes through the main wheel well before exiting overboard.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In case of engine failure, the crossover duct allows bleed air from the operating engine to heat up the inoperative side's wing leading edge.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Temperature Logic:</strong> WAI temperature/CAS logic: OFF = no indication. ON with temperature increasing = transitioning to target. ON with temperature &gt;= 100F and &lt; 180F = normal operation (target 130F). ON for more than 2 minutes with temperature &lt; 100F = \"L/R Wing Temperature Low.\" ON with temperature &gt;= 180F = \"L/R Wing Overheat.\"</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--automatic-activation-logic\">Automatic Activation Logic</h2>\n<p>Prior to entering icing conditions, or when icing is detected by the Ice Detection System, the crew should select wing and cowl anti-ice systems to ON.</p>\n<p><strong>The WAI and CAI systems can be activated:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Manually by the crew - no altitude restriction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Automatically when ice is detected by the Ice Detection System, from 1,500 ft AGL to 35,000 ft MSL.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the WAI system is in AUTO, the wing anti-ice valves open automatically and allow hot engine bleed air to heat the wing leading edges. If the CAI system is in AUTO, the cowl anti-ice valves open automatically and allow hot engine bleed air to heat the engines' cowl inlets.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic activation of the WAI/CAI is inhibited above 35,000 ft MSL. If already selected ON, they will remain ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>\"L-R Ice Detected\" CAS is inhibited on the ground because AMBER CAS messages are considered \"No-Go\" messages.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--crew-procedures-icing-ops\">Crew Procedures / Icing Ops</h2>\n<p>Holding in icing conditions: flaps 0 (UP).</p>\n<p><strong>Use of flaps in icing conditions is restricted to:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Approach</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Landing</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The Limitations Study Guide additionally specifies a minimum holding speed of 160 KCAS in icing conditions - that figure is not independently stated in these system notes but is consistent and should still be applied.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--pitot-static-aoa-tat-probe-heat\">Pitot-Static / AOA / TAT Probe Heat</h2>\n<p>Probes are electrically heated to prevent ice formation. Probe heaters are turned on after engine start.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Below 60 kts: air data probes (pitot-static/AOA) are heated to 150C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Above 60 kts: air data probes are heated to 300C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TAT probes are only heated above 60 kts, OR when both thrust levers are advanced beyond 30 degrees.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anti-Ice Heater switch positions covered: AOA (Left, Right) and Probe (Upper, Lower).</p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--windshield-heat\">Windshield Heat</h2>\n<p>Protects pilot and copilot front/side windshields from ice accumulation AND fogging, using electrically-powered heating elements within each windshield. Heats windshields slowly to avoid rapid temperature changes. Windshield heat cycles ON and OFF to ensure a window temperature between 104F and 114F.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Main AC powers the Left-Front (LF) and Right-Side (RS) windshield zones.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>R Main AC powers the Right-Front (RF) and Left-Side (LS) windshield zones.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--cabin-window-heat\">Cabin Window Heat</h2>\n<p>Provides electric heat to fourteen (14) cabin windows. Prevents the formation of condensation.</p>\n<p><strong>Power to the cabin window heaters is applied when the CABIN WDO HT pushbutton in the Copilot Overhead Panel (COP) is ON AND:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Aircraft is airborne, OR</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maintenance is being performed, OR</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cabin windows need to be defrosted while the aircraft is on the ground.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Manual override capability is ground-only, with a ten (10) minute limit - irreparable damage can occur due to lack of airflow beyond that time.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--evs-window-heat\">EVS Window Heat</h2>\n<p>Provides protection against ice accumulation on the EVS windshield. Electrically heated (12C - 14C).</p>\n<p><strong>Modes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Manual: two (2) minutes ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Automatic: triggered by Weight-On-Wheels (WOW) Air, \"L-R Ice Detected,\" or L/R Cowl anti-ice switch ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cyclic heat is applied to the EVS windshield as follows:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Gear Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cycle</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gear UP</td>\n<td>One (1) minute ON / seven (7) minutes OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gear DOWN</td>\n<td>Continuously ON</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--cas-message-summary\">CAS Message Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>CAS Message</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning / Trigger</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L-R Ice Detected</td>\n<td>Ice Detection System probe(s) sensed 0.020 in. of ice; inhibited on the ground</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L-R Cowl Anti-Ice ON</td>\n<td>Cowl anti-ice valve(s) open (manual, AUTO, or auto-triggered by ice detection)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L-R Wing Anti-Ice ON</td>\n<td>Wing anti-ice valve(s) open (manual, AUTO, or auto-triggered by ice detection)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R (or L) Cowl Valve Fail Open</td>\n<td>Cowl anti-ice valve failed in the open position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L Cowl Anti-Ice Hi (abnormal pressure)</td>\n<td>Cowl anti-ice pressure outside 1.6 - 33 psi normal band</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cowl Anti-Ice Miscompare</td>\n<td>L/R cowl anti-ice pressure difference exceeds 7.0 +/- 1 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L-R Wing Temperature Low</td>\n<td>WAI selected ON &gt; 2 minutes with temperature &lt; 100F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L-R Wing Overheat</td>\n<td>WAI selected ON with temperature &gt;= 180F</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This table consolidates CAS messages referenced across these notes; always confirm exact CAS wording and priority against the current CMS/CAS message list.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"ice-and-rain-protection--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_ice_and_rain_protection_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Ice and Rain Protection System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Ice and Rain Protection section (01-30), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for icing-conditions definition, automatic anti-ice inhibition above 35,000 ft, and flap-use restrictions in icing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Cold Weather Operations Guide (this folder) - covers operational ground deicing/anti-icing fluid procedures and the RCAM contaminated-runway tables, not duplicated here; cross-referenced for the icing-conditions and system-review overlap.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","ice-and-rain-protection--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","ice-and-rain-protection--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","System Scope","ice-and-rain-protection--system-scope"],["h2","Icing Conditions Definition","ice-and-rain-protection--icing-conditions-definition"],["h2","Ice Detection System","ice-and-rain-protection--ice-detection-system"],["h2","Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) Pressure Indications","ice-and-rain-protection--cowl-anti-ice-cai-pressure-indications"],["h2","Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) Temperature Indications","ice-and-rain-protection--wing-anti-ice-wai-temperature-indications"],["h2","Automatic Activation Altitude Envelope","ice-and-rain-protection--automatic-activation-altitude-envelope"],["h2","Probe / Windshield / Window Heat Setpoints","ice-and-rain-protection--probe-windshield-window-heat-setpoints"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","ice-and-rain-protection--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / Scope","ice-and-rain-protection--overview-scope"],["h2","Icing Conditions Definition","ice-and-rain-protection--icing-conditions-definition-1"],["h2","Ice Detection System","ice-and-rain-protection--ice-detection-system-1"],["h2","Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) System","ice-and-rain-protection--cowl-anti-ice-cai-system"],["h2","Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) System","ice-and-rain-protection--wing-anti-ice-wai-system"],["h2","Automatic Activation Logic","ice-and-rain-protection--automatic-activation-logic"],["h2","Crew Procedures / Icing Ops","ice-and-rain-protection--crew-procedures-icing-ops"],["h2","Pitot-Static / AOA / TAT Probe Heat","ice-and-rain-protection--pitot-static-aoa-tat-probe-heat"],["h2","Windshield Heat","ice-and-rain-protection--windshield-heat"],["h2","Cabin Window Heat","ice-and-rain-protection--cabin-window-heat"],["h2","EVS Window Heat","ice-and-rain-protection--evs-window-heat"],["h2","CAS Message Summary","ice-and-rain-protection--cas-message-summary"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","ice-and-rain-protection--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"oxygen":{"t":"Oxygen System Study Guide","sub":"Crew, Passenger, Portable, and Therapeutic Oxygen · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Oxygen System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide Oxygen System (01-35) section","html":"<h1 id=\"oxygen--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Oxygen System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Oxygen System section (01-35) of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (built from the FAA-approved AFM) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (cylinder pressure, relief valve settings, mask certification altitudes, cabin altitude triggers, PBE oxygen supply, FAR 91 crew oxygen requirements) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: cylinder storage, pressure regulation, servicing, crew oxygen masks (EROS), passenger oxygen masks, therapeutic oxygen, Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE), and the FAR 91 / AFM oxygen requirements.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The two limitation figures that appear in both documents - crew/passenger oxygen masks \"not approved for use above 40,000 ft cabin altitude\" and passenger masks \"will not provide sufficient oxygen for prolonged use above 34,000 ft cabin altitude\" - were cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide Oxygen System (01-35) section and found consistent, word-for-word. No corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Limitations guide additionally notes \"prolonged operation above 25,000 ft cabin altitude with passengers aboard is not recommended\" - this figure does not appear in the handwritten Oxygen System notes; it is a separate AFM guidance item, not a contradiction, and is repeated here for completeness (see Requirements table, Part 1).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: cylinder capacity and pressure (123.4 ft^3, 1,800 +/-50 psi), pressure regulation (55-80 psi supply line) and relief valve settings, the oxygen service panel and filler valve, crew mask delivery modes (Normal / 100% / Emergency), automatic passenger mask deployment cabin-altitude triggers (14,750 +/-250 ft, or 15,750 +/-250 ft with HIGH ALT selected), therapeutic oxygen receptacle procedure, and PBE (Protective Breathing Equipment) specifications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"oxygen--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--oxygen-system-overview\">Oxygen System Overview</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>Storage of gaseous oxygen under high pressure and delivery to crew and passengers as an alternate means of breathing (depressurization, smoke/fumes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinders</td>\n<td>Two (2) identical tanks plumbed together into a single system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinder construction</td>\n<td>Aluminum cylinder wrapped in Kevlar for reinforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinder location</td>\n<td>Underneath the floor, near the main entrance doorway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinder capacity</td>\n<td>123.4 ft^3 each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinder pressure</td>\n<td>1,800 +/-50 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common line</td>\n<td>Feeds both cockpit (crew O2 masks) and cabin (pax O2 masks)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--overview-purpose\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Purpose</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--pressure-regulation-relief\">Pressure Regulation &amp; Relief</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinder pressure</td>\n<td>1,800 +/-50 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulator output / supply line pressure</td>\n<td>55-80 psi (reduced by cylinder pressure regulators)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-pressure relief (bottles &amp; high-pressure lines)</td>\n<td>Opens above 2,775 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low-pressure relief (bottle low-pressure supply line)</td>\n<td>Opens above 90 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thermal (temperature) relief</td>\n<td>225F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relief port routing</td>\n<td>Teed together, connected to the overboard discharge indicator (right side of aircraft)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--pressure-regulation-relief-valves\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Pressure Regulation &amp; Relief Valves</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--servicing\">Servicing</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fill pressure</td>\n<td>1,800 psi @ 70F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oxygen type</td>\n<td>Aviator Breathing Oxygen, MIL-PRF-27210</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service location</td>\n<td>O2 Service Panel, right side of aircraft (maintenance function)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service panel gauges</td>\n<td>Direct-reading gauges, one each for Crew and Pax</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Servicing note</td>\n<td>No oil/grease on oxygen fittings (fire hazard) - filler valve on panel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--servicing-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Servicing</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--crew-oxygen-masks-eros\">Crew Oxygen Masks (EROS)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mask assemblies</td>\n<td>Three (3) crew oxygen mask/regulator assemblies (pilot, copilot, observer/jump seat)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goggles</td>\n<td>Non-attachable goggles (pilot &amp; copilot, and observer)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Donning time</td>\n<td>Quick-donning, &lt;= 5 seconds, one-hand operation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delivery modes</td>\n<td>Normal (N) - diluted O2/cabin air; 100% - on-demand 100% O2; Emergency - pressurized 100% O2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certification altitude</td>\n<td>Certified to a cabin altitude of 40,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive pressure</td>\n<td>Automatically switches to positive pressure at 35,000 ft cabin altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quick-donning requirement</td>\n<td>Above FL250, crew masks must be in the quick-donning position (don within 5 seconds)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--crew-oxygen-masks-eros-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Crew Oxygen Masks (EROS)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--passenger-oxygen-masks\">Passenger Oxygen Masks</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Certification altitude</td>\n<td>Certified to a cabin altitude of 40,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency descent only</td>\n<td>Do not provide sufficient oxygen above 34,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mask quantity</td>\n<td>Exceeds the number of passenger seats by at least 10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td>Manual or automatic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic deployment - normal</td>\n<td>Cabin altitude of 14,750 +/-250 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic deployment - HIGH ALT selected</td>\n<td>Cabin altitude of 15,750 +/-250 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Activation</td>\n<td>Pull on lanyard to remove pin and activate O2 flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selector positions</td>\n<td>AUTO / MAN, each side (L/R)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--passenger-oxygen-masks-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Passenger Oxygen Masks</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--requirements-operations-far-91\">Requirements / Operations (FAR 91)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Requirement</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Above 41,000 ft</td>\n<td>One pilot must be on oxygen - FAR 91</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Above 35,000 ft, one pilot leaves the cockpit</td>\n<td>Remaining pilot must be on oxygen - FAR 91</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crew &amp; passenger masks</td>\n<td>Not approved for use above 40,000 ft cabin altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passenger masks</td>\n<td>Will not provide sufficient oxygen above 34,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Above FL250</td>\n<td>Crew masks in quick-donning position (don within 5 seconds)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic pax mask deployment</td>\n<td>14,750 +/-250 ft (15,750 +/-250 ft with HIGH ALT selected)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dispatch oxygen quantity</td>\n<td>Refer to AFM 01-35-10 to determine required oxygen quantity for departure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-check: the Limitations Study Guide (01-35) additionally states that prolonged operation above 25,000 ft cabin altitude with passengers aboard is not recommended - this AFM guidance does not appear in the handwritten notes but does not contradict them.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--requirements-operations-far-91-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Requirements / Operations</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--portable-protective-breathing-equipment-pbe\">Portable / Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Manufacturer/type</td>\n<td>Essex PBE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>Self-contained portable breathing device - protects against smoke/fumes while fighting a cabin fire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantity / location</td>\n<td>One (1) PBE installed in the aft lavatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oxygen cylinders</td>\n<td>Two (2), holding a total of 36 liters of aviator-grade oxygen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duration</td>\n<td>15-minute supply of oxygen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Status indicator</td>\n<td>Blue = serviceable (checkmark); Magenta/pink = unserviceable (X)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In-use light</td>\n<td>Battery-operated: green = service, red = end of service</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#oxygen--protective-breathing-equipment-pbe\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"oxygen--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--overview-purpose\">Overview / Purpose</h2>\n<p><strong>The Oxygen System is about the storage of gaseous oxygen under high pressure and its delivery to crew and passengers as an alternate means of breathing in the event of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Depressurization</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Smoke/fumes</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>System architecture:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Two (2) identical tanks are plumbed together to form a single system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each bottle: aluminum cylinder wrapped in Kevlar for reinforcement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tanks have a capacity of 123.4 ft^3 each, pressurized to 1,800 +/-50 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Located underneath the floor, near the main entrance doorway.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A common line feeds both the cockpit (crew O2 masks) and the cabin (pax O2 masks).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--cylinder-storage-pressure\">Cylinder Storage &amp; Pressure</h2>\n<p>The two crew/pax oxygen bottles are located underneath the cabin floor near the main entrance doorway, each plumbed to its own regulator before joining the crew/cabin distribution lines.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of bottles</td>\n<td>Two (2), identical, plumbed together</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction</td>\n<td>Aluminum cylinder, Kevlar-wrapped for reinforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity</td>\n<td>123.4 ft^3 each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressure</td>\n<td>1,800 +/-50 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Location</td>\n<td>Underneath the floor, near the main entrance doorway</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Two separate distribution circuits (Crew and Pax) draw from the two bottles via the oxygen system panel; both are labeled and color-keyed on the overboard discharge indicator and service panel.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--pressure-regulation-relief-valves\">Pressure Regulation &amp; Relief Valves</h2>\n<p>Cylinder pressure regulators reduce system pressure to 55-80 psi prior to the supply line: 1,800 psi (bottle) -&gt; Regulator -&gt; 55-80 psi (supply line) -&gt; O2 masks.</p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen system pressure relief valves:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>High-pressure system: relieves above 2,775 psi (bottles and high-pressure lines).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Low-pressure system: relieves above 90 psi (bottle low-pressure supply line).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Thermal (temperature) relief: 225F.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pressure relief ports are teed together and connected to the overboard discharge indicator on the right side of the aircraft. A green (filled) indicator disc = OK; a white (empty) indicator disc = discharged. Checked during pre-flight and post-flight inspections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--servicing-1\">Servicing</h2>\n<p>The crew and pax oxygen tanks are serviced through a panel on the right side of the aircraft (maintenance function), which includes the oxygen filler valve and direct-reading gauges for Crew and Pax.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fill pressure: 1,800 psi @ 70F.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oxygen type: Aviator Breathing Oxygen, MIL-PRF-27210.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No oil or grease near oxygen fittings/panel (fire hazard) - clearly marked on the source diagrams.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O2 service panel gauges are direct-reading (no aircraft power required to read bottle pressure).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cockpit Oxygen System panel (separate from the maintenance service panel) has its own Crew and Passenger gauges plus an ON/OFF switch for passenger oxygen, and is DC-powered from L ESS DC and R ESS DC.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--system-test-indications\">System Test / Indications</h2>\n<p><strong>Oxygen Masks - crew mask/regulator functional test sequence:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Expected Indication</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Check oxygen supply pressure</td>\n<td>Gauge in green arc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Oxygen shutoff control ON</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Press and hold regulator \"press to test/reset\" for 1-2 seconds</td>\n<td>Blinker changes from yellow cross to solid gray (no leak); hissing stops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>While holding Press-To-Test, push Emergency</td>\n<td>Hissing resumes; release both and hissing stops</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Passenger oxygen system test panel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>PASS OXYGEN TEST switch - momentary test button.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PASS OXYGEN ON light (green) - illuminates to confirm passenger oxygen valve opened.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Associated CAS annunciation: \"PASSENGER OXYGEN ON.\"</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--crew-oxygen-masks-eros-1\">Crew Oxygen Masks (EROS)</h2>\n<p><strong>Crew oxygen is provided by EROS mask/regulator assemblies:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Three (3) crew oxygen mask/regulator assemblies (pilot, copilot, and observer/jump seat).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pilot and copilot: non-attachable goggles.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Observer (jump seat): non-attachable goggles.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Provide crew low-pressure oxygen.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Quick-donning: &lt;= 5 seconds, one-hand operation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Although crew and passengers share a common oxygen system (the same two bottles), the delivery methods differ - the crew regulator adds three selectable delivery modes:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Description</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal (N)</td>\n<td>Diluted mixture of O2 and cabin air</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>On-demand flow of 100% O2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency</td>\n<td>Pressurized flow of 100% O2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Crew masks are certified to a cabin altitude of 40,000 ft and automatically switch to positive pressure at 35,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mask assembly includes goggles, regulator, and an inflatable harness for rapid donning.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--passenger-oxygen-masks-1\">Passenger Oxygen Masks</h2>\n<p>Passenger oxygen masks provide cabin occupants oxygen until the airplane is able to descend to an altitude where ambient air is dense enough that supplemental oxygen is no longer required.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Certified to a cabin altitude of 40,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency descent only - do not provide sufficient oxygen above 34,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deployed either manually or automatically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The number of pax masks installed varies but will normally exceed the number of passenger seats by at least 10%.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Automatic deployment cabin-altitude triggers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cabin altitude of 14,750 +/-250 ft (normal).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cabin altitude of 15,750 +/-250 ft with ALT SELECT / HIGH ALT selected.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Passenger Oxygen ON/OFF/AUTO/MAN selector switches (one per side) allow the crew to manually command passenger oxygen; pulling on the mask lanyard removes a pin and activates O2 flow to that mask.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--therapeutic-oxygen\">Therapeutic Oxygen</h2>\n<p>Therapeutic oxygen is available through a receptacle in the cabin, for medical use unrelated to cabin depressurization.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Administering oxygen should be done in consultation with a medical doctor - call MedAire or a similar medical provider.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The THERAPEUTIC OXYGEN ON switch starts the flow of oxygen to the receptacle.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Annunciated via the CAS message \"THERAPEUTIC OXYGEN ON.\"</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--protective-breathing-equipment-pbe\">Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE)</h2>\n<p>A PBE (Essex PBE) is a self-contained portable/personal breathing device. It is designed to safeguard against the harmful effects of smoke/fumes by providing breathing oxygen while fighting a cabin fire.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>One (1) PBE is installed in the aft lavatory.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The PBE has two (2) oxygen cylinders that hold a total of 36 liters of aviator-grade oxygen.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PBEs provide a 15-minute supply of oxygen.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>PBE pre-flight status indication:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Blue status indicator dot = serviceable (OK).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Magenta/pink status indicator dot = unserviceable (do not use).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PBE service / end-of-service light: mounted slightly below eye level on the left inner side of the hood. Provides monitoring of oxygen activation, flow, and end of oxygen supply.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Green light = service (oxygen flowing normally).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Red light = end of service (oxygen supply exhausted).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen--requirements-operations-far-91-1\">Requirements / Operations (FAR 91)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Above 41,000 ft: one pilot must be on oxygen - FAR 91.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Above 35,000 ft: one pilot must be on oxygen if the other pilot leaves the cockpit - FAR 91.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crew and passenger masks are not approved for use above 40,000 ft cabin altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passenger masks will not provide sufficient oxygen above 34,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Above FL250, crew masks must be in the quick-donning position, allowing donning within five (5) seconds.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic deployment of passenger oxygen masks at 14,750 +/-250 ft (15,750 +/-250 ft with HIGH ALT selected).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Refer to AFM 01-35-10 to determine required oxygen quantity for departure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide, Oxygen System (01-35) section: the 40,000 ft mask-use limit and the 34,000 ft passenger-mask-adequacy limit match exactly. The Limitations guide adds one figure not present in these notes - \"prolonged operation above 25,000 ft cabin altitude with passengers aboard is not recommended\" - included here as supplementary AFM guidance, not a discrepancy.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"oxygen--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_oxygen_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Oxygen System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Oxygen System section (01-35), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for the mask certification/adequacy altitude figures (40,000 ft, 34,000 ft).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","oxygen--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","oxygen--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Oxygen System Overview","oxygen--oxygen-system-overview"],["h2","Pressure Regulation & Relief","oxygen--pressure-regulation-relief"],["h2","Servicing","oxygen--servicing"],["h2","Crew Oxygen Masks (EROS)","oxygen--crew-oxygen-masks-eros"],["h2","Passenger Oxygen Masks","oxygen--passenger-oxygen-masks"],["h2","Requirements / Operations (FAR 91)","oxygen--requirements-operations-far-91"],["h2","Portable / Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE)","oxygen--portable-protective-breathing-equipment-pbe"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","oxygen--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / Purpose","oxygen--overview-purpose"],["h2","Cylinder Storage & Pressure","oxygen--cylinder-storage-pressure"],["h2","Pressure Regulation & Relief Valves","oxygen--pressure-regulation-relief-valves"],["h2","Servicing","oxygen--servicing-1"],["h2","System Test / Indications","oxygen--system-test-indications"],["h2","Crew Oxygen Masks (EROS)","oxygen--crew-oxygen-masks-eros-1"],["h2","Passenger Oxygen Masks","oxygen--passenger-oxygen-masks-1"],["h2","Therapeutic Oxygen","oxygen--therapeutic-oxygen"],["h2","Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE)","oxygen--protective-breathing-equipment-pbe"],["h2","Requirements / Operations (FAR 91)","oxygen--requirements-operations-far-91-1"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","oxygen--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"pressurization":{"t":"Pressurization System Study Guide","sub":"Cabin Pressure Controller, TROV, and Emergency Descent Mode · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Pressurization System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (AFM 01-21-10, 01-35-10)","html":"<h1 id=\"pressurization--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Pressurization System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by function rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the relevant sections of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (AFM 01-21-10 Cabin Pressurization Control and 01-35-10 Oxygen) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (PSI differential limits, rate limits, CAS trip points, oxygen altitudes, EDM parameters) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Function: the Cabin Pressure Controller (CPC) and Cabin Pressure Acquisition Module (CPAM), the three operational modes (Auto/Semi/Manual), the Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve (TROV) and its power sources, the Pressure Relief Valve (PRV) safety function, rotor-burst structural protection, Emergency Descent Mode (EDM), Cabin Pressure Low CAS trip points, and oxygen requirements.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum cabin pressure differential of 10.48 psi (PRV second/\"MAX 2\" metering chamber, the hard structural limit) and the 0.3 psi maximum differential for taxi/takeoff/landing (to permit opening the emergency exit doors and main entrance door) both cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (01-21-10) and found consistent - no corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>All oxygen figures - one pilot on oxygen above 41,000 ft (FAR 91), masks not approved above 40,000 ft cabin altitude, one pilot on oxygen above 35,000 ft if the other leaves the cockpit, passenger masks insufficient above 34,000 ft cabin altitude, quick-donning position required above FL250, automatic passenger mask deployment at 14,750 ft +/-250 ft (15,750 ft +/-250 ft with HIGH ALT option) - cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (01-35-10) and found consistent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: the CPC/CPAM channel-comparison and fault-isolation logic (310 ft disagreement threshold), the TROV electrical power-source hierarchy (five-tier priority from dual generators down to the HMG and main batteries), the PRV two-chamber positive-relief scheme (10.28 psi \"MAX 1\" caution chamber, 10.48 psi \"MAX 2\" warning/structural chamber) and the -0.25 psi negative-relief chamber, the full Emergency Descent Mode (EDM) autopilot/autothrottle/FMA sequence, and the four-tier \"Cabin Pressure Low\" CAS trip-point table by landing field elevation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Note: this guide covers pressurization control specifically (TROV, CPC/CPAM, PRV, pressurization modes, EDM). Temperature/air conditioning control (packs, ECS) is addressed separately in the G550 Air Conditioning Study Guide, where one exists, to avoid duplication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"pressurization--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--psi-altitude-limits\">PSI / Altitude Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal cruise (typ.)</td>\n<td>FL510 -&gt; 10.17 psid -&gt; ~6,000 ft cabin altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAX 1 - caution (\"C\")</td>\n<td>10.28 psid - CAS \"CABIN DIFFERENTIAL - 10.28\" (PRV 1st metering chamber opens)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAX 2 - warning (\"W\")</td>\n<td>10.48 psid - CAS \"CABIN DIFFERENTIAL - 10.48\" (PRV 2nd metering chamber opens) - hard structural limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAX 3 - negative relief</td>\n<td>-0.25 psid (no CAS message)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAX 4 - taxi / takeoff / landing</td>\n<td>0.3 psid maximum, to allow opening of Emergency Exit Doors (EED, 6th/7th windows) and Main Entrance Door (MED)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-pressurization target</td>\n<td>0.25 psid (approx. LFE - 500 ft)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cabin rate of climb after takeoff</td>\n<td>500 fpm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPC channel disagreement threshold</td>\n<td>310 ft (channels compare to each other, then to CPAM)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pressurization--pressurization-profile-full-cycle\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: PSI / Altitude Limits &amp; Pressurization Profile</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--operational-modes\">Operational Modes</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Layer</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Selections</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CPC operational modes (3)</td>\n<td>AUTO -&gt; SEMI -&gt; FAULT/MANUAL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressurization modes (2)</td>\n<td>FLIGHT -&gt; LANDING</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pressurization--auto-mode\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Auto / Semi / Manual Modes</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--pressure-relief-valve-prv-summary\">Pressure Relief Valve (PRV) Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Trip Point</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive relief - 1st chamber (MAX 1)</td>\n<td>10.28 psid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive relief - 2nd chamber (MAX 2)</td>\n<td>10.48 psid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative relief (MAX 3)</td>\n<td>-0.25 psid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground pressurization limiting</td>\n<td>PRV opens 60 seconds after touchdown (WOW)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pressurization--pressure-relief-valve-prv\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Pressure Relief Valve (PRV)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--cabin-pressure-low-cas-trip-points\">Cabin Pressure Low CAS Trip Points</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Landing Field Elevation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cabin Altitude Trip</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault / Manual</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>&gt;= 8,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>&gt; 14,000 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 15,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>9,500 - 14,000 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 14,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>7,500 - 9,500 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 10,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>Sea Level - 7,500 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 8,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pressurization--cabin-pressure-low---cas-trip-points\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Cabin Pressure Low CAS Trip Points</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--oxygen-requirements\">Oxygen Requirements</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude / Trigger</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Requirement</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 41,000 ft</td>\n<td>One pilot must be on oxygen (FAR 91)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 40,000 ft cabin altitude</td>\n<td>Crew and passenger masks NOT approved for use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 35,000 ft, one pilot leaves cockpit</td>\n<td>Remaining pilot must be on oxygen (FAR 91)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 34,000 ft cabin altitude</td>\n<td>Passenger masks will not provide sufficient oxygen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; FL250</td>\n<td>Crew masks in quick-donning position (don within 5 sec)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14,750 ft +/- 250 ft cabin altitude</td>\n<td>Automatic passenger oxygen mask deployment (15,750 ft +/- 250 ft with HIGH ALT option)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> See AFM 01-35-10 to determine required oxygen quantity for departure.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pressurization--oxygen-requirements-operations\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Oxygen Requirements / Operations</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--emergency-descent-mode-edm-trigger\">Emergency Descent Mode (EDM) Trigger</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Autopilot</td>\n<td>ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aircraft altitude</td>\n<td>&gt;= FL400</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAS message</td>\n<td>\"CABIN PRESSURE LOW\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commanded lateral turn</td>\n<td>90 degrees left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed target (VMO)</td>\n<td>340 knots</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Altitude preselect</td>\n<td>15,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#pressurization--emergency-descent-mode-edm\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Emergency Descent Mode (EDM)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"pressurization--part-2---full-detail-by-function\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY FUNCTION</h1>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--overview-trov-concept\">Overview / TROV Concept</h2>\n<p>The pressurization system controls cabin pressure by modulating the Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve (TROV) in order to achieve and maintain an optimum cabin pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Pressurized areas / cabin air flow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Pressurized cabin and pressurized baggage compartment, separated from unpressurized areas by the aft primary bulkhead and aft secondary bulkhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cool, conditioned, dehumidified air enters the cabin from the L Pack and R Pack (via the ECS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cabin air exits the pressure vessel by being exhausted overboard via the TROV (a shutter-type door on the right side of the fuselage).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Normal cabin differential pressure is approximately 10.17 psi at FL510, corresponding to roughly a 6,000 ft cabin altitude.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--cabin-pressure-controller-cpc\">Cabin Pressure Controller (CPC)</h2>\n<p>The \"brains\" of the pressurization system - a microprocessor located in the REER (Right Electronic Equipment Rack) which makes all logical decisions. Located at the bottom of the REER.</p>\n<p><strong>The CPC receives input from:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>FMS 1 / FMS 2, or the Cabin Pressure Control Panel (CPCP)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Air Data System: ADS 1, ADS 2, ADS 3</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Modular Avionics Units: MAU 1, MAU 2, MAU 3</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cabin Pressure Acquisition Module (CPAM)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Weight-on-Wheels (WOW - Squat/\"G\")</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CPC has two (2) channels; one channel is active at a time while the other remains on standby. The active channel changes by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Removing power (Left or Right Main Battery)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cycling the main entrance door or baggage door</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Selecting / deselecting MANUAL mode</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--cabin-pressure-acquisition-module-cpam\">Cabin Pressure Acquisition Module (CPAM)</h2>\n<p>A self-contained unit located beneath the REER; a standalone source of cabin pressure information, independent of the CPC channels.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The two CPC channels continuously compare cabin pressure data with each other.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If channels 1 and 2 differ by 310 ft or more, they each compare against the CPAM (which acts as arbitrator).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Any channel that differs from the CPAM by 310 ft or more is failed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The CPCS (Cabin Pressure Control System) automatically selects the remaining operative channel.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Example from the notes: Channel 1 = 4,950 ft, Channel 2 = 5,300 ft (350 ft apart, exceeds 310 ft) -&gt; compare each to CPAM = 4,980 ft -&gt; Channel 2 (320 ft off CPAM) fails -&gt; \"CPCS CHANNEL 2 FAIL\".</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--auto-mode\">Auto Mode</h2>\n<p>Normal mode of operation (fully automatic). The CPC controls pressurization based on the pressurization schedule and receives input from FMS/MCDU data. Requires AC power (IDGs, APU GEN, or the HMG via L ESS DC -&gt; E-INV -&gt; ESS AC). Pressure profile modes (Flight / Landing) are automatic.</p>\n<p>Pre-pressurization to 0.25 psi (approx. LFE - 500 ft) is initiated if the door is closed AND any of the following occur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Taxi speed &gt; 9 knots, or</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Throttles advanced &gt;= 15 degrees Thrust Lever Angle (TLA), or</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Manually selected by the crew.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Caution:</strong> If returning to the ramp, deselect FLIGHT in order to depressurize the cabin prior to opening the main door.</em></p>\n<p>Cabin rate of climb after takeoff: 500 fpm. During descent, as the aircraft crosses 1,000 ft below cruise altitude, the pressure profile changes from FLIGHT to LANDING (CPC uses FMS data to calculate the required descent rate). If the aircraft levels off for three (3) minutes at or above FL250, the pressure profile reverts from LANDING back to FLIGHT.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pressurization--pressurization-profile-full-cycle\">Pressurization Profile (full cycle)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Phase</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detail</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Taxi out, &gt; 9 kt</td>\n<td>FLIGHT</td>\n<td>LFE - 500 ft, @ 300 fpm, delta-P 0.25 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Climb / cruise</td>\n<td>FLIGHT</td>\n<td>FL510 -&gt; 10.17 psi cabin differential</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,000 ft below cruise altitude (descent)</td>\n<td>LANDING</td>\n<td>LFE - 250 ft, @ 150-300 fpm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On WOW (landing)</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Cabin climbs @ 500 fpm to LFE; PRV opens 60 sec after touchdown</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--semi-mode\">Semi Mode</h2>\n<p>Alternate normal mode of operation (semi-automatic). Used when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>FMS data is not available or is invalid</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Directed by the checklist</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crew discretion (QFE operations, or the crew wants more control over the system)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The crew enters data via the Cabin Pressure Control Panel (CPCP) - Aircraft/Cabin altitude, Landing Elevation, Baro Correction, and Rate Limit. Requires the same AC power as Auto mode. Pressure profile modes (Flight / Landing) remain automatic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--manual-mode\">Manual Mode</h2>\n<p>If both CPC channels are inoperative (\"CPCS FAIL - SELECT MANUAL\"), the crew can still control pressurization manually by selecting FAULT/MANUAL (\"CABIN PRESSURE MANUAL\").</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The crew manually controls the outflow valve (TROV) in order to climb, maintain, and descend the cabin pressure altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The crew uses the MAN HOLD knob (DESCEND / CLIMB) on the Cabin Pressure Control panel.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Manual mode requires L ESS DC power: L Main Battery -&gt; L ESS DC -&gt; TROV (manual actuator).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Cabin Pressure Acquisition Module (CPAM) still provides cabin altitude, cabin rate, and cabin differential data to the crew even in Manual mode.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--thrust-recovery-outflow-valve-trov\">Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve (TROV)</h2>\n<p>Located on the right side of the fuselage. Controlled by the CPC in Auto/Semi modes; controlled by the crew in Manual mode. Shutter-type door design that minimizes drag. Three (3) electrical actuators (motors): two (2) AC and one (1) DC.</p>\n<p><strong>TROV motor power source priority (five tiers, from the notes):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Priority</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Power Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Path to CPC / TROV</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>L GEN ON + R GEN ON</td>\n<td>L IDG + R IDG -&gt; L Main AC + R Main AC -&gt; ESS AC -&gt; CPC 1/2 -&gt; TROV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>L GEN ON, R GEN OFF (or vice versa)</td>\n<td>Single-side IDG -&gt; Main AC -&gt; ESS AC -&gt; CPC -&gt; TROV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>APU GEN ON</td>\n<td>APU GEN -&gt; L/R Main AC -&gt; ESS AC -&gt; CPC -&gt; TROV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>MASTER / L ESS / R ESS ON + HMG switch ON</td>\n<td>HMG -&gt; L/R Stby AC -&gt; AUX TRU -&gt; L/R ESS DC -&gt; E-INV -&gt; ESS AC -&gt; CPC 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Main Batteries (L &amp; R) ON</td>\n<td>L Main Batt -&gt; L ESS DC -&gt; E-INV -&gt; ESS AC -&gt; CPC 1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--pressure-relief-valve-prv\">Pressure Relief Valve (PRV)</h2>\n<p>Located just in front of the TROV. The purpose of the PRV is to protect the aircraft from damage due to excessive positive or negative pressure. It independently senses cabin pressure using aircraft static ports located on the right side of the fuselage (of three static ports there: two feed the PRV, one feeds the CPAM).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detail</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Positive differential pressure relief</td>\n<td>Two metering sections. 1st chamber opens at 10.28 psi (MAX 1); 2nd chamber opens at 10.48 psi (MAX 2).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Negative differential pressure relief</td>\n<td>Opens at -0.25 psi (MAX 3). No CAS message is generated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Ground pressurization limiting</td>\n<td>PRV opens sixty (60) seconds after touchdown (WOW).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--rotor-burst-structural-protection\">Rotor Burst / Structural Protection</h2>\n<p>The physical location of the TROV, as well as the availability of the aft secondary pressure bulkhead, ensures that cabin pressurization is not affected by a rotor burst event.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A rotor burst is depicted striking the engine / depressurized baggage compartment area.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The aft primary and aft secondary bulkheads isolate the pressurized cabin from the baggage compartment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The pressurized cabin (10.17 psi) and the TROV remain unaffected because they sit forward of / independent from the area where a rotor burst would strike.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--emergency-descent-mode-edm\">Emergency Descent Mode (EDM)</h2>\n<p>EDM engages automatically when: Autopilot is ON, aircraft altitude is at or above FL400, and a \"CABIN PRESSURE LOW\" CAS message is generated.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Autothrottles engage if disengaged; throttles retard to idle; FMA power display on PFD -&gt; FLCH.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Guidance Panel (GP) lateral mode deselected; FMA lateral display -&gt; EDM; commands a 90-degree left turn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>GP vertical mode -&gt; FLCH; FMA vertical display -&gt; IAS with ASEL.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>GP speed mode -&gt; MAN; VMO (340 knots) displays in the speed window.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Altitude - 15,000 ft loaded in the preselect window.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>FMA sequence during descent: [340 | EDM FLCH IAS | 15,000] (AP 1 / AT 1 engaged). At level-off: FMA vertical display transitions ASEL -&gt; ALT and FMA speed display transitions 340 -&gt; 250, giving [250 | EDM IAS ALT | 15,000].</p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> EDM is canceled via the autopilot disconnect button, or by deselecting AP on the Guidance Panel.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--cabin-pressure-low---cas-trip-points\">Cabin Pressure Low - CAS Trip Points</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Landing Field Elevation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cabin Altitude Trip (\"CABIN PRESSURE LOW\")</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault / Manual</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>&gt;= 8,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>&gt; 14,000 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 15,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>9,500 ft - 14,000 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 14,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>7,500 ft - 9,500 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 10,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto / Semi</td>\n<td>Sea Level - 7,500 ft</td>\n<td>&gt;= 8,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pressurization--oxygen-requirements-operations\">Oxygen Requirements / Operations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Above 41,000 ft: one pilot must be on oxygen (FAR 91).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crew and passenger masks are NOT approved for use above 40,000 ft cabin altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Above 35,000 ft: one pilot must be on oxygen if the other pilot leaves the cockpit (FAR 91).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passenger masks will not provide sufficient oxygen above 34,000 ft cabin altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Above FL250: crew masks must be in the quick-donning position, which allows donning within five (5) seconds.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic deployment of passenger oxygen masks occurs at 14,750 ft +/- 250 ft cabin altitude (15,750 ft +/- 250 ft with the HIGH ALT option).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> See AFM 01-35-10 to determine the required oxygen quantity for departure.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"pressurization--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_pressurization_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Pressurization System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Cabin Pressurization Control (01-21-10) and Oxygen (01-35-10) sections, cross-checked and confirmed consistent for maximum cabin differential pressure (10.48 psi), maximum differential during taxi/takeoff/landing (0.3 psi), and all oxygen altitude figures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Air Conditioning Study Guide (this folder, where it exists) - covers temperature control (packs/ECS); this guide is scoped to pressurization control only, to avoid duplication.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","pressurization--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","pressurization--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","PSI / Altitude Limits","pressurization--psi-altitude-limits"],["h2","Operational Modes","pressurization--operational-modes"],["h2","Pressure Relief Valve (PRV) Summary","pressurization--pressure-relief-valve-prv-summary"],["h2","Cabin Pressure Low CAS Trip Points","pressurization--cabin-pressure-low-cas-trip-points"],["h2","Oxygen Requirements","pressurization--oxygen-requirements"],["h2","Emergency Descent Mode (EDM) Trigger","pressurization--emergency-descent-mode-edm-trigger"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY FUNCTION","pressurization--part-2---full-detail-by-function"],["h2","Overview / TROV Concept","pressurization--overview-trov-concept"],["h2","Cabin Pressure Controller (CPC)","pressurization--cabin-pressure-controller-cpc"],["h2","Cabin Pressure Acquisition Module (CPAM)","pressurization--cabin-pressure-acquisition-module-cpam"],["h2","Auto Mode","pressurization--auto-mode"],["h2","Semi Mode","pressurization--semi-mode"],["h2","Manual Mode","pressurization--manual-mode"],["h2","Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve (TROV)","pressurization--thrust-recovery-outflow-valve-trov"],["h2","Pressure Relief Valve (PRV)","pressurization--pressure-relief-valve-prv"],["h2","Rotor Burst / Structural Protection","pressurization--rotor-burst-structural-protection"],["h2","Emergency Descent Mode (EDM)","pressurization--emergency-descent-mode-edm"],["h2","Cabin Pressure Low - CAS Trip Points","pressurization--cabin-pressure-low---cas-trip-points"],["h2","Oxygen Requirements / Operations","pressurization--oxygen-requirements-operations"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","pressurization--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"electrical":{"t":"Electrical System Study Guide","sub":"AC / DC Generation, Distribution, and Standby/Emergency Power · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Electrical Power System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide, Electrical Power sec","html":"<h1 id=\"electrical--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Electrical System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Electrical Power section (01-24) of the G550 Limitations Study Guide already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (voltages, frequencies, generator/TRU ratings, battery specs, timings) in one place, with jump-links into Part 2.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: AC bus architecture and split-bus logic, BPCU/GCU control and protection, IDGs, the APU generator as backup AC, the Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG), external AC/DC power, TRUs and the DC system, the Ground Service Bus (GSB), main batteries, the E-Inverter, emergency batteries, and a Normal-Emergency power summary.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified against the Limitations Study Guide:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The Limitations guide (01-24) only documents Standby Electrical Power/HMG operating rules: do not operate the HMG when normal AC power is available (except L/R ESS DC bus failure procedures), HMG post-start functional test is permitted, and speed brakes on HMG power must be operated slowly (handle moved stowed-to-extended in 3 seconds or more). All three items are confirmed consistent with these notes (see HMG section) - no corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Limitations guide APU section separately lists \"Generator output: 40 kVA, sea level to 45,000 ft\" for the APU generator - consistent with the APU GEN rating documented here (40 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase, comes online at 99% RPM + 2 seconds).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: the full AC/DC bus architecture (split-bus with bus tie relays), the two Bus Power Control Units (BPCU) and four Generator Control Units (GCU) and their fault/reset logic, No-Break Power Transfer (NBPT) logic, IDG/external-power/TRU/GSB ratings and priority schemes, main and emergency battery specifications and duty cycles, and the E-Inverter (Standby Inverter).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"electrical--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--system-overview\">System Overview</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>AC power produced</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DC power produced</td>\n<td>28 VDC, via Transformer Rectifier Units (TRU)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AC power uses</td>\n<td>Motors, heaters, chargers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DC power uses</td>\n<td>Control functions / steady-state operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Networks</td>\n<td>Two (2) separate L / R systems/networks, each with its own AC and DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Split bus system</td>\n<td>Prevents a short on one side from affecting the other; operative side CAN power the inoperative side (via bus tie relays)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power Distribution Boxes (PDB)</td>\n<td>L PDB (in LEER) feeds L Main AC / Ess AC; R PDB (in REER) feeds R Main AC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Master control units</td>\n<td>Two (2) Bus Power Control Units (BPCU) - \"the brains\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total microprocessors</td>\n<td>Six (6): 2 BPCUs + 4 GCUs (L IDG, APU GEN, R IDG, HMG GEN)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#electrical--overview-system-architecture\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / System Architecture</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--generator-power-source-ratings\">Generator &amp; Power-Source Ratings</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Rating</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Output</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L / R IDG (engine-driven)</td>\n<td>40 kVA each</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; CSD holds generator at 12,000 RPM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APU GEN</td>\n<td>40 kVA</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; online at 99% RPM + 2 sec; 100% available on ground</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HMG GEN (Hydraulic Motor Generator)</td>\n<td>10 kVA</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; backup AC only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External AC Power (EXT PWR)</td>\n<td>30 kVA</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E-Inverter (Standby Inverter)</td>\n<td>1 kVA</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz; converts 28 VDC to 115 VAC, Phase A only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TRU (Transformer Rectifier Unit)</td>\n<td>250 amps</td>\n<td>Converts 115 VAC to 28 VDC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External DC Power</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>Powers all DC buses; can power the GSB</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#electrical--ac-bus-architecture-split-bus\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: AC Bus Architecture / Split Bus</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--main-emergency-battery-specs\">Main &amp; Emergency Battery Specs</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Main Batteries (L/R)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Emergency Batteries (E-BATT x4)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chemistry</td>\n<td>NiCad</td>\n<td>Sealed lead-acid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Count / weight</td>\n<td>2, 21 cells, 95 lb each</td>\n<td>4 (2x Avionics, 2x Lighting)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rating</td>\n<td>28 VDC, 53 amp/hour</td>\n<td>24 V, 9 amp/hour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Location</td>\n<td>Tail compartment</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turns on when</td>\n<td>Manually selected ON, or auto for APU start / AUX hyd pump / ESS DC backup</td>\n<td>ESS DC bus(es) &lt; 20 V, even momentarily</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Endurance</td>\n<td>30 min power to ESS DC buses after 2 failed APU start attempts</td>\n<td>~30 min power to L Emergency / R Emergency / Flight Instrument buses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min voltage to start APU</td>\n<td>22 V</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold-soak limit</td>\n<td>Remove from A/C if soaked &lt;= -20C; store between -20C and +40C</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recharge</td>\n<td>By L/R Main AC buses via L/R Batt Charger</td>\n<td>By integrated charger/TRU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#electrical--main-batteries\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Main Batteries</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#electrical--emergency-batteries\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Emergency Batteries</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--priority-logic-cheat-sheet\">Priority Logic Cheat-Sheet</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Scheme</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Priority Order</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>BPCU bus-power logic (general)</td>\n<td>ESS before MAIN / L before R</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AUX TRU backup priority (failed ESS/MAIN TRU)</td>\n<td>1. L ESS DC 2. R ESS DC 3. L MAIN DC 4. R MAIN DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground Service Bus (GSB) power source priority</td>\n<td>1. R Main DC 2. EXT DC 3. R Main Batt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No-Break Power Transfer (NBPT) requires</td>\n<td>An IDG AND no failure (matches phase of IDGs/APU GEN)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Break (non-NBPT) transfer occurs if</td>\n<td>No IDG available, and/or a GEN failure, engine failure, or fire handle pulled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Galley bus power source requirement</td>\n<td>1 GEN source on the ground; 2 GEN sources in the air</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> GCUs can be reset by cycling the associated generator switch. BPCU-protected buses reset via the AC/DC reset switch on the Electrical Power Control synoptic if the fault is no longer present.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#electrical--bus-power-control-units-bpcu-generator-control-units-gcu\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: BPCU / GCU</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"electrical--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--overview-system-architecture\">Overview / System Architecture</h2>\n<p><strong>The Electrical Power System produces both AC and DC power:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>115 VAC is generated in order to produce 28 VDC via Transformer Rectifier Units (TRU): 115 VAC -&gt; TRU -&gt; 28 VDC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AC power feeds motors, heaters, and chargers. DC power feeds control functions and steady-state operations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>There are two (2) separate systems/networks - L and R - each with its own AC and DC side.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Split bus system:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A split bus system prevents a short on one side from affecting the other side.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The operative side CAN power the inoperative side, via the bus tie relays (when closed).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Power Distribution Boxes (PDB): the L PDB feeds the L Main AC bus and (through it) the Ess AC bus; the R PDB feeds the R Main AC bus.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Four generation sources feed the AC buses through the bus tie relays: L IDG, APU GEN, EXT AC, and R IDG. The bus tie relays are closed for Normal operation (either operative source can back up the other side) and open for Emergency operation (each side isolated).</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--ac-bus-architecture-split-bus\">AC Bus Architecture / Split Bus</h2>\n<p><strong>AC generation and bus tree (top to bottom):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Sources: L IDG, APU GEN, EXT AC, R IDG - each feeds through a bus tie relay into L Main AC or R Main AC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L Main AC feeds the Ess AC bus (normally one-directional, L -&gt; R, shown with an arrow); Ess AC can also be backed up from R Main AC if L Main AC is unavailable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L Main AC feeds L Standby AC; R Main AC feeds R Standby AC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG GEN) sits between L Standby AC and R Standby AC and also feeds the AUX TRU.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Normal vs. Emergency bus-tie state:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NORMAL: bus tie relays CLOSED - an operative IDG side can power the inoperative side.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>EMERGENCY: bus tie relays OPEN - each side isolated to contain the fault.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>DC bus tree (top to bottom): L Ess TRU, L Main TRU, AUX TRU (center, backup), R Main TRU, R Ess TRU each feed their own DC bus (L Ess DC, L Main DC, R Main DC, R Ess DC); R Main DC also feeds the Ground Service Bus (GSB) and EXT DC ties in there too. L/R Main DC are backed up by L/R Main Batt via a dashed (emergency) path into L/R Ess DC.</p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--bus-power-control-units-bpcu-generator-control-units-gcu\">Bus Power Control Units (BPCU) / Generator Control Units (GCU)</h2>\n<p>The electrical power system is controlled by two (2) identical and interchangeable microprocessors called BPCUs (L BPCU, R BPCU) - \"the brains.\"</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Control and make all logical decisions for electrical distribution and protection - \"traffic cops,\" protectors of the buses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Close and open contactors and/or relays to efficiently supply power to the buses, and to protect/isolate the system from faults.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Output critical findings to the CAS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Provide protection, power, and logic to the AC/DC RESET switch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Monitor external AC/DC power quality before allowing it onto the aircraft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Control the No-Break Power Transfer (NBPT).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>BPCU logic: ESS before MAIN, L before R. Located in the Left/Right Electronic Equipment Rack (LEER / REER).</p>\n<p><strong>Fault detection, protection, and notification sequence (bus-level fault, e.g. L IDG):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Fault detected by L BPCU -&gt; bus contactor opened and locked out to protect the bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. L BPCU notifies the crew via CAS message: \"L AC Power Fail\" (with \"L AC Reset\" available).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Can be reset by the crew via the AC/DC RESET switch if the fault is no longer present.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generator Control Units (GCU): four (4) identical, independent, and interchangeable microprocessors - L IDG/GCU, APU GEN/GCU, R IDG/GCU (interchangeable with each other), and HMG GEN/GCU (NOT interchangeable with the others). GCUs control generator output quality (voltage, frequency, amperage) and provide fault protection. Located in the LEER and REER.</p>\n<p>If a GEN's voltage, frequency, or amperage falls outside parameters: the GCU takes the generator offline and notifies the associated BPCU, which notifies the crew via CAS (\"L/R AC Power Fail\" and \"L/R Generator Fail\"). A GCU can be reset by cycling the associated generator switch.</p>\n<p><strong>No-Break Power Transfer (NBPT):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Controlled by the L BPCU. Power transfer without a momentary interruption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Matches the phases of the IDGs and/or APU GEN before transferring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NO BREAK requires: an IDG AND no failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>BREAK (momentary interruption) occurs when: No IDG available, and/or a Failure - GEN failure, engine failure, or a fire handle pulled.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Dispatch with an IDG U/S is NOT permitted, per AFM OIS G550-2016-03 (as transcribed in the source notes; that OIS number is also referenced for APU sealant in the APU notes - confirm the current AFM OIS index before relying on this cross-reference).</em></p>\n<p>Galley buses require: one (1) GEN source on the ground; two (2) GEN sources in the air.</p>\n<p><strong>Generator switches:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>ON (pressed in): IDG powers its respective AC bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>OFF (pushed out, unpowered): produces \"L GENERATOR OFF\" CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pressed in but failed/isolated from its bus: produces \"R AC POWER FAIL\" CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--integrated-drive-generators-idg\">Integrated Drive Generators (IDG)</h2>\n<p>Two (2) engine-driven IDGs (L IDG, R IDG), located on the engine's accessory gearbox.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>IDG = Constant Speed Drive (CSD) + oil-cooled generator (oil cooled by fan air).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rated at 40 kVA; produces 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The CSD converts variable engine speed to a constant speed at the generator: 12,000 RPM.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--apu-generator-backup-ac\">APU Generator (Backup AC)</h2>\n<p>The APU provides an auxiliary source of: (1) electrical AC power on the ground, and (2) backup electrical AC power in flight.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The APU can be started with L Main Batt or R Main Batt power.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When the APU reaches 99% RPM + two (2) seconds, the APU generator comes online and can power all AC and DC buses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU GEN rated at 40 kVA; produces 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; 100% available at ground level up to 45,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Refer to AFM OIS G550-2016-03 (APU Sealant) for APU in-flight operation limitations - see the companion G550 APU Study Guide for the full APU in-flight restriction list.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--hydraulic-motor-generator-hmg-standby-power\">Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG) / Standby Power</h2>\n<p>The HMG is a backup AC generator and is part of the Standby Electrical Power System.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Available with MASTER ON, L ESS ON, R ESS ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Driven by the L Hydraulic System, or by the Power Transfer Unit (PTU, pump/motor) if the L system is unavailable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powers L Standby AC, R Standby AC, and (through them) the AUX TRU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rated at 10 kVA; produces 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase when the HMG switch is ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Produces AC power in the event of a dual IDG AND APU GEN failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the main landing gear wheel well.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> With the HMG ON, the thirty (30) minute limit on the main batteries is no longer a factor. The AUX TRU produces DC voltage and will power the L/R Ess DC buses when selected by the crew, with a ten (10) second interval between selections.</em></p>\n<p>HMG operation is permitted ONLY when normal AC power generation is not available (L IDG, R IDG, and APU GEN all unavailable). In this scenario the E-Inverter converts 28 VDC (from L/R Ess DC via the AUX TRU chain) to 115 VAC to power the Ess AC bus.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Cross-Check:</strong> Speed brake deployment on HMG power is OK as long as the handle is moved from stowed to fully extended in three (3) seconds or more (produces \"SPEED BRAKE EXTENDED\"). Cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (01-24) and confirmed consistent.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--external-ac-dc-power\">External AC / DC Power</h2>\n<p><strong>External AC power (EXT PWR):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Receptacle located on the right side of the fuselage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>30 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Can power all AC buses, and through the TRUs, all DC buses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The BPCU checks the quality of power before allowing it onto the aircraft.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>External DC power (EXT DC, annunciated AVAIL):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Receptacle located on the right side of the fuselage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powers all DC buses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Can be used to power the Ground Service Bus (GSB).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--transformer-rectifier-units-tru-dc-system\">Transformer Rectifier Units (TRU) / DC System</h2>\n<p>TRUs are powered by the L Main AC / R Main AC buses. A TRU converts 115 VAC to 28 VDC.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Ess TRU -&gt; L Ess DC. L Main TRU -&gt; L Main DC. R Main TRU -&gt; R Main DC (+ GSB). R Ess TRU -&gt; R Ess DC. AUX TRU is the backup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L Ess TRU, L Main TRU, R Main TRU, and R Ess TRU each normally power their own bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AUX TRU powers the L/R AUX DC buses and will take over the duties of a failed Ess or Main TRU using this priority: 1. L Ess DC, 2. R Ess DC, 3. L Main DC, 4. R Main DC (ESS before MAIN, L before R).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TRU switches allow the opposite Main AC bus to power a Main TRU that lost power due to failure of its own Main AC bus (e.g. L Main AC feeds R AC input to R Main TRU if R Main AC failed, and vice versa).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TRUs are rated at 250 amps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TRUs are located underneath the floor.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> TRU/cabin load-limit reference (OM 03-02-00): forward cabin temperature &gt;= 106F allows 50% load, &lt;= 95F allows 100% load; with a single pack operating, forward cabin temperature &lt; 106F allows 50% load. This ties cabin thermal load to electrical/TRU loading limits per the Operating Manual - confirm current OM figures before use.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--ground-service-bus-gsb\">Ground Service Bus (GSB)</h2>\n<p>Used for ground operations \"when you don't want to wake up the beast\" (i.e., without an APU start):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Refueling operations, engine oil servicing, potable water servicing, hydraulic fluid servicing, operation of wheel well lights.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three (3) GSB switches: Forward Exterior Switch Panel, System Monitor/Test Panel, Tail Compartment.</p>\n<p>Power source priority for the GSB: 1. R Main DC, 2. EXT DC, 3. R Main Batt.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The rotating beacon light is powered by the GSB when R Main Batt is the source of power.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>At least one of the following must be open when using a GSB switch: the Forward Exterior Switch Panel access, the Main Entrance Door, or the Tail Compartment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--main-batteries\">Main Batteries</h2>\n<p>Two (2) main batteries (L Main Batt, R Main Batt), located in the tail compartment.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NiCad, 21 cells, 95 lb each.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>28 VDC, 53 amp/hour.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Start the APU - uses L Main Batt only, but both battery switches must be selected ON. Minimum 22 V required to start the APU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Operate the AUX hydraulic pump - uses both L/R Main Batt.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Power the L/R Ess DC buses - uses both L/R Main Batt if no other source of DC power is available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Switchlights illuminate to indicate the batteries are discharging.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Main batteries can power the Ess DC buses for thirty (30) minutes after two (2) failed APU start attempts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Must be removed from the aircraft in cold-soaked conditions (&lt;= -20C) and stored in a location warmer than -20C and cooler than +40C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If &lt;= 22 V but not less than 7 V, the batteries can be charged with EXT DC power connected and battery switches ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Normally recharged by the L/R Main AC buses via the L/R Batt Charger, located in the tail compartment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--e-inverter-standby-inverter\">E-Inverter (Standby Inverter)</h2>\n<p>The Ess AC bus is normally powered by the L Main AC bus; if L Main AC is not available, Ess AC can also be powered by the R Main AC bus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The E-Inverter (E-INV), renamed Standby Inverter, is a backup source of AC power to the Ess AC bus (Phase A only) by converting 28 VDC to 115 VAC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rated 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 1 kVA. Located underneath the floor.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In the unlikely event that normal AC power (IDG) or backup AC power (APU GEN) is not available, the HMG can continue to power the E-INV (via L/R Ess DC -&gt; AUX TRU -&gt; HMG chain).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In the unlikely event that the HMG is also not available, the main batteries can power the E-INV (via L/R Ess DC).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Phase A (0A) powers: Cabin Pressure Channel 1, R Pitot heater, and Standby Pitot heater.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> In the event of failure, the E-INV can be deferred per the Minimum Equipment List (MEL). During the pre-flight inspection, with main battery switches pressed IN and no AC power available, E-INV operation can be confirmed via Cabin Pressure Control: FLIGHT/LANDING = LANDING, FAULT/MANUAL = MANUAL, AUTO/SEMI = AUTO (no FAULT light) when the E-INV is working.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--emergency-batteries\">Emergency Batteries</h2>\n<p>Emergency Power System - power of last resort.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Sealed lead-acid; approximately thirty (30) minutes of endurance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>24 V, 9 amp/hour.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Come ON when the L and/or R Ess DC bus voltage drops below 20 V, even momentarily.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Four (4) E-BATTs: 2x Avionics (each with a Charger TR), 2x Lighting (each with a Charger TR). Recharged by an integrated charger/transformer rectifier.</p>\n<p>Power the following buses: L Emergency, R Emergency, Flight Instrument.</p>\n<p><strong>After a Break power transfer, the E-BATTs will come ON. When ON (Lights/Av Power), the following equipment is powered:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency lighting - cabin emergency lights, exterior emergency lights.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pilot's Audio Control Panel (ACP).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>MCDU 1 - standby engine instruments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>MCDU 3 - backup radios (VHF 1 / NAV 1).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Two (2) clocks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Three (3) IRUs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Standby Flight Display (SFD).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Electronic Bearing &amp; Distance Indicator (EBDI).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Landing gear indication.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrical--normal---emergency-power-summary\">Normal - Emergency Power Summary</h2>\n<p>Source-by-source summary of what each power source keeps alive, from most-normal to most-degraded:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Source Available</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Buses Powered</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L IDG and R IDG (Normal)</td>\n<td>All AC and DC buses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L IDG or R IDG (one only)</td>\n<td>All AC and DC buses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APU GEN</td>\n<td>All AC and DC buses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HMG GEN</td>\n<td>L/R Standby AC, L/R Ess DC, and (via E-INV, 28 VDC -&gt; 115 VAC) Ess AC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L Main Batt / R Main Batt (2 failed APU start attempts)</td>\n<td>L/R Ess DC for 00:30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency batteries (Ess DC buses &lt; 20 V)</td>\n<td>L Emergency, R Emergency, Flight Instrument for approximately 00:30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h1 id=\"electrical--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_electrical_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Electrical Power System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\" (Author's books: \"An Aviator's Journey\" and \"Journey Around the World.\")</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Electrical Power section (01-24), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for the HMG operating rules (do not operate with normal AC available except L/R Ess DC bus failure; post-start HMG functional test permitted; speed brake &gt;= 3 seconds on HMG power) and the APU generator output (40 kVA, sea level to 45,000 ft).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","electrical--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","electrical--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","System Overview","electrical--system-overview"],["h2","Generator & Power-Source Ratings","electrical--generator-power-source-ratings"],["h2","Main & Emergency Battery Specs","electrical--main-emergency-battery-specs"],["h2","Priority Logic Cheat-Sheet","electrical--priority-logic-cheat-sheet"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","electrical--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / System Architecture","electrical--overview-system-architecture"],["h2","AC Bus Architecture / Split Bus","electrical--ac-bus-architecture-split-bus"],["h2","Bus Power Control Units (BPCU) / Generator Control Units (GCU)","electrical--bus-power-control-units-bpcu-generator-control-units-gcu"],["h2","Integrated Drive Generators (IDG)","electrical--integrated-drive-generators-idg"],["h2","APU Generator (Backup AC)","electrical--apu-generator-backup-ac"],["h2","Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG) / Standby Power","electrical--hydraulic-motor-generator-hmg-standby-power"],["h2","External AC / DC Power","electrical--external-ac-dc-power"],["h2","Transformer Rectifier Units (TRU) / DC System","electrical--transformer-rectifier-units-tru-dc-system"],["h2","Ground Service Bus (GSB)","electrical--ground-service-bus-gsb"],["h2","Main Batteries","electrical--main-batteries"],["h2","E-Inverter (Standby Inverter)","electrical--e-inverter-standby-inverter"],["h2","Emergency Batteries","electrical--emergency-batteries"],["h2","Normal - Emergency Power Summary","electrical--normal---emergency-power-summary"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","electrical--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"apu":{"t":"Auxiliary Power Unit (APU) Study Guide","sub":"Honeywell RE220 · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 APU system · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide and AFM APU limitations (01-49)","html":"<h1 id=\"apu--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 APU study notes (Honeywell RE220) into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by system rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the APU section of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (built from the FAA-approved AFM, Chapter 01-49) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (EGT, RPM, altitudes, starter limits, generator output, fuel burn) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by System: master switch logic, the Electronic Control Unit (ECU) and its two operating-limit modes, starting/starter limits, bleed air, electrical/generator, fuel supply, altitude envelope, in-flight operation restrictions, fire detection and extinguishing, shutdown sequence, and oil servicing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All limitation figures (EGT 1,050C start / 732C running, 106% max rotor speed, 45,000 ft max operating altitude, 39,000 ft guaranteed start, 22 V minimum battery voltage, 25,000 ft max altitude for BR710C4-11 assisted air start, 1,500 ft AGL for bleed-air-on takeoff) cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide APU section (01-49) and found consistent - no corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: ECU logic and two-mode operating-limits scheme (Non-Essential/Ground vs. Essential/In-Flight), the six APU operational modes, fire detection/extinguishing sequence, shutdown sequence with altitude-dependent RPM decay, and the surge control valve / bleed air augmentation valve (BAAV) altitude triggers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"apu--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"apu--apu-core-limits\">APU Core Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Honeywell RE220 - single-shaft, constant-speed gas turbine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control</td>\n<td>Electronic Control Unit (ECU), microprocessor with embedded software, located in AEER</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max EGT - start</td>\n<td>1,050C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max EGT - running</td>\n<td>732C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max rotor speed (RPM)</td>\n<td>106%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel burn</td>\n<td>264 lb/hour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enclosure</td>\n<td>Fireproof titanium case, contains a fire for 15 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground operation</td>\n<td>Unattended ground operation is prohibited</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#apu--overview-purpose\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Purpose</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--starting-starter-limits\">Starting / Starter Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Power Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main batteries (both selected ON; starter uses L Main Batt only)</td>\n<td>Minimum 22 V; 3 consecutive attempts then 1-hr cooldown; 6 successful consecutive starts at 10-min intervals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External DC power (EXT PWR)</td>\n<td>3 attempts with 15-min interval between, then 1-hr cooldown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire test - proper indications</td>\n<td>8 indications (2x fire warning lights, APU FIRE message, 2x fire handles/discharge, and associated CAS - see Part 2)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#apu--starting-starter-limits-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Starting / Starter Limits</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--altitude-envelope\">Altitude Envelope</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Event</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>45,000 ft</td>\n<td>Maximum operating altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>43,000 ft</td>\n<td>May attempt start in flight (not guaranteed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39,000 ft</td>\n<td>Guaranteed in-flight start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35,000 ft</td>\n<td>Bleed Air Augmentation Valve (BAAV) opens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30,000 ft</td>\n<td>Maximum altitude for APU-assisted engine start (general)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25,000 ft</td>\n<td>Maximum altitude for BR710C4-11 assisted air start (engine-specific limit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16,500 ft</td>\n<td>Surge control valve opens (prevents compressor stall on in-flight start)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,500 ft AGL</td>\n<td>Minimum altitude to select APU bleed air ON for takeoff (bleed-off takeoff), +1.4% gross weight - see Performance Handbook</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> APU bleed air is available to start engines on the ground or in flight from sea level up to 30,000 ft in general, but the BR710C4-11 engine specifically limits assisted air starts to 25,000 ft and below.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#apu--altitude-envelope-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Altitude Envelope</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--electrical-generator\">Electrical / Generator</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Generator output</td>\n<td>40 kVA, 3-phase, 115 V, 400 Hz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comes online</td>\n<td>At 99% RPM + 2 seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground role</td>\n<td>Primary AC power source on the ground</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In-flight role</td>\n<td>Backup AC power (dual/single IDG failure only)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#apu--generator-electrical-power\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Generator / Electrical Power</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--shutdown-sequence-timing\">Shutdown Sequence Timing</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cool-down Behavior</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>On ground / Non-Essential mode</td>\n<td>Cool-down bypassed; APU shuts down immediately on any discrepancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 20,000 ft</td>\n<td>RPM remains at 100% for 60 seconds, then ECU signals overspeed to shut down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;= 20,000 ft</td>\n<td>RPM decreases 1/2% per second for 60 seconds (to ~70% RPM), then ECU signals overspeed to shut down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After landing (Essential mode)</td>\n<td>APU remains in Essential mode 15 minutes before ECU shuts it down, if not needed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#apu--shutdown-sequence\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Shutdown Sequence</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"apu--part-2---full-detail-by-system\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"apu--overview-purpose\">Overview / Purpose</h2>\n<p><strong>The APU (Honeywell RE220) supplies an auxiliary source of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>On the ground: (1) electrical AC power, and (2) pneumatic bleed air for air conditioning and engine start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In flight: (1) backup electrical AC power, and (2) pneumatic bleed air for engine air-start assistance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powered by a dedicated single-shaft, constant-speed gas turbine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Controlled by an Electronic Control Unit (ECU) - a microprocessor with embedded software.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Self-monitoring; protects itself against faults with an automatic shutdown.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Enclosed in a fireproof titanium case, capable of containing a fire for 15 minutes (damage to other systems occurs beyond this).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cannot be used for cabin pressurization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"apu--apu-master-switch-pre-start-logic\">APU Master Switch / Pre-Start Logic</h2>\n<p>When the MASTER switch is selected ON, the ECU is powered and performs the following pre-start sequence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Pre-start Built-In-Test (BIT) is performed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU oil temperature is sensed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU fuel shutoff valve opens.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU air inlet door (left side) opens.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If all parameters are OK, the APU READY light illuminates. It is still possible to start the APU in the absence of an APU READY light, provided the air inlet door is visually confirmed open.</p>\n<p><em><strong>R Cowl Open Light:</strong> APU exhaust gases exhaust overboard on the aft lower right side of the fuselage, under the engine pylon. To prevent damage to the engine cowl during maintenance, the starter is inhibited whenever the R Engine Cowl is open (R COWL OPEN light) - but only on the ground; the starter is NOT inhibited in flight.</em></p>\n<p>APU oil tank is heated to +21C to +43C regardless of whether the APU is in use, so it is always ready for start.</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--electronic-control-unit-ecu\">Electronic Control Unit (ECU)</h2>\n<p>The ECU is the \"brains\" of the APU - a microprocessor with embedded software that automatically controls the APU and interfaces with subsystems. It is located in the Aft Electronic Equipment Rack (AEER) and is powered through the MASTER switch from one of two sources (the ECU selects automatically if both are available):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>APU Power 1: L Batt Bus</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU Power 2: R Ess DC</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The ECU performs four functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Maintains required RPM and safe EGT during start, acceleration, idle, and full-load operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Monitors operational limits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Controls the air inlet door.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Controls protective shutdowns.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Six operational modes: Test -&gt; Starting -&gt; Idle -&gt; On Speed -&gt; Cooldown -&gt; Shutdown (with On Speed splitting into Non-Essential/Ground and Essential/In-Flight sub-modes).</p>\n<p><strong>The ECU uses two sets of operating limits:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Behavior</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-Essential (On Ground)</td>\n<td>The APU automatically shuts down to protect itself in the event of any discrepancy: cool-down mode is bypassed, the fuel shutoff valve closes, and the APU shuts down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Essential (In Flight) - \"APU ESSENTIAL\"</td>\n<td>The APU will NOT automatically shut down for certain failures. The safe condition of the aircraft takes precedence over possible damage to the APU. If a fault is detected while operating in flight, the crew is notified via a CAS message, but the APU keeps running unless the crew commands a shutdown.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>If the APU is not needed in flight: shut it down with the STOP switch (restart is possible if still airborne). If the APU is needed: allow it to continue running - after landing it remains in Essential mode for 15 minutes before the ECU shuts it down automatically, if not needed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--starting-starter-limits-1\">Starting / Starter Limits</h2>\n<p>The APU has a fully automatic start sequence and can be started with either L MAIN BATT or R MAIN BATT power. Minimum main battery voltage for APU start is 22 V (the APU starter uses L MAIN BATT only, but both battery switches must be selected ON).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Power Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Attempt Limits</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main batteries</td>\n<td>Three (3) consecutive start attempts, followed by a one (1) hour cooling period.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main batteries (successive)</td>\n<td>Six (6) successful and consecutive starts, with a ten (10) minute interval between starts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External DC power (EXT PWR ON)</td>\n<td>Three (3) start attempts with a fifteen (15) minute interval between starts, followed by a one (1) hour cooling period.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Starts are possible from sea level to 43,000 ft; a guaranteed start is available at or below 39,000 ft (see Altitude Envelope).</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--bleed-air-system\">Bleed Air System</h2>\n<p>The APU provides bleed air on the ground (air conditioning and engine start) and in flight (assisted air starts). The APU Load Control Valve (LCV) allows high pressure/temperature air into the left and right pneumatic manifolds. APU bleed air plumbing connects directly to the right manifold; selecting APU bleed air automatically opens the isolation valve to allow bleed air into the left manifold as well.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>After an APU ground start, the LCV will not open for 60 seconds, allowing the APU to operate lightly loaded (low EGT) - unless EGT is already warm (&gt;149C), in which case the 60-second delay is removed and APU pneumatic air is available immediately.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU bleed air is available immediately to restart an engine in flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>APU bleed air is available to start engines on the ground or in flight from sea level to 30,000 ft; the BR710C4-11 engine limits assisted air starts to 25,000 ft and below.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Bleed-Off Takeoff:</strong> For a bleed-off takeoff (engine bleed air OFF, APU bleed air ON), APU bleed air may be selected ON at 1,500 ft AGL per the QRH Alternate Normals Bleed Off Takeoff procedure; this adds about 1.4% gross weight per the Performance Handbook.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--generator-electrical-power\">Generator / Electrical Power</h2>\n<p>The APU generator (APU GEN) runs at a constant speed and provides 40 kVA, 3-phase, 115 V, 400 Hz. When the APU reaches 99% RPM plus two (2) seconds, the APU generator comes online and can power all AC and DC buses.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>On the ground: APU GEN is the primary AC power source (100% at ground level).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In flight: APU GEN provides backup AC power, usable up to 45,000 ft, and is only permitted in flight for single-IDG failure or dual-IDG failure (see In-Flight Operation Restrictions).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"apu--fuel-supply\">Fuel Supply</h2>\n<p>Fuel is normally supplied to the APU from the left fuel manifold, but can also be supplied from the right manifold by temporarily opening the fuel crossflow valve (annunciated by \"FUEL CROSSFLOW VALVE OPEN\").</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--altitude-envelope-1\">Altitude Envelope</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Altitude</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Event</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>45,000 ft</td>\n<td>Maximum operating altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>43,000 ft</td>\n<td>May start in flight (not guaranteed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39,000 ft</td>\n<td>Guaranteed in-flight start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35,000 ft</td>\n<td>Bleed Air Augmentation Valve (BAAV) opens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30,000 ft</td>\n<td>Maximum altitude, engine start (general) - *BR710C4-11 limited to &lt;= 25,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16,500 ft</td>\n<td>Surge control valve opens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,500 ft AGL</td>\n<td>Engine bleed air OFF takeoff (APU bleed air ON) minimum altitude</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>APU Surge Control Valve: opens whenever the APU is started in flight at or above 16,500 ft, to prevent a compressor stall. Exhausts through the surge valve exhaust pipe, next to the APU exhaust on the aft fuselage.</p>\n<p>APU Bleed Air Augmentation Valve (BAAV): opens at or above 35,000 ft. Introduces 400F engine bleed air into the APU intake, with a sixteen (16) second delay prior to APU start.</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--in-flight-operation-restrictions\">In-Flight Operation Restrictions</h2>\n<p><strong>In-flight operation of the APU is prohibited except for the following conditions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Single IDG failure (L GEN or R GEN OFF, corresponding IDG failed) - APU GEN backs up the affected side via the bus tie relays and Emergency AC Bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Dual IDG failure (both L GEN and R GEN OFF, both IDGs failed).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single engine failure (L Engine Fail or R Engine Fail).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Dual engine failure (L-R Engine Fail) - refer to AFM OIS G550-2016-03, APU Sealant.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These are the only in-flight APU operation scenarios documented in these notes. Always confirm the current AFM/QRH abnormal and emergency procedures before relying on APU-in-flight guidance.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--apu-fire-detection-system\">APU Fire Detection System</h2>\n<p>The APU overheat/fire detection system consists of a hermetically sealed helium-filled tube secured to the top of the APU enclosure. As enclosure temperature increases, the gas in the tube expands and pressure increases; a large temperature increase over a small length of tube indicates a fire and trips the pressure sensor, generating the APU FIRE indication (2x fire warning lights, \"APU FIRE\" CAS message, 2x fire handles).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fire bell sounds on the ground only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The ECU responds to a confirmed APU fire by: closing the APU fuel supply valve, closing the Load Control Valve, shutting off the APU generator, and closing the APU inlet door.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"apu--apu-fire-extinguishing-system\">APU Fire Extinguishing System</h2>\n<p>Powered by the L ESS DC bus (down to the main batteries). The guarded fire-extinguishing discharge switch is located on the APU control panel.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Discharges the left fire bottle (Halon) into the APU compartment; only one shot is available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Discharge generates the CAS message \"L FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE.\"</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"apu--apu-fire-test\">APU Fire Test</h3>\n<p>A proper test produces eight (8) indications: 2x fire warning (W) lights, \"APU FIRE\" message, \"APU FIRE DETECTOR FAIL\" message, and 2x fire handles (C). If on the ground, a fire bell (located in the nose wheel well) will also sound.</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--shutdown-sequence\">Shutdown Sequence</h2>\n<p>When the STOP switch is pressed, the OFF legend illuminates and the shutdown sequence commences:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Above 20,000 ft</strong></th>\n<th><strong>At/Below 20,000 ft</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>Unloads electrics and bleed air</td>\n<td>Unloads electrics and bleed air</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>APU enters cool-down mode; RPM remains at 100% for 60 seconds</td>\n<td>APU enters cool-down mode; RPM decreases 1/2% per second for 60 seconds (to ~70% RPM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>ECU signals an overspeed condition to shut down the APU</td>\n<td>ECU signals an overspeed condition to shut down the APU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>Any faults detected - CMC and/or CAS</td>\n<td>Any faults detected - CMC and/or CAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>APU air inlet door closes</td>\n<td>APU air inlet door closes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>When the APU MASTER switchlight is pressed at or below 70% RPM: (A) MASTER switch legend extinguishes, (B) APU fuel shutoff valve closes, (C) APU immediately shuts down, (D) inlet door begins to close. If the APU is operating, pressing the APU switchlight will shut down the APU immediately - this is NOT recommended (bypasses the normal cool-down sequence).</p>\n<h2 id=\"apu--oil-servicing\">Oil Servicing</h2>\n<p>APU oil quantity check: performed 15 to 30 minutes after shutdown, using either the Ground Service Synoptic window or the fluid quantity indicator in the tail compartment.</p>\n<h1 id=\"apu--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_auxiliary_power_unit_(apu)_system.pdf - handwritten G550 APU study notes (Honeywell RE220), Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - APU section (01-49), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for all limitation figures (EGT, RPM, altitudes, battery voltage, starter attempt limits, generator output).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","apu--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","apu--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","APU Core Limits","apu--apu-core-limits"],["h2","Starting / Starter Limits","apu--starting-starter-limits"],["h2","Altitude Envelope","apu--altitude-envelope"],["h2","Electrical / Generator","apu--electrical-generator"],["h2","Shutdown Sequence Timing","apu--shutdown-sequence-timing"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM","apu--part-2---full-detail-by-system"],["h2","Overview / Purpose","apu--overview-purpose"],["h2","APU Master Switch / Pre-Start Logic","apu--apu-master-switch-pre-start-logic"],["h2","Electronic Control Unit (ECU)","apu--electronic-control-unit-ecu"],["h2","Starting / Starter Limits","apu--starting-starter-limits-1"],["h2","Bleed Air System","apu--bleed-air-system"],["h2","Generator / Electrical Power","apu--generator-electrical-power"],["h2","Fuel Supply","apu--fuel-supply"],["h2","Altitude Envelope","apu--altitude-envelope-1"],["h2","In-Flight Operation Restrictions","apu--in-flight-operation-restrictions"],["h2","APU Fire Detection System","apu--apu-fire-detection-system"],["h2","APU Fire Extinguishing System","apu--apu-fire-extinguishing-system"],["h2","Shutdown Sequence","apu--shutdown-sequence"],["h2","Oil Servicing","apu--oil-servicing"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","apu--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"fuel":{"t":"Fuel System Study Guide","sub":"Two Wing Tanks, Four Hoppers, Two Boost Pumps per Hopper · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Fuel System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide Fuel section","html":"<h1 id=\"fuel--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Fuel System study notes (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Fuel section of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (built from the FAA-approved AFM, Chapter 01-28) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (capacities, weights, temperatures, imbalance limits) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: wing tank structure, capacities/weights/refueling, hopper tanks, boost pumps, hydraulic/fuel heat exchangers, fuel shutoff valves, fuel &amp; engine fuel temperature (including the Heated Fuel Return System), fuel imbalance indications and balancing procedures, the engine fuel system, fuel filtration, APU fuel supply, HMG/emergency electrical fuel-system power, and zero fuel weight/CG/ground fueling notes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All limitation figures cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide Fuel section (01-28) and found fully consistent - no corrections needed: total usable fuel 41,300 lb / 18,734 kg / 6,118 US gal (matches the source notes' capacity figure exactly at 60F / 6.767 lb per gallon); maximum fuel imbalance 1,000 lb at takeoff and 2,000 lb in flight; fuel tank temperature warning/caution thresholds (-37C low warning, -35C to -36C low caution, +54C high warning); the cold-fuel descent rule (tank temp &lt;= -30C in flight with &lt; 5,000 lb total remaining, descend to SAT -60C or warmer, maintain M0.80 or greater); and engine fuel temperature limits (-40C min, +140C max continuous, +165C max for 15 minutes).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: hopper tank capacity (190 gal / 1,283 lb each), boost pump bus assignments and amperage, ejector pump flow rate (4,450 lb/hr), the fuel imbalance display thresholds (arrows appear at 100 lb, full-scale deflection at 500 lb, full scale turns amber at 1,000 lb - distinct from the 1,000/2,000 lb crew limits above), full Heated Fuel Return System (HFRS) logic and inhibit conditions, the engine fuel system pressure schedule (25 psi boost -&gt; 150 psi LP -&gt; 1,200 psi HP -&gt; FMU -&gt; HP SOV -&gt; 20 nozzles), fuel filtration bypass differential (25 psi), APU fuel manifold routing, and which fuel-system components remain powered under HMG/emergency electrical configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"fuel--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--capacities-weights\">Capacities &amp; Weights</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Total usable fuel</td>\n<td>41,300 lb (60F, fuel density 6.767 lb/gal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Usable fuel - each wing tank (L/R)</td>\n<td>20,650 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hopper tank capacity (each of 4)</td>\n<td>190 gal / 1,283 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum ramp weight (fuel upload limit)</td>\n<td>91,400 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum takeoff weight (MTOW, fuel upload limit)</td>\n<td>91,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zero Fuel Weight (ZFW)</td>\n<td>54,500 lb (must stay within ZFW C.G. envelope, AFM 01-03-70)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing tank compartments</td>\n<td>Six (6) per tank, separated by baffle ribs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Refueling methods</td>\n<td>Single-point pressure refueling (35-55 psi); overwing gravity refueling</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Fuel may be uploaded beyond 41,300 lb provided ramp weight, MTOW, and loaded C.G. limits are respected.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fuel--overview-tank-structure\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Tank Structure &amp; Capacities</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--fuel-tank-engine-fuel-temperature-limits\">Fuel Tank &amp; Engine Fuel Temperature Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temperature - Warning (low)</td>\n<td>&lt;= -37C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temperature - Caution (low)</td>\n<td>-35C to -36C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temperature - Warning (high)</td>\n<td>&gt;= +54C (delay takeoff)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine fuel temperature - minimum</td>\n<td>-40C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine fuel temperature - max (continuous)</td>\n<td>+140C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine fuel temperature - max (15-min transient)</td>\n<td>+165C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jet fuel freezing point</td>\n<td>-40C (-40F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HFRS auto ON / auto OFF</td>\n<td>ON at 0C; OFF at 10C fuel tank temperature</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cold fuel rule: if fuel tank temp &lt;= -30C in flight with &lt; 5,000 lb total fuel remaining, descend to where SAT is -60C or warmer and maintain minimum Mach 0.80.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fuel--fuel-engine-fuel-temperature-heated-fuel-return-system-hfrs\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Fuel &amp; Engine Fuel Temperature / HFRS</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--fuel-imbalance\">Fuel Imbalance</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Imbalance arrows first appear</td>\n<td>100 lb imbalance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Imbalance arrows - full-scale deflection</td>\n<td>500 lb imbalance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Imbalance arrows - full scale turns amber (caution)</td>\n<td>1,000 lb imbalance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum fuel imbalance - takeoff</td>\n<td>1,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum fuel imbalance - in flight</td>\n<td>2,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum fuel imbalance - gravity refueling</td>\n<td>2,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Proceed with balancing before imbalance reaches</td>\n<td>1,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The 100/500/1,000 lb figures are EICAS display thresholds (arrow appearance, full-scale, amber); the 1,000/2,000 lb figures are the AFM crew limits - do not confuse the two scales.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fuel--fuel-imbalance-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Fuel Imbalance</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--boost-pumps-hopper-tanks\">Boost Pumps &amp; Hopper Tanks</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Boost pumps</td>\n<td>Two (2) per hopper - one Main, one Alternate; identical and interchangeable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boost pump discharge pressure</td>\n<td>25 psi (low pressure)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boost pump current draw</td>\n<td>&lt; 25 amps each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main pump power source</td>\n<td>L Main -&gt; L ESS DC; R Main -&gt; R ESS DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alternate pump power source</td>\n<td>L Alt -&gt; L MAIN DC; R Alt -&gt; R MAIN DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Without boost pump pressure - below 20,000 ft</td>\n<td>Engine suction-feeds successfully</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Without boost pump pressure - at/above 20,000 ft</td>\n<td>Engine runs erratically and flames out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ejector pump flow (hopper fill, no moving parts)</td>\n<td>4,450 lb/hour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> All operable boost pumps must be selected ON for all phases of flight unless fuel balancing is in progress or as directed by the checklist.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fuel--hopper-tanks\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Hopper Tanks / Boost Pumps</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"fuel--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--overview-tank-structure\">Overview / Tank Structure</h2>\n<p>The fuel system consists of two (2) wing tanks which store all fuel and feed the main engines and the APU via low-pressure, electrically-driven boost pumps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The wing tanks are part of the internal wing structure and do not have bladders.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each wing has +3 degrees of dihedral.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each wing tank is divided into six (6) compartments by baffle ribs, which prevent rapid C.G. changes due to fuel slushing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flapper valves at the bottom of each baffle rib allow fuel to travel one-way, from compartment to compartment, towards the fuel hoppers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel below the flapper valves moves towards the hoppers through small orifices called weep holes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fuel Vent System</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The tanks are vented via NACA vents to provide positive internal pressure and to protect against over- and under-pressurization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The vent system is fully automatic and requires no electrical power.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>During refueling, vapors and air escape through the vents as fuel enters the tanks (prevents wing rupture from positive pressure).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>During fuel consumption in flight, air enters the tanks through the vents (prevents wing collapse from negative pressure).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Plenum (Vent Tank)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The plenum, also known as the vent tank, catches fuel vented from the fuel vent system during transient maneuvers; this fuel is drawn back into the tanks when stable flight resumes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The plenum allows for a two percent (2%) fuel expansion.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The plenum should be drained of any fuel prior to takeoff, via the fuel tank plenum drain.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--capacities-weights-refueling\">Capacities, Weights &amp; Refueling</h2>\n<p>Total fuel capacity: 41,300 lb, at 60F and a fuel density of 6.767 lb/gallon.</p>\n<p>It may be possible to upload fuel quantities in excess of 41,300 lb, provided the following are not exceeded:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum ramp weight: 91,400 lb.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum takeoff weight (MTOW): 91,000 lb.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Loaded aircraft remains within C.G. limit.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Refueling methods:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Single-point pressure refueling (35-55 psi).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Overwing gravity refueling.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Zero Fuel Weight (ZFW) &amp; C.G. (AFM 01-03-70)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Zero Fuel Weight: 54,500 lb.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ZFW C.G. must fall within the ZFW C.G. envelope (approximately 50,000-54,500 lb across roughly 35-45% MAC); the fueled airplane C.G. will then remain within limits for taxi, takeoff, in flight, and landing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Ground Fueling:</strong> During ground fueling operations: weather radar must be selected OFF (both the fueling aircraft and, if within 300 ft, any other aircraft with radar on); the fuel truck and the aircraft must be bonded.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--hopper-tanks\">Hopper Tanks</h2>\n<p>The hopper tanks are segregated tanks within the wing tanks, located adjacent to the centerline rib at the lowest point within each wing tank. It is from the hopper tanks that fuel is drawn to feed the engines and the APU.</p>\n<p><strong>Hopper tanks are kept full via two independent means:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Flapper-type valves (gravity): three (3) per hopper, allowing one-way gravity flow of fuel from the wing tank into the hopper.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Ejector pumps (no moving parts): use motive flow from boost pump pressure to draw fuel from the wing tanks into the hopper tanks; low-pressure, high-volume; deliver a steady 4,450 lb/hour.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hopper tank capacity: 190 gallons / 1,283 lb each.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Excess fuel in the hoppers can spill back into the wing tanks via a gap above the hopper walls.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--boost-pumps\">Boost Pumps</h2>\n<p>The hopper tanks contain the electrically-driven boost pumps, which deliver low-pressure (25 psi) fuel to the engines and APU. There are two (2) boost pumps per hopper - Main and Alternate - identical and interchangeable, located in the wheel well and attached to the aft portion of the hopper.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Pump</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Power Source</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L Main boost pump</td>\n<td>L ESS DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L Alternate boost pump</td>\n<td>L MAIN DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Main boost pump</td>\n<td>R ESS DC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Alternate boost pump</td>\n<td>R MAIN DC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Each boost pump draws less than 25 amps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>All operable boost pumps must be selected ON for all phases of flight, unless fuel balancing is in progress or as directed by the checklist.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Without boost pump pressure: below 20,000 ft the engine will suction-feed; at or above 20,000 ft the engine will run erratically and flame out.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Pump Switch Indications:</strong> Boost pump switch indications: switch pushed out and unpowered shows \"OFF\"; switch pushed in and operating shows blank; switch pushed in but failed shows blank and generates the CAS message \"L (or R) ALT FUEL PUMP FAIL.\"</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--hydraulic-fuel-heat-exchangers\">Hydraulic / Fuel Heat Exchangers</h2>\n<p>The hopper tanks also contain the hydraulic fluid-to-fuel heat exchangers. The heat exchanger unit for each hydraulic system is installed inside the offside fuel hopper (e.g., the L Hyd Sys heat exchanger is inside the Right hopper, and the R Hyd Sys heat exchanger is inside the Left hopper).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Hot hydraulic fluid flows continuously through the heat exchanger without any pilot input.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hot hydraulic fluid is cooled while cold fuel in the hopper is warmed up - a mutually beneficial exchange.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--fuel-shutoff-valves-sov\">Fuel Shutoff Valves (SOV)</h2>\n<p>There are three (3) fuel shutoff valves (SOV): (1) Left engine (left wheel well), (2) Right engine (right wheel well), and (3) APU (no visible indication of valve position). All are located in the wheel well, attached to the aft portion of the hopper.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Main engine SOV is operated by the respective FIRE handle in the cockpit and powered by its respective DC ESS bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SOV position indicator (open/closed) is visible in the wheel well for the L and R engine valves.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The APU fuel shutoff valve is located in the left wheel well and has no cockpit position indication.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--fuel-engine-fuel-temperature-heated-fuel-return-system-hfrs\">Fuel &amp; Engine Fuel Temperature / Heated Fuel Return System (HFRS)</h2>\n<p>Jet fuel has a freezing point of -40C (-40F).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temperature - Warning (low)</td>\n<td>&lt;= -37C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temperature - Caution (low)</td>\n<td>-35C to -36C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temperature - Warning (high)</td>\n<td>&gt;= +54C (delay takeoff)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine fuel temperature - max (15-min transient) / max (continuous) / min</td>\n<td>+165C / +140C / -40C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Cold Fuel Rule:</strong> If in flight with a fuel tank temperature of -30C and less than 5,000 lb total fuel remaining: descend to an altitude where SAT is -60C or warmer and maintain a speed of Mach 0.80 or greater.</em></p>\n<p>The HFRS prevents fuel tank temperatures from getting too cold during long-range, high-altitude flights. It sends fuel heated by the Fuel Oil Heat Exchanger (FOHE) into the wing tanks. The FOHE cools down hot engine oil and warms up cold fuel.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Controlled by FADEC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Auto ON at 0C fuel tank temperature; Auto OFF at 10C fuel tank temperature.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Delivers three (3) gallons of heated fuel at 50C per minute.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>HFRS is inhibited under the following conditions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>a) Fuel tank temperature &gt; 10C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>b) Crossflow valve open.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>c) Engine thrust lever setting at high power (&gt; 2,250 pph).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>d) HFRS switch selected OFF.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>e) Engine FIRE handle pulled / not stowed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>f) Low fuel pressure / quantity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>g) Engine RUN switch OFF.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--fuel-imbalance-1\">Fuel Imbalance</h2>\n<p>Fuel imbalance arrows appear on the Fuel Qty display when a fuel imbalance condition exists; arrow color and deflection indicate severity, and the higher-quantity side shows the higher arrow.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Threshold</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Display Behavior</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>100 lb imbalance</td>\n<td>Arrows first appear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>500 lb imbalance</td>\n<td>Full-scale arrow deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,000 lb imbalance</td>\n<td>Full-scale arrows turn amber (caution)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Maximum Fuel Imbalance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>In flight</td>\n<td>2,000 lb (proceed with balancing before imbalance reaches 1,000 lb)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Takeoff</td>\n<td>1,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gravity refueling</td>\n<td>2,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Two methods are available to balance fuel:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Method 1 - Inter-Tank Valve</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Autopilot ON, level flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Manually adjust rudder trim towards the heavy wing (approximately 1/2 ball displacement).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Open Inter-Tank Valve and monitor fuel progress.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Close Inter-Tank Valve when within approximately 200 lb of balance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>5. Retrim rudder.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Method 2 - Crossflow Valve</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Note: ensure fuel tank temperature is above 0C prior to turning a boost pump OFF.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>1. Open Crossflow Valve.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Turn OFF boost pumps, one at a time, on the lighter wing (this allows fuel from the heavy tank to feed both engines).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Turn ON boost pumps as balance is approached.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Close Crossflow Valve when desired balance is achieved.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Crossflow Valve Timer:</strong> The crossflow valve has a five (5) minute timer to alert the crew that it is still open: the CAS message \"FUEL CROSSFLOW VALVE OPEN\" turns amber (caution) with a double-chime aural tone, and the crossflow valve on the fuel synoptic page also turns amber. After reassessing the imbalance, reset the timer by cycling the crossflow valve closed and, if required, reopening it.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Do not use boost pumps to balance fuel when tank temperature is at or below 0C (cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide Fuel section - consistent).</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--engine-fuel-system\">Engine Fuel System</h2>\n<p>Metered fuel flows from the tank boost pumps to the fuel nozzles; introduction of fuel is controlled by FADEC. Low-pressure (LP) and high-pressure (HP) pumps are driven by the engine's accessory gearbox. The Fuel/Oil Cooler (FCOC) extracts heat from hot engine oil.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detail</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Boost pump discharge</td>\n<td>25 psi, low pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LP pump</td>\n<td>Draws from boost pump; can suction-feed the engine at or below 20,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FCOC</td>\n<td>150 psi; extracts heat from hot engine oil into the fuel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HP pump</td>\n<td>Boosts to 1,200 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FMU (Fuel Metering Unit)</td>\n<td>Meters fuel; fuel return line runs at 50C back to the wing tank (via Fuel Return OFF/AUTO valve)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HP SOV</td>\n<td>Final shutoff before the fuel nozzles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel nozzles</td>\n<td>Twenty (20) nozzles per engine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--fuel-filtration\">Fuel Filtration</h2>\n<p>The fuel filtration system prevents contaminants from entering the wing tanks and engines. Filtration is accomplished at four points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. The gravity fueling adapter/filter (overwing gravity refueling).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. The inlets of all four (4) boost pumps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Prior to the HP pump (LP filter).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Prior to the Fuel Metering Unit (FMU).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Filter Bypass Indications:</strong> A 25 psi differential across a fuel filter indicates an impending bypass, annunciated as \"L FUEL FILTER\" or \"R FUEL FILTER\" (impending bypass in the indicated engine), or \"L-R FUEL FILTER\" (impending bypass in both engines).</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--apu-fuel-supply\">APU Fuel Supply</h2>\n<p>Fuel is normally supplied to the APU from the left fuel manifold, but can also be supplied from the right manifold by temporarily opening the fuel crossflow valve (annunciated by \"FUEL CROSSFLOW VALVE OPEN\").</p>\n<h2 id=\"fuel--hmg-emergency-electrical-operations\">HMG / Emergency Electrical Operations</h2>\n<p>The Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG) is an emergency AC/DC power source. With the HMG switch ON (Master, L ESS, R ESS all selected ON), the HMG generator/GCU feeds L and R Standby AC directly and, via the AUX TRU, the L ESS DC and R ESS DC buses (which in turn power an emergency inverter to L/R ESS AC).</p>\n<p><strong>Because Main boost pumps, the Inter-Tank Valve, and the Crossflow Valve are all powered from the ESS DC buses, these remain available under HMG/emergency electrical configuration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Main boost pump - powered by L ESS DC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>R Main boost pump - powered by R ESS DC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Inter-Tank Valve - powered by L ESS DC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crossflow Valve - powered by L ESS DC.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The Alternate boost pumps are powered from the MAIN DC buses and are therefore NOT available under HMG/emergency electrical configuration - only the Main boost pumps remain.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"fuel--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_fuel_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Fuel System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Fuel section (01-28), cross-checked and confirmed fully consistent for all limitation figures found in both documents: total usable fuel (41,300 lb), maximum fuel imbalance (1,000 lb takeoff / 2,000 lb in flight), fuel tank temperature warning/caution thresholds, the cold-fuel descent rule, and engine fuel temperature limits. No corrections were required.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","fuel--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","fuel--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Capacities & Weights","fuel--capacities-weights"],["h2","Fuel Tank & Engine Fuel Temperature Limits","fuel--fuel-tank-engine-fuel-temperature-limits"],["h2","Fuel Imbalance","fuel--fuel-imbalance"],["h2","Boost Pumps & Hopper Tanks","fuel--boost-pumps-hopper-tanks"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","fuel--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / Tank Structure","fuel--overview-tank-structure"],["h2","Capacities, Weights & Refueling","fuel--capacities-weights-refueling"],["h2","Hopper Tanks","fuel--hopper-tanks"],["h2","Boost Pumps","fuel--boost-pumps"],["h2","Hydraulic / Fuel Heat Exchangers","fuel--hydraulic-fuel-heat-exchangers"],["h2","Fuel Shutoff Valves (SOV)","fuel--fuel-shutoff-valves-sov"],["h2","Fuel & Engine Fuel Temperature / Heated Fuel Return System (HFRS)","fuel--fuel-engine-fuel-temperature-heated-fuel-return-system-hfrs"],["h2","Fuel Imbalance","fuel--fuel-imbalance-1"],["h2","Engine Fuel System","fuel--engine-fuel-system"],["h2","Fuel Filtration","fuel--fuel-filtration"],["h2","APU Fuel Supply","fuel--apu-fuel-supply"],["h2","HMG / Emergency Electrical Operations","fuel--hmg-emergency-electrical-operations"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","fuel--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"hydraulic":{"t":"Hydraulic System Study Guide","sub":"Two Independent Systems - L / R, AUX Pump, PTU, and HMG · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Hydraulic System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide, Hydraulics (01-29)","html":"<h1 id=\"hydraulic--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Hydraulic System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Hydraulics (01-29) section of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (built from the FAA-approved AFM) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (pressures, reservoir quantities, pump outputs, accumulator pre-charge, PTU/HMG limits) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: system components, the independent L and R hydraulic systems, the AUX pump sub-system, the Power Transfer Unit (PTU), the Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG), the fluid-to-fuel heat exchanger, shutoff valves/fire handles, the hydraulic boost actuators (flight control \"users\"), and powerplant failure effects.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Reservoir quantities and accumulator pre-charge cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide Hydraulics (01-29) section and found fully consistent: max pressurized reservoir quantities Left 4.8 gal / Right 1.6 gal (matches \"considered full at\" figures in these notes), and accumulator pre-charge 1,200 psi at 70F/21C for both L and R systems (matches exactly). No corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Limitations guide adds one detail not spelled out in these handwritten notes: the accumulator pre-charge tolerance of +/-25 psi per 10F/5C deviation from 70F - included in the Quick-Reference table below for completeness.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: full component breakdown (reservoir, shutoff valve, engine-driven pump, filter manifold, heat exchanger, accumulator), the AUX pump and PTU sub-systems and their automatic activation logic, the HMG standby-AC backup chain, the standby rudder control valve, DPI/BWI preflight inspection procedures, and the effects of single-engine and EDP failures on flight control redundancy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"hydraulic--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--system-overview\">System Overview</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluid</td>\n<td>Skydrol or HyJet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>Storage and delivery of hydraulic fluid under high pressure to actuate various systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main systems</td>\n<td>Two (2): Left (L) and Right (R), fully independent and isolated from each other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sub-systems</td>\n<td>Two (2), supporting the L system: AUX Pump and Power Transfer Unit (PTU)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System pressure</td>\n<td>3,000 psi +/- 300 psi (typical main pump output)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reservoir pre-charge (bootstrap)</td>\n<td>60 psi - compressed by bootstrap to prevent pump cavitation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reservoir quantity check</td>\n<td>System must be pressurized for an accurate quantity reading</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#hydraulic--system-overview-components\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: System Overview / Components</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--reservoir-quantities-pump-outputs\">Reservoir Quantities &amp; Pump Outputs</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>System</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Total Capacity</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Reservoir</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Considered Full At</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Left (L)</td>\n<td>20.6 gal</td>\n<td>Largest reservoir - dual chamber 3.7 + 2.0 gal = 5.7 gal</td>\n<td>4.8 gal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Right (R)</td>\n<td>7.0 gal</td>\n<td>Smallest reservoir - 1.8 gal</td>\n<td>1.6 gal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (01-29): \"Max reservoir quantities (pressurized): Left 4.8 gal, Right 1.6 gal\" - matches exactly.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Pump</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Output</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Pressure</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L &amp; R main EDPs</td>\n<td>18-28 gal/min (idle to takeoff power)</td>\n<td>3,000 psi +/- 300 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AUX pump</td>\n<td>2 gal/min</td>\n<td>3,000 psi +/- 300 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PTU</td>\n<td>22 gal/min</td>\n<td>3,000 psi +300/-400 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L &amp; R accumulator pre-charge</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1,200 psi @ 70F/21C, +/-25 psi per 10F/5C deviation (Limitations Guide detail)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#hydraulic--filters-differential-pressure-indicators-dpis\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Filters / DPI Inspection</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--dpi-count-by-manifold\">DPI Count by Manifold</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Manifold</strong></th>\n<th><strong>DPIs</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L Hydraulic System Manifold</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Hydraulic System Manifold</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PTU Manifold</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AUX Pump Manifold</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> An extended DPI indicates a filter bypass requiring a maintenance action prior to flight by a technician or trained/authorized individual.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--aux-pump-ptu-automatic-activation\">AUX Pump &amp; PTU Automatic Activation</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Trigger</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>WOW (ground) + brake pedal application &gt; 10 degrees, hydraulic pressure &lt; 1,500 psi</td>\n<td>AUX Boost Pump comes ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aux Boost Pump supply line pressure &lt; 20 psi</td>\n<td>Aux Boost Pump ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aux Boost Pump supply line pressure = 25 psi</td>\n<td>Aux Boost Pump OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nosewheel WOW (air) + STBY RUD switch ON</td>\n<td>Standby Rudder Control Valve provides AUX pressure/fluid to rudder/YD1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L Hydraulic System pressure &lt; 1,500 psi</td>\n<td>PTU comes ON automatically</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#hydraulic--auxiliary-aux-hydraulic-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: AUX Hydraulic System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#hydraulic--power-transfer-unit-ptu\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Power Transfer Unit (PTU)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--hmg-hydraulic-motor-generator-core-numbers\">HMG (Hydraulic Motor Generator) Core Numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rated output</td>\n<td>10 kVA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Produces</td>\n<td>115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driven by</td>\n<td>L Hydraulic System fluid OR the PTU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Permitted use</td>\n<td>Only when normal AC power generation is not available (dual IDG + APU GEN failure)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AUX TRU output</td>\n<td>DC voltage, powers L ESS DC / R ESS DC buses, 10-second interval between crew selections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Location</td>\n<td>Main landing gear wheel well</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#hydraulic--hydraulic-motor-generator-hmg\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--ptu-inhibit-conditions\">PTU Inhibit Conditions</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Effect</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L Hydraulic System &lt; 1.0 g</td>\n<td>PTU prevented from coming ON automatically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PWR XFR UNIT switch OFF/ARM (not ON)</td>\n<td>PTU prevented from coming ON automatically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Hydraulic System temperature &gt; 104C</td>\n<td>PTU prevented from coming ON automatically</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The PTU cannot actuate the left thrust reverser or flight controls - it can only pressurize L system fluid for other L-system users.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"hydraulic--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--system-overview-components\">System Overview / Components</h2>\n<p>The Hydraulic System is about the storage and delivery of hydraulic fluid (Skydrol or HyJet) under high pressure to actuate various systems.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Two (2) main systems: Left (L) and Right (R) Hydraulic Systems.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Supported by two (2) sub-systems: the AUX Pump and the Power Transfer Unit (PTU).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The L system flies and lands the aircraft; the R system flies only; the L system (with the AUX pump) also supports ground maintenance operations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--individual-components\">Individual Components</h2>\n<p><strong>Reservoir: to store fluid.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Compressed by bootstrap (60 psi) to prevent hydraulic pump cavitation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the tail compartment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>System must be pressurized for accurate quantity checks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Shutoff Valve: to shut off hydraulic fluid to the engine in the event of engine fire or failure.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the tail compartment and activated via the fire handles (L and R).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pump (Engine-Driven Pump, EDP): to pressurize the system.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engine-driven, located in the engine gearbox.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Constant-pressure, variable-volume pump.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pressurizes hydraulic fluid to 3,000 +/- 300 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flow rate varies with power setting: 18-28 gal/min (idle to takeoff power).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Filter Manifold: to filter hydraulic fluid and control direction of flow.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the tail compartment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contains several Differential Pressure Indicators (DPIs) - see next section.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydraulic Fluid-to-Fuel Heat Exchanger: to cool hydraulic fluid and warm up cold fuel.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the offside fuel hopper; continuous flow, no pilot input required (see dedicated section below).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Accumulator: to absorb system shocks.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Pre-charged to 1,200 psi @ 70F.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Absorbs fluid shocks within the system; serviced with nitrogen.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the tail compartment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--filters-differential-pressure-indicators-dpis\">Filters / Differential Pressure Indicators (DPIs)</h2>\n<p>DPIs (buttons on top of the filter manifolds) are inspected for condition - flushed (normal) vs. extended (bypass) - during the exterior preflight inspection.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Manifold</strong></th>\n<th><strong>DPIs</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L Hydraulic System Manifold</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Hydraulic System Manifold</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PTU Manifold</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AUX Pump Manifold</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>An extended DPI indicates a filter bypass, which requires a maintenance action prior to flight by an aircraft maintenance technician or a trained and authorized individual.</p>\n<p><em><strong>BWI Reset Procedure:</strong> The PTU Bearing Wear Indicator (BWI), if popped, can be reset per the AFM Exterior Preflight Inspection checklist. If extended: (1) reset, (2) note time in logbook, (3) continued operation for a maximum of 50 flight hours is allowed, (4) check BWI prior to each flight.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--l-hydraulic-system\">L Hydraulic System</h2>\n<p>Independent and isolated from the Right Hydraulic System. Supported by the AUX Pump and PTU sub-systems.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Total capacity: 20.6 gal. Largest reservoir: 5.7 gal (dual chamber, 3.7 + 2.0 gal). Considered full at 4.8 gal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Five (5) filters / DPIs on the L Hydraulic System Manifold.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powered by the Left Engine-Driven Pump (EDP), mounted on the engine gearbox.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Constant-pressure, variable-volume pump; pressurizes fluid to 3,000 +/- 300 psi; flow rate 18-28 gal/min (idle to takeoff power).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Failure of the L EDP results in the loss of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Left thrust reverser.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Redundant flight controls and Yaw Damper 1 (YD1) - ailerons, elevators, rudder, and flight spoilers/speed brakes retain R-system hydraulic power, but lose the L-system boost.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--r-hydraulic-system\">R Hydraulic System</h2>\n<p>Independent and isolated from the Left Hydraulic System.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Total capacity: 7.0 gal. Smallest reservoir: 1.8 gal. Considered full at 1.6 gal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Three (3) filters / DPIs on the R Hydraulic System Manifold.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powered by the Right Engine-Driven Pump (EDP), mounted on the engine gearbox.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Constant-pressure, variable-volume pump; pressurizes fluid to 3,000 +/- 300 psi; flow rate 18-28 gal/min (idle to takeoff power).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Failure of the R EDP results in the loss of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Right thrust reverser.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Redundant flight controls and Yaw Damper 2 (YD2) - ailerons, elevators, rudder, and flight spoilers/speed brakes retain L-system hydraulic power, but lose the R-system boost.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. The PTU, which requires R system fluid and pressure to drive its motor.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--auxiliary-aux-hydraulic-system\">Auxiliary (AUX) Hydraulic System</h2>\n<p>Supplements the Left Hydraulic System. Uses left system fluid; its reservoir is physically located within the L Hydraulic System reservoir (2.0 gal of the L reservoir dual chamber).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Powered by the AUX pump, located in the right landing gear wheelwell.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Electrically powered by the L ESS DC bus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Operates either automatically or manually; 3,000 psi @ 2 gal/min.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>An AUX boost pump, installed in the supply line, comes ON when pressure is below 20 psi and turns OFF once pressure reaches 25 psi.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Functions on the ground:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Auto-latch feature: brake pedal application (with low hydraulic pressure) automatically brings the AUX pump ON - triggered by Weight-on-Wheels (WOW) on the ground and brake pedal application greater than 10 degrees, when brake pressure is below 1,500 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Maintenance operations (e.g., gear operation while on jacks).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Preflight inspection: gear doors opening and closing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Functions in the air:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If both the L and R Hydraulic Systems fail in flight, the AUX system (via the Standby Rudder Control Valve) provides fluid and pressure to the rudder actuator and Yaw Damper 1 (YD1).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Activated by: Nosewheel Weight-Off-Wheels (WOW, i.e., airborne) + STBY RUD switch selected ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Capable of powering:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Nose Wheel Steering (NWS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Main Entrance Door (MED).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Flaps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Brakes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>5. Standby Rudder (via the Standby Rudder Control Valve).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The AUX pump reservoir capacity is 2.0 gal - it is physically part of the L Hydraulic System reservoir dual chamber (3.7 + 2.0 = 5.7 gal total), not a separate tank.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--power-transfer-unit-ptu\">Power Transfer Unit (PTU)</h2>\n<p>Backup to the Left Hydraulic System engine-driven pump (operational redundancy). The PTU is a motor/pump assembly located in the tail compartment (right side).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The motor is driven by R system pressurized fluid. The pump is driven by the motor, and its job is to pressurize L system fluid.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It comes ON automatically if L Hydraulic System pressure is below 1,500 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Output: 22 gal/min @ 3,000 psi +300/-400 psi.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>It cannot actuate:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Left thrust reverser.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flight controls.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The PTU cannot operate without:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Hydraulic System fluid.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>R Hydraulic System fluid AND pressure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The PTU is prevented from coming ON automatically if:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Hydraulic System &lt; 1.0 g.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PWR XFR UNIT switch is OFF/ARM (not selected ON).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>R Hydraulic System temperature &gt; 104C.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The PTU helps retract the landing gear following a left engine failure after V1 (a regulatory requirement). The PTU can also power the HMG (Hydraulic Motor Generator - see next section).</p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Pulling the Left Fire Handle does NOT shut off the supply of Left Hydraulic System fluid to the PTU - the shutoff valve only isolates fluid to the Left EDP, not the PTU circuit.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--hydraulic-motor-generator-hmg\">Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG)</h2>\n<p>The Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG) is a backup AC generator. It is part of the Standby Electrical Power System (requires MASTER, L ESS, and R ESS all ON).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Driven by either the L Hydraulic System OR the PTU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Powers the L Standby AC bus, R Standby AC bus, and (via the AUX TRU) DC voltage to the L ESS DC / R ESS DC buses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rated at 10 kVA; produces 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The HMG produces AC power in the event of a dual IDG (L IDG and R IDG) AND APU GEN failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>HMG operation is permitted only when normal AC power generation is not available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The AUX TRU produces DC voltage and will power the L ESS DC / R ESS DC buses when selected by the crew, with a ten (10) second interval required between selections.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Located in the main landing gear wheelwell.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Related Note:</strong> Speed brake deployment is acceptable as long as the handle is moved from stowed to fully extended in three (3) seconds or more; faster deployment may trigger a \"SPEED BRAKE EXTENDED\" CAS message.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--hydraulic-fluid-to-fuel-heat-exchanger\">Hydraulic Fluid-to-Fuel Heat Exchanger</h2>\n<p>Purpose: to cool hydraulic fluid and to warm up cold fuel.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The heat exchanger unit for each hydraulic system is located inside the offside fuel hopper.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hot hydraulic fluid flows continuously through the heat exchanger without any pilot input.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hot hydraulic fluid is cooled while cold fuel in the hopper is warmed up.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--shutoff-valves-fire-handles\">Shutoff Valves / Fire Handles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fire Handles: pulling the L or R fire handle closes the corresponding hydraulic shutoff valve, isolating hydraulic fluid from that engine-driven pump. With the handle stowed, the shutoff valve is open and the EDP is pressurized.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The hydraulic shutoff valves are located in the tail compartment. They isolate hydraulic fluid from the engine-driven pumps only (not from the PTU or AUX system circuits).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The valves are motor-operated and are energized only when the fire handles in the cockpit are pulled up.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--hydraulic-boost-actuators-flight-control-users\">Hydraulic Boost Actuators (Flight Control Users)</h2>\n<p>Each hydraulic boost actuator receives fluid from both the L and R systems (except the rudder, which is normally powered by the L system alone, backed up by the AUX/standby rudder circuit in flight).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Surface</strong></th>\n<th><strong>L Sys</strong></th>\n<th><strong>R Sys</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ailerons</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Dual hydraulic boost, both sides feed both aileron actuators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Dual hydraulic boost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No (normal)</td>\n<td>R system not normally used; AUX/Standby Rudder Valve backs up L system loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flight Spoilers / Speed Brakes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Dual hydraulic boost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Loss of L system hydraulic power removes the L-side boost from ailerons, elevators, rudder, and flight spoilers/speed brakes (R side still functions where equipped). Loss of R system hydraulic power removes the R-side boost from ailerons, elevators, and flight spoilers/speed brakes (rudder unaffected, since it normally uses L system only).</p>\n<h2 id=\"hydraulic--powerplant-failure-effects\">Powerplant Failure Effects</h2>\n<p>The failure of either engine results in the loss of the Engine-Driven Hydraulic (EDP) pump for that side.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Failure</strong></th>\n<th><strong>PTU Response</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Components Lost</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L Engine Fail</td>\n<td>PTU takes over the duties of the inoperative L EDP as soon as L Hydraulic System pressure drops below 1,500 psi</td>\n<td>Redundant hydraulic power to flight controls; Left thrust reverser; Left Yaw Damper (YD1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Engine Fail</td>\n<td>Not applicable - the R Hydraulic System becomes unavailable (PTU itself needs R system fluid/pressure)</td>\n<td>Redundant hydraulic power to flight controls; Right thrust reverser; Right Yaw Damper (YD2); the PTU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> An \"L Engine Fail\" or \"R Engine Fail\" CAS message inhibits all other messages associated with that engine failure, such as \"L Hyd System Fail\" or \"R Hyd System Fail\" - those messages will not separately annunciate once the engine-failure message is displayed.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"hydraulic--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_hydraulic_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Hydraulic System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Hydraulics section (01-29), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for all limitation figures: max pressurized reservoir quantities (Left 4.8 gal, Right 1.6 gal) and accumulator pre-charge (1,200 psi @ 70F/21C, +/-25 psi per 10F/5C deviation). No corrections were needed; the Limitations guide only added the deviation-tolerance detail, now folded into the Quick-Reference table above.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","hydraulic--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","hydraulic--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","System Overview","hydraulic--system-overview"],["h2","Reservoir Quantities & Pump Outputs","hydraulic--reservoir-quantities-pump-outputs"],["h2","DPI Count by Manifold","hydraulic--dpi-count-by-manifold"],["h2","AUX Pump & PTU Automatic Activation","hydraulic--aux-pump-ptu-automatic-activation"],["h2","HMG (Hydraulic Motor Generator) Core Numbers","hydraulic--hmg-hydraulic-motor-generator-core-numbers"],["h2","PTU Inhibit Conditions","hydraulic--ptu-inhibit-conditions"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","hydraulic--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","System Overview / Components","hydraulic--system-overview-components"],["h2","Individual Components","hydraulic--individual-components"],["h2","Filters / Differential Pressure Indicators (DPIs)","hydraulic--filters-differential-pressure-indicators-dpis"],["h2","L Hydraulic System","hydraulic--l-hydraulic-system"],["h2","R Hydraulic System","hydraulic--r-hydraulic-system"],["h2","Auxiliary (AUX) Hydraulic System","hydraulic--auxiliary-aux-hydraulic-system"],["h2","Power Transfer Unit (PTU)","hydraulic--power-transfer-unit-ptu"],["h2","Hydraulic Motor Generator (HMG)","hydraulic--hydraulic-motor-generator-hmg"],["h2","Hydraulic Fluid-to-Fuel Heat Exchanger","hydraulic--hydraulic-fluid-to-fuel-heat-exchanger"],["h2","Shutoff Valves / Fire Handles","hydraulic--shutoff-valves-fire-handles"],["h2","Hydraulic Boost Actuators (Flight Control Users)","hydraulic--hydraulic-boost-actuators-flight-control-users"],["h2","Powerplant Failure Effects","hydraulic--powerplant-failure-effects"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","hydraulic--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"flight-controls":{"t":"Flight Control System Study Guide","sub":"Primary & Secondary Flight Control System (PFCS/SFCS) · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Flight Control System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (Airspeed, Load Acceleration, F","html":"<h1 id=\"flight-controls--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Flight Control System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by control axis/subsystem rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (Airspeed Limitations 01-03-40, Flight Load Acceleration 01-03-50, Flight Controls 01-27, and Use of Flaps 01-30-40) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (deflection limits, trim ranges, Vfe speeds, g-load limits, altitude limits, min speeds after a jam) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: overview/architecture and manual reversion, the Hard Over Prevention System (HOPS), ailerons, elevators, rudder (incl. standby rudder control valve and the automatic overload limiting system), the yaw damper system, hydraulic boost actuators, flaps/horizontal stabilizer, and spoilers/speed brakes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Flap-related figures cross-checked and confirmed consistent with the Limitations Study Guide: Vfe 250 KCAS (Flaps 10), 220 KCAS (Flaps 20), 170 KCAS (Flaps 39); max altitude for extending/flying flaps 25,000 ft (10 or 20) and 20,000 ft (Flaps 39); flight load acceleration -1.0g to +2.5g (Flaps UP), 0g to +2.0g (Flaps 10/20), 0g to +2.0g up to MLW / 0g to +1.5g above MLW (Flaps 39 DOWN).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added system-level detail not present in the Limitations guide: HOPS logic and how it behaves differently for ailerons (both actuators shut off) vs. elevators and rudder (only the affected actuator/hydraulic system is shut off); the standby rudder control valve and its nosewheel-WOW/STBY RUD ON trigger logic; the automatic overload limiting system for the rudder (limits travel and ramps hydraulic pressure to 3,000 psi on loss of one hydraulic system, with a SINGLE RUDDER CAS message); dual-channel/dual-computer (YD1/YD2, FGC1/FGC2) fail-operational yaw damper architecture; and the 125-KCAS minimum approach speed guidance after a jammed aileron or elevator is manually split.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"flight-controls--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--system-architecture-basics\">System Architecture Basics</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Power</td>\n<td>Hydraulically-powered (6:1 boost); mechanical (pushrods/cables/bell cranks) backup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manual reversion</td>\n<td>Retained on complete loss of hydraulic fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary FCS</td>\n<td>Ailerons + elevators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secondary FCS</td>\n<td>Spoilers + horizontal stabilizer/flaps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flight-controls--overview-system-architecture\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / System Architecture</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--deflection-trim-quick-numbers\">Deflection &amp; Trim Quick Numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Surface</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Deflection / Trim</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ailerons</td>\n<td>Up 11 deg / Down 11 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aileron trim tab (L aileron only, heated 175F +/-20F)</td>\n<td>Up 15 deg / Down 15 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roll augmentation (mid + outboard spoiler panels)</td>\n<td>Up aileron augmented up to 47 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n<td>Up 24 deg / Down 13 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevator trim tabs - electrical</td>\n<td>Up 21 deg / Down 7 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevator trim tabs - mechanical</td>\n<td>Up 22 deg / Down 8 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevator trim tabs</td>\n<td>Heated 175F +/-20F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder</td>\n<td>Up to 22 deg, depending on speed (Automatic Overload Limiting System)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder trim (moves entire rudder surface)</td>\n<td>7.5 deg L/R</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground spoilers</td>\n<td>55 deg +/- 4 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed brakes (in-flight)</td>\n<td>Up to 30 deg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Minimum speed on approach after a jammed aileron or elevator is manually split and disconnected: 125 KCAS, to ensure adequate airflow over the flight controls.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flight-controls--roll-flight-controls---ailerons\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Ailerons and Elevators</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--flaps---vfe-g-loads-altitude\">Flaps - Vfe, G-Loads, Altitude</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Flap Setting</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Vfe</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max G-Load</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max Altitude (extend/fly)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Stab Shift</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 (UP)</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>-1.0g to +2.5g</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>-1.5 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>250 KCAS</td>\n<td>0g to +2.0g</td>\n<td>25,000 ft</td>\n<td>-2.7 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20 (T/O APP)</td>\n<td>220 KCAS</td>\n<td>0g to +2.0g</td>\n<td>25,000 ft</td>\n<td>-3.6 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39 (DOWN)</td>\n<td>170 KCAS</td>\n<td>0 to +2.0g (&lt;=MLW) / 0 to +1.5g (&gt;MLW)</td>\n<td>20,000 ft</td>\n<td>-4.6 deg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> All figures cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (01-03-40, 01-03-50) and confirmed consistent.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flight-controls--flaps-horizontal-stabilizer\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Flaps / Horizontal Stabilizer</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--rudder-yaw-damper-key-numbers\">Rudder / Yaw Damper Key Numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder Limit CAS message</td>\n<td>Maximum rudder hydraulic assist condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single Rudder CAS message</td>\n<td>Loss of one hydraulic system; remaining system ramped to 3,000 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standby rudder control valve trigger</td>\n<td>Nosewheel WOW (air) + STBY RUD switch ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standby rudder valve feeds</td>\n<td>Rudder / Yaw Damper #1 actuator, plus AUX systems (flaps, NWS, brakes, MED)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yaw damper channels / computers</td>\n<td>2 channels (YD1/YD2), 2 computers (FGC1/FGC2) - fail operational</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flight-controls--yaw-flight-controls---rudder\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Rudder and Yaw Damper</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--hops---behavior-by-surface\">HOPS - Behavior by Surface</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Surface</strong></th>\n<th><strong>HOPS Response to a Sensed Fault</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ailerons</td>\n<td>Both hydraulic actuators (L and R aileron hydraulics) are shut off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n<td>Only the affected actuator is shut off; flight continues on the remaining actuator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudder</td>\n<td>Only the affected hydraulic system(s) to the rudder actuator is shut off</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flight-controls--hard-over-prevention-system-hops\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Hard Over Prevention System (HOPS)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"flight-controls--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--overview-system-architecture\">Overview / System Architecture</h2>\n<p>The G550 flight controls are hydraulically-powered but retain full manual capability should there be a complete loss of hydraulic fluid.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Manually-controlled: control yoke (roll/pitch) and rudder pedals (yaw).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mechanically-operated: pushrods, cables, and bell cranks connect the cockpit controls to the hydraulic boost actuators.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hydraulically-boosted: 6-to-1 boost ratio at each control surface actuator.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary Flight Control System (PFCS): ailerons and elevators. Secondary Flight Control System (SFCS): spoilers and the horizontal stabilizer/flaps.</p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--hard-over-prevention-system-hops\">Hard Over Prevention System (HOPS)</h2>\n<p>A Hard Over Prevention System (HOPS) compares the hydraulic pressure sensed into each servo against the pressure sensed out of the servo. If there is a difference, HOPS shuts off hydraulic fluid from the affected system(s) to prevent an uncommanded (hard-over) surface deflection.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Ailerons: if one aileron actuator is jammed, both ailerons are affected - HOPS shuts off L and R aileron hydraulics together (L-R Aileron Hydraulics OFF).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Elevators: HOPS shuts off only the affected actuator (e.g., R Elevator Hydraulics OFF); flight continues normally with the remaining actuator.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rudder: HOPS shuts off only the affected hydraulic system(s) supplying the single rudder actuator (e.g., Rudder Hydraulics OFF for one side).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> No hydraulic pressure to a jammed surface means manual reversion - the force required to move the controls is much greater than normal.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--roll-flight-controls---ailerons\">Roll Flight Controls - Ailerons</h2>\n<p>Left and right ailerons are each driven by their own yoke via pushrods, cables, and bell cranks, which activate hydraulic boost actuators (L Sys / R Sys) at each aileron.</p>\n<p>Left and right ailerons are mechanically connected but can be split in the event of a jam.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Jammed ailerons procedure: use rudder to level the wings, manually disconnect the ailerons, and fly the aircraft with the operating aileron.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum speed on approach after disconnecting: 125 KCAS, to ensure adequate airflow over the flight controls.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Aileron deflection: Up 11 deg / Down 11 deg.</p>\n<p>Aileron trim tab (left aileron only): Up 15 deg / Down 15 deg. The trim tab is heated (175F +/-20F, powered by R Main AC).</p>\n<p>Roll augmentation: the mid and outboard spoiler panels augment the up-going aileron, adding up to 47 deg of additional roll authority.</p>\n<p>HOPS activation on the ailerons: if one aileron is jammed, both ailerons are affected. Loss of hydraulic pressure results in manual reversion, and the force required to move the ailerons is much greater.</p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--pitch-flight-controls---elevators\">Pitch Flight Controls - Elevators</h2>\n<p>Left and right elevators are each driven by their own control column via pushrods, cables, and bell cranks, which activate hydraulic boost actuators (L Sys / R Sys) at each elevator.</p>\n<p>Left and right elevators are mechanically connected but can be split in the event of a jam.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Jammed elevators procedure: manually disconnect the elevators, and fly the aircraft with the operating elevator.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum speed on approach after disconnecting: 125 KCAS, to ensure adequate airflow over the flight controls.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevator deflection: Up 24 deg / Down 13 deg.</p>\n<p>Elevator trim tabs: electrically controlled by switches on the control columns, or manually controlled by control wheels located on each side of the center pedestal. Trim tabs are heated (175F +/-20F).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Trim Method</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Up</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Down</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrically</td>\n<td>21 deg</td>\n<td>7 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanically</td>\n<td>22 deg</td>\n<td>8 deg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>HOPS activation on the elevators: only the affected actuator is shut off (both L and R hydraulic pressure to that actuator). Flight continues with the remaining actuator. If both actuators are affected, there is no hydraulic pressure = manual reversion, and force to move the elevators is much greater.</p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--yaw-flight-controls---rudder\">Yaw Flight Controls - Rudder</h2>\n<p>A single rudder is operated by the pilots' pedals and the yaw damper system. Rudder pedal movement actuates a series of cables and bell cranks that in turn actuate a dual tandem hydraulic boost actuator dedicated to moving the rudder.</p>\n<p>A \"Rudder Limit\" CAS message indicates the maximum rudder hydraulic assist condition.</p>\n<p>Rudder trim is accomplished by moving the entire rudder surface via a rudder trim control wheel on the center pedestal (7.5 deg L/R).</p>\n<h3 id=\"flight-controls--standby-rudder-control-valve\">Standby Rudder Control Valve</h3>\n<p>The standby rudder control valve provides AUX system fluid and pressure to the rudder actuator and Yaw Damper #1 in the event that both L and R hydraulic systems fail in flight.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>AUX pressure and fluid feed the standby rudder valve, which supplies the Rudder/YD1 actuator and other AUX systems: flaps, NWS (nosewheel steering), brakes, and MED.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Trigger logic: Nosewheel WOW (weight-off-wheels, i.e., in the air) + STBY RUD switch ON = standby rudder valve supplies the Rudder/YD1 actuator.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"flight-controls--hops-activation---rudder\">HOPS Activation - Rudder</h3>\n<p>HOPS shuts off the affected hydraulic system(s) feeding the rudder actuator (e.g., \"Rudder Hydraulics OFF\"). Flight continues on the remaining rudder hydraulic system.</p>\n<h3 id=\"flight-controls--automatic-overload-limiting-system\">Automatic Overload Limiting System</h3>\n<p>Prevents excessive overload on the vertical stabilizer during high speeds by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Limiting rudder travel (less travel available at high speed, more at low speed), and</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Limiting hydraulic pressure to the rudder actuator.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If there is a loss of one hydraulic system, pressure from the remaining system is ramped up to 3,000 psi and a \"Single Rudder\" CAS message is displayed.</p>\n<p>Rudder deflection: up to 22 deg L/R, depending on speed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--yaw-damper-system\">Yaw Damper System</h2>\n<p>The yaw damper system damps Dutch Roll tendencies associated with the G550's swept-back wing design.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Element</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Redundancy</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Channels</td>\n<td>Two (2): YD1, YD2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flight guidance computers</td>\n<td>Two (2): FGC1, FGC2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-connection</td>\n<td>YD1 &lt;-&gt; FGC1/FGC2; YD2 &lt;-&gt; FGC1/FGC2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Fail operational: if a YD and/or FGC fails, the remaining YD and/or FGC automatically takes over the duties of the failed unit.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--hydraulic-boost-actuators\">Hydraulic Boost Actuators</h2>\n<p>Each primary/secondary flight control surface is driven by a dedicated hydraulic boost actuator fed by the L and R hydraulic systems (with the rudder additionally backed up by the AUX/standby rudder control valve system):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Ailerons: L Sys and R Sys feed a crossed/interconnected actuator at each aileron (see HOPS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Elevators: L Sys and R Sys feed a crossed/interconnected actuator at each elevator (see HOPS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rudder: L Sys and R Sys feed a single dual-tandem rudder actuator; AUX/standby valve backs up Rudder/YD1 in the event of dual hydraulic system failure in flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flight spoilers / speed brakes: L Sys and R Sys feed hydraulic boost actuators at each spoiler panel.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--flaps-horizontal-stabilizer\">Flaps / Horizontal Stabilizer</h2>\n<p>Fowler-type, single-surface flaps. The horizontal stabilizer moves in conjunction with flap movement in order to minimize pitch changes as the flaps extend/retract and the center of lift moves.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Flap Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Horizontal Stabilizer Shift</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 (UP)</td>\n<td>-1.5 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>-2.7 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>-3.6 deg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39 (DOWN)</td>\n<td>-4.6 deg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Flap/Stab Control Unit (FCU, channels A and B) is located in the AEER, powered by L Ess DC and R Ess DC. Flap/stab indications on the PFD (HSI) disappear 30 seconds after the flaps have reached the UP (zero) position.</p>\n<p><strong>Flaps are actuated by three means:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Electrically-controlled: FCU channels A/B, powered by L/R Ess DC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hydraulically-powered: AUX, L Hyd Sys, and PTU (power transfer unit) feed a hydraulic motor.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mechanically-actuated: a flexible drive shaft and gearbox differentially drive four ballscrew actuators - L outboard, L inboard, R inboard, and R outboard.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Horizontal stabilizer: electrically-controlled and actuated. FCU channels A/B (powered by L/R Standby AC) drive Motor A and Motor B, which jointly drive the stabilizer actuator.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Flap Setting</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Vfe</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max G-Load</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max Altitude (extend/fly)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 (UP)</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>-1.0g to +2.5g, up to MTOW</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>250 KCAS</td>\n<td>0g to +2.0g, up to MTOW</td>\n<td>&lt;= 25,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>220 KCAS</td>\n<td>0g to +2.0g, up to MTOW</td>\n<td>&lt;= 25,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39 (DOWN)</td>\n<td>170 KCAS</td>\n<td>0g to +2.0g (&lt;= MLW) / 0g to +1.5g (&gt; MLW)</td>\n<td>&lt;= 20,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-checked and confirmed consistent with the Limitations Study Guide, Airspeed Limitations (01-03-40) and Flight Load Acceleration (01-03-50) sections.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"flight-controls--spoilers-speed-brakes\">Spoilers / Speed Brakes</h2>\n<p>Six (6) spoiler panels total, driven by two (2) hydraulic actuators per wing.</p>\n<p><strong>Spoilers serve three functions:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Panels Used</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Deflection</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Roll augmentation</td>\n<td>Mid and outboard panels</td>\n<td>Up to 47 deg (augments up-going aileron)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Speed brakes (in-flight)</td>\n<td>All panels, via speed brake handle</td>\n<td>Up to 30 deg (\"SPEED BRAKE EXTENDED\" CAS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Ground spoilers (on ground)</td>\n<td>All panels</td>\n<td>55 deg +/- 4 deg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Spoilers are electrically-controlled via the speed brake handle (extend/retract) and hydraulically-powered by L Sys and R Sys.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Caution:</strong> Do NOT extend spoilers in flight with gear down or Flaps 39 (DOWN) selected - prohibited. Do NOT arm ground spoilers for touch-and-go landings.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"flight-controls--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_flight_control_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Flight Control System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Airspeed Limitations (01-03-40), Flight Load Acceleration (01-03-50), Flight Controls (01-27), and Use of Flaps (01-30-40) sections, cross-checked and confirmed consistent for all limitation figures (Vfe speeds, g-load limits, flap/gear extension altitude limits).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","flight-controls--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","flight-controls--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","System Architecture Basics","flight-controls--system-architecture-basics"],["h2","Deflection & Trim Quick Numbers","flight-controls--deflection-trim-quick-numbers"],["h2","Flaps - Vfe, G-Loads, Altitude","flight-controls--flaps---vfe-g-loads-altitude"],["h2","Rudder / Yaw Damper Key Numbers","flight-controls--rudder-yaw-damper-key-numbers"],["h2","HOPS - Behavior by Surface","flight-controls--hops---behavior-by-surface"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","flight-controls--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / System Architecture","flight-controls--overview-system-architecture"],["h2","Hard Over Prevention System (HOPS)","flight-controls--hard-over-prevention-system-hops"],["h2","Roll Flight Controls - Ailerons","flight-controls--roll-flight-controls---ailerons"],["h2","Pitch Flight Controls - Elevators","flight-controls--pitch-flight-controls---elevators"],["h2","Yaw Flight Controls - Rudder","flight-controls--yaw-flight-controls---rudder"],["h2","Yaw Damper System","flight-controls--yaw-damper-system"],["h2","Hydraulic Boost Actuators","flight-controls--hydraulic-boost-actuators"],["h2","Flaps / Horizontal Stabilizer","flight-controls--flaps-horizontal-stabilizer"],["h2","Spoilers / Speed Brakes","flight-controls--spoilers-speed-brakes"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","flight-controls--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"fire-protection":{"t":"Fire Protection System Study Guide","sub":"Detection, Notification, Fire Fighting, and Smoke Evacuation · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Fire Protection System · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (cold-weather portable ","html":"<h1 id=\"fire-protection--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Fire Protection System study notes into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by function rather than by page order. The Fire Protection System covers the whole aircraft — engines, APU, baggage compartment, and lavatories/galley — and breaks down into four pillars the original author uses as the outline: (1) Detection, (2) Notification, (3) Fire Fighting, and (4) Smoke Evacuation.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: bottle/shot assignments, trip points, indication counts, and portable equipment counts in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Function: engine fire detection (dual-loop), engine fire extinguishing (two-bottle cross-feed system), engine fire test and fault test, overheat detection, smoke detection (baggage/lavatories/galley), crew notification and CAS messages, smoke evacuation, portable fire extinguishers, and PBE (smoke goggles).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scope note - APU fire:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The APU has its own dedicated fire detection loop (helium-filled tube) and draws its extinguishing agent from the same L bottle (Shot 2) used by the left engine. Full APU fire detection/extinguishing/test detail is covered in the companion G550 APU Study Guide (Fire Detection and Fire Extinguishing sections) - this guide gives only the cross-reference needed to understand how the APU fits into the aircraft-wide two-bottle system, to avoid duplicating that content.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide: the cold-weather table there lists two portable extinguisher removal temperatures — Amerex extinguisher must be removed at -65C, Kidde extinguisher must be removed at -40C. These are two different portable extinguisher models installed in different cabin locations; both figures are consistent with these notes' \"3x Halon + 1x water portable\" count and are now cross-referenced here rather than restated as new limits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Limitations guide has no dedicated Fire Protection section of its own (fire/smoke system logic is not a certified numerical \"limitation\" in the AFM sense) - so all engine/overheat/smoke system detail below comes from these notes with no other guide to cross-check bottle counts, trip points, or CAS message text against. Always confirm current values against the AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Bottle-to-engine/APU cross-feed logic (L bottle = Shot 2 = feeds either engine or the APU; R bottle = Shot 1 = feeds either engine only) and the outward/inward fire-handle rotation logic were cross-checked internally across the source notes' own diagrams (pages 1, 4, 12-14, 17, 19-20) and found fully self-consistent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"fire-protection--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--the-four-pillars\">The Four Pillars</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Pillar</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Covers</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Detection</td>\n<td>Fire detection (engine nacelle temp wires; APU helium tube), smoke detection (baggage, fwd/aft lavatories), overheat detection (10 thermal-switch-monitored areas)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Notification</td>\n<td>Fire (W lights, CAS, fire handles), smoke (W lights, CAS), overheat (red/amber CAS + W or C lights depending on severity)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Fire Fighting</td>\n<td>Two engine/APU fire bottles (cross-feed), 3x Halon + 1x water portable extinguishers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Smoke Evacuation</td>\n<td>Baggage compartment smoke evacuation valve; cabin smoke via internal baggage door</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fire-protection--overview-the-four-pillars\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--engine-fire-bottle-cross-feed-key-numbers\">Engine Fire Bottle Cross-Feed (Key Numbers)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of engine/APU fire bottles</td>\n<td>Two (2), identical single-shot bottles, located in tail compartment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extinguishing agent</td>\n<td>Halon 1301, under high pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L bottle</td>\n<td>Shot 2 - feeds EITHER engine (L or R) OR the APU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R bottle</td>\n<td>Shot 1 - feeds EITHER engine (L or R) only - not available to the APU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire handle rotated OUTWARD</td>\n<td>Discharges Shot 1 (R bottle) into that engine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire handle rotated INWARD</td>\n<td>Discharges Shot 2 (L bottle) into that engine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine fire - which shot first?</td>\n<td>Shot 1 (R bottle) used first, to preserve the L bottle (Shot 2) for the APU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power source</td>\n<td>L ESS DC and R ESS DC buses (down to main batteries)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAS on discharge</td>\n<td>\"L FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE\" or \"R FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overpressure relief</td>\n<td>Vents into the tail compartment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Because the APU can only be fed by the L bottle (Shot 2), an engine fire is fought with the R bottle (Shot 1) first whenever possible, keeping the L bottle in reserve for the APU.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fire-protection--engine-fire-extinguishing-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Engine Fire Extinguishing System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--overheat-detection-trip-points\">Overheat Detection Trip Points</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Area Monitored</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Trip Point</strong></th>\n<th><strong>CAS Type</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleed-air-related areas</td>\n<td>250F</td>\n<td>Red overheat CAS (Warning)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic equipment areas</td>\n<td>150F</td>\n<td>Amber overheat CAS (Caution)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Ten (10) areas total are monitored by thermal switches across the aircraft.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fire-protection--overheat-detection-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overheat Detection System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--engine-fire-test---indications\">Engine Fire Test - Indications</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Test</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Indications Produced (per engine)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire Test (tests detection system)</td>\n<td>2x overhead loop lights, 2x CAS messages (\"Engine Fire Loop Alert\" + \"[L/R] Engine Fire\"), 2x master warning (W) lights, fire handle pops, fuel control switch to OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault Test (tests detection FAULT circuit, not the loops)</td>\n<td>5x overhead lights (Loop A/B Fault x2 each side + Fault Test), 1x CAS (\"Fire Detection Loop Fault\"), 2x master caution (C) lights</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> \"Good test, good engine. Bad test, bad engine.\" - a faulty/failed loop can be deselected, allowing the system to run as a single-loop system.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fire-protection--engine-fire-test-fault-test\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Engine Fire Test / Fault Test</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--portable-fire-extinguishers-and-pbe\">Portable Fire Extinguishers and PBE</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Equipment</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Quantity</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Portable Halon fire extinguishers</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Forward area, mid-cabin area, aft area near engine/PBE station</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portable water fire extinguisher</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Mid-cabin, overhead stow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Aft station, near the aft portable Halon extinguisher</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cold-weather cross-check (Limitations guide): Amerex portable extinguisher must be removed at -65C; Kidde portable extinguisher must be removed at -40C - two different models installed in the cabin, consistent with this count.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fire-protection--portable-fire-extinguishers-pbe\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Portable Fire Extinguishers / PBE</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"fire-protection--part-2---full-detail-by-function\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY FUNCTION</h1>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--overview-the-four-pillars\">Overview / The Four Pillars</h2>\n<p><strong>The Fire Protection System is organized around four functions, applied across engines, APU, baggage compartment, and lavatories/galley:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Detection - Fire detection (engine nacelle temperature-sensitive wires; APU compartment helium-filled tubes), Smoke detection (optical sensors - baggage compartment, forward and aft lavatories), Overheat detection (ten areas monitored by thermal switches).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Notification - Crew notification of fire, smoke, or overheat conditions via master warning/caution lights, CAS messages, fire handles, and (on the ground) an aural fire bell.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Fire Fighting - Engine and APU fire bottles (two-bottle cross-feed system) plus portable Halon/water extinguishers throughout the cabin.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. Smoke Evacuation - Baggage compartment smoke evacuation valve (deflates the exterior door seal to vent smoke overboard via differential pressure); cabin smoke can also be vented via the internal baggage door.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--engine-fire-detection-system\">Engine Fire Detection System</h2>\n<p><strong>Dual-loop fire detection system: each engine has two (2) fire loops, Loop A and Loop B.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A loop is a temperature-sensitive coaxial wire: a coaxial center wire surrounded by semi-conducting glass/oxide material, covered by a stainless-steel outer sheath.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>As temperature increases, the resistance around the center wire decreases (inverse relationship) - this is how the loop signals a fire condition.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Loops are routed throughout the engine nacelle, comprised of a series of detector segments/elements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each loop sends raw data to the Fire Detection Control Unit (FDCU) - the \"brains\" of the system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The FDCU determines whether a loop is: (1) Fault, (2) Failed, or (3) sensing an actual Fire, then notifies the crew via CAS message(s).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A faulty/failed loop can be deselected (Loop A or B OFF); the system then operates as a single-loop system for that engine.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This is the same style of dual-loop detection concept as the APU's helium-tube detector, but the engine loops use temperature-sensitive resistance wire rather than a sealed gas-filled tube. See the companion APU Study Guide for the APU-specific detector.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--engine-fire-extinguishing-system\">Engine Fire Extinguishing System</h2>\n<p>Available any time the L ESS DC and R ESS DC buses are powered. The system has two (2) identical single-shot fire extinguishing bottles located in the tail compartment, each containing Halon 1301 extinguishing agent under high pressure. In the event of overpressure, the agent is vented into the tail compartment.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Bottle</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Shot #</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Can Feed</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L Bottle</td>\n<td>Shot 2</td>\n<td>Either engine (L or R) OR the APU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R Bottle</td>\n<td>Shot 1</td>\n<td>Either engine (L or R) only - NOT available to the APU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Each engine has its own fire handle. Pulling a fire handle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Shuts off fuel at the tank (fuel shutoff valve closes).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Shuts off hydraulic fluid downstream from the reservoir (hydraulic shutoff valve closes for that side's hydraulic system).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. Trips the associated IDG (generator drops off line; bus tie relays close automatically to keep that side's main AC bus powered from the other IDG).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rotating the fire handle then discharges a bottle into that engine nacelle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Rotating OUTWARD discharges Shot 1 (R bottle) - CAS: \"R FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rotating INWARD discharges Shot 2 (L bottle) - CAS: \"L FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE.\"</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> In the event of an engine fire, the right bottle/Shot 1 is used first. This saves the other bottle (L/Shot 2) for the APU, since the APU can only be fed by Shot 2.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--engine-fire-test-fault-test\">Engine Fire Test / Fault Test</h2>\n<p><strong>Engine Fire Test - tests the fire detection system (the loops) for each engine.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If a loop does not illuminate during test, it is because of: an open loop, no continuity, or a defective fire detection circuit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>\"Good test, good engine.\" / \"Bad test, bad engine.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When the test switchlight is pressed, per engine: two (2) overhead lights (Loop A, Loop B), two (2) CAS messages (\"Engine Fire Loop Alert\" and \"[L/R] Engine Fire\"), two (2) master warning (W) lights, the fire handle pops out, and the fuel control switch moves from RUN to OFF.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Fire Fault Test - tests the fire detection FAULT circuit, NOT the loops themselves.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>When the switchlight is pressed and held: five (5) overhead lights (Loop A Fault, Loop B Fault, Fault Test, Loop A Fault, Loop B Fault), one (1) CAS message (\"Fire Detection Loop Fault\"), two (2) master caution (C) lights.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A faulty loop can be deselected (e.g., R Eng Loop B set OFF) so the system continues to operate on the remaining loop.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--apu-fire-cross-reference\">APU Fire (Cross-Reference)</h2>\n<p>The APU has its own dedicated fire detection loop (a helium-filled tube around the APU enclosure, rather than the engines' resistance-wire loops) and is enclosed in a fireproof titanium case. Full APU-specific fire detection and extinguishing detail (helium-tube physics, ECU shutdown response, APU fire test indications) is covered in the companion G550 APU Study Guide - only the aircraft-wide bottle logic is repeated here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The APU can only be extinguished with the L Fire Bottle (Shot 2) - the R bottle (Shot 1) is not plumbed to the APU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Discharging the APU FIRE EXT switchlight uses the L Fire Bottle and displays \"L FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE\" on the CAS. After this, the L bottle is no longer available to either engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In the event of an APU fire, the ECU automatically shuts down the APU; the QRH then directs the crew to (1) select the APU MASTER switchlight OFF, and (2) press the APU FIRE EXT switchlight to discharge the bottle into the APU.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#fire-protection--apu-fire-cross-reference\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; See the G550 APU Study Guide: \"APU Fire Detection System\" and \"APU Fire Extinguishing System\" sections for full detail</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--overheat-detection-system\">Overheat Detection System</h2>\n<p>Ten (10) areas of the aircraft are monitored by thermal switches for overheat conditions, distinct from the fire-loop and smoke-detector systems:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Area Type</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Trip Point</strong></th>\n<th><strong>CAS Severity</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleed-air-related areas</td>\n<td>250F</td>\n<td>Red overheat CAS (Warning) - W lights</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic equipment areas</td>\n<td>150F</td>\n<td>Amber overheat CAS (Caution) - C lights</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>An overheat condition is a lower-severity precursor warning distinct from an actual fire/smoke indication - it flags excessive heat in bleed air ducting or electronic equipment bays before a fire condition develops.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--smoke-detection-system\">Smoke Detection System</h2>\n<p>Smoke detectors use optical sensors, installed in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Baggage compartment</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Forward lavatory</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Aft lavatory</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Galley</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Detection triggers crew notification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Forward Lavatory Smoke, Galley Smoke, Aft Lavatory Smoke, Aft Baggage Smoke - each with 2x master warning (W) lights and corresponding CAS message.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--crew-notification-cas-messages\">Crew Notification / CAS Messages</h2>\n<p><strong>Fire indications (2x master warning lights each):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Engine Fire, R Engine Fire, APU Fire, Aft Baggage Flame, Aft Lavatory Flame, Forward Lavatory Flame, Galley Flame.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Associated fire extinguisher discharge CAS (\"FIRE EXT DISCH'D\") and fire handle pop-out for engine/APU fires.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Smoke indications (2x master warning lights each):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Forward Lavatory Smoke, Galley Smoke, Aft Lavatory Smoke, Aft Baggage Smoke.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Overheat indications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Red Overheat CAS (250F, bleed-air related) - 2x master warning (W) lights.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Amber Overheat CAS (150F, electrical/electronic related) - 2x master caution (C) lights.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> On the ground only, an aural \"fire bell\" also sounds for confirmed fire conditions (located in the nose wheel well area) - this is not heard in flight.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--smoke-evacuation-system\">Smoke Evacuation System</h2>\n<p><strong>Baggage compartment smoke evacuation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Smoke is evacuated by deflating the seal around the baggage compartment exterior door.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Differential pressure (high to low, cabin to outside) forces smoke to exit the aircraft through the deflated seal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Emergency Smoke Evacuation VLV (valve) is selected to VENT/SMOKE to deflate the seal; the internal baggage door remains closed during evacuation so smoke is drawn from the compartment and out, not into the cabin.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After venting, the seal can be reinflated (VLV back to NORMAL OPS) and the Bag Compt Vent Valve Reset toggle held up for 10 seconds to repressurize the baggage compartment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cabin smoke evacuation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Smoke in the cabin can also be vented overboard by partially opening the internal baggage door, allowing conditioned air flow to carry cabin smoke aft into the baggage compartment and out through the same exterior door path.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Sequence: Smoke Evacuation Valve controls the seal (deflate to vent, reinflate to restore pressurization); the Bag Compt Vent Valve Reset toggle (hold up 10 sec) restores normal baggage compartment pressurization after the seal is reinflated.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-protection--portable-fire-extinguishers-pbe\">Portable Fire Extinguishers / PBE</h2>\n<p><strong>Portable fire-fighting equipment carried in the cabin:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Three (3) Halon portable fire extinguishers, positioned throughout the cabin (forward, mid-cabin, and aft near the aft PBE station).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>One (1) water portable fire extinguisher, mid-cabin.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>One (1) Protective Breathing Equipment (PBE) station, located aft near the aft engine/cabin door area.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-check against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (cold-weather table): the Amerex portable fire extinguisher must be removed at -65C; the Kidde portable fire extinguisher must be removed at -40C. These are two distinct extinguisher models installed in different cabin positions - both figures are consistent with, and now cross-referenced to, this notes' 3+1 portable extinguisher count.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"fire-protection--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_fire_protection_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Fire Protection System study notes, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - cold-weather table cross-checked for portable fire extinguisher removal temperatures (Amerex -65C, Kidde -40C); found consistent, no dedicated Fire Protection limitations section exists in that guide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 APU Study Guide (this folder) - cross-referenced for full APU-specific fire detection/extinguishing detail, to avoid duplicating that content here.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","fire-protection--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","fire-protection--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","The Four Pillars","fire-protection--the-four-pillars"],["h2","Engine Fire Bottle Cross-Feed (Key Numbers)","fire-protection--engine-fire-bottle-cross-feed-key-numbers"],["h2","Overheat Detection Trip Points","fire-protection--overheat-detection-trip-points"],["h2","Engine Fire Test - Indications","fire-protection--engine-fire-test---indications"],["h2","Portable Fire Extinguishers and PBE","fire-protection--portable-fire-extinguishers-and-pbe"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY FUNCTION","fire-protection--part-2---full-detail-by-function"],["h2","Overview / The Four Pillars","fire-protection--overview-the-four-pillars"],["h2","Engine Fire Detection System","fire-protection--engine-fire-detection-system"],["h2","Engine Fire Extinguishing System","fire-protection--engine-fire-extinguishing-system"],["h2","Engine Fire Test / Fault Test","fire-protection--engine-fire-test-fault-test"],["h2","APU Fire (Cross-Reference)","fire-protection--apu-fire-cross-reference"],["h2","Overheat Detection System","fire-protection--overheat-detection-system"],["h2","Smoke Detection System","fire-protection--smoke-detection-system"],["h2","Crew Notification / CAS Messages","fire-protection--crew-notification-cas-messages"],["h2","Smoke Evacuation System","fire-protection--smoke-evacuation-system"],["h2","Portable Fire Extinguishers / PBE","fire-protection--portable-fire-extinguishers-pbe"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","fire-protection--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"powerplant":{"t":"Powerplant (Engine) Study Guide","sub":"Two (2) Rolls-Royce BR700-710C4-11 · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on the G550 Powerplant system · cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide Powerplant section (01-71 to 01-80)","html":"<h1 id=\"powerplant--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 Powerplant study notes (Rolls-Royce BR700-710C4-11) into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by system rather than by page order, and cross-checked against the Powerplant section of the G550 Limitations Study Guide (built from the FAA-approved AFM, Chapter 01-71 through 01-80) already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every hard number (thrust, spool speeds, LP%/HP%/TGT by condition, start cycles, crosswind/tailwind limits, reverse thrust limits) in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by System: engine overview and construction, FADEC/EEC, ignition system, engine start sequence and modes, the full engine operating limits table, ground start and crosswind/tailwind limits, takeoff limitations, in-flight limits and airstart envelope, thrust reversers, fuel system, oil system, and the engine restart decision logic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified against the Limitations Study Guide:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All limitation figures in the handwritten notes were found consistent with the Limitations Guide Powerplant section (01-71 to 01-80): TGT limits (700C ground start / 850C airstart / 900C takeoff, 5-min AEO / 10-min OEI / 860C max continuous), oil temperature (-30C start / +20C taxi-takeoff / +160C max), fuel temperature (-40C min / +140C max / +165C 15-min transient), engine start cycles (3 min / 15 sec, repeated, then 3 min / 15 min cooldown), ground start crosswind/tailwind (30 kt / 20 kt), crosswind takeoff procedure (&gt;20 kt crosswind - limit fan speed to &lt;=66% LP RPM until 20 kt forward speed, then slam-accelerate within 5 seconds, add 600 ft field length), airstart envelope (&lt;=25,000 ft, &lt;=250 KCAS starter-assist / &gt;=251 KCAS windmill, max TGT 850C), and reverse thrust (70% LP RPM, 30-second limit, idle reverse by 60 KCAS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Two figures appear only in the Limitations Guide and not in these handwritten notes - added here for completeness: maximum overspeed (101.5% LP / 99.8% HP, 20 sec) and maximum overtemperature (905C TGT, 20 sec), plus the detailed oil pressure schedule (shutdown required below 25 psi; minimum for takeoff 35 psi at &lt;72.3% HP or 45 psi at &gt;90.0% HP).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The \"42% RPM\" figure appears in both sources describing two different systems at the same point in the start sequence: the handwritten notes state igniters are automatically turned off at 42% HP RPM during start, while the Limitations Guide states the starter may be re-engaged up to 42% HP RPM (starter cut-out) - these are consistent, not duplicative: both describe FADEC/starter logic around the same RPM milestone in a normal start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"powerplant--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-general\">Engine General</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine</td>\n<td>Two (2) Rolls-Royce BR700-710C4-11 - high-bypass turbofan, twin-spool, FADEC-controlled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrust</td>\n<td>15,385 lb @ ISA +15C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bypass ratio</td>\n<td>4:1 (approx. 25% of core air through the HP core, remainder bypassed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LP (inner) spool - 100%</td>\n<td>7,431 RPM (single-stage fan / 3-stage LP turbine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HP (outer) spool - 100%</td>\n<td>15,898 RPM (10-stage compressor / 2-stage HP turbine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critical engine</td>\n<td>Right engine (AFM - Performance, Section 5.01.10)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction</td>\n<td>Titanium inlet cowling; high thrust-to-weight ratio for fuel efficiency and noise reduction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#powerplant--engine-overview-general\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Engine Overview / General</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-operating-limits-core-table\">Engine Operating Limits (Core Table)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max LP%</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max HP%</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max TGT</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Limit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground start</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>700C</td>\n<td>Momentary; max 150C prior to introducing fuel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airstart (relight)</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>850C</td>\n<td>Momentary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Takeoff</td>\n<td>101.1</td>\n<td>99.6</td>\n<td>900C</td>\n<td>5 min AEO / 10 min OEI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum continuous (MCT)</td>\n<td>101.0</td>\n<td>98.9</td>\n<td>860C</td>\n<td>Unrestricted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum overspeed *</td>\n<td>101.5</td>\n<td>99.8</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>20 sec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum overtemperature *</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>905C</td>\n<td>20 sec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reverse thrust</td>\n<td>70.0</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>30 sec</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> * Overspeed and overtemperature limits are not shown in the handwritten source notes; added here from the G550 Limitations Study Guide for a complete reference table.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#powerplant--engine-operating-limits\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Engine Operating Limits</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--ground-start-crosswind-tailwind-limits\">Ground Start / Crosswind-Tailwind Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum oil temperature (start)</td>\n<td>-30C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum bleed air (start)</td>\n<td>40 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum TGT prior to fuel introduction</td>\n<td>150C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum TGT - ground start</td>\n<td>700C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max crosswind - ground start</td>\n<td>30 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max tailwind - ground start</td>\n<td>20 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine start cycles</td>\n<td>3 min / 15 sec cooling - repeat - 3 min / 15 min cooling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rotor bow risk window</td>\n<td>&gt; 20 minutes and &lt; 5 hours since last shutdown</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#powerplant--ground-start-crosswind-tailwind-limits-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Ground Start / Crosswind-Tailwind Limits</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--takeoff-limitations\">Takeoff Limitations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Alternate Control (LP) mode takeoff</td>\n<td>Prohibited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum oil temperature - takeoff</td>\n<td>20C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum TGT - takeoff</td>\n<td>900C (5 min AEO / 10 min OEI, not beyond V1 for OEI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crosswind takeoff (&gt;20 kt X-wind)</td>\n<td>Limit fan speed &lt;=66% LP RPM until 20 kt forward speed, then slam-accelerate to EPR (rated/flex) within 5 sec; add 600 ft to required field length</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APU bleed-off takeoff</td>\n<td>APU bleed air selectable ON at 1,500 ft AGL (QRH Alt. Normals NG-11); adds ~1.4% gross weight (Perf. Handbook PA-47, Note 3)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#powerplant--takeoff-limitations-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Takeoff Limitations</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--in-flight-limits-airstart-envelope\">In-Flight Limits / Airstart Envelope</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum continuous thrust (MCT)</td>\n<td>860C TGT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airstart envelope - maximum altitude</td>\n<td>25,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Starter-assist airstart</td>\n<td>&lt;= 250 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Windmill airstart</td>\n<td>&gt;= 251 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum TGT - airstart</td>\n<td>850C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FADEC protection during start</td>\n<td>None - crew must monitor limits manually</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#powerplant--in-flight-limits-airstart-envelope-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: In-Flight Limits / Airstart Envelope</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--thrust-reversers-landing\">Thrust Reversers / Landing</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Max reverse thrust</td>\n<td>70% LP RPM, 30-second limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cancel reverse thrust</td>\n<td>Reach idle by 60 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Idle reverse (taxi)</td>\n<td>No time limit; power-back not approved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Before shutdown</td>\n<td>Recommended: operate engines at idle for 3 minutes before shutdown</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#powerplant--thrust-reversers-landing-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Thrust Reversers / Landing</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"powerplant--part-2---full-detail-by-system\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-overview-general\">Engine Overview / General</h2>\n<p>Two (2) Rolls-Royce BR700-710C4-11 engines. Designation breakdown: BR700 (engine identifier) - 710 (engine type) - C (rating standard) - 4 (mechanical standard) - 11 (customer application, G550).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>15,385 lb of thrust @ ISA +15C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>High-bypass turbofan, 4:1 bypass ratio (approximately 25% of intake air passes through the HP core; the remainder is bypassed around it).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FADEC-controlled. Engine Pressure Ratio (EPR) = output / input.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Titanium inlet cowling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>High thrust-to-weight ratio, contributing to fuel efficiency and noise reduction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Right engine is designated the critical engine (AFM - Performance, Section 5.01.10).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Twin-spool construction - the HP and LP compressor sections are each driven by their own coaxial shaft (a shaft within a shaft):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Rotor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Construction</strong></th>\n<th><strong>100% RPM</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>LP (inner spool)</td>\n<td>Single-stage fan driven by a three (3)-stage LP turbine</td>\n<td>7,431 RPM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HP (outer spool)</td>\n<td>Ten (10)-stage compressor driven by a two (2)-stage HP turbine</td>\n<td>15,898 RPM</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Airflow path: air enters the LP fan, is split into core flow (through the HP compressor and annular combustion chamber) and bypass flow (around the core), then the mixed flow exits aft. The FADEC is mounted on top of the engine case; the accessory gearbox (AGB) is mounted on the bottom.</p>\n<p><strong>Accessory Gearbox (AGB): uses power extracted from the HP compressor shaft to drive:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Integrated Drive Generator (IDG)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel Metering Unit (FMU)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel pump</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oil pump and breather</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Permanent Magnet Alternator (PMA)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hydraulic pump</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fans turn counter-clockwise when viewed from the front.</p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--fadec-electronic-engine-controller-eec\">FADEC / Electronic Engine Controller (EEC)</h2>\n<p>Full Authority Digital Electronic Control (FADEC): mounted on the outside, upper portion of each engine. Consists of three (3) major components: the Electronic Engine Controller (EEC, dual channel A/B), the Data Entry Plug (DEP), and the Permanent Magnet Alternator (PMA).</p>\n<p><strong>FADEC provides engine start protection under two conditions only:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>On the ground only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>With the START MASTER switch in the ON position only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The FADEC controls and responds to EPR requirements, and provides: engine protection, improved handling, better fuel efficiency, and prolonged engine life.</p>\n<h3 id=\"powerplant--electronic-engine-controller-eec\">Electronic Engine Controller (EEC)</h3>\n<p>The \"brains\" of the FADEC. Dual channels - one active and one standby; the fuel control switch changes channels. The EEC controls engine idle speed. Idle Speed Control is based on EPR with thrust levers at idle, using three (3) modes:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Trigger</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Low Idle</td>\n<td>Flaps &lt; 22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High Idle</td>\n<td>Flaps &gt; 22, weight-off-wheels (air), or wheel speed &lt; 53 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground Idle</td>\n<td>Aircraft weight-on-wheels (ground); touchdown + 5 seconds (delay allows full and rapid spool-up, within 8 seconds, if needed at short final)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The EEC has three (3) control modes, each referencing a different parameter:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Primary Control Mode - EPR / HP RPM at idle.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Alternate Control Mode - LP RPM.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Reverse Thrust Control Mode - LP RPM.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Independent Overspeed Protection (IOP): both EEC channels must agree (both indicate an overspeed condition) before the EEC commands the FMU to shut off fuel to the engine. If only one channel indicates an overspeed, no shutdown occurs - this prevents a single-channel fault from shutting down a healthy engine.</p>\n<h3 id=\"powerplant--data-entry-plug-dep\">Data Entry Plug (DEP)</h3>\n<p>The DEP contains engine trim data. This trim data is utilized by the EEC to make all engines produce the same thrust for a given EPR command (e.g., both engines showing 95.4 EPR TO/GA).</p>\n<h3 id=\"powerplant--permanent-magnet-alternator-pma\">Permanent Magnet Alternator (PMA)</h3>\n<p>Primary source of EEC power once the engine accelerates above 35% HP RPM. Below 35% HP RPM, the ESS DC BUS powers the EEC; above 35% HP RPM, the PMA takes over as the EEC power source.</p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--ignition-system\">Ignition System</h2>\n<p>Dual-channel ignition exciter (A and B) on each engine, controlled by the dual-channel EEC and powered by 28 VDC. Generates a high-voltage pulse transmitted through ignition leads to ignition plugs in the combustor.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Ground starts: one (1) igniter is used.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Airstarts: two (2) igniters are used (higher sparking rate).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Manual ignition: two (2) igniters are used.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Igniters are turned off automatically at 42% HP RPM during engine start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>There is no time limit on the use of continuous ignition.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>EECs alternate channels and igniters across consecutive start attempts, in this order:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. EEC Channel A / Igniter 1</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. EEC Channel B / Igniter 1</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3. EEC Channel A / Igniter 2</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>4. EEC Channel B / Igniter 2</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Example - Right engine start with Igniter 1 failed: 1st attempt EEC A / Igniter 1 (fails), 2nd attempt EEC B / Igniter 1 (fails), 3rd attempt EEC A / Igniter 2 (succeeds). The rotation lets the system automatically work around a single failed igniter or channel.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ignition modes:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>#</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Description</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Auto Start</td>\n<td>START MASTER ON + Select Start L/R ENG ON. One igniter only; EEC alternates channels; fuel control switches change igniter plugs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Alternate Start</td>\n<td>CRANK MASTER ON + Select Start L/R ENG ON, or manually select CONT IGN L/R ON. Uses two (2) igniters; no FADEC protection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Inclement Weather Mode</td>\n<td>T30 probe senses moisture and activates continuous ignition automatically.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Auto-Relight Mode</td>\n<td>Activates automatically on an HP, LP, or TGT abnormality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Quick-Restart Mode</td>\n<td>For an inadvertent engine shutdown in flight; return the fuel control to RUN within 30 seconds.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-start-sequence-modes\">Engine Start Sequence / Modes</h2>\n<p>See \"Ignition System\" above for the five ignition/start modes (Auto Start, Alternate Start, Inclement Weather Mode, Auto-Relight Mode, Quick-Restart Mode) and the igniter/channel rotation logic used across repeated start attempts.</p>\n<p>Idle logic during descent/approach (Low Idle -&gt; High Idle -&gt; Ground Idle) is described under \"FADEC / Electronic Engine Controller\" above; the Ground Idle 5-second touchdown delay allows the EEC to command full and rapid engine spool-up (within 8 seconds) if a go-around is required at the final approach fix.</p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-operating-limits\">Engine Operating Limits</h2>\n<p><strong>Engine Oil Temperature:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>-30C - minimum for start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>+20C - minimum for taxi / takeoff.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>+160C - maximum.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Fuel Temperature:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>-40C - minimum.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>+140C - maximum (unrestricted).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>+165C - maximum, 15-minute transient.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Start Cycles (cycle / cooling period):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cycle</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cooling Period</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3 minutes</td>\n<td>15 seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 minutes</td>\n<td>15 seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 minutes</td>\n<td>15 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Three consecutive start attempts of 3 minutes each; after the first two, a 15-second cooling period is sufficient, but after the third consecutive attempt a full 15-minute cooling period is required.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Engine TGT (Turbine Gas Temperature):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>TGT</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>150C</td>\n<td>Maximum prior to introducing fuel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>700C</td>\n<td>Start - ground (momentary)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>850C</td>\n<td>Start - in-flight / airstart (momentary)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>900C</td>\n<td>Takeoff (5 minutes AEO)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>900C</td>\n<td>One-engine-inoperative (OEI) (10 minutes, not beyond V1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>860C</td>\n<td>Maximum continuous thrust (MCT)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Engine Oil Level Check:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Check between 15 and 30 minutes (&gt;15 / &lt;30 min) after shutdown.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Check on the last flight of the day.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Check every 24 cumulative flight hours.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Two additional figures from the G550 Limitations Study Guide (not shown in the handwritten notes): maximum overspeed 101.5% LP / 99.8% HP for 20 seconds, and maximum overtemperature 905C TGT for 20 seconds. The Limitations Guide also details the oil pressure schedule: engine shutdown required below 25 psi; minimum for takeoff 35 psi (&lt;72.3% HP) or 45 psi (&gt;90.0% HP); minimum to complete the flight 25 psi / 35 psi respectively.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--ground-start-crosswind-tailwind-limits-1\">Ground Start / Crosswind-Tailwind Limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum oil temperature for start: -30C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum bleed air for start: 40 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum TGT prior to introducing fuel: 150C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum TGT - ground start: 700C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum crosswind for ground start: 30 kt.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum tailwind for ground start: 20 kt.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Engine start cycles and rotor bow risk window (&gt;20 minutes and &lt;5 hours since last shutdown - motoring may be required per AFM/AOM guidance) are the same as shown in \"Engine Operating Limits\" above.</p>\n<h3 id=\"powerplant--static-ground-run-keep-out-zone\">Static Ground Run / Keep-Out Zone</h3>\n<p>Stabilized static ground operation between 66% and 80% LP RPM is prohibited; the engine must transit through this band in 10 seconds or less (forward thrust only). Automatic protection: the parking brake must be set, and this protection is also active when the aircraft is in reverse thrust.</p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--takeoff-limitations-1\">Takeoff Limitations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff in Alternate Control (LP) mode is prohibited.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum oil temperature for takeoff: +20C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum TGT for takeoff: 900C (5 minutes AEO / 10 minutes OEI, not beyond V1 for OEI).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Crosswind Takeoff Limitation (&gt;20 kt crosswind):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Crosswind component &gt;= 20 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Limit fan speed to &lt;= 66% LP RPM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Once forward speed &gt;= 20 kt...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>...slam-accelerate to EPR (rated or flex) within &lt;= 5 seconds; autothrottle is acceptable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Add 600 ft to the required field length when using this crosswind takeoff technique.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"powerplant--engine-bleeds-off-takeoff\">Engine Bleeds-Off Takeoff</h3>\n<p>Per the QRH Alternate Normals Bleed-Off Takeoff Procedure (NG-11): APU bleed air may be selected ON at 1,500 ft AGL. This adds approximately 1.4% gross weight per the Performance Handbook (PA-47, Note 3).</p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--in-flight-limits-airstart-envelope-1\">In-Flight Limits / Airstart Envelope</h2>\n<p><strong>Maximum Continuous Thrust (MCT): 860C TGT.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Airstart Envelope:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum altitude for airstart: 25,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Starter-assist airstart: &lt;= 250 KCAS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Windmill airstart: &gt;= 251 KCAS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum TGT during airstart: 850C.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> No FADEC protection is provided during an in-flight airstart - the crew must monitor TGT and RPM manually against the limits above.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--thrust-reversers-landing-1\">Thrust Reversers / Landing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum reverse thrust: 70% LP RPM, 30-second limit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cancel reverse thrust to reach idle by 60 KCAS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Idle reverse for taxi has no time limit; power-back (taxiing backward under reverse thrust) is not approved.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> It is recommended to operate the engines at idle for 3 minutes before shutdown, to allow even thermal stabilization of the hot section before fuel/ignition is cut.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-fuel-system\">Engine Fuel System</h2>\n<p>Delivers metered fuel from the wing tanks' boost pumps to the fuel spray nozzles (20 per engine). Introduction of fuel is controlled by the FADEC. The Low Pressure (LP) and High Pressure (HP) fuel pumps are driven by the engine's accessory gearbox.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The Fuel Cooled Oil Cooler (FCOC) extracts heat from hot engine oil, using fuel as the heat sink.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The LP pump can suction-feed the engine at or below 20,000 ft (i.e., without boost pump pressure).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fuel flow path: wing tank -&gt; LP pump (25 psi) -&gt; LP pump stage (150 psi) -&gt; FCOC -&gt; HP pump (1,200 psi) -&gt; Fuel Metering Unit (FMU, fuel temperature ~50C at this point) -&gt; High Pressure Shutoff Valve (HP SOV) -&gt; 20 fuel spray nozzles. A fuel return line (OFF/AUTO) and crossflow valve are also part of the system.</p>\n<h3 id=\"powerplant--fuel-shutoff-and-fire-handles\">Fuel Shutoff and Fire Handles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel control switches shut off fuel at the pylon.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each engine has its own fire handle (L / R). Pulling a fire handle shuts off fuel at the tank (upstream of the pylon shutoff).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The fuel Shutoff Valve (SOV) position indicator is located in the wheel well, showing OPEN or CLOSED.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-oil-system\">Engine Oil System</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine oil tank capacity</td>\n<td>32.8 US pints / 15.5 liters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressure pumps</td>\n<td>One (1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scavenge pumps</td>\n<td>Four (4)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The oil pump, oil filter, and FCOC are located at, and driven by, the engine's accessory gearbox. A magnetic chip detector is installed downstream of the FCOC to catch ferrous debris. Oil is distributed to the accessory gearbox, front bearing chamber, and rear bearing chamber, then returned to the tank by the one (1) pressure and four (4) scavenge pumps.</p>\n<p><strong>Engine oil servicing methods:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Conventional gravity servicing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Remote replenishment system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>See \"Engine Operating Limits\" above for oil temperature limits (-30C start / +20C taxi-takeoff / +160C max) and the oil level check schedule.</p>\n<h2 id=\"powerplant--engine-restart-decision-logic\">Engine Restart Decision Logic</h2>\n<p><strong>Do NOT attempt a restart if the engine shutdown was caused by:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fire.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FOD (Foreign Object Damage).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Frozen (e.g., ice ingestion / frozen components).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Restart decision - Yes or No:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Decision</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Path</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cautions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>YES</td>\n<td>Airstart - Automatic (per checklist)</td>\n<td>No FADEC protection during start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>YES</td>\n<td>Airstart - Windmilling (per checklist)</td>\n<td>No FADEC protection during start; consider icing conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NO</td>\n<td>Engine Shutdown in Flight checklist</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Related crossflow/electrical actions during restart:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1. Open the crossflow valve.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2. Select the Emergency Power panel to OFF, then re-arm the emergency batteries (E-BATTs) via ON / ARM / OFF.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"powerplant--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_powerplant_system.pdf - handwritten G550 Powerplant study notes (Rolls-Royce BR700-710C4-11), Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - Powerplant section (01-71 to 01-80), cross-checked and confirmed consistent for all limitation figures present in both sources (TGT, oil/fuel temperatures, start cycles, crosswind/tailwind limits, reverse thrust limits, airstart envelope). Two figures (overspeed/overtemperature limits and the detailed oil pressure schedule) were sourced from the Limitations Guide only, since they do not appear in the handwritten notes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","powerplant--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","powerplant--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Engine General","powerplant--engine-general"],["h2","Engine Operating Limits (Core Table)","powerplant--engine-operating-limits-core-table"],["h2","Ground Start / Crosswind-Tailwind Limits","powerplant--ground-start-crosswind-tailwind-limits"],["h2","Takeoff Limitations","powerplant--takeoff-limitations"],["h2","In-Flight Limits / Airstart Envelope","powerplant--in-flight-limits-airstart-envelope"],["h2","Thrust Reversers / Landing","powerplant--thrust-reversers-landing"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SYSTEM","powerplant--part-2---full-detail-by-system"],["h2","Engine Overview / General","powerplant--engine-overview-general"],["h2","FADEC / Electronic Engine Controller (EEC)","powerplant--fadec-electronic-engine-controller-eec"],["h2","Ignition System","powerplant--ignition-system"],["h2","Engine Start Sequence / Modes","powerplant--engine-start-sequence-modes"],["h2","Engine Operating Limits","powerplant--engine-operating-limits"],["h2","Ground Start / Crosswind-Tailwind Limits","powerplant--ground-start-crosswind-tailwind-limits-1"],["h2","Takeoff Limitations","powerplant--takeoff-limitations-1"],["h2","In-Flight Limits / Airstart Envelope","powerplant--in-flight-limits-airstart-envelope-1"],["h2","Thrust Reversers / Landing","powerplant--thrust-reversers-landing-1"],["h2","Engine Fuel System","powerplant--engine-fuel-system"],["h2","Engine Oil System","powerplant--engine-oil-system"],["h2","Engine Restart Decision Logic","powerplant--engine-restart-decision-logic"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","powerplant--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"limitations":{"t":"Limitations Study Guide","sub":"Consolidated from AFM Chapter 01 (FAA Approved, Revision 44, 2023-01-27), · Gulfstream Operating Manual, and G550-specific operational supplements","html":"<h1 id=\"limitations--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide restructures and cross-checks the previous limitations document against the FAA-approved G550 Airplane Flight Manual (AFM), Chapter 01 - Limitations (Revision 44, 2023-01-27), the G550 Operating Manual (Contaminated Runway Performance, Chapter 05-90-00), and the Cold Weather Operations reference. Section numbers in parentheses, e.g. (01-03-40), refer to the corresponding AFM chapter/section so you can jump to the original wording when needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: the numbers you need cold for a checkride or line check (speeds, weights, altitudes, temperatures).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Limitations by AFM Chapter: the complete limitation text, organized exactly as Chapter 01 of the AFM, including items commonly omitted from summary sheets (minimum crew, passenger briefing, fuel grades, RVSM dispatch equipment, data recording, USB ports, etc.).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Corrections made to the prior draft:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Vfe entries restored with their Mach limits (10 / 20 / 39 degrees are each limited to 0.60 Mt in addition to the KCAS value).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>VMCG note added: decreases about 1 kt per 1,000 ft from sea level to 15,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crosswind-by-surface-condition table corrected - the G550 has no single fixed crosswind limit for wet/contaminated runways; it is read off the Canadian Runway Friction Index (CRFI) chart. Maximum demonstrated dry-runway crosswind (28 kt) added; the CRFI method replaces the placeholder blank cells.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Recommended taxi speed corrected - this is not an AFM limitation; it is a performance-planning assumption (20 kt) used in brake-energy calculations, not a hard limit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Added sections missing from the prior draft: Minimum Flight Crew, Maximum Occupants detail (additional crewmember rule for 10-19 pax), Passenger Briefing, Category II operations requirements, Engine Fuel Grades, RVSM required equipment table, Data Link recording effectivity by serial number, USB ports in the cockpit, full Engine Operating limits table (including overtemperature and reverse-thrust time limits), and EFVS visibility/RVR minima.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>All weight, speed, and temperature figures verified line-by-line against the FAA-approved AFM text (Revision 44, 2023-01-27).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"limitations--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--weights-01-03-60\">Weights (01-03-60)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Weight</strong></th>\n<th><strong>lb</strong></th>\n<th><strong>kg</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum Ramp Weight (MRW)</td>\n<td>91,400</td>\n<td>41,458</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum Takeoff Weight (MTOW)</td>\n<td>91,000</td>\n<td>41,277</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum Landing Weight (MLW)</td>\n<td>75,300</td>\n<td>34,155</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum Zero Fuel Weight (MZFW) - FAA</td>\n<td>54,500</td>\n<td>24,721</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum Zero Fuel Weight (MZFW) - JAA/EASA</td>\n<td>52,000</td>\n<td>23,587</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> MTOW may be further restricted by climb performance, brake energy, tire speed, or field length. MLW may be further restricted by climb requirements.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--sec_weight\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-03-60 / 01-03-70 (Weight / Center of Gravity)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--fuel-01-28-10-01-03-80-01-28-30\">Fuel (01-28-10 / 01-03-80 / 01-28-30)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Usable fuel - total</td>\n<td>41,300 lb (18,734 kg) / 6,118 US gal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Usable fuel - each tank (L/R)</td>\n<td>20,650 lb (9,367 kg) / 3,059 US gal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max fuel imbalance - takeoff</td>\n<td>1,000 lb (453.6 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max fuel imbalance - in flight</td>\n<td>2,000 lb (907.2 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temp - Warning (high)</td>\n<td>&gt;= +54C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temp - Caution (low)</td>\n<td>-35C to -36C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel tank temp - Warning (low)</td>\n<td>&lt;= -37C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine fuel temp - min / max unrestricted / max transient (15 min)</td>\n<td>-40C / +140C / +165C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cold fuel rule: if fuel tank temp &lt;= -30C in flight with &lt; 5,000 lb total fuel remaining, descend to where SAT is -60C or warmer and maintain minimum Mach 0.80.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--fuel-01-28\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-28 (Fuel)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--airspeed-limitations-01-03-40\">Airspeed Limitations (01-03-40)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Speed</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mmo / Vmo</td>\n<td>See Altitude/Mach envelope - max 0.885 Mt (FL320-FL440), 0.86 Mt (FL440-FL510)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max speed for RVSM ops</td>\n<td>0.87 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vmc - Air</td>\n<td>112 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vmc - Landing</td>\n<td>110 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vmc - Ground</td>\n<td>107 KCAS (Sea Level) tapering to 86 KCAS (15,000 ft); ~1 kt/1,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maneuvering speed guidance (Va)</td>\n<td>Confine large control deflections/maneuvers to below 206 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vfe - Flaps 10 (Takeoff)</td>\n<td>250 KCAS / 0.60 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vfe - Flaps 20 (T/O APP)</td>\n<td>220 KCAS / 0.60 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vfe - Flaps 39 (DOWN)</td>\n<td>170 KCAS / 0.60 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vle (gear extended, doors open or closed)</td>\n<td>250 KCAS / 0.70 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vlo - normal operation (raise/lower)</td>\n<td>225 KCAS / 0.70 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vlo - alternate (lower only)</td>\n<td>175 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turbulence penetration - below 10,000 ft</td>\n<td>240 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turbulence penetration - at/above 10,000 ft</td>\n<td>270 KCAS / 0.80 Mt, whichever is less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum holding speed in icing</td>\n<td>160 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mach trim / electric elevator trim INOP</td>\n<td>Mmo reduced to 0.80 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum tire speed</td>\n<td>195 kt (ground speed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency stabilizer trim (armed, A/P engaged, jammed elevator)</td>\n<td>270 KCAS / 0.80 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yaw damper INOP - above 10,000 ft (max)</td>\n<td>260 KCAS / 0.80 Mt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yaw damper INOP - below 10,000 ft (max)</td>\n<td>250 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yaw damper INOP - above 20,000 ft (min)</td>\n<td>210 KTAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yaw damper INOP - below 20,000 ft (min)</td>\n<td>Per fuel/flap schedule - see Part 2, 01-03-40</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--sec_speed\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-03-40 (Airspeed Limitations)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--flight-load-acceleration-01-03-50\">Flight Load Acceleration (01-03-50)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Configuration</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limit (up to max weight shown)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps UP (0)</td>\n<td>-1.0 g to +2.5 g, up to MTOW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps 10 or 20</td>\n<td>0 g to +2.0 g, up to MTOW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps 39 (DOWN) - up to MLW</td>\n<td>0 g to +2.0 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps 39 (DOWN) - above MLW</td>\n<td>0 g to +1.5 g</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--sec_load\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-03-50 (Flight Load Acceleration)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--altitudes-temperatures-01-03-20-01-03-30\">Altitudes &amp; Temperatures (01-03-20 / 01-03-30)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum operating altitude</td>\n<td>51,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - Yaw Damper INOP</td>\n<td>45,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - single air conditioning pack</td>\n<td>48,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - extending/flying gear extended</td>\n<td>20,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - extending/flying flaps 39 (DOWN)</td>\n<td>20,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - extending/flying flaps 10 or 20</td>\n<td>25,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max airport pressure altitude (T/O or Ldg) - SN 5001-5059 w/o ASC 040</td>\n<td>12,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max airport pressure altitude - SN 5060+ or SN 5001-5059 w/ ASC 040</td>\n<td>14,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max airport pressure altitude - SN 5323+ with HLFE Pax O2 Controller</td>\n<td>15,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum operating static air temperature</td>\n<td>-70C (Mach schedule required down to -80C - see table below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum Mach schedule -70C to -80C</td>\n<td>-70=.67, -72=.71, -74=.76, -76=.80, -78=.84, -80=.87</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--sec_altitude\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-03-20 / 01-03-30 (Temperature &amp; Altitude)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--cold-weather-action-points-consolidated\">Cold-Weather Action Points (consolidated)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Temp (C)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Applies to</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action / Limit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-70</td>\n<td>All</td>\n<td>Minimum operational temperature (below this, Mach schedule above applies to -80C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-65</td>\n<td>Fire ext.</td>\n<td>Amerex fire extinguisher must be removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-50</td>\n<td>APU</td>\n<td>Minimum ambient temp for APU start on the ground</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-40</td>\n<td>Fire ext. / Fuel</td>\n<td>Kidde fire extinguisher must be removed; minimum engine fuel temperature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-40</td>\n<td>Start-up</td>\n<td>Engine/APU start-up prohibited below this temperature without heating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-37</td>\n<td>Fuel tank</td>\n<td>Minimum fuel tank temperature (Warning below this)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-30</td>\n<td>Engine oil</td>\n<td>Minimum oil temp for engine start and throttle advance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-30</td>\n<td>Fuel (in flight, &lt;5,000 lb)</td>\n<td>Descend to SAT -60C or warmer, min Mach 0.80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-30</td>\n<td>APU fuel</td>\n<td>Minimum fuel temperature for APU start on the ground</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-28 / -53.5</td>\n<td>Life rafts</td>\n<td>Should be removed below -28C; hard limit at -53.5C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-20 or below</td>\n<td>Batteries</td>\n<td>Must be removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-20</td>\n<td>APU battery</td>\n<td>Minimum battery temperature for APU start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-15</td>\n<td>APU EGT/RPM</td>\n<td>Indications may be unreadable; also threshold for overnight cold-soak guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-10</td>\n<td>Generators</td>\n<td>OFF during start until idle; 45-sec crank cycle at max HP/LP before fuel introduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to -14</td>\n<td>Water system</td>\n<td>Purge water if aircraft unheated &gt; 90 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;=10 + visible moisture</td>\n<td>Wing/cowl A/I</td>\n<td>Anti-ice ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Cockpit</td>\n<td>Minimum temp for operating DUs, IRUs, SFD, EBDI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Engine oil</td>\n<td>Minimum oil temp for takeoff power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>54</td>\n<td>Fuel tank</td>\n<td>Maximum fuel tank temperature (Warning at/above)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+35 (95F) or warmer</td>\n<td>APU</td>\n<td>Heat-soak guidance applies to next start</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> See Part 2, 01-30 (Ice and Rain Protection) and the separate Cold Weather Operations reference for full procedures.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--ice-and-rain-protection-01-30\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-30 (Ice and Rain Protection)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--runway-wind-crosswind-01-02-10\">Runway, Wind &amp; Crosswind (01-02-10)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Runway wet definition</td>\n<td>&gt;25% of surface covered by visible dampness/water &lt;= 1/8 in (3 mm) deep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runway contaminated definition</td>\n<td>&gt;25% of surface covered by frost/ice/snow/slush, or standing water &gt; 1/8 in (3 mm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum runway slope</td>\n<td>+2% uphill / -2% downhill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum tailwind - takeoff and landing</td>\n<td>10 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum crosswind - dry runway (max demonstrated)</td>\n<td>28 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum crosswind - wet/contaminated runway</td>\n<td>No single figure - read from Canadian Runway Friction Index (CRFI) chart; e.g., dry snow ~12mm deep gives CRFI 0.21-0.39 -&gt; ~1-14 kt allowable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum crosswind - engine ground start</td>\n<td>30 kt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum tailwind - engine ground start</td>\n<td>20 kt (special technique required above 10 kt - see Part 2, 01-71-20)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommended taxi speed</td>\n<td>Not an AFM limitation. 20 kt is the planning assumption used in brake-energy calculations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#limitations--departure-arrival-airport-01-02\"><em><strong><u>→ Full detail: Part 2, §01-02 (Departure / Arrival Airport)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"limitations--part-2---full-limitations-by-afm-chapter-01\">PART 2 - FULL LIMITATIONS BY AFM CHAPTER 01</h1>\n<p><em>Structure mirrors AFM Chapter 01 exactly, section numbers shown in parentheses.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--conditions-of-operation-01-00-10\">Conditions of Operation (01-00-10)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Certificate and operational limitations are part of the type/airworthiness certificate and apply at all times, regardless of type of operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Performance limitations show compliance with 14 CFR Part 21 and Part 25.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The airplane must be operated per the loading schedule in the separate Weight and Balance manual.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--aircrew-and-occupants-01-01\">Aircrew and Occupants (01-01)</h2>\n<p><strong>Minimum Flight Crew (01-01-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Pilot and copilot.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maximum Number of Occupants (01-01-20)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Total occupants shall not exceed 22.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passengers shall not exceed 19 (per emergency exit requirements), nor the number of approved seating accommodations for takeoff/landing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FAA-certified airplanes only: an additional trained crewmember (not the pilot or copilot) is required on all flights carrying 10-19 passengers, trained in Gulfstream elliptical-exit evacuation and passenger-flow procedures per the operator G550-OMS-01 training program, with training records available for FAA inspection.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Passenger Briefing (01-01-30)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>In addition to the general safety briefing, a preflight briefing on configuration-specific egress procedures/exits is required before each flight, including a detailed explanation of the optimum method for evacuating through the over-wing Gulfstream elliptical exits.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--departure-arrival-airport-01-02\">Departure / Arrival Airport (01-02)</h2>\n<p><strong>Runway, Slope and Wind Conditions (01-02-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Wet / contaminated runway definitions as in Quick-Reference table above.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum slopes: +2% uphill, -2% downhill.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum tailwind for takeoff and landing: 10 kt.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Smooth and/or grooved hard-surfaced runways only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>High Elevation Airport Operations (01-02-20)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Max approved airport pressure altitude for takeoff/landing: 12,000 ft (SN 5001-5059 w/o ASC 040); 14,500 ft (SN 5060+ or SN 5001-5059 with ASC 040); 15,000 ft (SN 5323+ with HLFE Passenger Oxygen Controller).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--performance-01-03\">Performance (01-03)</h2>\n<p><strong>Types of Airplane Operations Permitted (01-03-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Transport Category - Land; Day/Night VFR; IFR.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Enhanced Flight Vision System (EFVS) operations - with limits in 01-34-70.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Category I approach operations (Category II approved for HUD - see 01-34-80).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flight into known icing; Extended overwater flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Polar navigation - TRUE heading must be selected prior to N73/S60 latitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RVSM airspace operations - with limits in 01-34-110.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Category II operations - requires ASC 014A (or later) and civil-authority authorization (LOA/Ops Specs).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AFN, ADS-C and CPDLC data link operations - complies with DO-258A; requires civil-authority authorization; guidance in AC 120-70 and ICAO GOLD manual.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ASC 084D+: SBAS receiver authorized for WAAS and EGNOS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ADS-B Out - Version 1 (DO-260A) for ASC 114A/SN 5467-5545; Version 2 (DO-260B) for ASC 105C/111A or SN 5546+; complies with 14 CFR 91.225/91.227, AC 20-165A, EASA AMC 20-24.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Data Link: ATN B1 (ASC 115, VDL M2); FANS 1/A(+) (SN 5001-5339 w/ ASC 084D, 096, 111A, or SN 5340+; PBCS RCP/RSP valid for SN 5001-5595 w/ ASC 912A or SN 5596+).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Navigation: complies with RNP RNAV per DO-236/DO-283. Approved specifications: Oceanic/Remote (formerly Class II Nav), NAT-HLA (MNPS), RNAV-10/RNP-10, B-RNAV/RNAV-5/RNP-5, RNP-4, RNAV-2/RNAV-1/P-RNAV, RNP-2 (domestic/offshore and oceanic/remote), RNP-1, RNP APCH (to LNAV/LNAV-VNAV/LPV minima), RNP AR APCH (to RNP 0.1 with ASC 084+), A-RNP, and VNAV (baro-VNAV/SBAS; QFE prohibited).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"anchor\" id=\"limitations--sec_altitude\"></span><strong>Minimum / Maximum Operating Temperature (01-03-20)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>See Quick-Reference Altitude/Temperature table above for the full envelope and cold-Mach schedule.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maximum Operating Altitudes (01-03-30)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>See Quick-Reference table above.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"anchor\" id=\"limitations--sec_speed\"></span><strong>Airspeed Limitations (01-03-40)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Full speed schedule in Quick-Reference table above.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Yaw Damper Inoperative - Minimum Speed Schedule Below 20,000 ft</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Fuel (lb)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>SL-5,000 ft Flaps 0/10/20</strong></th>\n<th><strong>SL-5,000 ft Flaps 39</strong></th>\n<th><strong>5,000-20,000 ft Flaps 0/10/20</strong></th>\n<th><strong>5,000-20,000 ft Flaps 39</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>23,000</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>135</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26,000</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>153</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29,000</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>153</td>\n<td>160</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>32,000</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>168</td>\n<td>160</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35,000</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>184</td>\n<td>160</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>38,000</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>160</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>41,300</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>VREF</td>\n<td>217</td>\n<td>160</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Abbreviated from the full AFM table (01-03-40); intermediate 1,000 lb increments follow a linear progression. VREF = approach speed for landing in the current flap setting.</em></p>\n<p><span class=\"anchor\" id=\"limitations--sec_load\"></span><strong>Flight Load Acceleration (01-03-50)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>See Quick-Reference table above.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"anchor\" id=\"limitations--sec_weight\"></span><strong>Weight (01-03-60) / Center of Gravity (01-03-70)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Weights per Quick-Reference table above.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Zero-fuel gross weight CG must be within the allowable ZFW CG envelope (FAA or JAA chart as applicable); the fueled airplane CG is then within limits for all fuel loads.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maximum Fuel Imbalance (01-03-80)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff: 1,000 lb (453.6 kg). In flight: 2,000 lb (907.2 kg).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Begin fuel-load balancing before imbalance exceeds 1,000 lb, using crossflow or intertank valve.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When balancing via crossflow, keep boosted fuel pressure available to the engines and fuel temp above 0C before turning boost pump(s) OFF.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>CAUTION - engine runs on suction feed only at/below 20,000 ft; above that, it will run erratically and flame out if the crossflow valve is not open with at least one boost pump ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Eye-to-Wheel Height (01-03-90)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum eye-to-wheel height, unloaded main gear, landing attitude (3.3 nose up): 15.6 ft (4.76 m).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--airplane-servicing-01-12\">Airplane Servicing (01-12)</h2>\n<p><strong>Engine Fuel Grades (01-12-30)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Kerosene type (NATO F24/F34/F35): Jet A, Jet A-1, JP-8, and equivalent British/Canadian/French/CIS/Chinese specifications. Mixing is permissible.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Wide-cut JP-4 type (NATO F40): Jet B, JP-4, and equivalent specifications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>High flash point JP-5 type (NATO F44): JP-5 and equivalent specifications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF) per ASTM D7566 Table 1 (and annexes) is regarded as ASTM D1655 fuel and is fully fungible with other D1655 fuel once re-identified.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--air-conditioning-and-bleed-air-01-21\">Air Conditioning and Bleed Air (01-21)</h2>\n<p><strong>Cabin Pressurization Control (01-21-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum cabin pressure differential: 10.48 psi.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum cabin pressure differential for taxi, takeoff, or landing: 0.3 psi.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Internal Baggage Door (01-21-20)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Must remain closed above 40,000 ft; access above 40,000 ft permitted only if closed again after exiting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>On a single ECS pack, baggage compartment access allowed only at/below 45,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Time open above 40,000 ft limited to 5 minutes; crew must ensure door closed and message extinguished within that time.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--auto-flight-01-22\">Auto Flight (01-22)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Mach Trim (01-22-10): must be ON during all flight operations (except as provided for Mach Trim Failure). If inoperative, Mmo reduced to 0.80 Mt.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Coupled Go-Around (01-22-20): single-engine autopilot-coupled go-around is NOT approved.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Autopilot (01-22-30): minimum engage height 200 ft AGL; minimum disengage height 60 ft AGL (ILS/LPV) or 50 ft below DA/MDA for all other ops; max demonstrated altitude loss on coupled go-around: 60 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency Stabilizer Trim (01-22-40): max speed with emergency trim armed, autopilot engaged, jammed elevator: 270 KCAS / 0.80 Mt.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Autothrottle on Single-Engine Approaches (01-22-50): use of autothrottle during single-engine approaches is prohibited.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--communications-01-23\">Communications (01-23)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>CVR Test Function (01-23-10): operative via AV900 ACP for SN 5183+; SN 5001-5182 must use the CVR control panel on the copilot side console.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>8.33 kHz Spacing (01-23-20): Honeywell VHF radios comply with 8.33 kHz spacing and FM immunity requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>HF Radio Operations (01-23-30): do not use HF radios in the hangar or during refueling/defueling; keep personnel clear of the vertical tail while transmitting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Satellite Communications (01-23-40): SATCOM systems do NOT meet AC 20-150B and are not approved as SATVOICE equipment for ATS communications.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--electrical-power-01-24\">Electrical Power (01-24)</h2>\n<p><strong>Standby Electrical Power / HMG (01-24-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Speed brakes may be used on HMG power only if operated slowly (~3 seconds for full range).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Do not operate HMG when normal AC power is available, except for L/R ESS DC Bus Failure procedures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>HMG functional test after engine start is permitted.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--flight-controls-01-27\">Flight Controls (01-27)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Stall Warning / Stall Barrier (01-27-10): two systems installed; dispatch with one inoperative per MMEL. Operative systems must be ON during all flight operations unless selected OFF for procedural reasons.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Speed Brakes (01-27-20): not approved for extension with flaps at 39 (DOWN) or landing gear extended, in flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic Ground Spoilers (01-27-30): takeoff permitted with them inoperative if anti-skid is operative, 20 flaps used, and cowl/wing anti-ice not in use (MMEL). If a touch-and-go is planned, GND SPLR switch must be OFF and manual-spoiler landing distances considered.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--fuel-01-28\">Fuel (01-28)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Usable Fuel Capacities (01-28-10): see Quick-Reference table. Uploading beyond 41,300 lb is possible provided max ramp/takeoff weight and CG limits are respected.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Boost Pumps (01-28-20): all operable pumps ON for all flight phases unless balancing. Do not use boost pumps to balance fuel when tank temp is at or below 0C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel Tank Temperature (01-28-30): see Quick-Reference table; cold-fuel descent rule applies below -30C with &lt; 5,000 lb remaining.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--hydraulics-01-29\">Hydraulics (01-29)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Max reservoir quantities (pressurized): Left 4.8 gal, Right 1.6 gal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Accumulator pre-charge (L &amp; R): 1,200 psi at 70F/21C, +/-25 psi per 10F/5C deviation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--ice-and-rain-protection-01-30\">Ice and Rain Protection (01-30)</h2>\n<p><strong>General (01-30-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Icing conditions exist (ground or flight) when SAT &lt;= +10C and visible moisture is present, or when surface snow/ice/standing water/slush on ramps, taxiways or runways may be ingested or freeze on engines/nacelles/probes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff prohibited with frost, ice, snow, or slush adhering to wings, control surfaces, engine inlets, or other critical surfaces.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Visual + tactile wing check required when OAT &lt; 42F (6C) or wing fuel temp cannot be confirmed above 32F (0C), combined with visible moisture, water on wing, dew point spread &lt;=5F (3C), or frost-conducive conditions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wing / Cowl Anti-Icing (01-30-20 / 01-30-30)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Wing A/I required if icing is imminent or immediately upon ice detection on wings, winglets, or windshield edges.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cowl A/I required for taxi/takeoff when SAT &lt;=10C (50F) with visible moisture, precipitation, or wet runway present; and in flight per the cowl anti-ice temperature chart.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>At low power below 1C (34F), run engines at 40% LP for 10 seconds just before takeoff and at intervals &lt;=60 minutes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic anti-ice is inhibited above 35,000 ft - must be manually selected if required above that altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ice-shedding technique: retard one engine at a time to idle for 5 seconds, then accelerate to 90% LP.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Use of Flaps (01-30-40)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>In icing conditions, flap use is restricted to takeoff, approach, and landing only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Holding in icing conditions is limited to 0 (UP) flaps only, at minimum 160 KCAS.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--indicating-recording-01-31\">Indicating / Recording (01-31)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Electronic Checklist (01-31-10): approved for use; if it conflicts with the AFM, the AFM governs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AGM Reversion (01-31-20): DISPLAY UNIT CONTROL switches operative on the ground only - do not reselect them in flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Video Function (01-31-30): display of any video unrelated to airplane operation (including cabin entertainment feed) is prohibited.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>USB Ports in the Cockpit (01-31-40): may only be used to transfer XM Weather data to an EFB; cords must not interfere with safe operation and must be removed/stowed for taxi, takeoff, and landing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Data Recording (01-31-50): data-link recording not required on aircraft manufactured before Dec 2010 (Part 135) / Apr 2012 (Part 91). Part 135: SN 5001-5308 exempt, SN 5309-5374 need ASC 097B, SN 5375+ equipped in production. Part 91: SN 5001-5374 exempt, SN 5375+ equipped in production.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--landing-gear-01-32\">Landing Gear (01-32)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Anti-Skid (01-32-10): takeoff permitted with anti-skid inoperative if runway is dry, ground spoilers operative, 20 flaps used, and cowl/wing A/I not in use (MEL).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--navigation-01-34\">Navigation (01-34)</h2>\n<p><strong>Inertial Reference System / IRS (01-34-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>No provision for IRS \"Down Mode Align.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Certified alignment to 78 degrees latitude; 15-minute alignment required between 70 and 78 degrees.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>North of 73N / south of 60S: switch heading reference from MAG to TRUE (loss of valid MAG heading from IRS).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Align-in-Motion (AIM) has no restriction, but time can be &lt;10 or &gt;20 minutes with no heading change, no acceleration change, or an east-west trajectory with near-zero sensed rotation. Alignment time increases with latitude.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Airborne Weather Radar (01-34-20)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Do not operate during refueling, or within 300 ft (92 m) of other refueling operations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Otherwise, do not operate within 49 ft (15 m) of ground personnel (24-in antenna).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Flight Management System / FMS (01-34-30)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Current Nav Database required to fly any FMS approach; if out of date, verify lat/long of each waypoint before every flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Polar Operations defined as 89 degrees latitude or above.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>GPS/SBAS TSO-C145C Class 3 complies with AC 20-138D for en route, terminal, non-precision, and vertically-guided approaches (to LNAV / LNAV-VNAV / LPV minima as applicable).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Non-WGS-84 / partially-compliant airspace: FMS+GPS may still be used for SIDs/STARs/en route; for ILS/VOR/ADF approaches, raw data must be primary; RNAV(GNSS) approaches usable if chart is annotated \"PAN OPS.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RNP AR APCH requires specific operational approval (OpSpecs/LOA/MSpecs) - see GAC-OIS-02 / GAC-OIS-07.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>GPS updating must be disabled for non-WGS-84/NAD-83 airspace unless alternate procedures apply.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum demonstrated RNP: 0.10 with autopilot (LNAV), 0.30 without autopilot.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Complies with DO-258A for AFN/ADS-C/CPDLC - engineering approval only; operational approval from local authority required (AC 90-117).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeoff and Landing Data / TOLD (01-34-40)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>TOLD Software ID V1.0+ approved; results must be checked for reasonableness against AFM Section 5 - Performance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>TCAS (01-34-50)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>TCAS II v7.1 (SN 5380+ or SN 5001-5379 w/ ASC 103) or v7.0 (SN 5001-5379 without ASC 103), equivalent to ACAS II.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RA/TA aural messages inhibited below 500 ft +/-100 ft radio altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deviation from ATC clearance authorized to the extent necessary to comply with an RA.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TA information is an aid to visual acquisition only - do not maneuver on TA alone.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Compliance with an RA is required unless the pilot judges it unsafe; maneuvering opposite an RA is prohibited unless visually confirmed as the only safe option.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Prompt return to cleared altitude once \"Clear of Conflict\" is announced.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>With one engine inoperative, select TA ONLY as the operating mode.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>EGPWS (01-34-60)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Deviation from ATC clearance authorized to comply with an EGPWS warning.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Navigation must not be predicated on the Terrain Display - situational awareness only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Terrain display must be selected OFF (TERRAIN INHIBIT ON) within 15 NM of a departure/destination airport not in the EGPWS database.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>EFVS Landing System (01-34-70)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Requires HUD (I or II) and EVS (I or II) system descriptions immediately available to the crew.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>To 100 ft above TDZE: visual cues (14 CFR 91.176(b)(3)) must be seen without EVS aid to continue descent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>To touchdown/rollout: visual references (91.176(a)(3)) must be distinctly visible/identifiable via EFVS; requires crew training per the FAA GV-SP FSB Report and operational approval under AC 90-106.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flight Director or autopilot with vertical guidance (ILS or FMS vertical path) required for all IMC EFVS approaches.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>To touchdown/rollout demonstrated on HUD II + EVS II, SN 5276 and subsequent, per AC 20-167A.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Straight-in offset &gt;3 degrees prohibited; Rad Alt and Flare Cue required; approved down to 1,000 ft RVR, or 1/4 sm (400 m) visibility if TDZ RVR unavailable.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>HUD (01-34-80)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Category I and Category II HUD operations approved. HUD does not provide NDB approach capability - fly NDB approaches via FMS using HUD for guidance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>TAWS (01-34-90) / FM Immunity (01-34-100)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Production EGPWS meets Class A TAWS requirements (AC 25-23).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Honeywell nav receivers comply with FAA TSO, RTCA, and ICAO Annex 10 FM immunity standards.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>RVSM Dispatch Capability (01-34-110)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Authorized per 14 CFR Part 91 Appendix G.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Before takeoff, altimeters set to local QNH; displayed altitude within +/-75 ft of known field elevation, and the two primary altimeters within +/-75 ft of each other.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Equipment</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Installed</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Required for RVSM</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Flight Guidance Computers</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ATC Transponder / Altitude Reporting</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1 (both alt.-reporting systems operative on remaining transponder)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Altitude Alerting System</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air Data System</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Max RVSM operating limit: Mach 0.87 (see RVSM Envelope, AFM Figure 1, 01-34-110).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Electronic Charts (01-34-120)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>PlaneView charts are FAA-certified, equivalent to a Class 3 EFB with Type C software.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Without ASC 084D: paper charts or an accepted Class 1/2 EFB with airport/departure/arrival/approach charts must be readily available (function may fail on DMU/LAN/data-card failure).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>With ASC 084D+ and current database: no additional paper charts or Class 1/2 EFB required.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Check chart effectivity dates before first flight of the day; if expired or flagged \"may contain outdated data,\" check NOTAMs, update the database, or use current paper charts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The airplane symbol on the chart display must not be used for navigation (situational awareness/taxi only).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>VGP Mode (01-34-140) / SV-PFD (01-34-150)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>VGP: use the \"Vectors\" transition-to-final feature when vectored, or initiate via a published IAF. Cannot be used during circling approaches (ASC 084+) or armed while Preview mode is active.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SV-PFD synthetic terrain must be deselected in non-WGS-84 areas; may not be used for navigation, terrain, or obstacle avoidance - EGPWS remains authoritative. ASC 061: one pilot must retain a 2/3 PFD display; ASC 061A+: both pilots may display SV-PFD.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--oxygen-system-01-35\">Oxygen System (01-35)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Above FL250, crew masks must be in quick-donning position (don within 5 seconds); headsets/hats/eyeglasses may interfere and should be considered.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crew and passenger oxygen masks not approved for use above 40,000 ft cabin altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passenger masks are for emergency descent only; they will not provide sufficient oxygen for prolonged use above 34,000 ft cabin altitude, and prolonged operation above 25,000 ft cabin altitude with passengers aboard is not recommended.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--eicas-indications-01-45\">EICAS Indications (01-45)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Parameter</strong></th>\n<th><strong>White/Normal</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Amber/Caution</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Red/Warning</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>TGT</td>\n<td>0-860C</td>\n<td>860-900C</td>\n<td>&gt;=900C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LP RPM</td>\n<td>0-101.0%</td>\n<td>101.0-101.1%</td>\n<td>&gt;=101.1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HP RPM</td>\n<td>0-98.9%</td>\n<td>98.9-99.6%</td>\n<td>&gt;=99.6%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oil Temp</td>\n<td>+20C to 160C</td>\n<td>-30C to +19C</td>\n<td>&gt;=160C or &lt;=-31C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oil Pressure</td>\n<td>&gt;=35 psi</td>\n<td>26-34 psi</td>\n<td>0-25 psi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel Tank Temp</td>\n<td>-34C to +53C</td>\n<td>-35C to -36C</td>\n<td>&gt;=+54C or &lt;=-37C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APU RPM</td>\n<td>0-103%</td>\n<td>104-105%</td>\n<td>&gt;=106%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> EPR command marker range 0.85-1.80 (no limitation marks). EVM (LP/HP) amber &gt;=0.80 IPS. Engine A/I pressure amber &gt;=33 psi. APU EGT has no limitation markings.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--auxiliary-power-unit-apu-01-49\">Auxiliary Power Unit / APU (01-49)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Honeywell RE220</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max EGT - start</td>\n<td>1,050C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max EGT - running</td>\n<td>732C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max rotor speed (all conditions)</td>\n<td>106%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - guaranteed start</td>\n<td>39,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Altitude range - start possible (not guaranteed)</td>\n<td>39,000-43,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational limit</td>\n<td>45,000 ft and below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - APU bleed for takeoff</td>\n<td>1,500 ft AGL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max altitude - bleed air start assist</td>\n<td>25,000 ft (below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generator output</td>\n<td>40 kVA, sea level to 45,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min battery voltage for APU start</td>\n<td>22 V</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min battery temp for APU start</td>\n<td>-20C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min fuel temp for APU start</td>\n<td>-30C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Starting limits - battery power</td>\n<td>3 consecutive attempts, then 1-hr cooldown; successful starts limited to 6 at 10-min intervals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Starting limits - external DC cart</td>\n<td>3 attempts, 15-min cooldown between, 1-hr cooldown after 3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--doors-01-52\">Doors (01-52)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Interior Doors (01-52-10): main entry door curtain, acoustic door system, and all mid-cabin curtains/doors must be fully open for taxi, takeoff, and landing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"limitations--powerplant-01-71-to-01-80\">Powerplant (01-71 to 01-80)</h2>\n<p><strong>Engine Operation - BR700-710C4-11 (01-71-10)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max LP%</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max HP%</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max TGT</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Limit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground start</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>700C</td>\n<td>Momentary; max 150C prior to start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airstart (relight)</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>850C</td>\n<td>Momentary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Takeoff</td>\n<td>101.1</td>\n<td>99.6</td>\n<td>900C</td>\n<td>5 min AEO / 10 min OEI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum continuous</td>\n<td>101.0</td>\n<td>98.9</td>\n<td>860C</td>\n<td>Unrestricted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum overspeed</td>\n<td>101.5</td>\n<td>99.8</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>20 sec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum overtemperature</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>905C</td>\n<td>20 sec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reverse thrust</td>\n<td>70.0</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>30 sec; static reverse limited to 30% LP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Max reverse thrust must be selected only above 60 kt airplane speed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Engine Ground Start (01-71-20)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Max crosswind 30 kt; max tailwind 20 kt. For tailwind &gt;10 kt, allow max cranking RPM and confirm positive LP RPM before selecting fuel control to RUN.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Airstart (01-71-30)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Preferred: Normal Airstart - Automatic. Alternatives: Manual Airstart - Starter Assist; Manual Airstart - Windmilling. See Airstart Envelope chart.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeoff Power (01-71-40)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Alternate Control (LP) mode takeoff is prohibited. Minimum acceptable takeoff power per AFM 05-12-20 EPR/LP tables.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Crosswind Takeoff (01-71-50)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Above 20 kt crosswind: limit fan speed to &lt;66% LP until 20 kt forward speed, then slam-accelerate to takeoff power. Add 600 ft to required field length.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Static Ground Run (01-71-60)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Stabilized static operation between 66-80% LP RPM is prohibited; transit through this band in &lt;=10 sec (forward thrust only).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Fuel / Oil Temperatures (01-73 / 01-79)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engine fuel temp: -40C min; +140C max unrestricted; +165C max 15-min transient.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Max fuel tank temperature: +54C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oil inlet temp: -30C min for start; +20C min for takeoff power; +160C max. External heating required to reach -30C in cold weather; between -30C and +20C use only taxi-required thrust.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oil pressure: engine shutdown required below 25 psi. Minimum for takeoff: 35 psi (&lt;72.3% HP) / 45 psi (&gt;90.0% HP). Minimum to complete flight: 25 psi / 35 psi respectively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Continuous (Airstart) Ignition (01-74-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>No duty-cycle time limitation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Thrust Reversers (01-78-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cancel reverse thrust to reach idle by 60 KCAS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Idle reverse for taxi has no time limit; power-back is not approved.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deploy/stow at least once every 100 hours.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If used in an emergency to stop the airplane, record and report for maintenance action.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Dispatch with an inoperative reverser on a wet runway: reduce available runway length by 1,400 ft, or increase accelerate-stop distance by 1,400 ft.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Starter Duty (01-80-10)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>3 start cycles max, 3 min per cycle, 15-sec delay between cycles; after 3 cycles, wait &gt;=15 min before further use.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Starter may be re-engaged up to 42% HP RPM (starter cut-out).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"limitations--appendix---contaminated-wet-runway-crosswind-reference\">Appendix - Contaminated / Wet Runway Crosswind Reference</h1>\n<p>The G550 has no single AFM crosswind figure for wet or contaminated runways. Gulfstream has not conducted dedicated flight testing for these surfaces; instead, the Operating Manual (Contaminated Runway Performance, 05-90-00) directs use of the Transport Canada-published chart relating Canadian Runway Friction Index (CRFI) to a recommended maximum crosswind component (headwind conditions only). The maximum crosswind demonstrated to the FAA on a dry runway is 28 kt - a demonstrated value, not a hard dry-runway limit.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Runway Surface Contamination</strong></th>\n<th><strong>CRFI Equivalent</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dry snow on pavement - depth &lt; 3 mm</td>\n<td>0.16 - 0.76</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dry snow on pavement - 3-12 mm depth</td>\n<td>0.21 - 0.39</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Example: dry snow at 12 mm (1/2 in) depth -&gt; CRFI 0.21-0.39 -&gt; recommended crosswind approx 1-14 kt. Always consult the full CRFI chart and Table for other contaminant types/depths before dispatch.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"limitations--sources-consulted\">Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Airplane Flight Manual (AFM), Chapter 01 - Limitations, FAA Approved, Revision 44, 2023-01-27 (primary source for all limitation values in this guide).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Operating Manual, Contaminated Runway Performance, Chapter 05-90-00, Revision 44, 2023-01-27.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cold Weather Operations G550 reference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Prior \"Limitations G550\" working document (Luciano Rocha) - restructured and cross-checked against the above.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This is a personal study aid. Always defer to the current, effective AFM and Operating Manual for the airplane and configuration actually being flown.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","limitations--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","limitations--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Weights (01-03-60)","limitations--weights-01-03-60"],["h2","Fuel (01-28-10 / 01-03-80 / 01-28-30)","limitations--fuel-01-28-10-01-03-80-01-28-30"],["h2","Airspeed Limitations (01-03-40)","limitations--airspeed-limitations-01-03-40"],["h2","Flight Load Acceleration (01-03-50)","limitations--flight-load-acceleration-01-03-50"],["h2","Altitudes & Temperatures (01-03-20 / 01-03-30)","limitations--altitudes-temperatures-01-03-20-01-03-30"],["h2","Cold-Weather Action Points (consolidated)","limitations--cold-weather-action-points-consolidated"],["h2","Runway, Wind & Crosswind (01-02-10)","limitations--runway-wind-crosswind-01-02-10"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL LIMITATIONS BY AFM CHAPTER 01","limitations--part-2---full-limitations-by-afm-chapter-01"],["h2","Conditions of Operation (01-00-10)","limitations--conditions-of-operation-01-00-10"],["h2","Aircrew and Occupants (01-01)","limitations--aircrew-and-occupants-01-01"],["h2","Departure / Arrival Airport (01-02)","limitations--departure-arrival-airport-01-02"],["h2","Performance (01-03)","limitations--performance-01-03"],["h2","Airplane Servicing (01-12)","limitations--airplane-servicing-01-12"],["h2","Air Conditioning and Bleed Air (01-21)","limitations--air-conditioning-and-bleed-air-01-21"],["h2","Auto Flight (01-22)","limitations--auto-flight-01-22"],["h2","Communications (01-23)","limitations--communications-01-23"],["h2","Electrical Power (01-24)","limitations--electrical-power-01-24"],["h2","Flight Controls (01-27)","limitations--flight-controls-01-27"],["h2","Fuel (01-28)","limitations--fuel-01-28"],["h2","Hydraulics (01-29)","limitations--hydraulics-01-29"],["h2","Ice and Rain Protection (01-30)","limitations--ice-and-rain-protection-01-30"],["h2","Indicating / Recording (01-31)","limitations--indicating-recording-01-31"],["h2","Landing Gear (01-32)","limitations--landing-gear-01-32"],["h2","Navigation (01-34)","limitations--navigation-01-34"],["h2","Oxygen System (01-35)","limitations--oxygen-system-01-35"],["h2","EICAS Indications (01-45)","limitations--eicas-indications-01-45"],["h2","Auxiliary Power Unit / APU (01-49)","limitations--auxiliary-power-unit-apu-01-49"],["h2","Doors (01-52)","limitations--doors-01-52"],["h2","Powerplant (01-71 to 01-80)","limitations--powerplant-01-71-to-01-80"],["h1","Appendix - Contaminated / Wet Runway Crosswind Reference","limitations--appendix---contaminated-wet-runway-crosswind-reference"],["h1","Sources Consulted","limitations--sources-consulted"]]},"cold-weather-ops":{"t":"Cold Weather Operations Guide","sub":"Consolidated and cross-checked against G550 AFM (Chapter 01 - Limitations, Chapter 02 - Normal Procedures / Alternate Normals), · AOM Production Aircraft Systems, and All Weather Operations and Procedures","html":"<h1 id=\"cold-weather-ops--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide restructures the prior Cold Weather Operations document into the same two-layer format as the G550 Limitations Study Guide, and cross-checks every temperature and speed figure against the values already verified in that guide (AFM Chapter 01, Revision 44, 2023-01-27).</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: the temperatures, speeds, and figures you need cold - cold-weather action points, minimum Mach schedule, icing maneuvering speeds, RCAM runway condition codes, and water/waste-tank draining limits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Procedures by Phase of Flight: the complete guidance organized the way you actually fly it - Preflight -&gt; APU/Engine Start -&gt; Taxi -&gt; Takeoff -&gt; Inflight -&gt; Approach/Landing -&gt; Taxi/Parking -&gt; Securing - plus Special Flight Operations, Contaminated Runway Performance, and Ground Deicing/Anti-Icing Procedures.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What changed from the original document:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All cold-weather temperature action points cross-checked against the Limitations Study Guide (AFM Chapter 01) - figures match; a few AFM-only items (Amerex/Kidde extinguisher removal, generator-OFF-during-start technique) are now cited with their AFM cross-reference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RCAM (Runway Condition Assessment Matrix) table, previously an embedded image, transcribed into a searchable table.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Water/Waste Tank Draining Determination table, previously an embedded image, transcribed into a searchable table.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Content reorganized from a loose topic list into a phase-of-flight flow so procedures read in the order they are actually used.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Diagrams retained as images where they are genuinely graphical (Altitude/Temperature envelope, Cowl Anti-Ice temperature/Mach chart, deicing fluid application diagram) rather than transcribed, since these are read visually.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"cold-weather-ops--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--cold-weather-temperatures-action-points\">Cold-Weather Temperatures &amp; Action Points</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Temperature</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limitation / Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-70C</td>\n<td>Minimum operational temperature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-65C</td>\n<td>Amerex fire extinguisher must be removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-50C</td>\n<td>Minimum ambient temperature for APU start (ground)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-40C</td>\n<td>Minimum engine fuel temperature; Kidde fire extinguisher must be removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-37C</td>\n<td>Minimum fuel tank temperature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-30C</td>\n<td>Minimum oil temperature for engine start and throttle advance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-28C</td>\n<td>Life rafts should be removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-20C or less</td>\n<td>Batteries must be removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-20C</td>\n<td>Minimum battery temperature for APU start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-15C</td>\n<td>APU EGT and RPM may be unreadable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-15C</td>\n<td>Purge water regardless of unheated duration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-10C</td>\n<td>Generators switched OFF during engine start (see AFM 02-08-120)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0C to -14C</td>\n<td>Purge water if aircraft will be unheated for more than 90 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18C</td>\n<td>Minimum cockpit temperature for operating DUs, IRUs, SFD, EBDI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20C</td>\n<td>Minimum oil temperature for takeoff power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10C or less + visible moisture</td>\n<td>Wing and cowl anti-ice ON</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide (AFM Chapter 01, Rev 44) - all figures match. See Part 2, Limitations, for the AFM section reference of each item.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#cold-weather-ops--limitations\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Part 2, Limitations (Minimum/Maximum Operating Temperature)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--minimum-mach-schedule-sat--70c-to--80c\">Minimum Mach Schedule (SAT -70C to -80C)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>SAT (C)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Minimum Mach</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-70</td>\n<td>.67</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-72</td>\n<td>.71</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-74</td>\n<td>.76</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-76</td>\n<td>.80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-78</td>\n<td>.84</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-80</td>\n<td>.87</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--fuel-tank-temperature\">Fuel Tank Temperature</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Range</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Indication / Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-35C to -36C</td>\n<td>Caution range - amber CAS: FUEL TANK TEMPERATURE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt; -37C</td>\n<td>Warning range - red CAS: FUEL TANK TEMPERATURE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;= -30C in flight, &lt; 5,000 lb fuel remaining</td>\n<td>Descend to where SAT is -60C or warmer; maintain minimum Mach 0.80</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#cold-weather-ops--sec_fuel\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Part 2, Limitations (Fuel Tank Temperature)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--icing---minimum-maneuvering-speeds-by-flap-setting\">Icing - Minimum Maneuvering Speeds by Flap Setting</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Flap Setting</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Minimum Speed</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 (UP)</td>\n<td>200 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>180 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20 (T/O APP)</td>\n<td>150 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39 (DOWN)</td>\n<td>Vref + 5 kt</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Recommended minimums for maneuvering in icing conditions in flight; separate from the AFM holding-in-icing minimum of 160 KCAS at 0 flaps.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#cold-weather-ops--inflight-procedures\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Part 2, Inflight Procedures (Ice and Icing)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--contaminated-runway---takeoff-landing-quick-limits\">Contaminated Runway - Takeoff / Landing Quick Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Takeoff</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Landing</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>39</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anti-skid</td>\n<td>Operative</td>\n<td>Operative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto ground spoilers</td>\n<td>Operative</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max standing water depth</td>\n<td>15 mm</td>\n<td>15 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Threshold speed</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Vref (min) to Vref + 10 (max)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other</td>\n<td>No clearway credit; advance to takeoff thrust before brake release</td>\n<td>Crosswind capability must be reduced vs. dry runway</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#cold-weather-ops--sec_runway\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Part 2, Contaminated Runway Performance Notes</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--rcam---runway-condition-assessment-matrix\">RCAM - Runway Condition Assessment Matrix</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>RwyCC</strong></th>\n<th><strong>AC 91-79A CHG 1 Description</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Pilot-Reported Braking Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Dry</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Frost; Wet (incl. damp, &lt;=1/8in water); &lt;=1/8in (3mm) slush/dry snow/wet snow</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>-15C and colder: compacted snow</td>\n<td>Good to Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Slippery when wet; dry/wet snow over compacted snow; &gt;1/8in dry/wet snow; warmer than -15C: compacted snow</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>&gt;1/8in (3mm) water or slush</td>\n<td>Medium to Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Ice</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Wet ice; slush over ice; water over compacted snow; dry/wet snow over ice</td>\n<td>Nil</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Transcribed from the embedded RCAM chart (source: AC 91-79A CHG 1) for searchability. See Part 2 for the full-resolution chart image.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--water-waste-tank---ramp-temperature-limits\">Water / Waste Tank - Ramp Temperature Limits</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Ramp Temp</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Max Time Without System Purge</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-15C to 0C</td>\n<td>Unrestricted, with APU bleed air ON and cabin temp &gt;= 10C (50F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt; -15C</td>\n<td>1:15 (h:min), with APU bleed air ON and cabin temp &gt;= 10C (50F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-7C to 0C</td>\n<td>1:30, with APU bleed air OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-15C to &lt; -7C</td>\n<td>0:30, with APU bleed air OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt; -15C</td>\n<td>N/A, with APU bleed air OFF</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Ramp Temp</strong></th>\n<th><strong>External Heat or APU Bleed Air (Galley Equipment)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-5C to 0C</td>\n<td>1 hour every 4 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-10C to &lt; -5C</td>\n<td>2 hours every 4 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt; -10C</td>\n<td>Continuous</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Transcribed from the embedded Water Tank Draining Determination Table for searchability. See Part 2 for the source image and the purge-water video link.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#cold-weather-ops--sec_watertank\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Part 2, Securing for Overnight / Water Tank Draining</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"cold-weather-ops--part-2---full-procedures\">PART 2 - FULL PROCEDURES</h1>\n<p><em>Organized the way the flight is actually flown: general guidance, limitations, ice protection systems, then ground ops through securing, followed by special operations and reference appendices.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--general-guidance\">General Guidance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Airplane start-up is prohibited after extended exposure to ambient temperatures below -40C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The airplane is considered cold-soaked if the time with airplane power off exceeds 5 hours.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Service the nose and main landing gear shock struts and tires prior to departure from a warm location, in preparation for cold-day operations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cockpit and cabin heating uses APU bleed air. Heating can be expedited by closing the main entry, internal baggage, and external baggage doors; with the main entry door open, the acoustic door can be closed to help.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the airplane will be cold-soaked, refuel it on the day of departure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--limitations\">Limitations</h2>\n<p><strong>Minimum Operating Temperature</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Approved temperatures for operation are shown in the Altitude/Temperature Operating Envelope (below).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum Mach schedule for SAT between -70C and -80C: -70=.67, -72=.71, -74=.76, -76=.80, -78=.84, -80=.87.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cold-weather-ops-1.jpg\"/></p>\n<p><em>Altitude / Temperature Operating Envelope (AFM Limitations, 01-03-20).</em></p>\n<p><span class=\"anchor\" id=\"cold-weather-ops--sec_fuel\"></span><strong>Fuel Tank Temperature</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Caution range: -35C to -36C causes an amber CAS message, FUEL TANK TEMPERATURE.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Warning range: less than -37C causes a red CAS message, FUEL TANK TEMPERATURE.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If fuel tank temperature is at or below -30C in flight with less than 5,000 lb of total fuel remaining, descend to an altitude where SAT is -60C or warmer and maintain a minimum speed of Mach 0.80.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Use of Flaps</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Use of flaps in icing conditions is restricted to takeoff, approach, and landing only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Holding in icing conditions is limited to 0 (UP) flaps only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Fuel and Oil (cold-weather figures)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engine fuel temperature minimum: -40C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Engine oil temperature minimum for starting: -30C; minimum for takeoff power: +20C.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--ice-and-rain-protection\">Ice and Rain Protection</h2>\n<p><strong>General</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Icing conditions exist on the ground or in flight when SAT is +10C or below and visible moisture in any form is present.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff is prohibited with frost, ice, snow, or slush adhering to the wings, control surfaces, engine inlets, or other critical surfaces.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A visual and tactile check of the wing leading edge and upper surface is required when: OAT &lt; 6C, or wing fuel temperature above 0C cannot be confirmed; and visible moisture is present, or water is present on the wing, or the dew point/OAT spread is 3C or less, or conditions are conducive to frost formation. [AFM Limitations 01-30-10]</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wing Anti-Icing [AFM Limitations 01-30-20]</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Required if icing conditions are imminent, or immediately upon detection of ice formation on wings, winglets, or windshield edges.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cowl Anti-Icing [AFM Limitations 01-30-30]</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Required for taxi and takeoff when SAT is 10C or below with visible moisture, precipitation, or a wet runway present.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When taxiing/holding at low power in temperatures below 1C, run the engine at 40% LP for 10 seconds just before takeoff and at intervals no greater than 60 minutes under these conditions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Required in flight per the temperature/Mach chart below, when visible moisture or precipitation is present or icing is observed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Engine vibration may increase when icing occurs; to help shed ice, retard one engine at a time to idle for 5 seconds, then accelerate to 90% LP before returning the power lever to its original position.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic anti-ice is inhibited above 35,000 ft - must be manually selected if required above that altitude.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cold-weather-ops-2.jpg\"/></p>\n<p><em>Cowl Anti-Ice required region by Static Air Temperature and Mach Number.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Use of Flaps [AFM Limitations 01-30-40]</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Restricted to takeoff, approach, and landing in icing conditions; holding limited to 0 (UP) flaps only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Fuel and Oil [AFM Limitations 01-73-10 / 01-79-10]</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engine fuel temperature minimum: -40C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oil inlet temperature: minimum -30C for starting, minimum +20C for takeoff power. External heating is required to raise oil temperature to -30C for cold-weather starting; between -30C and +20C, use only the thrust required for taxi.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--system-review---cowl-and-wing-anti-ice\">System Review - Cowl and Wing Anti-Ice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>AUTO mode: takeoff and climb between 1,500 ft and 35,000 ft; descent and approach between 35,000 ft and 0 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic anti-icing is unavailable above 35,000 ft (air temperature/moisture too low for icing) - engine bleed air is then fully available for pressurization and cabin heating.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cowl and wing anti-ice may be manually selected ON at any altitude when protection is required.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AUTO mode protection terminates when ice detectors no longer sense ice, in sequence: after 1 minute, the amber ICE DETECTED L/R message clears; after 3 minutes, the engine cowl anti-ice valves close; after 5 minutes, the wing anti-ice valves close. [AOM Production Aircraft Systems 2A-30-20]</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--operational-notes---ground-ops\">Operational Notes - Ground Ops</h2>\n<p><strong>Preflight</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Perform a detailed preflight inspection; ensure all critical areas are clear of icing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>APU Start / Power-Up</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>After an overnight/prolonged cold soak colder than -20C with ambient at or below -20C at start time: warm the batteries externally before attempting APU start (external AC cart if available).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After a cold soak at -15C or colder: APU EGT/RPM displays may be unreadable. Preheat the cockpit (emphasis on the overhead panel) if capable, then start normally while monitoring EGT/RPM.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After a cold soak at 0C or colder: warm the cockpit, cabin, and EER to 18C for 15 minutes after APU start before turning on any cockpit equipment or continuing normal checks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A slower-than-normal APU start is possible depending on ambient temperature and battery condition. If the APU READY light is hard to see, wait 2 minutes before pressing APU START.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum APU EGT during start must not exceed 1,050C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No charge indicated immediately after APU GEN comes online may indicate a battery charger failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Verify water system integrity prior to dispatch; cycle the parking brake 10 times, returning to the release position when complete.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Start</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Required any time engine oil temperature is -10C or colder (minimum oil temp -30C). See AFM Alternate Normals 02-08-120, Cold Weather Start and Operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Manually select L and R generator switches OFF prior to engine start; select GEN ON once engines stabilize at idle.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If GEN switches were not selected OFF before start and L/R GENERATOR FAIL and/or L/R AC POWER FAIL CAS messages appear as the engine reaches idle: select the affected GEN switch OFF for 15 seconds, then back ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cranking Cycle</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Run each engine through a crank cycle to confirm readiness for start. See AFM Alternate Normals 02-08-140.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Starting Procedure</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>See AFM Alternate Normals 02-08-120, Cold Weather Start and Operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oil pressure may temporarily exceed maximum limits until oil temperature rises.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>After Engine Start</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>COWL A/I must be ON during all ground operations when icing conditions or freezing fog are present.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Before Taxi</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Check wing flaps for normal operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Leave flaps retracted for taxi to avoid ice buildup.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Taxi</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Taxi as soon as possible after engine start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum oil temperature to accelerate engines for taxi power: -30C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use brakes to build brake temperature to a minimum of 100C, aiding moisture dissipation (3-5 minute delay between brake application and peak brake temperature).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Avoid setting the parking brake for any length of time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Adjust taxi speed to surface conditions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Do not use idle reverse thrust while taxiing on snow- or slush-covered taxiways (FOD risk).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In icing conditions below +1C, if holding at low power, accelerate to 40% LP for 10 seconds at intervals no greater than 60 minutes, then resume idle.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--operational-notes---pre-takeoff-takeoff\">Operational Notes - Pre-Takeoff / Takeoff</h2>\n<p><strong>Pre-Takeoff</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Conduct a final pre-takeoff inspection within 5 minutes of takeoff.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Repeat the wing visual/tactile check when OAT &lt; 6C or wing fuel temperature above 0C cannot be confirmed, combined with visible moisture, water on the wing, or frost-conducive conditions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Before Takeoff</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Extend the flaps and complete the BEFORE TAKEOFF checklist.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Closely monitor the FLAPS/STAB position indicator.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The airstart ignition must be selected ON for takeoff and landing on runways with standing water, slush, or snow. [AOM All Weather Operations and Procedures 07-01-00]</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeoff</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Do not use reduced (FLEX) thrust.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Check for stable engine operation before brake release (static takeoff).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use the minimum allowable V1 speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Selecting WING ANTI-ICE ON vents warm air into the main gear wheel well area, assisting in melting ice on the wheels.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Minimum engine oil temperature for takeoff power: 20C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Acceleration rate during the high-speed portion may deteriorate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the takeoff is rejected: deploy ground spoilers, use max reverse and anti-skid braking.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use rudder primarily down to 50 kt; if directional control is lost, reduce reverse thrust to idle reverse.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Delay gear retraction if practical.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--inflight-procedures\">Inflight Procedures</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If flight through icing conditions is necessary, turn engine and wing anti-icing ON before entering the conditions - these are anti-icing systems and must be used before ice builds up.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ice and Icing - Recommended Minimum Maneuvering Speeds</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>See Part 1 Quick-Reference table above (0=200 KCAS, 10=180 KCAS, 20=150 KCAS, 39=Vref+5 kt).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Normal Icing Cues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Temperatures below freezing combined with visible moisture.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ice on the winglets and/or leading edges.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ice detector annunciator.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Engine Icing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Can occur without wing icing (temperature below +8C).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Vibration may rise and fall as spontaneous shedding occurs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>To shed ice: retard one power lever at a time to idle for 5 seconds, then push momentarily to 90% LP RPM before returning to the original position.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pitot Icing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If the Air Data Module (ADM) fails due to pitot system icing, select STALL BARR to OFF to prevent erroneous control column shaker/pusher activation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pitot probe heat failure is evident from large airspeed and altimeter indication errors.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>AOA Vane Icing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If heater malfunction is evident, select STALL BARR to OFF to prevent erroneous shaker/pusher activation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wing Anti-Ice</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Regulated to maintain approximately 130F in the wing plenum; places the highest demand on the bleed air manifold, so it must be OFF before ground-starting either engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>WING HOT L/R indicates wing temperature greater than 180F; WING TEMPERATURE LOW L/R indicates wing temperature less than 100F.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--operational-notes---approach-landing-securing\">Operational Notes - Approach, Landing, Securing</h2>\n<p><strong>Approach</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>COWL/WING ANTI-ICE must be ON or AUTO during flight operations when icing conditions exist or are anticipated.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Select WING ANTI-ICE ON during descent if the departure airport had a contaminated runway. See AFM Alternate Normals 02-08-110.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Before Landing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Extend the landing gear earlier than normal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Landing gear temperature equalizes with the surrounding TAT within about 3 minutes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Select ANTI-SKID OFF, perform several brake applications to 3,000 psi, then select ANTI-SKID ON.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Consider cabin water purging before landing if practical.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Landing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Perform a firm touchdown.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Lower the nose wheel to the runway immediately as ground spoilers and thrust reversers deploy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Apply brakes smoothly with moderate-to-firm, constant pressure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Do not attempt to turn off the runway until reaching a safe, very slow taxi speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The airstart ignition must be selected ON for landing on runways with standing water, slush, or snow.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Taxi / Parking</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If severe icing conditions are present, keep COWL ANTI-ICE ON; engine ice shedding may be performed during prolonged ground operation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Make all turns at reduced speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Retract flaps when slush is present on the taxiway; do not retract above 10 degrees if significant leading-edge ice accumulation is suspected.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Check hydraulic fluid quantity before engine shutdown (hydraulic replenishing requires operating engines).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Service the waste tank within 20 minutes of aircraft electrical power shutdown.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"anchor\" id=\"cold-weather-ops--sec_watertank\"></span><strong>Securing for Overnight or Extended Periods on Ramp</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Wheel chocks in place and parking brake released.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Verify TROV closed; external protective covers and plugs installed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Complete potable water and waste tank servicing as soon as possible (see Water/Waste Tank quick-reference table, Part 1).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Drain all galley equipment containing water; cycle water taps several times to ensure supply lines are empty.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Remove life rafts if expected to be exposed to temperatures below -28C; remove main batteries if expected to be exposed to temperatures below -20C.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Purge Water System - Video Reference</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TgzfV_J8uPg</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--special-flight-operations\">Special Flight Operations</h2>\n<p><strong>High-Altitude Ice Crystal Icing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Ice crystals near thunderstorms in high concentrations can accrete on the engine rotor hub and spinner, raising LP vibration without noticeable power loss.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ice crystals are difficult for onboard weather radar to detect, so avoiding all ice-crystal conditions is not possible; normal thunderstorm-avoidance procedures help.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Recommended actions: avoid flying in visible moisture over storm cells; avoid convective regions by at least 20 NM; use radar antenna tilt to scan reflectivity ahead; recognize that heavy rain below likely indicates high ice-crystal concentrations above; fly upwind of the storm cell to avoid the spreading anvil and limit exposure; select Cowl Anti-Ice ON when SAT &lt; 10C and flying above convective activity; if EVM indications rise in VMC, turning Cowl Anti-Ice ON may help; if elevated EVM persists and engine ice accretion is suspected, see the Ice Shedding Procedure below.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ice Shedding Procedure (in flight)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>To assist shedding ice if high vibration occurs and conditions permit, with Cowl and Wing Anti-Ice ON: retard one engine at a time to idle for 5 seconds, then accelerate to 90% LP RPM before returning the power lever to its original position. Repeat if vibration persists.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cold-weather-ops-3.jpg\"/></p>\n<p><em>Ice Shedding Procedure (AFM 03-07-20) - full source text.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Heavy Rain / Hail Encounter</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The engine tolerates normal in-flight water ingestion rates; heavy rain or hail may cause engine parameter fluctuations, particularly a noticeable drop in indicated TGT.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If encountered: avoid rapid power adjustments if possible, select A/T OFF, and maintain as high a power setting as possible.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--contaminated-runway-performance-notes\">Contaminated Runway Performance Notes</h2>\n<p><strong>Definitions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Wet runway: sufficient moisture to appear reflective, but water depth not more than 3 mm (0.125 in).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contaminated runway: more than 25% of the runway surface covered with standing water, slush, loose snow (dry or wet), compacted snow, or ice.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Standing water: depth greater than 3 mm (0.125 in). Maximum allowable standing-water depth for takeoff on a contaminated runway is 10-15 mm.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For takeoff and landing on contaminated runways there is no longer a restriction requiring both thrust reversers operative - correction factors are provided if one or both are inoperative.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>RCAM (Runway Condition Assessment Matrix)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>See Part 1 Quick-Reference table above for the transcribed RwyCC / description / braking-action matrix.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cold-weather-ops-4.jpg\"/></p>\n<p><em>RCAM chart, source formatting (AC 91-79A CHG 1).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Takeoff Limitations on Contaminated Runways</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Flaps 20; anti-skid operative; auto ground spoilers operative.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum standing-water depth: 15 mm. No credit for clearways.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Advance power to takeoff thrust before brake release.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Landing Limitations on Contaminated Runways</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Flaps 39; anti-skid operative.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum standing-water depth: 15 mm.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Threshold speed: minimum Vref, maximum Vref + 10.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Crosswind capability for takeoff and landing on wet or contaminated runways must be reduced relative to dry-runway values.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cold-weather-ops--ground-deicing-anti-icing-procedures\">Ground Deicing / Anti-Icing Procedures</h2>\n<p><strong>General</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Deicing/anti-icing may be a one-step or two-step process; standard practice is to deice, then anti-ice if required, before takeoff.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>De-ice the wings (including winglets), vertical tail, and horizontal tail with approved de-ice fluid. Instruct ground crew to avoid spraying directly into engine/APU inlets and exhausts, windscreens, windows, probes, and ventilation inlets/exhausts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Apply anti-icing immediately after de-icing if desired, using SAE/ISO Type I/II/III/IV mixed with water in a one- or two-step process; this may extend holdover time. Type II/III/IV should not be applied forward of the wing leading edges.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Perform a post-deicing inspection immediately after deicing or during anti-icing: hands-on check of wing leading/upper/lower/trailing edges; visual check of stabilizers, elevator/rudder, APU and cooling air intakes/inlets/exhausts, engine inlets, flaps/spoilers/ailerons, fuel tank vents, emergency exits, antennas, fuselage, cockpit windows, pitot/AOA/TAT/ice probes and static ports, and landing gear.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Depart as soon as possible after the post-deicing inspection, especially in precipitation or high relative humidity (small temperature/dew point spread).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Holdover times vary considerably by fluid type and are shortened by wet snow, higher moisture content, a wet or warm aircraft surface, warm fluid, high wind velocity, or unfavorable wind direction.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cold-weather-ops-5.jpg\"/></p>\n<p><em>Deicing fluid application areas and direction of application.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Exterior Deicing With Engines or APU Operating</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Engines at IDLE power.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>L/R COWL A/I switches OFF; L ENG / R ENG / APU AIR switches OFF.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TROV CLOSED.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Extreme care must be taken to ensure deicing fluid does not enter the engine/APU inlets.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Holdover Times</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Refer to the Cold Weather Operations Manual for holdover time guidelines. The holdover clock starts at the beginning of fluid application.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anti-Icing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Do not use diluted deicing fluid for anti-icing - melting snow further dilutes the solution and refreezing occurs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Apply an even film on all surfaces per manufacturer recommendations; work from the highest part of the fuselage/wings downward. Correct fluid quantity is reached when it just starts to run off.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the nose has been anti-iced, wipe it down to prevent fluid running back onto the windshield; inspect and clean areas on and under wiper blades.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If fluid cannot be applied before light frost forms, anti-icing solution can still be applied with expected results; heavy frozen deposits must be removed by deicing first, followed by cold anti-icing solution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After anti-icing, a complete operational check of flight controls is mandatory; ensure engine controls and AOA probes operate freely.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contamination Check - Scenarios</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If takeoff occurs before the holdover table time expires, can you take off without a pre-takeoff contamination check? No - only operators covered by 14 CFR 121.629 may use holdover tables in lieu of a pre-takeoff contamination check.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>#1: Aircraft covered with ice, CAVOK day, de-ice pad en route to the runway, engines running - is a check required? Yes, it can be done anytime after de-icing with no prior-to-takeoff limit, since conditions are not conducive to further icing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>#2: Ramp covered with snow, CAVOK but cold, local aircraft de-icing, your aircraft was hangared - is a check required? No, if the preflight inspection showed the aircraft contamination-free and conditions are not conducive to icing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>#3: Aircraft was hangared but it is snowing; by start time it is covered; heated Type I followed by Type IV anti-ice is applied; it is still snowing - is a check required? Yes, it can be done anytime after de-icing, but must be completed within 5 minutes of takeoff, since conditions are conducive to further icing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"cold-weather-ops--appendix---sources-and-cross-references\">Appendix - Sources and Cross-References</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Airplane Flight Manual (AFM), Chapter 01 - Limitations (temperatures, fuel tank temperature, ice/rain protection limitations) - same edition verified in the G550 Limitations Study Guide, Revision 44, 2023-01-27.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 AFM, Chapter 02 - Normal Procedures / Alternate Normals: Cold Weather Start and Operation (02-08-120), Cranking Cycle (02-08-140), Wing Anti-Ice on Descent (02-08-110).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 AFM, 03-07-20 - Ice Shedding Procedure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 AOM (Airplane Operating Manual), Production Aircraft Systems, 2A-30-20 - Cowl and Wing Anti-Ice system logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 AOM, All Weather Operations and Procedures, 07-01-00 - airstart ignition requirement on contaminated runways.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AC 91-79A CHG 1 - Runway Condition Assessment Matrix (RCAM) source formatting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>14 CFR 121.629 - holdover table / pre-takeoff contamination check applicability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cold Weather Operations Manual - detailed deicing/anti-icing application guidelines and holdover times (referenced, not reproduced here).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Purge Water System video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TgzfV_J8uPg</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Prior \"COLD WEATHER OPS G550\" working document (Luciano Rocha) - restructured into phase-of-flight order and cross-checked against the above.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This is a personal study aid. Always defer to the current, effective AFM, AOM, and Cold Weather Operations Manual for the airplane and configuration actually being flown.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","cold-weather-ops--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","cold-weather-ops--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Cold-Weather Temperatures & Action Points","cold-weather-ops--cold-weather-temperatures-action-points"],["h2","Minimum Mach Schedule (SAT -70C to -80C)","cold-weather-ops--minimum-mach-schedule-sat--70c-to--80c"],["h2","Fuel Tank Temperature","cold-weather-ops--fuel-tank-temperature"],["h2","Icing - Minimum Maneuvering Speeds by Flap Setting","cold-weather-ops--icing---minimum-maneuvering-speeds-by-flap-setting"],["h2","Contaminated Runway - Takeoff / Landing Quick Limits","cold-weather-ops--contaminated-runway---takeoff-landing-quick-limits"],["h2","RCAM - Runway Condition Assessment Matrix","cold-weather-ops--rcam---runway-condition-assessment-matrix"],["h2","Water / Waste Tank - Ramp Temperature Limits","cold-weather-ops--water-waste-tank---ramp-temperature-limits"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL PROCEDURES","cold-weather-ops--part-2---full-procedures"],["h2","General Guidance","cold-weather-ops--general-guidance"],["h2","Limitations","cold-weather-ops--limitations"],["h2","Ice and Rain Protection","cold-weather-ops--ice-and-rain-protection"],["h2","System Review - Cowl and Wing Anti-Ice","cold-weather-ops--system-review---cowl-and-wing-anti-ice"],["h2","Operational Notes - Ground Ops","cold-weather-ops--operational-notes---ground-ops"],["h2","Operational Notes - Pre-Takeoff / Takeoff","cold-weather-ops--operational-notes---pre-takeoff-takeoff"],["h2","Inflight Procedures","cold-weather-ops--inflight-procedures"],["h2","Operational Notes - Approach, Landing, Securing","cold-weather-ops--operational-notes---approach-landing-securing"],["h2","Special Flight Operations","cold-weather-ops--special-flight-operations"],["h2","Contaminated Runway Performance Notes","cold-weather-ops--contaminated-runway-performance-notes"],["h2","Ground Deicing / Anti-Icing Procedures","cold-weather-ops--ground-deicing-anti-icing-procedures"],["h1","Appendix - Sources and Cross-References","cold-weather-ops--appendix---sources-and-cross-references"]]},"driftdown":{"t":"Driftdown Procedures & Systems Assessment","sub":"DRIFTDOWN PROCEDURES & SYSTEMS ASSESSMENT STUDY GUIDE · Consolidated from handwritten study notes on NAT/HLA oceanic procedures, engine-out · driftdown, and post-failure systems assessment","html":"<h1 id=\"driftdown--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts handwritten G550 study notes on \"Driftdown Procedures and Systems' Assessment\" into a searchable, color-coded reference. The source (49 handwritten pages) covers four distinct topics that build on each other: North Atlantic oceanic track procedures, the Equal Time Point (ETP) concept and its three variants, a full engine-failure driftdown scenario worked step-by-step, and a systems assessment of how an engine failure affects the electrical, hydraulic, fuel, and pneumatic systems. It was cross-checked against the G550 Limitations Study Guide already in your Study Guides folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Four layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: the driftdown performance numbers, ETP formula, and NAT procedural rules you need cold.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - NAT/Oceanic Background: the OTS, Equal Time Points, NAT contingency (loss of clearance) procedures, severe-weather deviations, and wake-turbulence/SLOP rules that set the stage for the scenario.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 3 - The Driftdown Scenario: the worked example (FL410, M0.85, 80,000 lb, ISA+10, left engine flames out after ETP2) and the 13-step driftdown checklist used to fly it, navigate it, and communicate it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 4 - Systems' Assessment: how a single-engine failure cascades through the electrical, hydraulic, fuel, and pneumatic systems.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Driftdown performance figures (FL410 initial altitude, ISA+10, 80,000 lb initial driftdown weight: descent Mach/KCAS 0.80/251, 39 min, 1,800 lb fuel, 264 NM, final \"start cruise\" speed 218 KCAS at 26,500 ft) are transcribed directly from the AOM Chapter 11 (ATA 11-05-00, Engine Out Driftdown) chart reproduced in the source notes and are internal to this single scenario - always pull the actual AOM chart for the aircraft's current weight/OAT/altitude combination before using these numbers operationally.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>General airspeed/altitude figures referenced in this guide (Mmo/Vmo envelope, max operating altitude) are consistent with the G550 Limitations Study Guide already in this folder; this source document does not itself define aircraft limitations, so no correction was needed - it documents oceanic procedure and a worked driftdown/systems example instead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The oxygen figures noted in the Limitations guide (passenger masks not for prolonged use above 34,000 ft cabin altitude; not recommended above 25,000 ft cabin altitude with passengers) are unrelated to this document's content - the source notes contain no oxygen-duration or emergency-descent-oxygen figures of their own, so nothing here needed reconciling against that section.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Source notes are marked \"for reference purposes only\" by the original author (Ivan Luciani, Code7700.com) - always confirm against the current AFM/AOM/QRH before operational use, especially the driftdown chart, which is weight/altitude/OAT-specific and must be re-pulled for the aircraft's actual conditions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"driftdown--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--driftdown-performance-worked-example\">Driftdown Performance (Worked Example)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial altitude / OAT</td>\n<td>41,000 ft / -56C (ISA +10C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial driftdown weight</td>\n<td>80,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial cruise speed</td>\n<td>M0.85 at 41,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Descent Mach / KCAS</td>\n<td>0.80 / 251 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>39 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel used</td>\n<td>1,800 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance</td>\n<td>264 NM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Start cruise\" speed / altitude (final driftdown)</td>\n<td>218 KCAS / 26,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rate of climb capability at final driftdown altitude</td>\n<td>200 fpm at MCT, at \"start cruise\" CAS (LRC speed)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This table is specific to the 80,000 lb / ISA+10 / FL410 scenario worked in the source notes. Always pull the current AOM Engine Out Driftdown chart (ATA 11-05-00) for the aircraft's actual weight, OAT, and initial altitude.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#driftdown--driftdown-performance-aom-procedure\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Driftdown Performance / AOM Procedure</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--equal-time-point-etp-formula\">Equal Time Point (ETP) Formula</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground distance to ETP</td>\n<td>(D x GSb) / (GSa + GSb), in NM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>Total distance, Airport Behind to Airport Ahead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GSa</td>\n<td>Ground speed continuing to Airport Ahead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GSb</td>\n<td>Ground speed returning to Airport Behind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worked example</td>\n<td>D=2,500 NM, GSa=520 kt, GSb=440 kt -&gt; ETP = (2,500 x 440)/(520+440) = 1,146 NM from Airport Behind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Three ETP types</td>\n<td>1) Loss of Engine ETP (1E INOP) - Engine Out Driftdown Charts. 2) Loss of Level ETP - Pressurization (DEPRESS) - Emergency Descent Procedure. 3) Maintain Level ETP - Medical (MEDICAL).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> ETP fuel calculations assume a straight line to the alternate and do NOT account for OTS tracks, weather deviations, or an instrument approach procedure - actual fuel burn to divert will be higher, especially if a Quad Four maneuver or a descent below the OTS before turning is required.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#driftdown--equal-time-point-etp\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Equal Time Point (ETP)</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--nat-vertical-lateral-longitudinal-separation\">NAT Vertical / Lateral / Longitudinal Separation</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Type</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Standard</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical</td>\n<td>1,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lateral - PBCS track</td>\n<td>1/2 degree or 25 NM (FL350-FL390); PBCS authorization required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lateral - Non-PBCS track</td>\n<td>1 degree or 60 NM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Longitudinal (Mach number technique) - Non-PBCS</td>\n<td>10 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Longitudinal (Mach number technique) - PBCS</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Westbound OTS crossing of 030W</td>\n<td>1130-1930Z (published by Shanwick at 2200Z)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eastbound OTS crossing of 030W</td>\n<td>0100-0800Z (published by Gander at 1400Z)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#driftdown--nat-hla-organized-track-system-ots-overview\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: NAT / HLA / OTS Overview</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--nat-contingency---loss-of-atc-clearance\">NAT Contingency - Loss of ATC Clearance</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Situation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Revised clearance not possible</td>\n<td>Turn 30 deg or more away from the track (direction based on nearby traffic, alternate direction, SLOP, etc.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Able to maintain assigned FL</td>\n<td>Acquire a same-direction 5 NM offset track; once established, offset climb/descend using 500 ft increments below FL410, and 1,000 ft increments at/above FL410</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unable to maintain assigned FL</td>\n<td>Minimize rate of descent; acquire same-direction 5 NM offset; descend to FL290 or lower; once below FL290, maintain a 500 ft vertical offset from normal levels and proceed until an ATC clearance is received</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In all cases</td>\n<td>Establish comms with ATC/nearby aircraft on 121.5 and 123.45 MHz; turn on all external lights; ensure transponder is ON</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#driftdown--nat-contingency-procedures-loss-of-atc-clearance\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: NAT Contingency Procedures</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--severe-weather-deviation-sand-rule\">Severe Weather Deviation / SAND Rule</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Deviation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;= 5 NM off track</td>\n<td>Maintain assigned flight level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 5 NM off track, turning NORTH</td>\n<td>DESCEND 300 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 5 NM off track, turning SOUTH</td>\n<td>CLIMB 300 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mnemonic</td>\n<td>SAND = South Ascend, North Descend</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#driftdown--deviations-around-severe-weather\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Deviations Around Severe Weather</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--wake-turbulence-slop\">Wake Turbulence / SLOP</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SLOP offsets (right of centerline only)</td>\n<td>Centerline, 1.0 NM right, or 2.0 NM right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Micro-SLOP</td>\n<td>1/10 NM increments up to 2.0 NM right of centerline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left of centerline</td>\n<td>NEVER slop left of centerline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ATC approval</td>\n<td>Not required for SLOP or micro-SLOP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordination with preceding aircraft</td>\n<td>On 123.45 MHz, if required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#driftdown--wake-turbulence-strategic-lateral-offset-procedures-slop\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Wake Turbulence / SLOP</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"driftdown--part-2---nat-oceanic-background\">PART 2 - NAT / OCEANIC BACKGROUND</h1>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--nat-hla-organized-track-system-ots-overview\">NAT / HLA / Organized Track System (OTS) Overview</h2>\n<p>The North Atlantic (NAT) High Level Airspace (HLA) hosts the Organized Track System (OTS) - a set of published oceanic tracks used to move the daily bulk flow of traffic between North America and Europe.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Uni-directional and concentrated flow of traffic between North America and Europe.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The OTS consists of two major alternating flows: a WESTBOUND flow departing Europe in the morning, and an EASTBOUND flow departing North America in the evening.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Westbound traffic crosses 030W between 1130-1930Z; OTS tracks are published by Shanwick at 2200Z.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Eastbound traffic crosses 030W between 0100-0800Z; OTS tracks are published by Gander at 1400Z.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tracks are based on minimum time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A Track Message Identification (TMI) number provides OTS coordinates and flight levels available on each track.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Special authorization, including RVSM, is required to operate in the NAT HLA.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The NAT's OTS presents considerable challenges:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Very congested oceanic airspace with reduced vertical and horizontal separation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Large distances to a limited number of suitable alternate airports.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No ATC radar surveillance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Direct pilot-controller voice communication is limited.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Separation standards:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Vertical separation: 1,000 ft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Lateral separation (PBCS track): 1/2 degree or 25 NM, FL350-FL390, PBCS (Performance-Based Communication and Surveillance) authorization required.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Lateral separation (Non-PBCS track): 1 degree or 60 NM.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Longitudinal separation (Mach number technique) - Non-PBCS track: 10 minutes; PBCS track: 5 minutes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Random routes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Random routes are those which remain clear of the OTS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Random routes can also join or leave an outer track, or cut across the OTS tracks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Random routes remain above or below OTS tracks in altitude.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Do NOT enter ETPs (or NAT-specific reference points) into the FMS as waypoints unless they are actual route points - otherwise ADS-C will send position reports of non-existing waypoints to ATC.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--equal-time-point-etp\">Equal Time Point (ETP)</h2>\n<p>An ETP is a geographical location along the route of flight at which it takes the same time to continue to the airport ahead as it does to return to the airport behind. ETPs are also referred to as \"Critical Points.\" ETPs are computed for long overwater flights and are based on ground speed (wind factor). In oceanic airspace, ETPs are also computed between suitable alternate airports, not just the origin/destination.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Formula</td>\n<td>Ground distance to ETP = (D x GSb) / (GSa + GSb), in NM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>Distance, Airport Behind to Airport Ahead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GSa</td>\n<td>Ground speed continuing to Airport Ahead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GSb</td>\n<td>Ground speed returning to Airport Behind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worked example</td>\n<td>TAS 480 KCAS, wind P40 kt (tailwind continuing), distance 2,500 NM, GSa 520 kt, GSb 440 kt -&gt; ETP = (2,500)(440)/(520+440) = 1,146 NM from the airport behind</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>There are three (3) types of ETPs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1) Loss of Engine ETP (1E INOP) - uses the Engine Out Driftdown Charts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2) Loss of Level ETP - Pressurization (DEPRESS) - uses the Emergency Descent Procedure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3) Maintain Level ETP - Medical (MEDICAL) - level flight maintained, no descent.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Practical notes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Plot ETPs on a paper plotting chart or digital chart.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Do NOT enter ETPs into the FMS, otherwise ADS-C will send position reports of non-existing waypoints to ATC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Alternate airports can be ahead or behind, and left or right of current position.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>As each waypoint is crossed, make a mental note and brief the direction to the relevant alternate airport - this could help decide direction of turn (left &gt;= 30 deg or right &gt;= 30 deg).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ETP fuel calculations assume a straight line to the alternate airport and do NOT take into account OTS tracks, weather deviations, or an instrument approach procedure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Quad Four maneuver (ICAO Doc 4444) and a descent below the OTS tracks before a turn to the alternate airport is made will require MORE fuel.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Starting the APU (as a backup AC power source) will increase fuel consumption.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--nat-contingency-procedures-loss-of-atc-clearance\">NAT Contingency Procedures (Loss of ATC Clearance)</h2>\n<p>Per NAT OPS Bulletin 2018-005, contingency procedures in NAT HLA airspace associated with an inability to comply with an assigned clearance:</p>\n<p><strong>Special Procedures - if a revised ATC clearance CANNOT be obtained:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1) Turn 30 degrees or more away from the track. Direction of turn (left or right) is based on the aircraft's position relative to other OTS tracks, direction to the alternate airport, SLOP, etc.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2) If ABLE to maintain the assigned flight level: (A) acquire a same-direction 5 NM offset track; (B) once established on the 5 NM offset, climb or descend using vertical increments of 500 ft below FL410, 500 ft at FL410, and 1,000 ft above FL410 relative to normal cruising levels.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3) If UNABLE to maintain the assigned flight level: (A) minimize rate of descent to what's operationally feasible; (B) acquire a same-direction 5 NM offset track; (C) descend to FL290 or lower; (D) once below FL290, establish and maintain a vertical offset of 500 ft from normal levels and proceed as required until an ATC clearance is received.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In all contingency scenarios:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Establish communication with ATC and nearby aircraft on 121.5 and 123.45 MHz.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Turn on all external lights.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ensure transponder is ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--deviations-around-severe-weather\">Deviations Around Severe Weather</h2>\n<p><strong>If a revised ATC clearance is not possible:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Deviation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;= 5 NM deviation</td>\n<td>Maintain assigned flight level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 5 NM deviation, turning NORTH</td>\n<td>DESCEND 300 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt; 5 NM deviation, turning SOUTH</td>\n<td>CLIMB 300 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Mnemonic: SAND = South Ascend, North Descend.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Establish communication with ATC and nearby aircraft on 121.5 and 123.45 MHz.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Turn on all external lights.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ensure transponder is ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--wake-turbulence-strategic-lateral-offset-procedures-slop\">Wake Turbulence / Strategic Lateral Offset Procedures (SLOP)</h2>\n<p>SLOP is a standard operating procedure throughout the NAT region and includes both SLOP and Micro-SLOP.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detail</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SLOP offsets</td>\n<td>Cleared track centerline, 1.0 NM right of centerline, or 2.0 NM right of centerline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Micro-SLOP</td>\n<td>1/10 NM increments up to 2.0 NM right of centerline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left offsets</td>\n<td>Do NOT SLOP left of centerline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ATC approval</td>\n<td>Not required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordination with preceding aircraft</td>\n<td>On 123.45 MHz, if required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wake turbulence encounter</td>\n<td>Must be reported</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h1 id=\"driftdown--part-3---the-driftdown-scenario\">PART 3 - THE DRIFTDOWN SCENARIO</h1>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--scenario-setup\">Scenario Setup</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>North Atlantic / Random Route / Westbound.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FL410, M0.85, 80,000 lb, ISA+10.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SLOP R2 (2.0 NM right of centerline). Left engine flames out after ETP2.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cockpit at time of failure: SPEED 0.85 / LNAV ON / HDG 270 / AP ON / VS-FPA 0000 / ALTITUDE 41,000 / PFD CMD L.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scenario objectives:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1) Review relevant driftdown procedures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2) Aviate, navigate, and communicate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3) Assess how an engine failure affects other systems.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--driftdown-performance-aom-procedure\">Driftdown Performance / AOM Procedure</h2>\n<p>AOM, Chapter 11 - Performance, ATA 11-05-00, Engine Out Driftdown. To attain the driftdown performance shown, the recommended driftdown procedure described below must be followed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>(A) At the failure of one engine, Maximum Continuous Thrust (MCT) is set and held on the operating engine during any deceleration required to reduce speed to the entry descent Mach. Any deceleration should be performed at the initial cruise altitude, before the start of descent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>(B) The descent Mach number should be maintained until the calibrated speed is intercepted. The calibrated speed is then held down to the final driftdown altitude (identified as \"cruise altitude\").</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>(C) At the final driftdown altitude, a 200 fpm rate of climb capability will be possible at MCT, at the \"start cruise\" calibrated airspeed shown (LRC speed).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>(D) Moderate thrust reductions are required at the \"cruise altitude\" to stabilize at the \"start cruise\" calibrated airspeed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial altitude (ft) / OAT</td>\n<td>41,000 ft / -56C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chart basis</td>\n<td>ISA +10C, initial driftdown weight 80,000 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Descent Mach / KCAS</td>\n<td>0.80 / 251 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>39 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel</td>\n<td>1,800 lb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance</td>\n<td>264 NM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start cruise (final) speed</td>\n<td>218 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cruise altitude (final driftdown altitude)</td>\n<td>26,500 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> S.E. RANGE (AOM 2B-26-140): the MCDU also calculates and displays single-engine range/time to fuel reserves and to zero fuel, at the optimum LRC altitude/speed when OEI (PERF, LSK 5R - S.E. Range).</em></p>\n<p>Driftdown speed schedule for this scenario: M0.85 (41,000 ft, initial cruise) -&gt; M0.80 (descent Mach) -&gt; 251 KCAS (descent KCAS, intercepted) -&gt; 218 KCAS (start cruise, held to 26,500 ft final driftdown altitude).</p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--step-driftdown-checklist\">13-Step Driftdown Checklist</h2>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--fly-the-aircraft\">1) Fly the Aircraft</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The autopilot will remain engaged.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The autothrottle will disconnect automatically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>There will be some yaw as the failed engine rolls back.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Regain and maintain directional control.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Set Maximum Continuous Thrust (MCT) on the operating engine.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--turn-away-from-the-track\">2) Turn Away From the Track</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Sync HDG, select HDG, and rotate the HDG knob 30 degrees or more toward the direction of the chosen alternate (e.g., toward XYZ3).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--create-a-right-5.0-nm-offset\">3) Create a Right 5.0 NM Offset</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>PROG -&gt; NEXT, NEXT -&gt; scratchpad: R5 -&gt; LSK 1R (Offset) -&gt; select LNAV on the guidance panel -&gt; confirm FMS is captured/annunciated.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--call-for-the-driftdown-chart\">4) Call for the Driftdown Chart</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>QRH -&gt; Engines - EB -&gt; Engine Out Driftdown Charts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PERF -&gt; LSK 1L (Gross Wt) -&gt; PREV, to confirm gross weight entering the chart.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--descend-below-the-ots-fl290\">5) Descend Below the OTS (&lt; FL290)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Set the single-engine cruise altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Select MAN and set the descent Mach number.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Select FLCH ON.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Deceleration from M0.85 to M0.80 should be at the initial cruise altitude (41,000 ft). Expect deceleration rate to increase while turning away from the track. Do not allow speed to decrease below M0.80. In order to maintain M0.80, the descent will likely commence before being established on the same-direction 5 NM lateral offset. Autothrottle must remain OFF to maintain the driftdown profile. The AFM, AOM, and QRH do not specify which vertical mode to use (SPEED/FLCH, or VS/FPA) - if FLCH results in speed oscillations as the AFCS corrects for speed deviations, consider using VS/FPA instead.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--communicate---atc-datalink-cpdlcads-c\">6) Communicate - ATC (Datalink: CPDLC/ADS-C)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NAV -&gt; LSK 1R (ATC) -&gt; ATC Logon/Status page -&gt; LSK 6L (ATC Index) -&gt; LSK 1L (EMERGENCY) -&gt; LSK 1L (MAYDAY).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Populate the Emergency Report (Mayday, Divert To, Descend To).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Verify it and send it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--other-traffic\">7) Other Traffic</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Broadcast your situation, position, and intentions on 121.5 and 123.45 MHz.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Turn on all external lights (landing lights do not function above 18,000 ft; pulse lights function without altitude restrictions).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Monitor TCAS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Look for contrails/traffic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--secure-the-failed-engine\">8) Secure the Failed Engine</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>QRH -&gt; Engines - EB -&gt; Engine Shutdown In Flight.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--start-the-apu\">9) Start the APU</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>QRH -&gt; Alternate Normal Procedures -&gt; APU in Flight Operation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--change-the-destination-airport\">10) Change the Destination Airport</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>FPL -&gt; PREV -&gt; scratchpad: alternate airport identifier -&gt; LSK (DEST).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--proceed-to-the-alternate-airport\">11) Proceed to the Alternate Airport</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Once safely below the OTS (&lt; FL290), select LNAV to proceed direct to the ETP alternate airport.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Update the flight plan winds.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If a revised ATC clearance has not been received, contact ATC and request one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Squawk transponder code 7700.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Set ADS-C to EMERGENCY.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--flight-crew-to-cabin-crew-test\">12) Flight Crew to Cabin Crew: TEST</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Letter</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>Type of emergency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>Exit / evacuation plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>Signals - \"two minutes, two minutes\"; \"ten seconds\"; \"EZ Victor\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>Time to prepare</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--flight-dispatch-maintenance-departments\">13) Flight Dispatch / Maintenance Departments</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Notify your dispatch team about your situation, intentions, and requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This can be done through your Communications Service Provider (CSP).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"driftdown--what-if---engine-failure-prior-to-etp2\">\"What If?\" - Engine Failure Prior to ETP2</h3>\n<p>If the engine had failed prior to crossing ETP2, a diversion to XYZ2 would have been necessary (instead of continuing to XYZ3). Once established on a same-direction 5 NM lateral offset, an expedited descent through FL290 (the bottom of the OTS tracks) would have been required BEFORE initiating a turn-back diversion across the flow of adjacent traffic above.</p>\n<p><strong>Summary of the three key actions when diverting back across oceanic traffic flow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>1st - Acquire a same-direction 5 NM offset.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>2nd - Expedited descent below the OTS (speed brake extended, e.g. VS -3000 fpm, MAN mode).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>3rd - DIR to the alternate airport once clear below the OTS.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"driftdown--part-4---systems-assessment\">PART 4 - SYSTEMS' ASSESSMENT</h1>\n<p>How does a single-engine failure affect the aircraft's other systems? Assessed here for the scenario's left-engine failure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--electrical-system\">Electrical System</h2>\n<p>The APU GEN and the R IDG (remaining operating engine's Integrated Drive Generator) power all AC and DC buses following a left-engine failure (loss of the L IDG).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>L Bus Tie (AUTO) closes to tie the L Main AC bus to the R Main AC bus (fed via APU GEN / R IDG), maintaining power to both sides.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Bus architecture: L Main AC / R Main AC (cross-tied via Ess AC) -&gt; L/R Standby AC -&gt; L Ess TRU, L Main TRU, Aux TRU, R Main TRU, R Ess TRU -&gt; L Main DC, R Main DC (with GSB), L Ess DC, R Ess DC -&gt; L Main Batt / R Main Batt as final backup.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--hydraulic-system\">Hydraulic System</h2>\n<p>The Power Transfer Unit (PTU) will take over the duties of the inoperative Engine-Driven Pump (EDP) as soon as L Hydraulic system pressure drops below 1,500 psi.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>PWR XFR UNIT selected ON; \"PTU HYD ON\" annunciates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The PTU pump/motor assembly transfers hydraulic power from the operating (right) system to pressurize the left system, using the right system's hydraulic power without transferring fluid between systems.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Result: loss of the LEFT thrust reverser (the left EDP-fed system can no longer support reverser deployment even with the PTU providing backup pressure for other left-side functions).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--fuel-system\">Fuel System</h2>\n<p>All fuel system components continue to operate normally after an engine failure. However, a fuel imbalance condition will develop over time, since only the operating engine is drawing fuel from its onside tank (e.g., 10,000 lb vs. 9,000 lb).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>QRH -&gt; Alternate Normal Procedures -&gt; Fuel Balancing in Flight.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"driftdown--pneumatic-system\">Pneumatic System</h2>\n<p>The remaining (operating) engine can provide the necessary bleed air (high pressure and temperature) via its onside manifold. Opening the isolation valve allows the operating engine to provide bleed air to the opposite side's ECS packs as well, so both L Pack and R Pack remain available.</p>\n<h1 id=\"driftdown--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>g550_driftdown_procedures_and_systems_assessment.pdf - handwritten G550 study notes on NAT/HLA oceanic procedures, Equal Time Points, engine-out driftdown, and post-failure systems assessment, Ivan Luciani / Code7700.com, \"for reference purposes only.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 Limitations Study Guide (this folder) - checked for related driftdown/engine-out/oxygen figures; the Limitations guide does not contain a dedicated driftdown or engine-out-ceiling section, and its general Mmo/Vmo and maximum-operating-altitude figures are consistent with (and independent of) the scenario-specific numbers in this guide, so no correction was required.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AOM Chapter 11 (ATA 11-05-00, Engine Out Driftdown) and AOM Chapter 2B (S.E. Range, PlaneView Pilot's Manual) - referenced within the source notes for the driftdown procedure text and MCDU S.E. Range page.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NAT OPS Bulletin 2018-005 - referenced within the source notes for NAT HLA contingency procedures.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> These notes are explicitly marked by the original author as reference material only. The driftdown performance table in Part 1/Part 3 is specific to one worked example (80,000 lb / ISA+10 / FL410) - always pull the current AOM Engine Out Driftdown chart for the aircraft's actual weight, OAT, and altitude before using these numbers operationally. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM/QRH and other approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","driftdown--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","driftdown--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Driftdown Performance (Worked Example)","driftdown--driftdown-performance-worked-example"],["h2","Equal Time Point (ETP) Formula","driftdown--equal-time-point-etp-formula"],["h2","NAT Vertical / Lateral / Longitudinal Separation","driftdown--nat-vertical-lateral-longitudinal-separation"],["h2","NAT Contingency - Loss of ATC Clearance","driftdown--nat-contingency---loss-of-atc-clearance"],["h2","Severe Weather Deviation / SAND Rule","driftdown--severe-weather-deviation-sand-rule"],["h2","Wake Turbulence / SLOP","driftdown--wake-turbulence-slop"],["h1","PART 2 - NAT / OCEANIC BACKGROUND","driftdown--part-2---nat-oceanic-background"],["h2","NAT / HLA / Organized Track System (OTS) Overview","driftdown--nat-hla-organized-track-system-ots-overview"],["h2","Equal Time Point (ETP)","driftdown--equal-time-point-etp"],["h2","NAT Contingency Procedures (Loss of ATC Clearance)","driftdown--nat-contingency-procedures-loss-of-atc-clearance"],["h2","Deviations Around Severe Weather","driftdown--deviations-around-severe-weather"],["h2","Wake Turbulence / Strategic Lateral Offset Procedures (SLOP)","driftdown--wake-turbulence-strategic-lateral-offset-procedures-slop"],["h1","PART 3 - THE DRIFTDOWN SCENARIO","driftdown--part-3---the-driftdown-scenario"],["h2","Scenario Setup","driftdown--scenario-setup"],["h2","Driftdown Performance / AOM Procedure","driftdown--driftdown-performance-aom-procedure"],["h2","13-Step Driftdown Checklist","driftdown--step-driftdown-checklist"],["h1","PART 4 - SYSTEMS' ASSESSMENT","driftdown--part-4---systems-assessment"],["h2","Electrical System","driftdown--electrical-system"],["h2","Hydraulic System","driftdown--hydraulic-system"],["h2","Fuel System","driftdown--fuel-system"],["h2","Pneumatic System","driftdown--pneumatic-system"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","driftdown--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"efvs-ops":{"t":"EFVS Operations Guide","sub":"Enhanced Flight Vision System · Consolidado a partir do RBAC 91 / normativa ANAC sobre Operações EFVS, · e do G550 AFM (Video Function / EVS-HUD Operational Procedures)","html":"<h1 id=\"efvs-ops--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>Este guia reorganiza o documento EFVS original no mesmo formato de duas camadas usado nos demais guias de estudo do G550, mantendo o conteúdo regulatório em português (RBAC 91 / ANAC) e o conteúdo operacional do AFM em inglês, como no original — sem tradução, apenas reorganizado e com referência cruzada.</p>\n<p><strong>Duas camadas de conteúdo:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Parte 1 - Tabelas de Referência Rápida: alturas-limite por tipo de aproximação (CAT I / APV / NPA), tabela completa de conversão RVR/CMV, tabela de conversão de visibilidade para CMV, requisitos de recência, e lista de proibições - o que você precisa de cabeça antes de uma aproximação EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Parte 2 - Conteúdo Completo: abreviaturas, conceitos, tipos de operação, referências visuais, arremetidas, proibições, responsabilidades do PIC, operações internacionais, experiência recente, tabelas de conversão com instruções completas, approach ban (UK/França), e os procedimentos AFM de Vídeo e EVS/HUD.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>O que foi verificado:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Conteúdo cruzado com o PDF fonte \"EFVS.pdf\" (mesma estrutura de sumário) - conteúdo consistente.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tabela RVR/CMV (46 linhas) transcrita integralmente para uma tabela pesquisável, mantendo os valores originais.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Alturas-limite (100 ft para CAT I, 200 ft para APV/NPA) e demais critérios numéricos (RVR &lt; 550m exige dois pilotos, deslocamento angular máximo 2°, ângulo de planeio máximo 3,7°) destacados na Parte 1 para consulta rápida.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"efvs-ops--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--alturas-limite-por-tipo-de-aproximação\">Alturas-Limite por Tipo de Aproximação</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Tipo de Aproximação</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Altura Mínima com EFVS (HAT)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Referência</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CAT I</td>\n<td>100 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira</td>\n<td>RBAC 91 / 14 CFR 91.176(b)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APV (com orientação vertical, via CDFA)</td>\n<td>200 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira</td>\n<td>RBAC 91</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NPA (sem orientação vertical, via CDFA)</td>\n<td>200 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira</td>\n<td>RBAC 91</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Abaixo dessas alturas, a aproximação só pode continuar se as referências visuais forem identificáveis a olho nu, sem o auxílio da imagem EFVS.</em></p>\n<p><a href=\"\\l\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Detalhe completo: Parte 2, Aproximações CAT I / APV-NPA</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--critérios-adicionais---aproximações-apvnpa-com-efvs\">Critérios Adicionais - Aproximações APV/NPA com EFVS</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Critério</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limite</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RVR reduzido mínimo com 1 piloto</td>\n<td>Não permitido - RVR &lt; 550m exige dois ou mais pilotos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Técnica de aproximação</td>\n<td>CDFA obrigatória</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deslocamento angular máx. (rumo final x rumo da pista)</td>\n<td>2°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ângulo de planeio máximo publicado</td>\n<td>3,7°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vento de través</td>\n<td>Não pode exceder o valor certificado para o equipamento EFVS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--referências-visuais-requeridas---resumo\">Referências Visuais Requeridas - Resumo</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Fase</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Referência aceita</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Abaixo de DA/DH ou MDA/MDH (com EFVS)</td>\n<td>ALS, ou cabeceira (início da pista / luzes de cabeceira / REIL) + zona de toque (superfície / luzes / sinalização / luzes de pista)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abaixo de 100 ft (CAT I) ou 200 ft (APV/NPA) - a olho nu, sem EFVS</td>\n<td>Cabeceira (superfície ou luzes/sinalização) e/ou zona de toque (superfície ou luzes/sinalização)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Diferença chave: as luzes do VGSI (indicador visual de rampa) NÃO contam como referência visual em Operação EFVS.</em></p>\n<p><a href=\"\\l\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Detalhe completo: Parte 2, Referências Visuais</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--proibições---aproximações-não-autorizadas-com-efvs\">Proibições - Aproximações Não Autorizadas com EFVS</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação por instrumentos para circular (exceto pouso direto).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação visual.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação CAT II.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação CAT III.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><a href=\"\\l\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Detalhe completo: Parte 2, Proibições</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--section\"></h2>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--experiência-recente-de-voo-efvs\">Experiência Recente de Voo EFVS</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Mínimo de 6 aproximações por instrumento usando EFVS nos últimos 6 meses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O piloto deve ter sido o único a manusear os controles de voo nessas aproximações.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Conduzidas no mesmo conjunto tipo de aeronave / equipamento EFVS para o qual busca manter a prerrogativa.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Podem ser diurnas ou noturnas, em qualquer condição climática, e cada uma deve terminar em pouso completo.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#efvs-ops--experiência-recente-de-voo-efvs-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Detalhe completo: Parte 2, Experiência Recente de Voo</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--tabela-de-conversão-rvrcmv---visão-natural-para-visão-intensificada\">Tabela de Conversão RVR/CMV - Visão Natural para Visão Intensificada</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>RVR/CMV normal (m)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>RVR/CMV reduzido (m)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>RVR/CMV normal (m)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>RVR/CMV reduzido (m)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>550</td>\n<td>350</td>\n<td>2700</td>\n<td>1800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>600</td>\n<td>400</td>\n<td>2800</td>\n<td>1900</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>650</td>\n<td>450</td>\n<td>2900</td>\n<td>1900</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>700</td>\n<td>450</td>\n<td>3000</td>\n<td>2000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>750</td>\n<td>500</td>\n<td>3100</td>\n<td>2000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>800</td>\n<td>550</td>\n<td>3200</td>\n<td>2100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>900</td>\n<td>600</td>\n<td>3300</td>\n<td>2200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>650</td>\n<td>3400</td>\n<td>2200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1100</td>\n<td>750</td>\n<td>3500</td>\n<td>2300</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1200</td>\n<td>800</td>\n<td>3600</td>\n<td>2400</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1300</td>\n<td>900</td>\n<td>3700</td>\n<td>2400</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1400</td>\n<td>900</td>\n<td>3800</td>\n<td>2500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>3900</td>\n<td>2600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1600</td>\n<td>1100</td>\n<td>4000</td>\n<td>2600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1700</td>\n<td>1100</td>\n<td>4100</td>\n<td>2700</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1800</td>\n<td>1200</td>\n<td>4200</td>\n<td>2800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1900</td>\n<td>1300</td>\n<td>4300</td>\n<td>2800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>1300</td>\n<td>4400</td>\n<td>2900</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2100</td>\n<td>1400</td>\n<td>4500</td>\n<td>3000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2200</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>4600</td>\n<td>3000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2300</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>4700</td>\n<td>3100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2400</td>\n<td>1600</td>\n<td>4800</td>\n<td>3200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2500</td>\n<td>1700</td>\n<td>4900</td>\n<td>3200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2600</td>\n<td>1700</td>\n<td>5000</td>\n<td>3300</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Exemplo: aproximação ILS CAT I com RVR de 700m -&gt; RVR reduzido de 450m, utilizável para planejamento e liberação do voo. Um valor de visibilidade reportado não pode ser usado diretamente nesta tabela - deve primeiro ser convertido para CMV (ver tabela abaixo).</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#efvs-ops--tabelas-rvrcmv---instruções-de-conversão\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Detalhe completo, incluindo instruções de conversão: Parte 2, Tabelas RVR/CMV</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--tabela-de-conversão-de-visibilidade-reportada-para-cmv\">Tabela de Conversão de Visibilidade Reportada para CMV</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Luzes na Operação</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Fator - Dia</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Fator - Noite</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sistemas de luzes de aproximação e de pista de alta intensidade</td>\n<td>1,5</td>\n<td>2,0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Qualquer outro tipo de sistema de luzes</td>\n<td>1,0</td>\n<td>1,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sem luzes</td>\n<td>1,0</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> CMV = visibilidade reportada x fator. Exemplo: visibilidade 1.000m, pista com luzes de alta intensidade, aproximação noturna -&gt; fator 2,0 -&gt; CMV = 2.000m. Conversão não pode ser usada quando o RVR relatado está disponível, para calcular mínimos de decolagem, ou para qualquer RVR mínimo inferior a 800m.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"efvs-ops--part-2---conteúdo-completo\">PART 2 - CONTEÚDO COMPLETO</h1>\n<p><em>Segue a estrutura do documento original: abreviaturas, conceitos, tipos de operação, referências visuais, arremetidas, proibições, responsabilidades, experiência recente, tabelas de conversão, approach ban, e os procedimentos AFM de vídeo e EVS/HUD.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--abreviaturas\">Abreviaturas</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Sigla</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Significado</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ALS</td>\n<td>Sistema de Luzes de Aproximação</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>APV</td>\n<td>Approach Procedure with Vertical Guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CDFA</td>\n<td>Continuous Descent Final Approach (3D equipamento de bordo / 2D cálculo manual)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CMV</td>\n<td>Converted Meteorological Visibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DA</td>\n<td>Decision Altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DH</td>\n<td>Decision Height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EFVS</td>\n<td>Enhanced Flight Vision System</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EVS</td>\n<td>Enhanced Vision System</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FAF</td>\n<td>Final Approach Fix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FD</td>\n<td>Flight Director</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FLIR</td>\n<td>Forward Looking Infrared</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FPARC</td>\n<td>Flight Path Angle Reference Cue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FPV</td>\n<td>Flight Path Vector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GP</td>\n<td>Superfície Eletrônica de Planeio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HDD</td>\n<td>Head-Down Display</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HUD</td>\n<td>Head-Up Display</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IAP</td>\n<td>Instrument Approach Procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MAP</td>\n<td>Missed Approach Point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MDA</td>\n<td>Minimum Descent Altitude (ponto de aproximação perdida)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MDH</td>\n<td>Minimum Descent Height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NPA</td>\n<td>Non-precision Approach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RVR</td>\n<td>Alcance Visual na Pista</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVS</td>\n<td>Synthetic Vision System</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VGSI</td>\n<td>Visual Glide Slope Indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VNAV</td>\n<td>Vertical Navigation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--conceitos\">Conceitos</h2>\n<p><strong>Continuous Descent Final Approach (CDFA)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Técnica, consistente com procedimentos de aproximação estabilizados, para voar o segmento de aproximação final de um procedimento NPA como uma descida contínua, sem nivelamento, de uma altitude/altura igual ou acima da altitude/altura do FAF até um ponto aproximadamente 15m (50 ft) acima da cabeceira da pista, ou o ponto onde a manobra de flare deve começar.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Crédito Operacional</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Redução dos mínimos operacionais do aeródromo, ou satisfação dos requisitos de visibilidade de voo, ou necessidade de menos instalações em terra, compensado pelas capacidades de bordo.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Exemplo: satisfazer o requisito de visibilidade de voo por meio de um EFVS certificado - a visão natural reportada pode ser menor que a prescrita, mas a visibilidade intensificada, conforme determinado pelo piloto, é suficiente para pousar.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pode incluir: (a) mínimo meteorológico abaixo do mínimo do aeródromo; (b) redução/satisfação dos requisitos de visibilidade; e/ou (c) redução das facilidades em solo, compensada pelo equipamento embarcado.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Operações EFVS permitem a substituição do RVR/CMV requeridos nos IAP.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--tipos-de-operação-efvs\">Tipos de Operação EFVS</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Uma Operação EFVS possibilita a realização de procedimentos de aproximação CAT I, APV, ou NPA (estes dois últimos com técnica CDFA) abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH até 100 ft ou mais acima da elevação da cabeceira.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Aproximações APV incluem:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NDB com orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>VOR com orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>LOC com orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RNP com orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RNAV com orientação vertical (LNAV/VNAV)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Aproximações NPA incluem:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NDB sem orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>VOR sem orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>LOC sem orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RNP sem orientação vertical</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RNAV sem orientação vertical (LNAV)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Planejamento e Início de Voo IFR para uma Operação EFVS</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Não obstante as seções 91.119 e 91.1039 do RBAC 91, caso o operador deseje usar uma aproximação EFVS, o planejamento e início de uma operação IFR podem ser feitos utilizando: os valores de DA/DH ou MDA/MDH previstos no IAP a ser executado; e o RVR/CMV reduzido especificado na tabela de conversão, correspondente ao RVR/Visibilidade do IAP a ser executado.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--aproximações-cat-i-utilizando-uma-operação-efvs\">Aproximações CAT I Utilizando uma Operação EFVS</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>O piloto pode iniciar uma aproximação CAT I em Operação EFVS se a última informação meteorológica disponível informar que o RVR está igual ou acima do especificado na coluna de RVR/CMV reduzidos da tabela de conversão para o IAP a ser utilizado.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O piloto pode continuar a aproximação abaixo da DA/DH até 100 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira desde que as referências visuais requeridas sejam exibidas e identificáveis no EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Abaixo de 100 ft, o piloto só pode continuar se for possível identificar as referências visuais com a visão natural, sem auxílio da imagem EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--aproximações-apv-ou-npa-utilizando-efvs\">Aproximações APV ou NPA Utilizando EFVS</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>O piloto pode iniciar a aproximação APV ou NPA em Operação EFVS se a última informação meteorológica disponível informar RVR igual ou acima do especificado na coluna de RVR/CMV reduzidos para a aproximação a ser utilizada.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O piloto pode continuar a aproximação abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH até 200 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira desde que as referências visuais requeridas sejam exibidas e identificáveis no HUD (ou equipamento equivalente) do EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Abaixo de 200 ft, o piloto só pode continuar se for possível identificar as referências visuais com a visão natural, sem auxílio da imagem EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Adicionalmente:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>RVR reduzido menor que 550m deve ser realizado com dois pilotos ou mais.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>É necessário o uso da técnica CDFA.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A aproximação deve usar um modo de orientação vertical aprovado pela certificação da aviônica da aeronave.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O segmento de aproximação do FAF até a cabeceira deve ser direto (straight-in).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A diferença angular máxima entre o rumo do segmento final e o rumo da pista não deve ser maior que 2°.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O ângulo de planeio do segmento final deve estar publicado e não ser maior que 3,7°.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A componente máxima de vento cruzado certificada para o equipamento EFVS não pode ser excedida.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--referências-visuais\">Referências Visuais</h2>\n<p><strong>(1) Para Descer e se Manter Abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>As seguintes referências visuais para a pista pretendida devem ser distintamente visíveis e identificáveis para o piloto usando o EFVS (e para o PM, se aplicável): o sistema de luzes de aproximação (ALS), se instalado; ou a cabeceira da pista e a zona de toque.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A cabeceira deve ser identificada por: início da superfície da pista; luzes de cabeceira; ou luzes de identificação de fim de pista (REIL).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A zona de toque deve ser identificada por: superfície da zona de toque; luzes de zona de toque; sinalizações de zona de toque; ou luzes de pista.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(2) Para Continuar a Aproximação Abaixo de 100 ou 200 Pés</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Conforme o tipo de aproximação (CAT I ou APV/NPA), as seguintes referências devem ser distintamente visíveis e identificáveis sem o auxílio do EFVS: a cabeceira da pista; as luzes ou sinalizações da cabeceira; a superfície da zona de toque; ou as luzes/sinalizações da zona de toque.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Diferença chave: as luzes do VGSI não devem ser usadas como referência visual em Operação EFVS - o piloto deve conseguir ver a cabeceira e a zona de toque mesmo que as luzes de aproximação não estejam visíveis.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--arremetidas\">Arremetidas</h2>\n<p>Durante uma Operação EFVS, o piloto deve iniciar uma arremetida na ou abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH sempre que:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Qualquer componente necessário do EFVS falhar.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>As referências visuais requeridas (1) não forem mais distintamente visíveis e identificáveis usando o EFVS ao descer abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>As referências visuais requeridas (2) não forem mais distintamente visíveis e identificáveis a olho nu ao descer abaixo de 100/200 ft acima da cabeceira.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A aeronave não estiver em posição de realizar uma descida para pouso na pista pretendida com razão de descida normal usando manobras normais.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--proibições\">Proibições</h2>\n<p>Um piloto não está autorizado a realizar Operações EFVS nos seguintes tipos de procedimento:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação por instrumentos para circular, exceto em aproximações para pouso direto.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação visual.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação CAT II.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Procedimento de aproximação CAT III.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--responsabilidades-do-piloto-em-comando\">Responsabilidades do Piloto em Comando</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Informar ao órgão de controle de tráfego aéreo a intenção de realizar o procedimento de aproximação com o emprego do EFVS. (Nota: a manifestação dessa intenção será interpretada pelo prestador de serviço de navegação aérea como declaração de que a tripulação e a aeronave estão aprovadas para a operação.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Notificar imediatamente ao órgão de controle qualquer anomalia ou deficiência encontrada nos equipamentos do sistema.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--operações-efvs-internacionais\">Operações EFVS Internacionais</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A maioria das Autoridades de Aviação Civil (AAC) estrangeiras exige aprovação específica para Operações EFVS; uma AAC estrangeira pode exigir que um operador do Brasil obtenha autorização emitida pela ANAC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A ANAC recomenda que operadores contatem a AAC de cada país onde planejam operar para determinar requisitos de equipamento, aprovação operacional, e demais requisitos - que podem diferir dos estabelecidos no Brasil.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Operadores de aeronaves civis registradas no Brasil operando fora do país devem cumprir a seção 91.703 do RBAC 91 e demais regulamentos aplicáveis.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Referências: Partes I, II e III do Anexo 6 da ICAO; ICAO Doc 9365 (Manual of All-Weather Operations); e a AIP do Estado em questão.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--experiência-recente-de-voo-efvs-1\">Experiência Recente de Voo EFVS</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Mínimo de 6 aproximações por instrumento usando EFVS nos últimos 6 meses anteriores à Operação EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O piloto deve ter sido o único a manusear os controles de voo nessas aproximações.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Devem ter sido conduzidas no mesmo conjunto tipo de aeronave / equipamento EFVS para o qual o piloto busca manter a prerrogativa.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Podem ser conduzidas de dia ou de noite, em qualquer condição climática, e cada aproximação deve terminar em pouso completo.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--tabelas-rvrcmv---instruções-de-conversão\">Tabelas RVR/CMV - Instruções de Conversão</h2>\n<p>Ver Parte 1 para as tabelas completas (RVR/CMV normal -&gt; reduzido; visibilidade reportada -&gt; CMV).</p>\n<p><strong>Quando usar</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A tabela RVR/CMV é uma ferramenta do RBAC 91 (ANAC) para calcular o crédito operacional do EFVS - ela não aparece no AFM/AOM da Gulfstream, que trata o EFVS apenas do ponto de vista de performance e limitações do equipamento.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Serve para duas situações apenas: planejamento de voo e início de uma aproximação IFR em Operação EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Não serve para continuar a aproximação abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH - a partir daí quem manda são as Referências Visuais exigidas (ver seção correspondente), não mais o RVR.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Não pode ser usada para calcular mínimos de decolagem, nem para qualquer RVR mínimo inferior a 800 m.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Como usar - passo a passo</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Passo 1: entre na tabela RVR/CMV com o valor normal - o mínimo de visibilidade publicado no próprio IAP que será voado (o valor \"sem EFVS\"). A tabela devolve o RVR/CMV reduzido correspondente.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passo 2: esse valor reduzido é o que precisa estar igual ou acima do RVR relatado no momento para autorizar o início da aproximação em Operação EFVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passo 3: se o aeródromo não reporta RVR (só visibilidade meteorológica), não é permitido usar esse valor diretamente na tabela - primeiro converta a visibilidade reportada para CMV usando a segunda tabela (fator conforme o tipo de iluminação da pista e se a aproximação é diurna ou noturna), e só então compare esse CMV com o reduzido obtido no Passo 1.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Restrições da conversão de visibilidade para CMV: (1) não pode ser usada quando o RVR relatado já está disponível; (2) não serve para mínimos de decolagem; (3) não se aplica a qualquer RVR mínimo inferior a 800 m. Se o RVR for relatado acima do valor máximo avaliado pelo operador do aeródromo (ex.: \"RVR superior a 1.500 m\"), esse valor não conta como relatado para o item (1).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Detalhe adicional para aproximações APV/NPA: se o RVR reduzido calculado ficar abaixo de 550 m, a operação exige dois pilotos - não pode ser feita por um piloto só.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Exemplo:</strong> Exemplo prático: ILS CAT I com mínimo publicado de 700 m (RVR/CMV normal). Na tabela, 700 -&gt; reduzido de 450 m. Se o RVR relatado no momento for 450 m ou mais, a aproximação pode ser iniciada como Operação EFVS, mesmo o mínimo do procedimento sendo 700 m. Se em vez de RVR só houver visibilidade reportada de 1.000 m, pista com luzes de aproximação/pista de alta intensidade, aproximação noturna (fator 2,0): CMV = 1.000 x 2,0 = 2.000 m - valor muito acima do reduzido de 450 m, então a aproximação também pode ser iniciada.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Resumo do fluxo de decisão:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Mínimo publicado do IAP -&gt; Tabela 1 (RVR/CMV normal -&gt; reduzido) -&gt; comparar com o RVR relatado (ou, na falta dele, com a visibilidade convertida para CMV pela Tabela 2) -&gt; se o relatado for igual ou maior que o reduzido, a aproximação EFVS pode ser iniciada/planejada.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--approach-ban\">Approach Ban</h2>\n<p><strong>Approach Ban UK</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Requisitos para início e continuação de uma aproximação (approach ban), aplicáveis a todas as operações, conforme EASA Air Ops.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Uma aeronave pode iniciar uma aproximação por instrumentos independentemente do RVR/visibilidade relatado, mas a aproximação não deve continuar abaixo de 1.000 ft acima do aeródromo se o RVR/visibilidade relevante estiver abaixo do mínimo especificado para pouso.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Se, após passar 1.000 ft, o RVR/visibilidade relatado cair abaixo do mínimo aplicável, a aproximação pode continuar até a DA/H ou MDA/H.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A aproximação pode continuar abaixo da DA/H ou MDA/H e o pouso pode ser completado desde que a referência visual requerida esteja estabelecida na DA/H ou MDA/H e seja mantida.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O RVR da zona de toque é sempre o valor controlador.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Approach Ban France</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Uma aproximação por instrumentos pode ser iniciada independentemente do RVR/VIS relatado, mas não deve continuar além do outer marker (ou posição equivalente) se o RVR/VIS relatado for menor que os mínimos aplicáveis. Quando o RVR não estiver disponível, os valores podem ser derivados da conversão da visibilidade relatada.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Se, após passar o outer marker (ou posição equivalente), o RVR/VIS relatado cair abaixo do mínimo aplicável, a aproximação pode continuar até a DA(H) ou MDA(H).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Onde não existir outer marker ou posição equivalente, o piloto deve decidir continuar ou abandonar a aproximação antes de descer abaixo de 1.000 ft acima do aeródromo no segmento final. Se a MDA(H) for 1.000 ft ou mais acima do aeródromo, o operador deve estabelecer uma altura para cada procedimento, abaixo da qual a aproximação não deve continuar se o RVR/VIS for menor que o mínimo aplicável.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A aproximação pode continuar abaixo da DA(H) ou MDA(H) e o pouso pode ser completado desde que a referência visual requerida esteja estabelecida na DA(H) ou MDA(H) e seja mantida.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>O RVR da zona de toque é sempre controlador; se relatados e relevantes, o RVR do ponto médio e do final de pista também são controladores. RVR mínimo do ponto médio: 125m (ou o RVR requerido para zona de toque, se menor). RVR mínimo do final de pista: 75m. Para aeronaves com sistema de orientação/controle de rollout, o RVR mínimo do ponto médio é 75m.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>\"Relevante\" neste contexto significa a parte da pista usada durante a fase de alta velocidade do pouso, até uma velocidade de aproximadamente 60 kt.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--afm---approved-use-of-video-function\">AFM - Approved Use of Video Function</h2>\n<p>Depending on the number and type of video input sources installed, these sources can be displayed in the cockpit for safety and security awareness on the ground and/or for safety awareness in flight.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>With security cameras installed, the crew can monitor movement around the airplane at the parking stand.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When maneuvering in confined areas or on narrow taxiways, the lower fuselage cameras can be used to monitor gear position relative to pavement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>During taxi operations in low visibility, the right seat pilot can monitor the EVS camera image on DU 3 while the left seat pilot looks outside for other ground traffic and obstructions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In flight, the external cameras can be used in the event of a malfunction (fuel leaks, landing gear failure to extend or retract, open gear doors, engine fires, among others). There is no AFM requirement to use the cameras in these events.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>During low visibility approaches, the right seat pilot can monitor the approach by displaying the EVS image on DU 3 (2/3 window) or on DU 4 (upper 1/6 window). DU 4 is recommended if DU 3 is displaying the approach chart - this lets the right seat pilot monitor the approach with the EVS image together with the PFD, while retaining the missed approach instructions to read to the left seat pilot if a go-around is necessary.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recommended Use of Video and Charts</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pre-Departure</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Crew uses one or both 2/3 DU windows to view the airport chart and SID; the 2/3 DU window can also display uplink weather along the route for strategic planning, and either the 2/3 or 1/6 DU windows can show internal/external camera displays for surface-movement observation around the airplane for security purposes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Taxi</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Once engines are started, display the airport diagram on DU 3 and, if used, replace the EVS image with the Map Display on DU 2.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeoff</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Switch to the SID in place of the airport diagram on DU 3. Once enroute, use uplink weather for checking conditions along the route/destination; external cameras used occasionally to check the exterior or during an abnormal occurrence.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Approach and Landing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Use DU 3 2/3 window for the STAR/approach while keeping the Map Display on DU 2. The copilot may select the EVS image if warranted (low visibility, haze/smog, night). With an instrument approach, the approach chart can stay on DU 2/3 with EVS on the upper 1/6 window adjacent to the PFD, or the EVS image can replace the approach chart on DU 3 2/3 after the FAF. Either way, the right seat pilot monitors the approach using EVS together with the PFD.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"efvs-ops--evshud---procedimentos-operacionais-até-100-ft-acima-da-zona-de-toque\">EVS/HUD - Procedimentos Operacionais até 100 ft Acima da Zona de Toque</h2>\n<p><strong>1. EFVS Performance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The demonstrated performance of the installed EFVS Landing System meets the criteria of AC 20-167A for EFVS operations conducted to 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation in accordance with 14 CFR Part 91.176(b). The EFVS Landing System does not provide a Visual Advantage when viewing LED lighting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Operational Procedures - General</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>For EVS to be available on the HUD, the FLIR and EVS options must be selected on the Display Controller (DC) HUD menu. Selecting FLIR (2L) triggers a built-in test and internal camera cooling - about 30 minutes before EVS imagery is available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Enter runway elevation and flight path angle (FPA) on the HUD menu page of either DC. The selected FPA displays on the HUD for an ILS or LPV approach, and the conformal runway symbol overlays the actual runway. Approach frequency, course, decision altitude, and NAV source are selected as for a standard head-down approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Pilot Flying (left seat) flies the approach and completes the landing using EVS/HUD information; EVS/HUD approaches require a qualified and current EVS pilot in the left seat.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Both pilots monitor flight instruments and CAS for malfunction indications; the PM monitors both PFDs to ensure they correspond; the PF ensures the appropriate flight director modes are selected on the guidance panel.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The EVS image may become partially obscured during a prolonged icing encounter as ice accretes on the forward edge of the EVS camera window frame.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Operational Procedures - Approach</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Pilots should ensure approach lighting is functioning and selected to high intensity at airports where pilot action is required.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Coupled or Manual Flight</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>EVS approaches may be manually flown or coupled to the autopilot. For approaches with vertical guidance with EVS, select AUTO/CDI on the DC HUD menu; the pilot manages the autopilot (if used) and verifies course guidance and the EVS picture agree - on ILS and LPV approaches the FPV should be positioned within the HUD conformal runway.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>To descend below DA or DH, the requirements of 14 CFR 91.176(b) or CAT.OP.MPA.110 of EU No. 965/2012 must be met. To descend below 100 feet HAT, visual cues must be seen without the aid of EVS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The flare cue advises the pilot when and at what rate to initiate the flare for touchdown - it is not flare guidance as used in Category III systems that guides the pilot to a touchdown within a guaranteed touchdown zone. The flare cue is presented only when the FPA setting on the DC HUD menu is between -2.49 and -5.00 degrees.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>At 100 ft HAT the flare cue starts to rise from below the FPV and moves vertically toward the horizon. Prior to or at 60 ft radar altitude, the pilot disconnects the autopilot (if used) and may follow the flare cue and flight director guidance, positioning the FPV wings between the parallel lines of the flare cue to begin the flare maneuver.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>During EFVS approaches, the PF must determine and see the visibility and visual reference requirements specified in 14 CFR 91.176(b)(3) for EFVS operations to 100 feet above the touchdown zone elevation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"efvs-ops--appendix---fontes\">Appendix - Fontes</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>RBAC 91 (ANAC) - Operações EFVS: seções relativas a planejamento IFR, aproximações CAT I/APV/NPA, referências visuais, arremetidas, proibições, responsabilidades do PIC, operações internacionais, e experiência recente.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>14 CFR Part 91.176(a)/(b) - critérios equivalentes FAA para operações EFVS até 100 ft (b) e até o pouso e corrida (a).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AC 20-167A - critérios de performance demonstrada para o EFVS Landing System instalado.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>EASA Air Ops / CAT.OP.MPA.110 - requisitos de approach ban (Reino Unido) e critérios equivalentes de descida abaixo de DA/DH.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ICAO Anexo 6, Partes I/II/III, e ICAO Doc 9365 (Manual of All-Weather Operations) - orientação para operações EFVS internacionais.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G550 AFM - Approved Use of Video Function; Recommended Use of Video and Charts; EVS to 100 Feet Above the Touchdown Zone Elevation Operational Procedures (Revision 44, 2023-01-27).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Documento fonte original \"EFVS.pdf\" (pasta Systems Reference) - mesma estrutura de sumário, conteúdo consistente.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Prior \"EFVS.docx\" working document (Luciano Rocha) - reorganizado no mesmo formato dos demais guias de estudo do G550.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Este é um material de estudo pessoal. Consulte sempre o RBAC 91, o AFM vigente, e a AIP do país de operação para a versão oficial e atualizada.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","efvs-ops--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","efvs-ops--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Alturas-Limite por Tipo de Aproximação","efvs-ops--alturas-limite-por-tipo-de-aproximação"],["h2","Critérios Adicionais - Aproximações APV/NPA com EFVS","efvs-ops--critérios-adicionais---aproximações-apvnpa-com-efvs"],["h2","Referências Visuais Requeridas - Resumo","efvs-ops--referências-visuais-requeridas---resumo"],["h2","Proibições - Aproximações Não Autorizadas com EFVS","efvs-ops--proibições---aproximações-não-autorizadas-com-efvs"],["h2","","efvs-ops--section"],["h2","Experiência Recente de Voo EFVS","efvs-ops--experiência-recente-de-voo-efvs"],["h2","Tabela de Conversão RVR/CMV - Visão Natural para Visão Intensificada","efvs-ops--tabela-de-conversão-rvrcmv---visão-natural-para-visão-intensificada"],["h2","Tabela de Conversão de Visibilidade Reportada para CMV","efvs-ops--tabela-de-conversão-de-visibilidade-reportada-para-cmv"],["h1","PART 2 - CONTEÚDO COMPLETO","efvs-ops--part-2---conteúdo-completo"],["h2","Abreviaturas","efvs-ops--abreviaturas"],["h2","Conceitos","efvs-ops--conceitos"],["h2","Tipos de Operação EFVS","efvs-ops--tipos-de-operação-efvs"],["h2","Aproximações CAT I Utilizando uma Operação EFVS","efvs-ops--aproximações-cat-i-utilizando-uma-operação-efvs"],["h2","Aproximações APV ou NPA Utilizando EFVS","efvs-ops--aproximações-apv-ou-npa-utilizando-efvs"],["h2","Referências Visuais","efvs-ops--referências-visuais"],["h2","Arremetidas","efvs-ops--arremetidas"],["h2","Proibições","efvs-ops--proibições"],["h2","Responsabilidades do Piloto em Comando","efvs-ops--responsabilidades-do-piloto-em-comando"],["h2","Operações EFVS Internacionais","efvs-ops--operações-efvs-internacionais"],["h2","Experiência Recente de Voo EFVS","efvs-ops--experiência-recente-de-voo-efvs-1"],["h2","Tabelas RVR/CMV - Instruções de Conversão","efvs-ops--tabelas-rvrcmv---instruções-de-conversão"],["h2","Approach Ban","efvs-ops--approach-ban"],["h2","AFM - Approved Use of Video Function","efvs-ops--afm---approved-use-of-video-function"],["h2","EVS/HUD - Procedimentos Operacionais até 100 ft Acima da Zona de Toque","efvs-ops--evshud---procedimentos-operacionais-até-100-ft-acima-da-zona-de-toque"],["h1","Appendix - Fontes","efvs-ops--appendix---fontes"]]},"flex-takeoff":{"t":"Reduced Thrust (FLEX) Takeoff Study Guide","sub":"Appendix A - Performance / Takeoff Planning · Consolidated from personal study notes on Reduced Thrust (FLEX) Takeoff · including the worked takeoff-planning example and Figure A-1 reference chart","html":"<h1 id=\"flex-takeoff--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts a personal study deck (\"Appendix A - Reduced Thrust (FLEX) Takeoff Considerations\", Luciano Rocha) into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by concept rather than by slide order.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: the FLEX/assumed-temperature concept in one line, the prohibited/limited/not-approved conditions, the three runway-length adjustment factors, and the worked-example result.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail: the assumed-temperature method and reverse-thrust-credit logic, every operating restriction, the interpolation rule, the complete worked takeoff-planning example (departure conditions through GO/NO-GO decision), and the Figure A-1 reference chart used to look up field length and V-speeds by weight.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was verified:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The worked example's intermediate results (headwind +123 ft, uphill slope -1,107 ft, inoperative ground spoilers -1,100 ft, adjusted runway length 4,066 ft, FLEX required field length 4,020 ft, \"ADF &gt; RFL = GO\") were checked arithmetically and against the 65,000 lb row of the source Figure A-1 chart - consistent, no corrections needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Figure A-1 (the full weight/temperature takeoff table) is reproduced as a reference image at the end of Part 2, since it is a multi-column numeric chart best read directly rather than retyped.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This is personal study material (not an excerpt of a CAE or other third-party proprietary course); always confirm current figures against the approved AFM/Performance Handbook before operational use.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"flex-takeoff--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--flex-takeoff---core-concept\">FLEX Takeoff - Core Concept</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Method</td>\n<td>\"Assumed temperature method\" - used when a takeoff EPR for actual ambient temperature is not required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum thrust used</td>\n<td>At least 75% of rated takeoff thrust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runway surface</td>\n<td>May be used on DRY or WET runway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EPR takeoff test interval</td>\n<td>Required every 100 flights or 100 hours, whichever occurs first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flex-takeoff--overview-concept---assumed-temperature-method\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Concept</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--restrictions-summary\">Restrictions Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Category</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Prohibited</td>\n<td>Runway contaminated with standing water, snow, slush, or ice; more than 25% of runway covered; contamination more than 0.125 in (3.2 mm) deep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limited</td>\n<td>Nil or uphill slopes only; no wind or headwind only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not Approved</td>\n<td>Use of Wing Anti-Icing bleed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground Spoilers</td>\n<td>Auto Ground Spoilers must be operative when using Flaps 10 degrees</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flex-takeoff--reducedflex-epr---prohibited\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Prohibited / Limited / Not Approved / Ground Spoilers</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--runway-length-adjustment-factors\">Runway Length Adjustment Factors</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Factor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Adjustment</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Headwind</td>\n<td>+2% of available field length per 5 kt of headwind (up to 40 kt)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uphill slope</td>\n<td>-18% of available field length per 1% of uphill slope (up to 2%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground spoilers inoperative</td>\n<td>-1,100 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crosswind &gt; 20 kt during takeoff roll</td>\n<td>+600 ft to required field length (fan speed limited &lt; 66% LP RPM until 20 kt forward speed, then slam acceleration to takeoff power required)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Data is valid for engine bleeds ON or OFF. Source: Figure A-1 notes.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flex-takeoff--adjusted-runway-length-calculation\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Adjusted Runway Length Calculation</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--worked-example---result-summary\">Worked Example - Result Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Takeoff weight / Flaps</td>\n<td>65,000 lb / Flaps 20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Available field length</td>\n<td>6,150 ft (dry, sea level, +20C, 5 kt headwind, 1.0% uphill, gnd spoilers INOP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjusted runway length</td>\n<td>4,066 ft (6,150 + 123 headwind - 1,107 slope - 1,100 spoilers-inop)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FLEX EPR / Assumed Temp</td>\n<td>1.47 / 45C (Rated EPR at 20C = 1.56)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V1 / Vr / V2 (FLEX)</td>\n<td>111 / 117 / 123 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Required field length (FLEX)</td>\n<td>4,020 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision</td>\n<td>ADF (4,066 ft) &gt; RFL (4,020 ft) = \"GO\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#flex-takeoff--worked-example---takeoff-planning\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Worked Example</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"flex-takeoff--part-2---full-detail\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL</h1>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--overview-concept---assumed-temperature-method\">Overview / Concept - Assumed Temperature Method</h2>\n<p>Reduced thrust (FLEX) takeoff is used when a takeoff EPR calculated for the actual ambient temperature is not required. Instead, an assumed (higher) temperature is used to compute a lower, \"flexed\" EPR - reducing engine wear while still guaranteeing at least 75% of rated takeoff thrust.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Referred to as the \"assumed temperature method.\"</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>At least 75% of rated takeoff thrust is used.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>May be used on a DRY or WET runway (subject to the reverse-thrust-credit differences below).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>An EPR takeoff test is required every 100 flights or 100 hours, whichever occurs first.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--reverse-thrust-credit---dry-vs.-wet-runway\">Reverse Thrust Credit - Dry vs. Wet Runway</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Runway</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Reverse Thrust Credit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dry</td>\n<td>No reverse thrust credit is taken in the accelerate-stop distance calculation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wet</td>\n<td>Reverse thrust IS credited in the accelerate-stop distance calculation. Maximum reverse thrust is used down to 80 KCAS, reverse idle by 60 KCAS, and reverse idle is maintained until full stop.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--reducedflex-epr---prohibited\">Reduced/FLEX EPR - Prohibited</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Runways contaminated with standing water, snow, slush, or ice.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>More than 25% of the runway is covered by contamination.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contamination is more than 0.125 inches (3.2 mm) deep.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"flex-takeoff--reducedflex-epr---limited\">Reduced/FLEX EPR - Limited</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>For nil or uphill slopes only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For no wind or headwind only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"flex-takeoff--reducedflex-epr---not-approved\">Reduced/FLEX EPR - Not Approved</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Use of Wing Anti-Icing bleed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"flex-takeoff--reducedflex-epr---ground-spoilers\">Reduced/FLEX EPR - Ground Spoilers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Auto Ground Spoilers must be operative when using Flaps 10 degrees.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--interpolation-rule\">Interpolation Rule</h2>\n<p>Interpolation between chart values is NOT recommended. Instead, always use the next conservative value:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Use the next HIGHER weight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use the next HIGHER ambient temperature.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use the next HIGHER airport pressure altitude.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use the next SHORTER field length.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--worked-example---takeoff-planning\">Worked Example - Takeoff Planning</h2>\n<p><strong>Departure Conditions:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Parameter</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Runway</td>\n<td>Dry, hard-surface runway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Available field length</td>\n<td>6,150 feet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airport pressure altitude</td>\n<td>Sea level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ambient temperature</td>\n<td>+20 degrees Celsius</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wind component</td>\n<td>5 kt headwind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runway slope</td>\n<td>1.0% uphill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Takeoff runway obstacles</td>\n<td>None</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Airplane Configuration:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Parameter</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Takeoff gross weight</td>\n<td>65,000 pounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flaps</td>\n<td>20 degrees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic ground spoilers</td>\n<td>INOPERATIVE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ECS</td>\n<td>ON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cowl anti-icing</td>\n<td>OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing anti-icing</td>\n<td>OFF</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Determine: adjusted runway length, and takeoff thrust and V-speeds.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--adjusted-runway-length-calculation\">Adjusted Runway Length Calculation</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Calculation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Starting field</td>\n<td>Available field length</td>\n<td>6,150 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wind correction</td>\n<td>Headwind correction: 5 kt = 2% | 6,150 x 2%</td>\n<td>+123 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slope correction</td>\n<td>Uphill slope correction: 1% = 18% | 6,150 x 18%</td>\n<td>-1,107 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground spoilers</td>\n<td>Auto ground spoiler correction: INOP</td>\n<td>-1,100 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjusted runway length</td>\n<td>6,150 + 123 - 1,107 - 1,100</td>\n<td>4,066 feet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--rated-epr-results-at-20c\">Rated EPR Results (at 20C)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Speed / Value</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>EPR</td>\n<td>1.56</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V1</td>\n<td>112 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vr</td>\n<td>117 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V2</td>\n<td>123 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vse</td>\n<td>157 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vref</td>\n<td>125 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max Temp</td>\n<td>55 degrees Celsius</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Field length (rated)</td>\n<td>3,600 feet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--maximum-temperature\">Maximum Temperature</h2>\n<p>MAX TEMP represents the highest allowable temperature for compliance with the limits of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Takeoff Climb</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum Brake Energy</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tire Speed</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Maximum Certified Temperature Limits</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>FLEX EPR Results:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Speed / Value</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>EPR</td>\n<td>1.47</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V1</td>\n<td>111 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vr</td>\n<td>117 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V2</td>\n<td>123 KCAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assumed Temp</td>\n<td>45 degrees Celsius</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjusted runway length</td>\n<td>4,066 feet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Required field length (FLEX)</td>\n<td>4,020 feet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Decision: ADF (Adjusted Field Length, 4,066 ft) &gt; RFL (Required Field Length, 4,020 ft) = \"GO\".</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"flex-takeoff--obstacle-clearance-verification\">Obstacle Clearance Verification</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Verify obstacle clearance based on the selected flap setting and the assumed temperature.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use the required field length as the accelerate-go reference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If obstacles are not cleared, reduce the assumed temperature by columns (re-enter the chart at a lower assumed temperature and recompute).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"flex-takeoff--figure-a-1---reference-chart\">Figure A-1 - Reference Chart</h1>\n<p>Reduced/FLEX and Rated EPR takeoff field length and V-speeds by takeoff weight (excerpt: 65,000-85,000 lb rows, dry hard-surface runway, sea level, Flaps 20, ECS ON, Cowl A/I OFF, Wing A/I OFF). Reproduced as an image because it is a dense multi-column numeric chart, best read directly rather than retyped. The 65,000 lb row matches the worked example above (FLEX field length 4,020 ft, Rated field length 3,600 ft).</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/flex-takeoff-1.jpg\"/></p>\n<p><em>Figure A-1 (source: Appendix A.pdf, personal study deck)</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"flex-takeoff--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>\"APPENDIX A.pdf\" - personal study deck (Keynote), Luciano Rocha - \"Reduced Thrust (FLEX) Takeoff Considerations,\" including Figure A-1 and a full worked takeoff-planning example.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This is personal study material, not an AFM/Performance Handbook excerpt. Always confirm current EPR, V-speed, and field-length figures against the approved G550 AFM/Performance Handbook before operational use.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","flex-takeoff--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","flex-takeoff--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","FLEX Takeoff - Core Concept","flex-takeoff--flex-takeoff---core-concept"],["h2","Restrictions Summary","flex-takeoff--restrictions-summary"],["h2","Runway Length Adjustment Factors","flex-takeoff--runway-length-adjustment-factors"],["h2","Worked Example - Result Summary","flex-takeoff--worked-example---result-summary"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL","flex-takeoff--part-2---full-detail"],["h2","Overview / Concept - Assumed Temperature Method","flex-takeoff--overview-concept---assumed-temperature-method"],["h2","Reverse Thrust Credit - Dry vs. Wet Runway","flex-takeoff--reverse-thrust-credit---dry-vs.-wet-runway"],["h2","Reduced/FLEX EPR - Prohibited","flex-takeoff--reducedflex-epr---prohibited"],["h2","Interpolation Rule","flex-takeoff--interpolation-rule"],["h2","Worked Example - Takeoff Planning","flex-takeoff--worked-example---takeoff-planning"],["h2","Adjusted Runway Length Calculation","flex-takeoff--adjusted-runway-length-calculation"],["h2","Rated EPR Results (at 20C)","flex-takeoff--rated-epr-results-at-20c"],["h2","Maximum Temperature","flex-takeoff--maximum-temperature"],["h2","Obstacle Clearance Verification","flex-takeoff--obstacle-clearance-verification"],["h1","Figure A-1 - Reference Chart","flex-takeoff--figure-a-1---reference-chart"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","flex-takeoff--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"door-system":{"t":"Door System Study Guide","sub":"Main Entrance Door, Emergency Exits, Baggage Doors, Tail Compartment & Service Doors · Consolidated from the Pilot Training Handbook - Systems, Section 2, \"Doors System\" · Rev. 5.68 - Sep. 2023 | used for personal study ","html":"<h1 id=\"door-system--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide converts the \"Doors System\" chapter (Section 2) of the Gulfstream G550 Pilot Training Handbook - Systems (FlightSafety International, Rev. 5.68, Sep. 2023) into a searchable, color-coded reference, organized by door/subsystem rather than by page order. The source material was consolidated and reorganized for personal study reference — it is NOT a substitute for the current AFM/AOM or the Pilot Training Handbook itself, and it should not be used for operational decision-making.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: door types, opening/closing methods, cockpit switch functions, and hard limitations in one place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Sub-Topic: general description, Main Entrance Door (hydraulic AUX system, free-fall behavior, manual backup), Emergency Exits and Removable Cabin Windows, Baggage Doors System (exterior and interior), Tail Compartment Door and Service Doors, Cockpit Controls and Indications, the DOORS synoptic / MAU monitoring, Normal System Operations flow, Abnormal/Emergency Operations, and Limitations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figures and diagrams:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The source contains photographs of the switch panels, the DOORS synoptic page, and interior/exterior door photos. These are described in text within the relevant Part 2 sections rather than reproduced as images.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No image-extraction tooling was used for this guide — all figure content below is a written description of what the source diagram/photo shows, not a direct reproduction.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"door-system--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--door-types-overview\">Door Types Overview</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Door</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Pressurized Section?</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Primary Purpose</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main Entrance Door</td>\n<td>Yes - sealed</td>\n<td>Normal passenger/crew access; also a Primary emergency exit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Removable Cabin Windows (x4)</td>\n<td>Yes - sealed</td>\n<td>Primary emergency exits (aft two windows each side)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior Baggage Door</td>\n<td>Yes - pneumatic seal</td>\n<td>Baggage/equipment loading; Secondary emergency exit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior Baggage Compartment Door</td>\n<td>Yes - acts as secondary pressure bulkhead</td>\n<td>Access from cabin to baggage compartment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tail Compartment Door</td>\n<td>No - unpressurized</td>\n<td>Access to tail compartment (preflight inspection)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service Doors (Radome, Ext. AC/DC Power, Oxygen, Hydraulic, etc.)</td>\n<td>No - unpressurized, ground use only</td>\n<td>Servicing/inspection access; monitored on Doors synoptic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> All doors are monitored by the Modular Avionics Units (MAU) and provide flight-deck indications any time a door is not closed and locked/latched. Doors in unpressurized sections have no seals and are restricted to ground operations.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#door-system--overview-general-description\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / General Description</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--main-entrance-door-quick-facts\">Main Entrance Door Quick Facts</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detail</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Opening</td>\n<td>Normally opened manually (gravity-assisted, slow open)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td>Normally closed by hydraulic pressure from the AUX system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Free-fall (after AUX-powered close)</td>\n<td>Trapped hydraulic fluid allows the door to gently free-fall outward until fully extended</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Free-fall (after manual close, no AUX pressure)</td>\n<td>No trapped fluid - door falls via its own weight until it hits the ground; extreme care required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manual backup (no AUX pressure)</td>\n<td>Door must be manually lifted into position and locked - requires multiple persons due to weight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground clearance</td>\n<td>Door/stairway usable with any combination of landing gear strut and tire inflation, no ground equipment needed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#door-system--main-entrance-door\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Main Entrance Door</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--emergency-exits-quick-reference\">Emergency Exits Quick Reference</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Type</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Exits</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary</td>\n<td>Main Entrance Door + four (4) Removable Cabin Windows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secondary</td>\n<td>Exterior Baggage Door (interior Baggage Door must be opened first from inside the cabin)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gear-up landing or ditching at sea</td>\n<td>Removable Cabin Windows are the primary exits - Main Entrance Door and/or Exterior Baggage Door may be unavailable (inadequate ground clearance if gear collapsed; may be below water line or blocked in a ditching)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#door-system--emergency-exits\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Emergency Exits / Removable Cabin Windows</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--baggage-doors-quick-reference\">Baggage Doors Quick Reference</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Door</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Key Facts</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior Baggage Door</td>\n<td>Inward-opening \"plug type,\" left aft fuselage; pneumatically inflated seal when closed/locked; operable from inside or outside</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior Baggage Compartment Door</td>\n<td>Slides left/right on tracks; seals baggage compartment from cabin; acts as secondary pressure bulkhead if primary bulkhead fails - cannot be opened while performing that duty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Altitude note</td>\n<td>Access above 40,000 ft limited to essential requirements (negates secondary-bulkhead protection) - see Limitations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#door-system--baggage-doors-system\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Baggage Doors System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--cockpit-controls-quick-reference\">Cockpit Controls Quick Reference</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Control</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Location</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Door Close Switchlight</td>\n<td>Miscellaneous Switch Panel (COP)</td>\n<td>Push IN: AUX pump starts, Main Entrance Door closes; ON caption extinguishes when closed/locked. Rarely used - stairs not fully visible from cockpit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Door Safety Switchlight</td>\n<td>Miscellaneous Switch Panel (COP)</td>\n<td>OUT (normal): electrically enables door close from inside/outside switches. IN: disconnects power from door system (maintenance use) - door will not close.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main Cabin Door Switch</td>\n<td>Aft bulkhead of LEER, near Main Cabin Door</td>\n<td>Normal switch used to close the Main Entrance Door; magnetically held CLOSE until closed/locked, then auto-returns to OFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outside Door Switch</td>\n<td>Forward External Switch Panel</td>\n<td>Closes Main Entrance Door from outside; no need to turn on External Battery Switch to operate it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#door-system--cockpit-controls-and-indications\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Cockpit Controls and Indications</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--limitations-quick-reference\">Limitations Quick Reference</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limitation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal baggage door, general</td>\n<td>Must remain closed above 40,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access above 40,000 ft</td>\n<td>Permitted, provided door is closed after exiting the compartment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time with door open above 40,000 ft</td>\n<td>Limited to five (5) minutes - crew must ensure door closed and message extinguished within 5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single ECS pack operation</td>\n<td>Baggage compartment access allowed only at or below 45,000 ft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#door-system--limitations\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Limitations</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"door-system--part-2---full-detail-by-sub-topic\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUB-TOPIC</h1>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--overview-general-description\">Overview / General Description</h2>\n<p>The G550 is equipped with doors for normal and emergency egress as well as for access to the pressurized and unpressurized sections of the fuselage.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Doors installed in the pressurized section are equipped with seals that maintain the aircraft's pressurization integrity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Doors installed in the unpressurized section have no seals and their use is restricted to ground operations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>All doors are monitored by the Modular Avionics Units (MAU) and provide indications to the flight deck any time a door is not closed and locked or latched.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--main-entrance-door\">Main Entrance Door</h2>\n<p>The Main Entrance Door is the primary means of aircraft access for passengers and crew. The door structure contains an attached folding stairway with hand rails. The door is capable of allowing entrance and exit with any combination of landing gear strut inflation and tire inflation, without support of ground equipment.</p>\n<p><strong>Opening and closing logic:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The door is normally opened manually, and closed by hydraulic pressure from the AUX (auxiliary hydraulic) system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the door was previously closed via the normal AUX hydraulic method, trapped hydraulic fluid within the system allows the door to gently free-fall outward until the door and stairway are fully extended.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Without AUX hydraulic system pressure available, the door must be manually lifted into position where it can be locked once closed - this requires the efforts of multiple persons due to the weight of the door.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the door was closed manually (without the aid of AUX hydraulic pressure), there is no trapped hydraulic fluid to allow a gentle free-fall on the next opening.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Warning:</strong> Extreme care must be taken when opening a manually-closed Main Entrance Door (no trapped hydraulic fluid) - the door will fall via its own weight until it hits the ground.</em></p>\n<p><em>Figure note (p. 2-13): the source includes an exterior photograph of the Main Entrance Door fully open, showing the folding stairway with integrated hand rails extended down to ground level.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--emergency-exits\">Emergency Exits</h2>\n<p><strong>There are two types of emergency exits on the G550: Primary and Secondary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Primary emergency exits: the Main Entrance Door and four Removable Cabin Windows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Secondary emergency exit: the exterior Baggage Door. The interior Baggage Door must first be opened to gain access to the baggage compartment and the Exterior Baggage Door from inside the passenger compartment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Gear-up landing or ditching at sea:</strong></p>\n<p>The primary emergency exits for a gear-up forced landing or a ditching at sea are the Removable Cabin Windows. The Main Entrance Door and/or Exterior Baggage Door may not be available in these scenarios:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If a gear is collapsed, the Main Entrance Door may not have adequate ground clearance to open fully.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In a ditching at sea, both doors may be below the water line or blocked.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"door-system--removable-cabin-windows\">Removable Cabin Windows</h3>\n<p>The G550 has four Removable Cabin Windows - the aft two windows on each side of the passenger cabin. Their primary purpose is to act as emergency exits.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Opened from inside by pulling down on a red T-handle near the top of the window, which unlatches it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The window is then pulled inward using two grip handles on each side to expose the opening to the outside.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Can also be opened from the outside via escape window release panels located below each window on the exterior fuselage, to aid rescue by ground personnel.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Figure note (p. 2-16): the source shows an interior photo of an oval Removable Cabin Window with a red \"Interior Emergency Release Handle\" (T-handle) mounted beside it and an illuminated \"EXIT\" placard.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--baggage-doors-system\">Baggage Doors System</h2>\n<p>The Baggage Doors System provides access to the baggage compartment from both the exterior of the aircraft and from within the main cabin, for loading and unloading baggage and equipment.</p>\n<p><strong>Exterior Baggage Door</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>An inward-opening \"plug type\" door located on the left aft portion of the fuselage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When closed and locked, a pneumatically inflated door seal fills the area between the door and fuselage structure, allowing the cabin to be pressurized and climate controlled for flight.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Can be opened from the inside or the outside.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interior Baggage Compartment Door</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Provides access to the baggage compartment from the main cabin and can completely seal the compartment from the main cabin.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Also acts as part of the secondary pressure bulkhead should the aircraft's primary pressure bulkhead fail - the baggage compartment would then become unpressurized while the main cabin remains pressurized, with this door now providing the main duty of a pressure bulkhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In the rare instance where this door is acting as the aircraft's primary pressure bulkhead, it will NOT be possible to open the door due to cabin differential pressure forces.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The door slides left and right on integral tracks to open and close.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Access to the baggage compartment is available during all phases of flight; however, entrance should be limited to essential requirements above 40,000 ft, since opening the door negates its safety feature as secondary pressure bulkhead.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Figure notes: p. 2-14 shows the Exterior Baggage Door closed, viewed from inside the baggage compartment (placards, handle, and inspection hardware visible on the door face). p. 2-15 shows the Interior Baggage Compartment Door closed, viewed from the aft tail-cone/baggage compartment, with an Inspection Viewport called out.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--tail-compartment-door\">Tail Compartment Door</h2>\n<p>The Tail Compartment Door provides access to the tail compartment, an unpressurized area of the aircraft not accessible from the cabin.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Attached to the door is a folding ladder allowing access to the tail compartment for preflight inspection of the components inside.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The telescoping extension ladder may also be equipped with a winch and work platform, and may be removed from its telescoping mountings for use as a stand-alone platform for servicing/maintenance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"door-system--service-doors\">Service Doors</h3>\n<p>The G550 has a number of Service Doors located throughout the aircraft to access various subsystems and for inspecting internal systems. The following Service Doors are monitored by the MAUs and have position indications on the Doors synoptic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Radome</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>External AC Power</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>External DC Power</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Oxygen Service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hydraulic Service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Forward External Switch Panel</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel Service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Lavatory Service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Water Service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tail Compartment Door</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--cockpit-controls-and-indications\">Cockpit Controls and Indications</h2>\n<p><em>Figure note: the source shows the Miscellaneous Switch Panel (COP), a small guarded four-switch panel with (left to right) DOOR CLOSE, DOOR SAFETY, LDG GR DUMP V, and FDR/CMC EVENT switchlights. Only the first two relate to the Doors System (the latter two relate to landing gear dump valve and the flight data recorder/CMC event marker, respectively).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Door Close Switchlight (COP)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Plastic guarded, momentary-action switchlight that can be used to close the Main Entrance Door.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Not often used, because the Main Entrance Door cannot be fully seen from the cockpit to ensure no persons are on the stairs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When pushed IN: the ON caption illuminates, the AUX pump starts, and the Main Entrance Door closes. When the door is fully closed and locked, the ON caption extinguishes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Door Safety Switchlight (COP)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>OUT (normal position): the aircraft's electrical system powers the Main Entrance Door's operating system and allows the door to close by either the internal or external switches.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IN (pressed): the aircraft's electrical system is disconnected from the door's operating system, and the ON caption illuminates - the door will NOT close by either the inside or outside switches. Generally used only during maintenance, to ensure the door doesn't inadvertently close.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Main Cabin Door Switch</strong></p>\n<p>Located near the Main Cabin Door on the aft bulkhead of the LEER, this is the switch normally used to close the Main Entrance Door. When the guard is raised and the switch is moved to CLOSE, the AUX pump starts and the Main Entrance Door closes. The switch is magnetically held in CLOSE until the door closes; when the door is closed and locked, the AUX pump stops and the switch automatically moves back to OFF.</p>\n<p><strong>Outside Door Switch</strong></p>\n<p>Located in the Forward External Switch Panel; when moved to CLOSE, the AUX pump starts and the Main Entrance Door closes. Magnetically held in CLOSE until the door closes, then auto-returns to OFF. There is no need to turn ON the External Battery Switch to operate the door - by design, to preclude accidentally leaving the External Battery Switch ON and depleting the batteries.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Pilot Note:</strong> Personal reminder (pilot annotation on source p. 2-11): do NOT turn on the batteries when closing the Main Entrance Door with the Outside Door Switch - it is not required and only risks battery depletion.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--mau-monitoring-and-the-doors-synoptic\">MAU Monitoring and the DOORS Synoptic</h2>\n<p>All doors are monitored by the Modular Avionics Units (MAU), which drive position indications on the DOORS synoptic page and generate flight-deck indications any time a door is not closed and locked/latched.</p>\n<p><em>Figure note (p. 2-12): the source shows a \"DOORS (2/3) Synoptic (All doors OPEN)\" example - a schematic aircraft outline (viewed from above) with labeled position indicators for: Radome, Nose Wheel Well, Exterior Switch, External Power, Main Cabin, Forward Lavatory, Oxygen Service, Fuel Service, Wheel Well (both sides), Aft Water, Interior Baggage Compartment, Forward Cowl (both engines), APU, External Baggage, Engine Access (both sides), Hydraulic Service, Aft Lavatory, and Tail Compartment. The \"(2/3)\" label indicates the DOORS synoptic normally pages through more than one screen of door groups; only one example page is included in this excerpt.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"door-system--normal-system-operations\">Normal System Operations</h3>\n<p><strong>A normal flight sequence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Departure: flight crew opens the Main Entrance Door by unlatching the Main Cabin Door exterior handle and letting the door slowly gravity-open. During preflight, the Tail Compartment Door is opened next and the ladder lowered for the tail compartment preflight inspection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Upon entry to the main cabin, the Interior Baggage Door is opened to access the compartment so the Exterior Baggage Door can be opened.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>As passengers arrive and baggage is loaded: Exterior Baggage Door is closed and locked, then the Interior Baggage Door is closed and latched.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After all passengers have enplaned and the stairs are verified clear, the Main Cabin Door is closed with the interior Main Cabin Door switch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After starting engines, the DOORS synoptic is checked to verify all doors have been closed and locked.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Arrival: the procedure is essentially reversed, until the Main Entrance Door is closed with the Outside Door Switch.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"door-system--abnormal-and-emergency-operations\">Abnormal and Emergency Operations</h3>\n<p>Abnormal and Emergency operation of the Doors System can be found in the AFM Quick Reference Procedures tab labeled \"Miscellaneous.\" This information can also be found in the OM chapter 2A-52-00.</p>\n<h2 id=\"door-system--limitations\">Limitations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limitation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal baggage door, general rule</td>\n<td>Shall remain closed above 40,000 feet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access above 40,000 ft</td>\n<td>Permitted, provided the door is closed after exiting the compartment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time above 40,000 ft with door open</td>\n<td>Limited to five (5) minutes - flight crew must ensure the internal baggage door is closed and the associated message is extinguished within 5 minutes of opening the door</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single ECS pack operation</td>\n<td>Access to the baggage compartment allowed only at or below 45,000 feet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h1 id=\"door-system--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>DOOR SYSTEM.pdf - excerpt from the Gulfstream G550 Pilot Training Handbook - Systems (FlightSafety International), Section 2, \"Doors System,\" Rev. 5.68, Sep. 2023 (18 pages) - used for personal study with the owner's authorization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This guide consolidates and reorganizes training-handbook material for personal study convenience. It is explicitly marked \"Training Only\" by the source publisher. Always refer to the current G550 AFM/AOM and other approved GAC manuals - and the AFM Quick Reference Procedures \"Miscellaneous\" tab / OM 2A-52-00 for abnormal and emergency door procedures - when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","door-system--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","door-system--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Door Types Overview","door-system--door-types-overview"],["h2","Main Entrance Door Quick Facts","door-system--main-entrance-door-quick-facts"],["h2","Emergency Exits Quick Reference","door-system--emergency-exits-quick-reference"],["h2","Baggage Doors Quick Reference","door-system--baggage-doors-quick-reference"],["h2","Cockpit Controls Quick Reference","door-system--cockpit-controls-quick-reference"],["h2","Limitations Quick Reference","door-system--limitations-quick-reference"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUB-TOPIC","door-system--part-2---full-detail-by-sub-topic"],["h2","Overview / General Description","door-system--overview-general-description"],["h2","Main Entrance Door","door-system--main-entrance-door"],["h2","Emergency Exits","door-system--emergency-exits"],["h2","Baggage Doors System","door-system--baggage-doors-system"],["h2","Tail Compartment Door","door-system--tail-compartment-door"],["h2","Cockpit Controls and Indications","door-system--cockpit-controls-and-indications"],["h2","MAU Monitoring and the DOORS Synoptic","door-system--mau-monitoring-and-the-doors-synoptic"],["h2","Limitations","door-system--limitations"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","door-system--appendix---sources-consulted"]]},"lighting-system":{"t":"Lighting System Study Guide","sub":"Flight Compartment - Passenger Compartment - Cargo/Service - Exterior - Emergency · Consolidated from the Pilot Training Handbook - Systems, Section 3, \"Lighting System\" · Rev. 5.68 - Sep. 2023 | used for personal study ","html":"<h1 id=\"lighting-system--how-this-guide-is-organized\">How This Guide Is Organized</h1>\n<p>This guide consolidates and reorganizes the G550 \"Pilot Training Handbook - Systems,\" Section 3, \"Lighting System\" (Rev. 5.68, Sep. 2023) into a searchable, color-coded reference, used for personal study with the owner’s authorization. It is not a substitute for the current AFM/AOM or Pilot Training Handbook — always confirm switch logic and procedures against the current approved manuals.</p>\n<p><strong>Two layers of content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Part 1 - Quick-Reference Tables: every switch, knob, and operating logic point, grouped by subsystem, for fast lookup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Part 2 - Full Detail by Subsystem: the five subsystems the source itself defines — Flight Compartment Lighting System, Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs, Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System, Exterior Lighting System, and Emergency Lighting System.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What was captured:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>All fourteen-plus flight compartment control knobs (Master Lighting Control, Overhead Panel, CB Panels, Pedestal Panel, Overhead Floodlight, Left/Right Console, Left/Right Glareshield-Flight Panel, Left/Right Console Flood, Pilot/Copilot Map Lights, and the Display Unit dimming knobs), with the specific press-in/push-out switchlight logic for every exterior light and cabin sign.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Operational logic and cautions called out in the source: the Master Control Knob’s OFF/night/ORIDE detent behavior, its exclusive brightness control over standby instruments when operating on E-Batts only, Pulse light deactivation logic, Landing/Taxi light altitude- and gear-position-based automatic extinguish/re-illuminate logic, the Emergency Lighting System’s automatic-activation-on-low-voltage logic, and the serial-number-dependent switch locations for the Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The source explicitly states there are no limitations established for the Lighting System — carried forward as-is.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"lighting-system--part-1---quick-reference-tables\">PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES</h1>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--overview\">Overview</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Item</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Value</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Internal and external illumination for individual systems and self-illuminating signs (excludes warning lights)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Five subsystems</td>\n<td>Flight Compartment; Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs; Cargo and Service Compartment; Exterior; Emergency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limitations</td>\n<td>None established for the Lighting System</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source</td>\n<td>Pilot Training Handbook - Systems, Section 3, Rev. 5.68 - Sep. 2023</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#lighting-system--overview-purpose\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Overview / Purpose</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--flight-compartment-lighting-system\">Flight Compartment Lighting System</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Knob / Switch</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Master Lighting Control</td>\n<td>Master control for annunciator, instrument/overlay, cockpit overhead floodlights, flight panel/glareshield, side console + flood, and SFD menu button lighting; OFF = daylight config (all full bright); CW past min-brightness detent = night config; full CW ORIDE = illuminates overhead + console floodlights</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead Panel (OVHD PNL) - PNL / LTS stack</td>\n<td>PNL: panel edgelighting, indicator dials, LCD backlights; LTS: switchlight brightness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CB Panels</td>\n<td>Edgelighting brightness for POP and CPOP circuit breaker panels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestal Panel (PED PNL) - PNL / LTS stack</td>\n<td>PNL: panel edgelighting, indicator dials, LCD backlights; LTS: switchlight brightness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead Floodlight</td>\n<td>Flood-type bulb aft of COP/CPOP circuit breaker panels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Console Knob</td>\n<td>Edgelights and dial face lights for each console panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Glareshield-Flight Panel - PNL / LTS stack</td>\n<td>PNL: edgelighting/LCDs; LTS: annunciator lights in glareshield and flight panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Console Flood</td>\n<td>Fluorescent-style flood light above each console panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pilot / Copilot Map Lights - YOKE / OVHEAD stack</td>\n<td>YOKE: yoke-mounted map light (ON/OFF button on inboard yoke horn); OVHEAD: adjustable overhead map light</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DU 1&amp;2 / DU 3&amp;4 dimming</td>\n<td>Dual-stack knobs dim Display Units 1-4; dimming will NOT turn a DU completely off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Annunciator Lights Test (ANN TEST)</td>\n<td>Momentary, System Test Panel; excludes Fire Handles, Fuel Control Switches, Oxygen Panel, and (pre-SN 5183) CVR Panel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> 10 o’clock tick mark on the lighting knobs = recommended starting position for night conditions. On E-Batts only, the Master Control Knob has exclusive brightness control over both cockpit clocks, ACP 1, SFD, EBDI, Ldg gear panel, brake accumulator gauge, and MCDU 1 &amp; 3 keyboards.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#lighting-system--flight-compartment-lighting-system-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Flight Compartment Lighting System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--passenger-compartment-lighted-panels-and-signs\">Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Switch</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Logic</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Seat Belt Sign</td>\n<td>Press IN: ON caption illuminates, Seat Belt signage at cabin locations illuminates. Push OUT: caption/signage extinguish.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No Smoking Sign</td>\n<td>Press IN: ON caption illuminates, No Smoking signage at cabin locations illuminates. Push OUT: caption/signage extinguish.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#lighting-system--passenger-compartment-lighted-panels-and-signs-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--cargo-and-service-compartment-lighting-system\">Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Area</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Purpose</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Baggage compartment</td>\n<td>Lighting for loading / unloading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tail compartment</td>\n<td>Lighting for inspection and maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lavatory service panel</td>\n<td>Servicing lighting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Water service panel</td>\n<td>Servicing lighting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single point refueling</td>\n<td>Refueling panel lighting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Switch location for these systems depends on aircraft serial number and varies slightly in operation.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#lighting-system--cargo-and-service-compartment-lighting-system-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--exterior-lighting-system\">Exterior Lighting System</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Light</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Key Logic</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beacon (BCN)</td>\n<td>Single light, bottom fuselage between main gear doors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strobe</td>\n<td>Wing tips + tail cone; 2 tubes each, only 1 active; auto-switch on tube failure + fault indicator visible in preflight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Navigation (NAV)</td>\n<td>Wing tips (2 bulbs each) + upper tail cone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ice Inspection (ICE)</td>\n<td>Fuselage over wings; shines down on leading edges for contamination check + night visibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logo (optional)</td>\n<td>Bottom of each horizontal stabilizer, illuminates both sides of vertical stabilizer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ramp</td>\n<td>Listed among exterior lights in the general description; no dedicated switchlight page in this source excerpt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Landing (L LDG / R LDG)</td>\n<td>Independent switches; auto-extinguish climbing through 18,000 ft, auto-activate descending through 17,700 ft if left ON; OUT = deactivated regardless of altitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pulse (optional)</td>\n<td>Landing Lights alternately flash 1 pulse/sec; deactivated by selecting L &amp; R Landing Lights ON (steady); pushing Pulse OUT = steady if Ldg Lts ON, else deactivated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Taxi</td>\n<td>3 lights on nose gear strut; requires gear down and locked; auto extinguish/re-illuminate with gear position; OUT = deactivated regardless of gear position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wing Tip Taxi (Wing Tip/Flood)</td>\n<td>Wingtip + underwing bulb; ground-obstacle awareness; wingtip extends ~5 ft beyond lighted circle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheel Well</td>\n<td>Nose + both main gear wells; preflight visibility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#lighting-system--exterior-lighting-system-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Exterior Lighting System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--emergency-lighting-system\">Emergency Lighting System</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Component / Logic</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detail</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Components</td>\n<td>Overwing Egress Lights, Underwing Egress Lights, Emergency Lighting Battery Packs (E-Batts), Emergency Lighting Control Switches, Main Entrance Door Emergency Lighting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto-activation</td>\n<td>Whenever Essential DC Buses sense &lt; 20 V and the system is ARMED</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crew alert</td>\n<td>Blue CAS advisory message (\"E-Batts ON\") — easy to miss among amber/red messages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Re-arm</td>\n<td>Re-arm after any power interruption; system deactivates and is armed for the next interruption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total electrical failure scenario</td>\n<td>Dual generator + APU + HMG failure + both aircraft batteries failed: approach flown on standby instruments powered by E-Batts only; Master Control Knob is the only knob that brightens cockpit clocks in this scenario</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Switchlight detail for the Emergency Lighting System is covered in the Electrical System chapter, not this excerpt.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#lighting-system--emergency-lighting-system-1\"><em><strong><u>-&gt; Full detail: Emergency Lighting System</u></strong></em></a></p>\n<h1 id=\"lighting-system--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem\">PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM</h1>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--overview-purpose\">Overview / Purpose</h2>\n<p>The Lighting System provides internal and external illumination (excluding warning lights) for individual systems and self-illuminating signs. It is divided into five subsystems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Flight Compartment Lighting System</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs System</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Exterior Lighting System</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency Lighting System</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>There are no limitations established for the Lighting System.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--flight-compartment-lighting-system-1\">Flight Compartment Lighting System</h2>\n<p>Provides direct and indirect lighting of the flight deck area. The system incorporates fourteen control knobs that vary the intensity of: Annunciator Lights, Instrument Lights, Overhead Panel Lights, Side Console Lights, Flight Panel and Glareshield Lights, Overhead Lights, Yoke Map Lights, plus a single Master Lighting Control knob.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--master-control-knob\">Master Control Knob</h3>\n<p>Provides master lighting control of: Annunciator Lighting, Instrument and Overlay Lighting, Cockpit Overhead Floodlights, Side Console Lighting and Flood Lighting, Flight Panel and Glareshield Lighting, and the Standby Flight Display (SFD) Menu Button.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Full counterclockwise (OFF): the other 4 knobs on the Cockpit Lights Control Panel are unpowered — cockpit lighting is configured for daylight flying (all annunciator lighting, LCDs, and backlit indicators fully bright).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Clockwise from OFF to the minimum-brightness detent: powers the other 4 knobs and configures affected cockpit lighting for night flying — provided those knobs are also at their minimum-brightness detents.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Further clockwise rotation past the minimum-brightness detent: increases brightness of all affected cockpit lights up to full brightness.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Full clockwise rotation to the ORIDE detent: illuminates the cockpit overhead floodlights and console floodlights.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>On E-Batts only: the knob has exclusive brightness control over both cockpit clocks, ACP 1, SFD, EBDI, Ldg gear panel, brake accumulator gauge, and MCDU 1 &amp; 3 keyboards.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The small \"tick\" mark at the 10 o’clock position on the Master Control Knob is the recommended starting position for night conditions.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--cockpit-lights-control-panel-knobs\">Cockpit Lights Control Panel Knobs</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Knob</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead Panel (OVHD PNL) - bottom knob \"PNL\"</td>\n<td>Edgelighting, indicator dials, and LCD backlights for the Cockpit Overhead Panel (COP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead Panel (OVHD PNL) - top knob \"LTS\"</td>\n<td>Brightness of the various switchlights on the Cockpit Overhead Panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CB Panels</td>\n<td>Edgelighting brightness for circuit breaker panels above the Pilot’s Overhead Panel (POP) and Copilot’s Overhead Panel (CPOP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestal Panel (PED PNL) - bottom knob \"PNL\"</td>\n<td>Edgelighting, indicator dials, and LCD backlights for the Center Pedestal Panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestal Panel (PED PNL) - top knob \"LTS\"</td>\n<td>Brightness of the various switchlights on the Center Pedestal Panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead Floodlight</td>\n<td>Flood-type light bulb located just aft of the COP and CPOP circuit breaker panels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> All Cockpit Lights Control Panel knobs share the same 10 o’clock tick-mark convention as the recommended night-flying starting position.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--left-right-side-console-lighting-panels\">Left / Right Side Console Lighting Panels</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Knob</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Function</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Console Knob</td>\n<td>Edgelights and dial face lights for the corresponding console panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Glareshield-Flight Panel - bottom \"PNL\"</td>\n<td>Edgelighting and LCDs for the corresponding half of the glareshield/flight panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Glareshield-Flight Panel - top \"LTS\"</td>\n<td>Annunciator lights located in the glareshield and flight panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Left / Right Console Flood</td>\n<td>Fluorescent-style flood light located above the corresponding console panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pilot / Copilot Map Lights - bottom \"YOKE\"</td>\n<td>Lighting on the bottom of the control yokes; ON/OFF button on the inboard horn of each pilot’s control yoke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pilot / Copilot Map Lights - top \"OVHEAD\"</td>\n<td>Adjustable overhead map light, positioned to shine where needed by the flight crew</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--display-units-du-lighting-panel\">Display Units (DU) Lighting Panel</h3>\n<p>Located on the Center Pedestal Panel.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>DU 1 and DU 2 Knob - dual-stack: bottom knob = Display Unit #1 brightness, top knob = Display Unit #2 brightness.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>DU 3 and DU 4 Knob - dual-stack: bottom knob = Display Unit #3 brightness, top knob = Display Unit #4 brightness.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> DU dimming will NOT turn a Display Unit completely off.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--annunciator-lights-test-system-test-panel\">Annunciator Lights Test (System Test Panel)</h3>\n<p>Momentary-action switchlight (ANN TEST) — while pressed IN and held, all annunciator lights throughout the cockpit remain illuminated. The following are NOT tested with this switch:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Fire Handles</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fuel Control Switches</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Oxygen Panel on the right side console</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Cockpit Voice Recorder Panel on the right side console (on SN 5183 and later, the CVR panel on the right console is removed)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--normal-operation\">Normal Operation</h3>\n<p>For night and low-light operations, set each lighting panel per individual needs. As ambient lighting conditions change, only the Master Control Knob needs to be adjusted — all cockpit lighting changes accordingly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--passenger-compartment-lighted-panels-and-signs-1\">Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs</h2>\n<p>Controls the lighted panels and signs for the Fasten Seat Belt and No Smoking annunciations located throughout the aircraft cabin. Both switches are on the same overhead \"Lighting Panel\" as the Exterior Lighting switches (PASS WARN section).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Switchlight</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Pressed IN</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Pushed OUT</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Seat Belt Sign</td>\n<td>ON caption illuminates; Seat Belt signage at the various main cabin locations illuminates</td>\n<td>ON caption extinguishes; lighted Seat Belt signage deactivates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No Smoking Sign</td>\n<td>ON caption illuminates; No Smoking signage at the various main cabin locations illuminates</td>\n<td>ON caption extinguishes; lighted No Smoking signage deactivates</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--cargo-and-service-compartment-lighting-system-1\">Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System</h2>\n<p>Provides lighting for loading and unloading of the baggage compartment, for inspection and maintenance in the tail compartment, lavatory service panels, water service panels, and for single point refueling.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Baggage compartment - loading/unloading lighting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tail compartment - inspection and maintenance lighting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Lavatory service panels - servicing lighting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Water service panels - servicing lighting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single point refueling - refueling panel lighting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Switch location for these systems depends on aircraft serial number and varies slightly in operation. Confirm exact switch locations for the specific tail number being flown.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--exterior-lighting-system-1\">Exterior Lighting System</h2>\n<p>Provides illumination and visibility while operating the aircraft in flight and on the ground. Composed of the Beacon Light, Strobe Lights, Navigation Lights, Ice Inspection Lights, Logo Lights, Ramp Lights, Landing Lights, Pulse Lights, Taxi Lights, Wing Tip Taxi Lights, and Wheel Well Lights. Most exterior light switchlights and the PASS WARN signage switches share a single overhead \"Lighting Panel.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--beacon-bcn\">Beacon (BCN)</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; the single Beacon light, located on the bottom fuselage between the main landing gear doors, is activated. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and the Beacon light deactivates.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--strobe\">Strobe</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; Strobe lights in each wing tip and the tail cone are activated. Each Strobe Light has two tubes, but only one operates at a time. If the active tube fails, the other tube begins operating automatically, and a circular-shaped fault indicator becomes visible inside the light assembly (visible during preflight inspection). Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and Strobe lights deactivate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--navigation-nav\">Navigation (NAV)</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; Nav lights in each wing tip (2 bulbs per wing tip) and the upper tail cone are activated. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and Nav lights deactivate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--ice-inspection-ice\">Ice Inspection (ICE)</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; Ice Inspection lights in the fuselage over the wings are activated. These lights shine down on the wings’ leading edges, enabling a pre-takeoff contamination check during low-light conditions, and increase the aircraft’s visual presence at night. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and Ice Inspection lights deactivate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--logo-optional\">Logo (optional)</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; Logo lights in the bottom surface of each horizontal stabilizer are activated, shining on both sides of the vertical stabilizer to illuminate the aircraft’s logo. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and Logo lights deactivate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--ramp\">Ramp</h3>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Ramp Lights are listed among the Exterior Lighting System components in the source’s general description, but this excerpt does not include a dedicated switchlight description page for them.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--landing-l-ldg-r-ldg\">Landing (L LDG / R LDG)</h3>\n<p>Left and Right Landing Lights are independent switchlights. Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; the Landing Light bulb located at each wing leading edge in the wing root fairing illuminates. If left ON, both Landing Lights automatically extinguish climbing through 18,000 ft, and automatically re-activate during descent through 17,700 ft. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes; Landing Lights are deactivated and stay deactivated regardless of altitude.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--pulse-optional\">Pulse (optional)</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; the Landing Lights will alternately flash at 1 pulse per second. When Pulse lighting is selected ON, the pulsing can also be deactivated by selecting the Left and Right Landing Lights to ON (they then operate steady). Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes; Landing Lights operate steadily if they have been selected ON — if the Landing Lights have not been selected ON when Pulse is pushed OUT, the Landing Lights are deactivated.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> Pulse light deactivation logic: selecting BOTH Landing Lights ON while Pulse is ON stops the flashing (lights go steady) without requiring Pulse to be switched off.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--taxi\">Taxi</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; the 3 identical Taxi lights located on the front of the steerable portion of the nose gear strut are activated — one bulb points straight ahead, two others point left and right of center. The nose gear must be down and locked for the Taxi Lights to operate: if the switchlight is pressed IN with the gear retracted, the Taxi Lights (and ON caption) extinguish, then re-illuminate upon gear extension. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes; Taxi lights are deactivated regardless of gear position.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--wing-tip-taxi-wing-tip-flood\">Wing Tip Taxi (Wing Tip / Flood)</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; Wingtip/Flood lights in the wing tip and on the bottom surface of the wing near the wing tip are activated. Designed to illuminate the taxi surface with two lighted circular shapes near the wing tip, for awareness of ground obstacles the wing tip might strike during taxi. The wingtip bulb shines forward; the bulb underneath the wing shines straight down. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and Wingtip/Flood lights deactivate.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> The wingtip/winglet actually extends about 5 feet further than the lighted circle shown on the taxi surface — Wing Tip Taxi Lights should NOT be used as the sole indication of wingtip clearance during taxi.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--wheel-well\">Wheel Well</h3>\n<p>Pressed IN: ON caption illuminates; lights inside the nose wheel well and both main gear wheel wells illuminate for preflight visibility of the wheel wells. Pushed OUT: ON caption extinguishes and Wheel Well lights deactivate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-system--emergency-lighting-system-1\">Emergency Lighting System</h2>\n<p>Provides lighting to facilitate a safe evacuation during an emergency in low-light conditions. Comprised of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Overwing Egress Lights</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Underwing Egress Lights</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency Lighting Battery Packs (E-Batts)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Emergency Lighting Control Switches</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Main Entrance Door Emergency Lighting</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> See the Electrical System chapter for information on the switchlights for the Emergency Lighting System — not included in this Lighting System excerpt.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--abnormal-and-emergency-operations\">Abnormal and Emergency Operations</h3>\n<p>The Emergency Lighting System activates automatically whenever the Essential DC Buses sense less than 20 volts AND the system is ARMED. A blue CAS advisory message alerts the crew that the E-Batts are now ON — but it can be easily missed, especially if the CAS is filled with amber and red messages. Simply re-arm the Emergency Lighting System after any power interruption; it will then deactivate and be armed for the next power interruption.</p>\n<p><em><strong>E-Batts-Only Scenario:</strong> In the unlikely event of a dual generator failure, APU failure, HMG failure, AND both aircraft batteries failing, the approach must be flown with standby instruments powered only by the E-Batts. If cockpit clocks are required for the approach in this scenario, the Master Control Knob is the only knob that will increase their brightness.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"lighting-system--limitations\">Limitations</h3>\n<p><em>There are no limitations established for the Lighting System.</em></p>\n<h1 id=\"lighting-system--appendix---sources-consulted\">Appendix - Sources Consulted</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>LIGHTING.pdf - excerpt from the G550 \"Pilot Training Handbook - Systems,\" Section 3, \"Lighting System\" (Rev. 5.68, Sep. 2023), 38 pages — used for personal study with the owner’s authorization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> This guide reorganizes training-handbook content by subsystem for personal study reference. It is not a substitute for the current G550 AFM/AOM or Pilot Training Handbook — always refer to the current approved GAC manuals when operating the aircraft.</em></p>\n","toc":[["h1","How This Guide Is Organized","lighting-system--how-this-guide-is-organized"],["h1","PART 1 - QUICK-REFERENCE TABLES","lighting-system--part-1---quick-reference-tables"],["h2","Overview","lighting-system--overview"],["h2","Flight Compartment Lighting System","lighting-system--flight-compartment-lighting-system"],["h2","Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs","lighting-system--passenger-compartment-lighted-panels-and-signs"],["h2","Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System","lighting-system--cargo-and-service-compartment-lighting-system"],["h2","Exterior Lighting System","lighting-system--exterior-lighting-system"],["h2","Emergency Lighting System","lighting-system--emergency-lighting-system"],["h1","PART 2 - FULL DETAIL BY SUBSYSTEM","lighting-system--part-2---full-detail-by-subsystem"],["h2","Overview / Purpose","lighting-system--overview-purpose"],["h2","Flight Compartment Lighting System","lighting-system--flight-compartment-lighting-system-1"],["h2","Passenger Compartment Lighted Panels and Signs","lighting-system--passenger-compartment-lighted-panels-and-signs-1"],["h2","Cargo and Service Compartment Lighting System","lighting-system--cargo-and-service-compartment-lighting-system-1"],["h2","Exterior Lighting System","lighting-system--exterior-lighting-system-1"],["h2","Emergency Lighting System","lighting-system--emergency-lighting-system-1"],["h1","Appendix - Sources Consulted","lighting-system--appendix---sources-consulted"]]}},"cards":{"aircraft-general-and-performance":[["What is the G550's max landing weight (MLW), max zero-fuel weight (MZFW), and max usable fuel?","MLW 75,300 lb; MZFW (FAA) 54,500 lb; max usable fuel 41,300 lb (~6,118 US gal). All confirmed consistent with the Limitations Study Guide."],["What is the G550's actual published wingspan, and what conflicting figure appears elsewhere in the source notes?","93 ft 6 in (28.50 m) per page 11 (\"WS.\") - matches the published spec and is treated as authoritative. Page 2 separately lists \"Span 97'3\"\", flagged as a probable notation error."],["What is the approach category and the corresponding weight basis?","Category C, based on approximately 75,300 lb."],["What is Vmca at sea level?","107 KCAS - matches the Limitations guide Vmc-Ground figure at sea level."],["How many oxygen bottles are installed, and what is the minimum voltage to start the APU?","2 oxygen bottles installed. Minimum 22 VDC to start the APU."],["What is the maximum tire ground speed and maximum demonstrated dry-runway crosswind?","Max tire ground speed 195 kt; max demonstrated dry-runway crosswind 28 kt."],["Below what battery voltage does the battery require a deep-cycle charge instead of a normal charge?","Below 7 VDC (minimum 7 VDC required for normal charging)."],["How far into the future do the IAS and altitude trend vectors on the PFD project?","6 seconds into the future."],["What happens to engine start attempts if both START MASTER and CRANK MASTER are selected ON simultaneously, and up to what %HP can the starter be re-engaged?","The engines will not start. The starter can be re-engaged up to 42% HP."],["What is the keep-out zone (LP spool %) during an engine run, and what engine model powers the G550?","66% to 80% LP (low-pressure spool). Engine model: Rolls-Royce BR710."],["What CAS message is generated by an air start attempted below 250 kt?","\"ASSISTED START\"."],["What is the minimum altitude window for a bleeds-off takeoff, and what is the single-pack maximum altitude in flight versus for pre-takeoff dispatch?","Bleeds-off takeoff: ground up to 1,500 ft AGL. Single-pack max altitude in flight: FL480. If dispatching with a single pack before takeoff: max altitude FL410 (more restrictive)."],["At what generator cold-start temperature threshold should generators be selected OFF for engine start, and why?","-10C or lower - select generators OFF to allow IDG oil to warm up."],["What are the PTU (Power Transfer Unit) auto-activation conditions?","LH hydraulic system pressure below 1,500 psi, RH system temperature below 104.4C, and at least 1 gallon of fluid trapped downstream of the check valve."],["What are the left and right hydraulic reservoir max quantities, and the accumulator pre-charge value?","Left reservoir 4.8 gal, Right reservoir 1.6 gal; accumulator pre-charge 1,200 psi at 70F/21C."],["Above what cabin altitude does auto anti-ice stop operating automatically, requiring manual selection?","Above 35,000 ft (both climb and cruise) - auto anti-ice does not operate automatically above that altitude and must be manually selected."],["What are the automatic anti-ice active altitude ranges during climb and during descent?","Climb: active from 1,500 ft AGL up to 35,000 ft MSL. Descent: active from 35,000 ft down to the ground."],["Under what two conditions does the pressurization system stay in / fail to enter FLIGHT mode?","Below 25,000 ft, pressurization stays in LANDING mode even after leveling off more than 3 minutes. Also, if no destination is entered on the MCDU, pressurization will not switch to FLIGHT mode."],["What time limit applies to accessing the aft baggage compartment door above 40,000 ft?","Only 5 minutes of access time above 40,000 ft."],["What color-codes distinguish essential-bus versus main-bus AC circuit breakers, and what does losing a generator in flight trigger?","RED circuit breakers = Essential bus; BLACK circuit breakers = Main bus. Losing a generator in flight de-powers the galley and triggers a load shed."],["Is takeoff permitted with only a single generator operating?","No - takeoff with a single generator is prohibited."],["What weight-and-balance figure appears on page 15 of the source notes, and why is it NOT the aircraft's MTOW?","70,400 lb with a 599.4 moment/arm - this is a specific worked weight-and-balance practice problem, not a limitation. The G550's actual MTOW is 91,000 lb per the Limitations Study Guide."]],"landing-gear-and-brakes":[["What type of landing gear does the G550 have?","Fully retractable, tricycle-type gear - one nose gear and two main gear, each with an oleo-pneumatic shock strut (dual wheels/tires), filled with MIL-H-5606 hydraulic fluid and pressurized with dry nitrogen."],["What is the correct strut extension range?","2 to 9 inches, and should be even with the opposite strut on the same gear."],["What are the nosewheel and main wheel tire pressures?","Nosewheel: 136 psi (20 lb, rated 225 mph). Main wheels: 199-207 psi min/max (82 lb each, rated 225 mph)."],["What protects the wheels from overheat/overpressure?","Four (4) fusible plugs per wheel melt at 390F to release tire pressure if overheated. One (1) safety plug per wheel deflates the tire if internal pressure exceeds 412.5 +/- 37.5 psi."],["What are Vlo (normal), Vlo (alternate), and Vle?","Vlo normal (raise/lower): 225 KCAS / 0.70 Mt. Vlo alternate (lower only, one-time use): 175 KCAS. Vle (gear extended, doors open or closed): 250 KCAS / 0.70 Mt."],["What is the maximum tire speed and the maximum altitude to operate/fly with the gear extended?","Maximum tire speed: 195 kt ground speed. Maximum altitude to operate the gear, or fly with it extended: 20,000 ft MSL."],["Is extending speed brakes with the gear extended in flight permitted?","No - prohibited. Speed brakes and landing gear extended together, in flight, is not approved."],["What electrical and hydraulic power is required for normal gear extension/retraction?","Electrical: L ESS DC and R MAIN DC. Hydraulic: normally the L Hydraulic System (engine-driven pump), or the PTU if that pump fails."],["What is the alternate (emergency) gear extension system, and is it reusable?","Two nitrogen bottles (nominally 3,100 +/- 50 psi at 70F; 150 in3 and 412 in3) in the nose gear wheel well port high-pressure nitrogen to the nose/main gear dump valves, extending the gear. It is a one-time-use system and does not close the gear doors."],["What is the Power Transfer Unit (PTU) used for?","It extends/retracts the gear if the L engine-driven hydraulic pump fails, and also helps retract the gear following a left engine failure after V1 (regulatory purpose)."],["What is the NWS handwheel steering range, and when does the overtravel indicator show?","80 (+/- 2) either direction; the overtravel indicator extends/raises at or above 84 and should not be visible in normal operation."],["What are the rudder-pedal nosewheel steering angles with NWS operative vs. failed?","NWS operative: Left 7 / Right 7. NWS failure: Left 16 / Right 16."],["How do the MLG and NLG WOW switches differ in ground logic?","MLG WOW switches (L/R) 'make' contact On-Ground (roller rides a cam that rotates to a low point after takeoff). The NLG WOW switch 'breaks' contact On-Ground (a microswitch, compressed = Ground, not compressed = Air)."],["What is the Combined WOW signal logic?","Combined WOW = Air mode only if BOTH L and R MLG WOW switches read Air; Combined WOW = Ground mode if EITHER (or both) L/R MLG WOW switch reads Ground."],["What must the crew do if a WOW FAULT CAS message appears after takeoff before retarding thrust levers?","Disarm the ground spoilers before retarding the thrust levers to idle - Combined WOW does not prevent ground spoiler deployment on a WOW FAULT, so failing to disarm them could cause the spoilers to deploy in flight, destroying lift."],["What triggers a WOW FAULT CAS message?","L and/or R MLG WOW switch = Air mode with speed < 50 kt, OR L and/or R MLG WOW switch = Ground mode with radar altitude > 147.5 ft."],["What hydraulic pressure and priority order supplies the normal brakes?","3,000 psi. Priority: (1) Normal - L Hydraulic System; (2) AUX pump or PTU if L Sys reservoir/engine-driven pump unavailable; (3) AUX pump only if L Sys is entirely unavailable."],["Describe emergency braking if there is no hydraulic pressure/fluid from the L Hydraulic System.","Accumulator pressure from the Parking/Emergency brake system provides 5 to 6 brake applications, applied simultaneously to all four wheel brakes, with NO anti-skid protection."],["What triggers the AUX pump auto-latch feature?","Brake pedals applied (>10) AND L Hydraulic System pressure < 1,500 psi - the AUX pump (if armed) is commanded ON automatically."],["What is the parking brake accumulator pre-charge and full charge pressure?","Pre-charged to 1,200 psi with nitrogen, hydraulically charged (by the AUX pump via the L Hydraulic System) to 3,000 psi."],["Down to what speed is anti-skid protection provided, and what happens below it?","10 kt; below this speed the main wheel brakes can be locked to allow tight turns while taxiing."],["Describe the three anti-skid protection modes.","Touchdown protection: brakes available at WOW Ground + 5 sec or wheel speed > 50 kt. Locked-wheel protection: releases pressure if a wheel is >30% slower than its paired wheel. Wheel spin-down: 3-second brake application before gear retraction; failure triggers 'WHEEL DESPIN FAIL' CAS message."],["What does the ECU try to maintain in normal anti-skid control?","Wheel rotation velocity within 2% of IRU-sensed ground speed (IRU #1: outboard wheels, IRU #2: inboard wheels)."],["How many safety pins are there in total, and what do they cover?","Six (6) total: three (3) gear pins (nose + two main gear downlocks) and three (3) door pins (one nose gear door pin, two main gear door pins)."],["What CAS/warning triggers 'TOO LOW, GEAR'?","Below 500 ft AGL and below 190 KCAS with the gear handle not DOWN; the GPWS/GND PROX OVRD switch silences the aural warning."],["When does the gear unsafe warning horn sound, and can it be silenced?","At or below 345 ft AGL with PLA <=5 and gear not down and locked (silenced by HORN SILENCE), or with flaps > 22 and gear not down and locked (HORN SILENCE will NOT silence this case)."]],"pneumatic":[["What are the four sources of pneumatic (bleed) air on the G550?","Left Engine (5th and 8th stage bleed), Right Engine (5th and 8th stage bleed), APU (via the Load Control Valve), and External (Ground) Air."],["What systems use pneumatic bleed air?","Engine starting, cowl/wing anti-ice, air conditioning/pressurization, potable water system, TAT probe airflow (ground only), and other systems."],["What are the Bleed Air Controllers (BACs), and where are they located?","Two (2) identical, interchangeable microprocessors - L BAC and R BAC - located in the Aft Electronic Equipment Rack (AEER); they are the brains of the pneumatic system, each controlling one independent manifold."],["What powers the L BAC and R BAC?","L BAC is powered by the L ESS DC bus; R BAC is powered by the R ESS DC bus."],["What is the purpose of keeping the L and R pneumatic manifolds normally isolated?","It prevents total loss of pneumatic air in the event of a leak in one of the manifolds; the isolation valve can connect them when needed."],["What are the PRSOV's function and power requirements?","Pressure Regulating/Shutoff Valve - one per engine, functions as an ON/OFF valve to the manifold, controlled by the L/R BLEED AIR switches; electro-pneumatic (needs electrical power to operate, pneumatic pressure to close); modulates to maintain up to 40 psi."],["What are the HPSOV's location and failure mode?","High-Pressure Shutoff Valve - one per engine on the 8th-stage line, commanded by the BACs to modulate open when 5th-stage bleed is insufficient; spring-loaded and fails CLOSED."],["Where is the isolation valve located, and what is its failure mode?","Located in the tail compartment between the L and R manifolds; electro-pneumatic (needs electrical power to operate, pneumatic pressure to open); powered by L ESS DC bus; fails frozen (remains in last position)."],["List the four conditions that open the isolation valve.","1) START MASTER selected ON, 2) CRANK MASTER selected ON, 3) APU bleed selected ON (APU stabilized at 100% RPM/60 sec, one-minute delay if EGT<149C) with WOW-Ground, 4) ISOLATION switchlight manually selected OPEN."],["Describe the 5th-stage and 8th-stage engine bleed flow paths.","5th-stage: port -> 5th-stage check valve -> joins 8th-stage line at a junction -> PRSOV -> manifold (cowl anti-ice taps upstream of the junction). 8th-stage: port -> 8th-stage check valve -> HPSOV -> same junction -> PRSOV -> manifold."],["How does APU bleed air reach the pneumatic manifolds?","The APU Load Control Valve (LCV) connects directly to the R manifold; selecting APU bleed automatically opens the isolation valve so APU air also reaches the L manifold."],["What is the sequence to bring APU bleed air online?","Weight-on-wheels (WOW) - Ground, APU stabilized at 100% RPM, then a one-minute delay if EGT is below 149C (delay removed if EGT already warm)."],["Is APU bleed air available for an in-flight engine restart?","Yes - APU bleed air is available immediately to restart an engine in flight (WOW - Air)."],["Where does External (Ground) Air connect, and what precaution applies?","Connects to the R (right) bleed air manifold, in parallel with the APU connection. Placard: 'DO NOT CONNECT WITHOUT ELECTRICAL POWER ON' - DC power is required to open the isolation valve and L/R PACK valves, preventing pack damage from unregulated external air."],["What is the function of the precooler heat exchanger, and where is it located?","Cools extremely hot engine bleed air (which would otherwise melt aluminum) using fan (LP) air before it enters the manifold; located in the engine pylon. Fan air enters through the fan air valve and exhausts under the pylon."],["What is the normal precooler outlet temperature, and when does it rise?","Normally 400F; rises to 500F when hotter air must travel farther through the crossover duct, due to wing anti-ice ON with only a single bleed source available."],["What bleed stage(s) feed wing anti-ice vs. cowl anti-ice?","Wing anti-ice uses either mid-stage (5th) or high-stage (8th), selected as needed. Cowl anti-ice uses mid-stage (5th) ONLY, tapped upstream of the 5th-stage check valve."],["What is the failure mode of the wing and cowl anti-ice valves?","Both wing anti-ice valves and cowl anti-ice valves fail OPEN."],["What are the pneumatic set points for ground idle and flight idle?","Ground idle: 14 psi on 8th stage. Flight idle: 24 psi on 8th stage."],["What pressure/temperature is produced at thrust above idle (5th stage)?","40 psi at 400F."],["What is the single-pack operation set point?","35 psi at 500F, on 8th stage (with the inoperative pack OFF)."],["What triggers the L/R BLEED PRESSURE HIGH and LOW CAS messages?","HIGH: manifold pressure exceeds 75 psi. LOW: manifold pressure drops below 5 psi for more than 10 seconds."],["What triggers the L/R BLEED AIR HOT CAS message?","Precooler inlet temperature exceeds 765F, OR precooler outlet temperature exceeds 553F."],["What set point applies when both wing anti-ice valves are ON at idle vs. above idle?","At idle: 8th stage, precooler 630F, output 40 psi/500F. Above idle: 5th stage, precooler 630F, output 40 psi/500F."]],"air-conditioning":[["What is the source of air for the Air Conditioning System?","High pressure, high temperature bleed air from the pneumatic system, via the bleed air manifold."],["How often is cabin air replaced, and does the G550 recirculate air?","All cabin air is replaced with fresh air every two (2) minutes; there is no air recirculation - it is a continuous flow-through system."],["What are the three main functions of the Air Conditioning System?","(1) Airflow control for the pressurization system, (2) cabin/cockpit temperature control (3 zones), (3) equipment cooling."],["What controls the Air Conditioning System, and where are they located?","Two identical, interchangeable microprocessors: L ACC and R ACC (Air Conditioning Controllers), located in the AEER next to the L and R Bleed Air Controllers (BACs)."],["What pressure and temperature is the bleed air supplied to each pack?","Up to 40 psi and 400F."],["What temperature is the air in the Common Cold Air Manifold?","35F, feeding all three cabin/cockpit zones."],["What is the trim air temperature, and where is it tapped from?","400F, tapped from the Hot Air Manifold; mixed into the cold air stream just before each zone outlet."],["What is the bleed air manifold set point?","35 +/- 3 psi (unchanged whether operating single or dual pack)."],["Where is the ozone converter located relative to the pack?","One per pack, immediately downstream of the pack valve, upstream of the pack itself."],["How many independently controlled temperature zones are there, and what are they?","Three: Cockpit, Forward (FWD) Cabin, and Aft Cabin - each with its own Cold/Hot knob and temperature readout."],["What is the AUTO mode temperature control range?","60F to 90F - ACCs automatically blend cold manifold air and trim air to hold the selected temperature."],["What is the MAN (Manual) mode temperature control range?","35F to 230F - crew directly commands the mix valve position via the Cold/Hot knob."],["What happens to the packs when START MASTER ON or CRANK MASTER ON is selected?","R Pack is shut OFF; L Pack and Ram Air are unaffected."],["What happens to the packs when both L Eng Start ON and R Eng Start ON are selected?","Both L Pack and R Pack shut OFF, to maximize bleed air flow to the Air Turbine Starter and reduce start time/thermal stress."],["Where is the Ram Air intake located, and when does the Ram Air check valve open?","Dorsal fin air intake, on top of the fuselage in front of the tail; the check valve opens only when Ram Air pressure exceeds cabin air pressure."],["What happens to the packs when Ram Air is selected ON?","Both L Pack and R Pack are shut off; cabin air is supplied directly from the Ram Air source."],["What are the three uses for Ram Air?","Overpressurization due to loss of system control, aircraft interior smoke removal, and ditching."],["What is the maximum altitude for operation on a single air conditioning pack?","48,000 ft."],["At or below what altitude may the internal baggage compartment door be opened when on single-pack operation?","45,000 ft and below."],["What altitude threshold switches the Equipment Cooling Fans between high and low speed, and what is the logic?","35,000 ft relay: above 35,000 ft, LEER/PDB (left) = low speed and REER/PDB (right) = high speed; at/below 35,000 ft the speeds reverse (LEER high, REER low)."],["What equipment do the cooling fans supply?","LEER, REER, PDBs (Power Distribution Boxes), cockpit center pedestal, AEER, and PSUs."],["What is the Thrust Recovery Outflow Valve (TROV) and where is it located?","The valve through which cabin air exits the pressurized vessel; located on the aft lower fuselage. It modulates to achieve and hold optimum cabin pressure."],["What is the maximum cabin pressure differential, and the max for taxi/takeoff/landing?","Maximum cabin pressure differential is 10.48 psi; maximum for taxi, takeoff, or landing is 0.3 psi (per Limitations Study Guide 01-21)."]],"ice-and-rain-protection":[["What are the two power sources for the Ice and Rain Protection System?","Hot engine bleed air (cowl and wing leading edges) and electrical power (probes, windshield, cabin windows, EVS window)."],["What defines icing conditions in flight or on the ground?","SAT between +10C and -40C AND visible moisture present (rain, snow, clouds, fog < 1 SM visibility, sleet, ice crystals)."],["What defines icing conditions on the ground / for takeoff due to surface contamination?","SAT +10C or below, operating on ramps/taxiways/runways covered with surface snow, ice, standing water, or slush."],["How many ice detector probes are there, where are they located, and at what frequency do they vibrate?","Two (2) probes, below the pilot and copilot windows, vibrating at 40,000 Hz."],["What triggers ice detection, and by how much does the frequency drop?","Accumulation of 0.020 inches of ice, which decreases probe frequency by approximately 130 Hz, triggering the 'L-R Ice Detected' CAS message."],["After ice is no longer detected, when do the Ice Detected, Cowl A/I, and Wing A/I messages extinguish?","'L-R Ice Detected' extinguishes after 1 minute; cowl A/I valves close and message extinguishes after 3 minutes; wing A/I valves close and message extinguishes after 5 minutes."],["What bleed air stage supplies the Cowl Anti-Ice (CAI) system, and what is the normal pressure range?","Mid-stage only (5th stage); normal pressure range is 1.6 - 33 psi."],["What is the CAI L/R miscompare threshold, and what type are the CAI valves?","7.0 +/- 1 psi difference triggers 'Cowl Anti-Ice Miscompare'; valves are electropneumatic (28 VDC electrically controlled, pneumatically operated), on/off only, and fail OPEN."],["What bleed air stages supply the Wing Anti-Ice (WAI) system, and what is the target temperature?","Mid or high-stage (5th or 8th stage HP compressor; high-stage used at low power such as descent); target temperature is 130F, maintained by modulating valves."],["What are the WAI CAS message temperature thresholds?","Normal ON range (no message): >=100F and <180F. 'Wing Temperature Low': ON >2 minutes with temp <100F. 'Wing Overheat': temp >=180F."],["What type of valve is the wing anti-ice valve, and what is its fail-safe mode?","Spring-loaded closed, pneumatically actuated, variable pressure regulator/shutoff (butterfly-type), located in the tail compartment; fails CLOSED."],["Does the engine spinner require anti-ice heating?","No - it has a passive de-icing design; the rubber tip distorts in flight and sheds ice, no heating required."],["Over what altitude range can WAI/CAI activate automatically, and above what altitude is auto-activation inhibited?","Automatic activation range is 1,500 ft AGL to 35,000 ft MSL; inhibited above 35,000 ft MSL (if already ON, it remains ON). Manual activation has no altitude restriction."],["Why is the 'L-R Ice Detected' CAS message inhibited on the ground?","Because AMBER CAS messages are considered 'No-Go' messages."],["What flap position is required when holding in icing conditions, and when else may flaps be used?","Flaps 0 (UP) when holding; flap use in icing conditions is otherwise restricted to takeoff, approach, and landing (min holding speed 160 KCAS per Limitations Guide)."],["At what temperatures are pitot-static/AOA air data probes heated below and above 60 kts?","Below 60 kts: heated to 150C. Above 60 kts: heated to 300C."],["When are TAT probes heated?","Only above 60 kts, or when both thrust levers are advanced beyond 30 degrees."],["What temperature band does windshield heat maintain, and which AC buses power which zones?","104F - 114F (cycles ON/OFF); L Main AC powers Left-Front (LF) and Right-Side (RS) zones, R Main AC powers Right-Front (RF) and Left-Side (LS) zones."],["How many cabin windows are electrically heated, and what is the ground manual override time limit?","14 cabin windows; manual override (ground only) is limited to 10 minutes - irreparable damage can occur from lack of airflow beyond that."],["What temperature range is the EVS window heated to, and what is the cyclic duty logic with gear up vs. down?","12C - 14C; gear UP cycles 1 minute ON / 7 minutes OFF, gear DOWN is continuously ON. Manual mode is 2 minutes ON."],["What triggers automatic EVS window heat activation?","Weight-On-Wheels (WOW) Air, 'L-R Ice Detected,' or L/R Cowl anti-ice switch ON."]],"oxygen":[["How many oxygen cylinders does the G550 have, and what is their capacity and pressure?","Two (2) identical tanks plumbed together into a single system; each has a capacity of 123.4 ft^3, pressurized to 1,800 +/-50 psi."],["What are the oxygen cylinders constructed of, and where are they located?","Aluminum cylinder wrapped in Kevlar for reinforcement; located underneath the floor near the main entrance doorway."],["What is the supply line pressure after the cylinder pressure regulators?","55-80 psi (reduced from 1,800 psi bottle pressure): 1,800 psi -> Regulator -> 55-80 psi -> O2 masks."],["At what pressure does the high-pressure oxygen relief valve open?","Above 2,775 psi (protects bottles and high-pressure lines)."],["At what pressure does the low-pressure oxygen relief valve open, and what is the thermal relief setting?","Low-pressure relief opens above 90 psi (bottle low-pressure supply line); thermal (temperature) relief is set at 225F."],["What does the overboard discharge indicator disc color mean?","Green (filled) disc = OK; white (empty) disc = discharged. Checked during pre-flight and post-flight inspections."],["What is the oxygen fill pressure/temperature and oxygen type used for servicing?","Fill pressure 1,800 psi @ 70F; Aviator Breathing Oxygen, MIL-PRF-27210."],["How many crew oxygen mask/regulator assemblies are there, and what is the quick-donning time?","Three (3) EROS assemblies (pilot, copilot, observer/jump seat); quick-donning in <= 5 seconds, one-hand operation."],["What are the three crew mask delivery modes?","Normal (N) - diluted O2/cabin air; 100% - on-demand 100% O2; Emergency - pressurized 100% O2."],["To what cabin altitude are crew masks certified, and when do they auto-switch to positive pressure?","Certified to 40,000 ft cabin altitude; automatically switch to positive pressure at 35,000 ft cabin altitude."],["To what cabin altitude are passenger masks certified, and above what altitude do they become insufficient?","Certified to 40,000 ft cabin altitude, but will not provide sufficient oxygen for prolonged use above 34,000 ft (emergency descent only)."],["What are the automatic passenger oxygen mask deployment cabin-altitude triggers?","14,750 +/-250 ft (normal); 15,750 +/-250 ft with ALT SELECT / HIGH ALT selected."],["How many passenger oxygen masks are installed relative to the number of seats?","Normally exceeds the number of passenger seats by at least 10%."],["What switch/message is associated with therapeutic oxygen?","THERAPEUTIC OXYGEN ON switch starts flow to the cabin receptacle; annunciated via CAS message 'THERAPEUTIC OXYGEN ON.'"],["How many PBEs are installed and where, and what is their oxygen duration?","One (1) PBE (Essex PBE) in the aft lavatory; two cylinders holding 36 liters total, providing a 15-minute oxygen supply."],["What do the PBE pre-flight status indicator colors mean?","Blue dot = serviceable (OK); Magenta/pink dot = unserviceable (do not use)."],["What do the PBE in-use light colors mean?","Green light = service (oxygen flowing normally); Red light = end of service (oxygen supply exhausted)."],["Per FAR 91, above what altitude must a pilot be on oxygen, and above what altitude if the other pilot leaves the cockpit?","Above 41,000 ft: one pilot must be on oxygen. Above 35,000 ft: one pilot must be on oxygen if the other pilot leaves the cockpit."],["Above FL250, what is required of crew masks?","Crew masks must be in the quick-donning position, allowing donning within 5 seconds."],["What does the Limitations Guide additionally recommend regarding prolonged operation with passengers aboard?","Prolonged operation above 25,000 ft cabin altitude with passengers aboard is not recommended (AFM guidance, supplementary to the system notes)."]],"pressurization":[["What is the maximum cabin pressure differential (hard structural limit), and which PRV chamber sets it?","10.48 psid (\"MAX 2\" warning) - the PRV 2nd metering chamber. This is the hard structural limit."],["What is the caution-level cabin differential pressure, and what CAS message does it trigger?","10.28 psid (\"MAX 1\") - CAS \"CABIN DIFFERENTIAL - 10.28\", when the PRV 1st metering chamber opens."],["What is the maximum cabin differential pressure allowed during taxi, takeoff, and landing, and why?","0.3 psid maximum, to allow opening of the Emergency Exit Doors and Main Entrance Door."],["What is the negative pressure relief limit, and does it generate a CAS message?","-0.25 psid (\"MAX 3\"); no CAS message is generated."],["What is normal cabin differential pressure at cruise, and what altitude/cabin altitude does that correspond to?","Approximately 10.17 psid at FL510, corresponding to roughly a 6,000 ft cabin altitude."],["What is the cabin rate of climb target after takeoff, and the pre-pressurization target before takeoff?","500 fpm cabin rate of climb after takeoff. Pre-pressurization target is 0.25 psid, approximately Landing Field Elevation (LFE) minus 500 ft."],["What two conditions (besides manual crew selection), with the door closed, trigger automatic pre-pressurization?","Taxi speed greater than 9 knots, or throttles advanced to 15 degrees or more Thrust Lever Angle (TLA)."],["At what disagreement threshold do the two CPC channels compare against the CPAM, and what happens to a channel that also disagrees with the CPAM?","310 ft. If channels 1 and 2 differ by 310 ft or more, each is compared to the CPAM; any channel differing from the CPAM by 310 ft or more is failed, and the CPCS auto-selects the remaining operative channel."],["What are the three CPC operational modes, and the two pressurization (profile) modes?","Operational modes: AUTO -> SEMI -> FAULT/MANUAL. Pressurization profile modes: FLIGHT -> LANDING."],["During descent, when does the pressure profile change from FLIGHT to LANDING, and when does it revert back to FLIGHT?","Changes to LANDING as the aircraft crosses 1,000 ft below cruise altitude. Reverts to FLIGHT if the aircraft levels off for 3 minutes at or above FL250."],["What powers Manual pressurization mode, and what control does the crew use?","L Main Battery -> L ESS DC -> TROV (manual actuator). The crew uses the MAN HOLD knob (DESCEND/CLIMB) on the Cabin Pressure Control Panel."],["How many electrical actuators does the TROV have, and what is the top-priority (Priority 1) power path?","Three (3) actuators - two AC and one DC. Priority 1: L GEN ON + R GEN ON -> L IDG + R IDG -> L Main AC + R Main AC -> ESS AC -> CPC 1/2 -> TROV."],["What is the lowest-priority (Priority 5) TROV power source, and what is its path?","Main Batteries (L & R) ON: L Main Batt -> L ESS DC -> E-INV -> ESS AC -> CPC 1."],["What are the three functions of the Pressure Relief Valve (PRV), and when does the ground pressurization limiting function activate?","1) Positive differential pressure relief (two chambers, 10.28/10.48 psid). 2) Negative differential pressure relief (-0.25 psid, no CAS). 3) Ground pressurization limiting - PRV opens 60 seconds after touchdown (on WOW)."],["What are the four conditions that trigger Emergency Descent Mode (EDM) to engage automatically?","Autopilot ON, aircraft altitude at or above FL400, and a \"CABIN PRESSURE LOW\" CAS message is generated."],["What lateral turn does EDM command, and what speed/altitude does it target?","A 90-degree left turn; speed target VMO 340 knots, altitude preselect 15,000 ft."],["How is EDM canceled?","Via the autopilot disconnect button, or by deselecting AP on the Guidance Panel."],["At what cabin altitude does \"CABIN PRESSURE LOW\" trip in FAULT/MANUAL mode, versus AUTO/SEMI mode with a landing field elevation above 14,000 ft?","FAULT/MANUAL: >= 8,000 ft cabin altitude (any LFE). AUTO/SEMI with LFE > 14,000 ft: >= 15,500 ft cabin altitude."],["Above what altitude must one pilot be on oxygen per FAR 91, and above what cabin altitude are crew/passenger masks not approved for use?","Above 41,000 ft, one pilot must be on oxygen. Crew and passenger masks are NOT approved for use above 40,000 ft cabin altitude."],["At what cabin altitude do passenger oxygen masks automatically deploy, and what is the value with the HIGH ALT option installed?","14,750 ft +/- 250 ft cabin altitude (standard); 15,750 ft +/- 250 ft with the HIGH ALT option."],["Above what altitude must crew oxygen masks be in the quick-donning position, and how fast must they be able to don them?","Above FL250; must be donnable within 5 seconds."],["Why does a rotor burst event not affect cabin pressurization?","The TROV's physical location and the aft secondary pressure bulkhead isolate the pressurized cabin from the area (engine/depressurized baggage compartment) where a rotor burst would strike; the pressurized cabin and TROV sit forward of/independent from that area."]],"electrical":[["What AC voltage/frequency does the G550 electrical system generate, and what DC voltage results?","115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase is generated; it is converted via TRUs to 28 VDC (115 VAC -> TRU -> 28 VDC)."],["What are the four AC generation sources that feed the AC buses through the bus tie relays?","L IDG, APU GEN, EXT AC, and R IDG."],["What is the rated output of each IDG, and what constant speed does the CSD hold the generator at?","40 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; the CSD holds the generator at a constant 12,000 RPM."],["What is the APU generator's rating, and when does it come online?","40 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; comes online at 99% RPM + 2 seconds, 100% available from sea level to 45,000 ft."],["What is the HMG (Hydraulic Motor Generator) rated at, and what drives it?","10 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase; driven by the L Hydraulic System, or by the Power Transfer Unit (PTU) if the L system is unavailable."],["What is External AC Power (EXT PWR) rated at?","30 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase."],["What is the E-Inverter (Standby Inverter) rated at, and what does it power?","1 kVA, 115 VAC, 400 Hz; converts 28 VDC to 115 VAC, Phase A only, backing up the Ess AC bus."],["What are TRUs rated at, and what do they convert?","250 amps; convert 115 VAC to 28 VDC."],["What are the main battery specs (chemistry, count, cells, weight, rating)?","Two (2) NiCad main batteries (L/R Main Batt), 21 cells, 95 lb each, 28 VDC, 53 amp/hour, located in the tail compartment."],["How long can the main batteries power the Ess DC buses, and under what condition?","Thirty (30) minutes, after two (2) failed APU start attempts."],["What is the minimum voltage required to start the APU with the main battery?","22 V minimum (uses L Main Batt only, but both battery switches must be selected ON)."],["What are the emergency battery (E-BATT) specs and how many are there?","Four (4) sealed lead-acid E-BATTs (2x Avionics, 2x Lighting), 24 V, 9 amp/hour, ~30 minutes endurance."],["At what voltage do the emergency batteries automatically come ON?","When L and/or R Ess DC bus voltage drops below 20 V, even momentarily."],["What is the BPCU priority logic for restoring power to buses?","ESS before MAIN, L before R."],["What is the AUX TRU backup priority order when an Ess or Main TRU fails?","1. L Ess DC, 2. R Ess DC, 3. L Main DC, 4. R Main DC (ESS before MAIN, L before R)."],["What is the Ground Service Bus (GSB) power source priority?","1. R Main DC, 2. EXT DC, 3. R Main Batt."],["How many GEN sources are required for the galley buses on the ground vs. in the air?","One (1) GEN source on the ground; two (2) GEN sources in the air."],["What are the requirements for a No-Break Power Transfer (NBPT), and when does a Break transfer occur instead?","NBPT requires an IDG AND no failure (matches IDG/APU GEN phases). A Break occurs when no IDG is available, and/or with a GEN failure, engine failure, or a fire handle pulled."],["What is the bus tie relay state in Normal vs. Emergency operation?","NORMAL: bus tie relays CLOSED (operative side can power the inoperative side). EMERGENCY: bus tie relays OPEN (each side isolated)."],["How many BPCUs and GCUs make up the electrical system's microprocessors, and are all GCUs interchangeable?","Six (6) total: 2 BPCUs + 4 GCUs (L IDG, APU GEN, R IDG, HMG GEN). L IDG/APU GEN/R IDG GCUs are interchangeable with each other; the HMG GEN GCU is NOT interchangeable with the others."]],"apu":[["What is the G550 APU model, and what type of engine is it?","Honeywell RE220, a single-shaft, constant-speed gas turbine, controlled by an Electronic Control Unit (ECU)."],["What are the max EGT for start and running, and the max rotor speed?","Max EGT start: 1,050C. Max EGT running: 732C. Max rotor speed: 106% (all conditions)."],["What is the APU's fuel burn rate, and how long can the titanium enclosure contain a fire?","Fuel burn: 264 lb/hour. The fireproof titanium case can contain a fire for 15 minutes before damage to other systems occurs."],["What is the minimum battery voltage for APU start, and which battery does the starter actually use?","Minimum 22 V. Both battery switches must be ON, but the APU starter uses L MAIN BATT only."],["What are the main-battery starter attempt limits for the APU?","3 consecutive attempts, then a 1-hour cooldown; up to 6 successful consecutive starts allowed at 10-minute intervals."],["What are the external DC power (EXT PWR) starter attempt limits?","3 attempts with a 15-minute interval between attempts, then a 1-hour cooldown after the 3rd."],["What is the APU's maximum operating altitude, and the guaranteed in-flight start altitude?","Maximum operating altitude: 45,000 ft. Guaranteed in-flight start: at or below 39,000 ft (start may be attempted, not guaranteed, up to 43,000 ft)."],["At what altitude does the Bleed Air Augmentation Valve (BAAV) open, and what does it do?","Opens at or above 35,000 ft; introduces 400F engine bleed air into the APU intake, with a 16-second delay prior to APU start."],["At what altitude does the APU Surge Control Valve open, and why?","At or above 16,500 ft, to prevent a compressor stall when the APU is started in flight."],["What altitude limits APU-assisted engine starts in general vs. specifically for the BR710C4-11 engine?","APU bleed air is available for assisted starts up to 30,000 ft in general, but the BR710C4-11 engine limits assisted air starts to 25,000 ft and below."],["At what minimum altitude may APU bleed air be selected ON for a bleed-off takeoff, and what weight penalty applies?","1,500 ft AGL per the QRH Alternate Normals Bleed Off Takeoff procedure; adds about 1.4% gross weight."],["What does the APU generator produce, and at what RPM/timing does it come online?","40 kVA, 3-phase, 115 V, 400 Hz. Comes online at 99% RPM plus 2 seconds."],["Up to what altitude can the APU generator provide backup AC power in flight, and under what conditions is it permitted?","Usable up to 45,000 ft; permitted in flight only for single-IDG failure or dual-IDG failure."],["What are the four in-flight conditions under which APU operation is permitted?","Single IDG failure, dual IDG failure, single engine failure, and dual engine failure (per AFM OIS G550-2016-03)."],["How long after an APU ground start is the Load Control Valve (LCV) delayed from opening, and what removes that delay?","60 seconds, allowing lightly-loaded (low EGT) operation - unless EGT is already warm (>149C), which removes the delay so pneumatic air is available immediately."],["How does the ECU respond to a confirmed APU fire?","Closes the APU fuel supply valve, closes the Load Control Valve, shuts off the APU generator, and closes the APU inlet door."],["What indications does a proper APU fire test produce?","8 indications: 2x fire warning lights, 'APU FIRE' message, 'APU FIRE DETECTOR FAIL' message, and 2x fire handles (plus a fire bell on the ground)."],["How long does the APU remain in Essential mode after landing before the ECU shuts it down automatically if not needed?","15 minutes."],["Above 20,000 ft, how does APU RPM behave during shutdown cool-down compared to at/below 20,000 ft?","Above 20,000 ft: RPM remains at 100% for 60 seconds, then the ECU signals an overspeed to shut down. At/below 20,000 ft: RPM decreases 1/2% per second for 60 seconds (to ~70% RPM), then overspeed shutdown."],["When should the APU oil quantity check be performed after shutdown?","15 to 30 minutes after shutdown, using the Ground Service Synoptic window or the fluid quantity indicator in the tail compartment."]],"fuel":[["What is the total usable fuel capacity of the G550, and at what conditions?","41,300 lb / 18,734 kg / 6,118 US gal, at 60F and a fuel density of 6.767 lb/gallon."],["How much usable fuel is in each wing tank, and what is each hopper tank's capacity?","20,650 lb per wing tank (L/R); each of the 4 hopper tanks holds 190 gal / 1,283 lb."],["What are the maximum ramp weight and MTOW that limit fuel upload beyond 41,300 lb?","Maximum ramp weight: 91,400 lb. Maximum takeoff weight (MTOW): 91,000 lb (also must stay within loaded C.G. limits)."],["What is the Zero Fuel Weight (ZFW), and what governs the ZFW C.G. envelope?","54,500 lb; ZFW C.G. must stay within the ZFW C.G. envelope (approx. 50,000-54,500 lb across roughly 35-45% MAC), per AFM 01-03-70."],["What are the fuel tank temperature warning and caution thresholds?","Low warning: <= -37C. Low caution: -35C to -36C. High warning: >= +54C (delay takeoff)."],["What are the engine fuel temperature limits (min, max continuous, max transient)?","Minimum -40C; maximum continuous +140C; maximum +165C for 15 minutes (transient)."],["What is the cold fuel rule for descent?","If fuel tank temp <= -30C in flight with less than 5,000 lb total fuel remaining: descend to where SAT is -60C or warmer and maintain minimum Mach 0.80."],["What are the maximum fuel imbalance limits for takeoff vs. in flight?","1,000 lb maximum at takeoff; 2,000 lb maximum in flight (also 2,000 lb max for gravity refueling); proceed with balancing before reaching 1,000 lb."],["At what imbalance thresholds do the EICAS fuel imbalance arrows appear, reach full-scale deflection, and turn amber?","Arrows first appear at 100 lb imbalance; full-scale deflection at 500 lb; full-scale arrows turn amber (caution) at 1,000 lb."],["How many boost pumps are there per hopper, and what is their discharge pressure and current draw?","Two (2) per hopper - Main and Alternate, identical/interchangeable; 25 psi discharge pressure; each draws less than 25 amps."],["What powers the Main vs. Alternate boost pumps?","L Main -> L ESS DC; R Main -> R ESS DC. L Alternate -> L MAIN DC; R Alternate -> R MAIN DC."],["What happens to the engine without boost pump pressure, below vs. at/above 20,000 ft?","Below 20,000 ft the engine will suction-feed successfully; at or above 20,000 ft the engine will run erratically and flame out."],["What is the ejector pump flow rate that keeps the hopper tanks full?","A steady 4,450 lb/hour, using motive flow from boost pump pressure (no moving parts)."],["How many flapper-type gravity valves feed each hopper tank from the wing tank?","Three (3) per hopper."],["What is the HFRS auto ON / auto OFF fuel tank temperature, and how much heated fuel does it deliver?","Auto ON at 0C; Auto OFF at 10C fuel tank temperature. Delivers 3 gallons of heated fuel at 50C per minute, controlled by FADEC."],["Name three conditions that inhibit the Heated Fuel Return System (HFRS).","Any of: fuel tank temp > 10C; crossflow valve open; thrust lever above 2,250 pph; HFRS switch OFF; engine FIRE handle pulled; low fuel pressure/quantity; or engine RUN switch OFF."],["What is the fuel filtration bypass differential pressure, and what CAS message does it trigger?","25 psi differential across a filter indicates impending bypass, annunciated as 'L FUEL FILTER,' 'R FUEL FILTER,' or 'L-R FUEL FILTER.'"],["Describe the engine fuel pressure schedule from boost pump to nozzles.","25 psi boost pump discharge -> LP pump -> 150 psi at the FCOC -> 1,200 psi HP pump -> FMU -> HP SOV -> 20 fuel nozzles per engine."],["Which boost pumps remain available under HMG/emergency electrical configuration, and why?","Only the L and R Main boost pumps (powered by L/R ESS DC); the Alternate pumps are powered by the MAIN DC buses and are NOT available under HMG/emergency configuration."],["What is required during ground fueling operations regarding weather radar and bonding?","Weather radar must be OFF (on the fueling aircraft, and on any other aircraft with radar on within 300 ft); the fuel truck and aircraft must be bonded."]],"hydraulic":[["How many main hydraulic systems does the G550 have, and what supports them?","Two independent systems, Left (L) and Right (R), each fully isolated from the other. They are supported by two sub-systems: the AUX Pump and the Power Transfer Unit (PTU)."],["What is the normal EDP (Engine-Driven Pump) system pressure and flow rate?","3,000 psi +/-300 psi; flow rate 18-28 gal/min (idle to takeoff power). Constant-pressure, variable-volume pump mounted on the engine gearbox."],["What is the reservoir bootstrap pre-charge pressure, and why?","60 psi - compresses the reservoir to prevent hydraulic pump cavitation."],["What are the max pressurized reservoir quantities for L and R systems ('considered full at')?","Left 4.8 gal, Right 1.6 gal (cross-checked and matching the Limitations Guide, Hydraulics 01-29)."],["What is the total fluid capacity and largest reservoir size of the L Hydraulic System?","Total capacity 20.6 gal; largest reservoir is the L reservoir, a dual chamber of 3.7 + 2.0 gal = 5.7 gal total."],["What is the total fluid capacity and reservoir size of the R Hydraulic System?","Total capacity 7.0 gal; smallest reservoir at 1.8 gal, considered full at 1.6 gal."],["How many DPIs (filters) are on each manifold, and what is the total?","L Hydraulic System Manifold: 5. R Hydraulic System Manifold: 3. PTU Manifold: 1. AUX Pump Manifold: 1. Total: 10."],["What is the accumulator pre-charge pressure and tolerance?","1,200 psi at 70F/21C, +/-25 psi per 10F/5C deviation from 70F (tolerance detail from the Limitations Guide)."],["What is the BWI reset procedure if the PTU Bearing Wear Indicator pops?","(1) Reset it per the AFM Exterior Preflight checklist, (2) note the time in the logbook, (3) continued operation is allowed for a maximum of 50 flight hours, (4) check the BWI prior to each flight."],["What powers the AUX Pump, and at what pressure/flow does it operate?","Electrically powered by the L ESS DC bus; operates automatically or manually at 3,000 psi @ 2 gal/min."],["What triggers the AUX Boost Pump ON and OFF?","ON when supply line pressure drops below 20 psi; OFF once pressure reaches 25 psi."],["What is the AUX pump's auto-latch (ground) trigger logic?","Weight-on-Wheels (WOW) on the ground plus brake pedal application greater than 10 degrees, when brake pressure is below 1,500 psi, automatically brings the AUX pump ON."],["In flight, how is the AUX system activated to power the standby rudder, and what does it feed?","Activated by Nosewheel WOW (airborne) + STBY RUD switch ON. It feeds the rudder actuator and Yaw Damper 1 (YD1) via the Standby Rudder Control Valve if both L and R systems fail."],["What five systems can the AUX pump power?","Nose Wheel Steering (NWS), Main Entrance Door (MED), Flaps, Brakes, and Standby Rudder (via the Standby Rudder Control Valve)."],["At what L system pressure does the PTU come on automatically, and what is its output?","PTU comes on automatically when L Hydraulic System pressure drops below 1,500 psi. Output: 22 gal/min @ 3,000 psi +300/-400 psi."],["What two things can the PTU NOT actuate?","The left thrust reverser and the flight controls - it can only pressurize L system fluid for other L-system users."],["List the three conditions that prevent the PTU from coming ON automatically.","L Hydraulic System quantity below 1.0 gal (< 1.0 g); PWR XFR UNIT switch OFF/ARM (not selected ON); R Hydraulic System temperature above 104C."],["Does pulling the Left Fire Handle shut off L system fluid supply to the PTU?","No - the shutoff valve only isolates fluid to the Left EDP, not the PTU circuit, so L fluid still reaches the PTU."],["What is the HMG (Hydraulic Motor Generator) rated output and what drives it?","Rated at 10 kVA, producing 115 VAC, 400 Hz, 3-phase. Driven by either the L Hydraulic System or the PTU."],["When is HMG operation permitted, and what does it power?","Only when normal AC power generation is unavailable (dual IDG - L IDG and R IDG - AND APU GEN failure). It powers the L and R Standby AC buses, and via the AUX TRU, DC voltage to the L ESS DC / R ESS DC buses."],["What is the required interval between AUX TRU crew selections?","Ten (10) seconds."],["What happens when the L EDP fails?","Loss of the Left thrust reverser, plus loss of redundant flight controls and Yaw Damper 1 (YD1) - ailerons, elevators, rudder, and spoilers/speed brakes keep R-system power but lose the L-system boost."],["What happens when the R EDP fails (three effects)?","Loss of the Right thrust reverser; loss of redundant flight controls and Yaw Damper 2 (YD2); and loss of the PTU, since it requires R system fluid and pressure to drive its motor."],["Where is the hydraulic fluid-to-fuel heat exchanger located, and what does it do?","Located in the offside fuel hopper; continuous flow with no pilot input, it cools hot hydraulic fluid while warming cold fuel."]],"flight-controls":[["What is the hydraulic boost ratio at each control surface actuator?","6-to-1 boost ratio, hydraulically-powered, with mechanical (pushrod/cable/bell crank) backup and full manual reversion on complete loss of hydraulic fluid."],["What surfaces make up the Primary Flight Control System (PFCS) vs. the Secondary Flight Control System (SFCS)?","PFCS: ailerons and elevators. SFCS: spoilers and the horizontal stabilizer/flaps."],["What does the Hard Over Prevention System (HOPS) do, and how does it detect a fault?","It compares hydraulic pressure sensed into each servo against pressure sensed out; if there's a difference, it shuts off hydraulic fluid from the affected system(s) to prevent an uncommanded hard-over surface deflection."],["How does HOPS respond differently for ailerons vs. elevators/rudder?","Ailerons: HOPS shuts off BOTH L and R aileron hydraulics together if one actuator is jammed. Elevators and rudder: HOPS shuts off only the affected actuator/hydraulic system; flight continues on the remaining side."],["What is the aileron deflection range?","Up 11 deg / Down 11 deg."],["What is the aileron trim tab range, and what is unique about it?","Up 15 deg / Down 15 deg, on the left aileron only. The trim tab is heated (175F +/-20F), powered by R Main AC."],["How much additional roll authority do the mid and outboard spoiler panels add?","Up to 47 deg of additional roll authority, augmenting the up-going aileron."],["What is the minimum approach speed after a jammed aileron or elevator is manually split and disconnected?","125 KCAS, to ensure adequate airflow over the flight controls."],["What is the elevator deflection range?","Up 24 deg / Down 13 deg."],["What are the electrical vs. mechanical elevator trim tab ranges?","Electrically: Up 21 deg / Down 7 deg. Mechanically: Up 22 deg / Down 8 deg. Both heated to 175F +/-20F."],["What is the rudder trim range and how is it accomplished?","7.5 deg L/R, via a rudder trim control wheel on the center pedestal that moves the entire rudder surface."],["What does the Automatic Overload Limiting System do for the rudder?","Limits rudder travel (less at high speed, more at low speed) and limits hydraulic pressure to the rudder actuator, to prevent excessive overload on the vertical stabilizer at high speed. Rudder deflection up to 22 deg L/R depending on speed."],["What happens when one hydraulic system is lost, affecting rudder pressure?","Pressure from the remaining hydraulic system is ramped up to 3,000 psi, and a 'Single Rudder' CAS message is displayed."],["What is the standby rudder control valve trigger logic, and what does it feed?","Nosewheel WOW (in the air) + STBY RUD switch ON. It supplies AUX system fluid/pressure to the Rudder/YD1 actuator, plus other AUX systems: flaps, NWS, brakes, and MED."],["What is the yaw damper system's redundancy architecture?","Two channels (YD1, YD2) and two flight guidance computers (FGC1, FGC2), cross-connected and fail-operational - if a YD and/or FGC fails, the remaining unit automatically takes over."],["What are the Vfe speeds for Flaps 10, 20, and 39?","Flaps 10: 250 KCAS. Flaps 20: 220 KCAS. Flaps 39 (DOWN): 170 KCAS."],["What are the max altitudes for extending/flying flaps?","25,000 ft for Flaps 10 or 20; 20,000 ft for Flaps 39."],["What are the flight load acceleration (g-load) limits by flap setting?","Flaps UP: -1.0g to +2.5g. Flaps 10/20: 0g to +2.0g. Flaps 39 DOWN: 0g to +2.0g up to MLW, or 0g to +1.5g above MLW."],["How are the flaps actuated (three means)?","Electrically-controlled (FCU channels A/B, powered by L/R Ess DC); hydraulically-powered (AUX, L Hyd Sys, and PTU feed a hydraulic motor); mechanically-actuated (flexible drive shaft/gearbox differentially drives four ballscrew actuators: L outboard, L inboard, R inboard, R outboard)."],["How many spoiler panels are there, and how are they driven?","Six (6) spoiler panels total, driven by two (2) hydraulic actuators per wing; electrically-controlled via the speed brake handle, hydraulically-powered by L and R systems."],["What is the ground spoiler deflection, and the in-flight speed brake deflection?","Ground spoilers: 55 deg +/-4 deg. Speed brakes (in-flight): up to 30 deg."],["What speed-brake deployment rate triggers a 'SPEED BRAKE EXTENDED' CAS message?","Deployment faster than moving the handle from stowed to fully extended in 3 seconds or more may trigger the CAS message; 3 seconds or slower is acceptable."],["When is spoiler extension in flight prohibited?","Do NOT extend spoilers in flight with gear down or with Flaps 39 (DOWN) selected; do NOT arm ground spoilers for touch-and-go landings."],["What does a 'Rudder Limit' CAS message indicate?","The maximum rudder hydraulic assist condition."]],"fire-protection":[["What are the Fire Protection System's four pillars?","1. Detection, 2. Notification, 3. Fire Fighting, 4. Smoke Evacuation - applied across engines, APU, baggage compartment, and lavatories/galley."],["How many fire loops does each engine have, and what type of sensor is used?","Two (2) fire loops per engine, Loop A and Loop B - each a temperature-sensitive coaxial wire (center wire, semi-conducting glass/oxide material, stainless-steel outer sheath)."],["How does an engine fire loop signal a fire condition?","As temperature increases, resistance around the center wire decreases (inverse relationship) - this is how the loop signals a fire."],["What does the FDCU do, and what can be done with a faulty/failed loop?","The Fire Detection Control Unit (FDCU) is the 'brains' of the system, determining whether a loop is Fault, Failed, or sensing an actual Fire. A faulty/failed loop can be deselected, so the system operates as a single-loop system for that engine."],["How many engine/APU fire bottles are there, what agent do they hold, and where are they located?","Two (2) identical single-shot bottles, containing Halon 1301 under high pressure, located in the tail compartment."],["What does the L bottle (Shot 2) feed, versus the R bottle (Shot 1)?","L bottle (Shot 2) feeds EITHER engine (L or R) OR the APU. R bottle (Shot 1) feeds EITHER engine only - it is NOT available to the APU."],["Rotating a fire handle outward vs. inward - what happens?","Rotating OUTWARD discharges Shot 1 (R bottle) - CAS 'R FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE.' Rotating INWARD discharges Shot 2 (L bottle) - CAS 'L FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE.'"],["For an engine fire, which bottle/shot is used first, and why?","Shot 1 (R bottle) is used first, to preserve the L bottle (Shot 2) in reserve for the APU, since the APU can only be fed by Shot 2."],["What three things happen when a fire handle is pulled?","1. Shuts off fuel at the tank (fuel shutoff valve closes). 2. Shuts off hydraulic fluid downstream from the reservoir for that side. 3. Trips the associated IDG (bus tie relays close automatically to keep that side's main AC bus powered from the other IDG)."],["What power source must be available for the engine fire extinguishing system to work?","L ESS DC and R ESS DC buses must be powered (down to main batteries)."],["In an overpressure event, where is the fire bottle agent vented?","Into the tail compartment."],["What indications appear during an Engine Fire Test, per engine?","2x overhead loop lights (Loop A, Loop B), 2x CAS messages ('Engine Fire Loop Alert' and '[L/R] Engine Fire'), 2x master warning (W) lights, the fire handle pops out, and the fuel control switch moves from RUN to OFF."],["What does the Engine Fire Fault Test check, and what indications does it produce?","It tests the fire detection FAULT circuit (not the loops themselves). Indications: 5x overhead lights (Loop A Fault, Loop B Fault, Fault Test x2 more), 1x CAS message ('Fire Detection Loop Fault'), 2x master caution (C) lights."],["How can the APU be extinguished, and what happens to bottle availability afterward?","Only with the L Fire Bottle (Shot 2), via the APU FIRE EXT switchlight, displaying 'L FIRE BOTTLE DISCHARGE' on the CAS. After discharge, the L bottle is no longer available to either engine."],["What does the ECU do automatically in the event of an APU fire, and what does the QRH direct the crew to do?","The ECU automatically shuts down the APU. The QRH then directs the crew to (1) select APU MASTER switchlight OFF, and (2) press the APU FIRE EXT switchlight to discharge the bottle into the APU."],["How many areas are monitored for overheat conditions, and what are the two trip points/CAS types?","Ten (10) areas total. Bleed-air-related areas: 250F, Red overheat CAS (Warning). Electronic equipment areas: 150F, Amber overheat CAS (Caution)."],["Where are smoke detectors (optical sensors) installed?","Baggage compartment, forward lavatory, aft lavatory, and galley."],["How is baggage compartment smoke evacuated?","The Emergency Smoke Evacuation VLV is selected to VENT/SMOKE, deflating the seal around the exterior baggage door; differential pressure forces smoke out through the deflated seal while the internal baggage door stays closed."],["How is the baggage compartment repressurized after smoke evacuation?","The VLV is returned to NORMAL OPS (seal reinflated) and the Bag Compt Vent Valve Reset toggle is held up for 10 seconds."],["How many portable fire extinguishers and PBE stations are carried in the cabin?","Three (3) Halon portable extinguishers (forward, mid-cabin, aft near PBE station), one (1) water portable extinguisher (mid-cabin), and one (1) PBE station (aft, near the aft Halon extinguisher)."],["What are the cold-weather removal temperatures for the two portable extinguisher models?","Amerex portable extinguisher must be removed at -65C; Kidde portable extinguisher must be removed at -40C (per the Limitations Study Guide cold-weather table)."],["When does the aural 'fire bell' sound, and where is it located?","Only on the ground, for confirmed fire conditions, located in the nose wheel well area - it is not heard in flight."],["What type of detector does the APU use, versus the engines?","The APU uses a dedicated helium-filled tube around the APU enclosure (housed in a fireproof titanium case), rather than the engines' temperature-sensitive resistance-wire loops."]],"powerplant":[["What engines power the G550, and what thrust do they produce?","Two (2) Rolls-Royce BR700-710C4-11 high-bypass turbofans, producing 15,385 lb of thrust each @ ISA +15C."],["What are the 100% RPM reference values for the LP and HP spools?","LP (inner) spool 100% = 7,431 RPM (single-stage fan / 3-stage LP turbine). HP (outer) spool 100% = 15,898 RPM (10-stage compressor / 2-stage HP turbine)."],["What is the maximum TGT for a ground start, and what is the momentary limit before fuel is introduced?","700C maximum TGT for ground start; 150C maximum TGT prior to introducing fuel."],["What are the takeoff TGT limits (AEO and OEI)?","900C TGT: 5 minutes AEO, 10 minutes OEI (not beyond V1 for OEI)."],["What is the Maximum Continuous Thrust (MCT) TGT limit?","860C TGT, unrestricted time."],["What are the maximum overspeed and overtemperature limits (from the Limitations Guide)?","Overspeed: 101.5% LP / 99.8% HP for 20 seconds. Overtemperature: 905C TGT for 20 seconds."],["What are the LP%/HP% and TGT limits for takeoff vs. MCT?","Takeoff: 101.1% LP / 99.6% HP / 900C TGT (5 min AEO / 10 min OEI). MCT: 101.0% LP / 98.9% HP / 860C TGT (unrestricted)."],["What are the engine oil temperature limits?","-30C minimum for start, +20C minimum for taxi/takeoff, +160C maximum."],["What are the engine fuel temperature limits?","-40C minimum, +140C maximum (unrestricted), +165C maximum for a 15-minute transient."],["What is the engine start cycle limit?","Three consecutive 3-minute start attempts; a 15-second cooling period after the first two, but a full 15-minute cooling period is required after the third consecutive attempt."],["What are the maximum crosswind and tailwind limits for a ground start?","30 kt maximum crosswind, 20 kt maximum tailwind for ground start."],["Describe the crosswind takeoff technique required above 20 kt crosswind.","Limit fan speed to <=66% LP RPM until reaching 20 kt forward speed, then slam-accelerate to EPR (rated/flex) within 5 seconds (autothrottle acceptable); add 600 ft to the required field length."],["What is the minimum bleed air pressure required for an engine start?","40 psi minimum bleed air for start."],["Describe the static ground run keep-out zone.","Stabilized static ground operation between 66% and 80% LP RPM is prohibited (forward thrust); the engine must transit through this band in 10 seconds or less. This protection is automatic when the parking brake is set and is also active in reverse thrust."],["What is the airstart envelope (altitude, speed, TGT)?","Maximum altitude 25,000 ft; starter-assist airstart <=250 KCAS; windmill airstart >=251 KCAS; maximum TGT during airstart 850C. No FADEC protection is provided during an in-flight airstart."],["What are the reverse thrust limits?","Maximum reverse thrust 70% LP RPM, 30-second limit; cancel reverse thrust to reach idle by 60 KCAS. Idle reverse for taxi has no time limit; power-back is not approved."],["Under what two conditions does FADEC provide engine start protection?","Only on the ground, and only with the START MASTER switch in the ON position."],["What are the EEC's three control modes and their reference parameters?","Primary Control Mode (EPR / HP RPM at idle), Alternate Control Mode (LP RPM), Reverse Thrust Control Mode (LP RPM)."],["Explain Independent Overspeed Protection (IOP).","Both EEC channels (A and B) must independently agree an overspeed condition exists before the EEC commands the FMU to shut off fuel; a single-channel fault will not shut down a healthy engine."],["What powers the EEC below and above 35% HP RPM?","Below 35% HP RPM the ESS DC BUS powers the EEC; above 35% HP RPM the Permanent Magnet Alternator (PMA) becomes the primary EEC power source."],["How many igniters are used for a ground start vs. an airstart?","Ground start: one (1) igniter. Airstart: two (2) igniters (higher sparking rate). Manual ignition also uses two (2) igniters."],["At what HP RPM are igniters automatically turned off during a normal start?","42% HP RPM."],["List the five engine start/ignition modes.","Auto Start, Alternate Start, Inclement Weather Mode, Auto-Relight Mode, and Quick-Restart Mode."],["What is the Quick-Restart Mode procedure for an inadvertent in-flight shutdown?","Return the fuel control switch to RUN within 30 seconds of the inadvertent shutdown."],["What triggers the Ground Idle mode, and what is the touchdown delay significance?","Ground Idle engages on weight-on-wheels; there is a 5-second delay after touchdown, which allows the EEC to command full and rapid engine spool-up (within 8 seconds) if a go-around is required at the final approach fix."],["Describe the engine oil pressure schedule (from the Limitations Guide).","Shutdown required below 25 psi; minimum for takeoff is 35 psi at <72.3% HP or 45 psi at >90.0% HP; minimum to complete the flight is 25 psi / 35 psi respectively."],["When must the engine oil level be checked?","Between 15 and 30 minutes after shutdown, on the last flight of the day, and every 24 cumulative flight hours."],["Describe the engine fuel flow path from tank to nozzles.","Wing tank -> LP pump (25 psi) -> LP pump stage (150 psi) -> Fuel Cooled Oil Cooler (FCOC) -> HP pump (1,200 psi) -> Fuel Metering Unit (FMU, ~50C) -> High Pressure Shutoff Valve (HP SOV) -> 20 fuel spray nozzles per engine."],["What is the difference between the fuel control switch shutoff and the fire handle shutoff?","The fuel control switch shuts off fuel at the pylon; pulling the fire handle shuts off fuel at the tank, upstream of the pylon shutoff."],["List the three engine shutdown causes where a restart should NOT be attempted.","Fire, FOD (Foreign Object Damage), or Frozen (e.g., ice ingestion/frozen components)."]],"limitations":[["What are the G550's Maximum Ramp Weight, Maximum Takeoff Weight, and Maximum Landing Weight?","MRW 91,400 lb (41,458 kg); MTOW 91,000 lb (41,277 kg); MLW 75,300 lb (34,155 kg)."],["What are the Maximum Zero Fuel Weights under FAA vs JAA/EASA?","FAA: 54,500 lb (24,721 kg). JAA/EASA: 52,000 lb (23,587 kg)."],["What is total usable fuel capacity, and the max fuel imbalance limits for takeoff and in flight?","Total usable fuel: 41,300 lb (18,734 kg) / 6,118 US gal. Max imbalance: 1,000 lb for takeoff, 2,000 lb in flight."],["What are the fuel tank temperature caution and warning ranges?","Caution (low): -35C to -36C. Warning (low): <= -37C. Warning (high): >= +54C."],["What is Mmo/Vmo for the G550, and the max speed for RVSM operations?","Max 0.885 Mt (FL320-FL440), 0.86 Mt (FL440-FL510) per the altitude/Mach envelope. Max RVSM operating speed: Mach 0.87."],["What are Vmc-Air, Vmc-Landing, and Vmc-Ground?","Vmc-Air 112 KCAS; Vmc-Landing 110 KCAS; Vmc-Ground 107 KCAS at sea level, tapering ~1 kt/1,000 ft to 86 KCAS at 15,000 ft."],["What are the Vfe limits for flaps 10, 20, and 39?","Flaps 10 (T/O): 250 KCAS / 0.60 Mt. Flaps 20 (T/O APP): 220 KCAS / 0.60 Mt. Flaps 39 (DOWN): 170 KCAS / 0.60 Mt."],["What is the maximum tire speed, and the minimum holding speed in icing?","Maximum tire speed: 195 kt ground speed. Minimum holding speed in icing (flaps 0): 160 KCAS."],["What are the flight load acceleration (G) limits for flaps UP vs flaps 39 above MLW?","Flaps UP (0): -1.0g to +2.5g up to MTOW. Flaps 39 (DOWN) above MLW: 0g to +1.5g."],["What is the G550's maximum operating altitude, and the max altitude with the yaw damper inoperative?","Maximum operating altitude: 51,000 ft. Max altitude with yaw damper INOP: 45,000 ft."],["What is the minimum operating static air temperature, and what applies below -70C?","-70C is the minimum operational temperature; below that down to -80C, a minimum Mach schedule applies (e.g., -80C requires Mach 0.87)."],["What is the maximum tailwind for takeoff/landing, and the maximum demonstrated dry-runway crosswind?","Maximum tailwind: 10 kt. Maximum demonstrated dry-runway crosswind: 28 kt (not a hard limit; wet/contaminated uses the CRFI chart)."],["What is the maximum cabin pressure differential, and the limit during taxi, takeoff, or landing?","Maximum cabin pressure differential: 10.48 psi. Limit for taxi/takeoff/landing: 0.3 psi."],["How long may the internal baggage door remain open above 40,000 ft, and what pack condition further restricts baggage access?","Time open above 40,000 ft is limited to 5 minutes. On a single ECS pack, baggage compartment access is allowed only at/below 45,000 ft."],["What are the autopilot minimum engage and disengage heights?","Minimum engage height: 200 ft AGL. Minimum disengage height: 60 ft AGL (ILS/LPV) or 50 ft below DA/MDA for other operations."],["What happens to Mmo if Mach trim is inoperative, and what is the emergency stabilizer trim speed limit?","Mmo is reduced to 0.80 Mt if Mach Trim is inoperative. Emergency stabilizer trim (armed, autopilot engaged, jammed elevator) limit: 270 KCAS / 0.80 Mt."],["What are the engine fuel temperature limits, and the maximum fuel tank temperature?","Engine fuel temp: -40C minimum, +140C max unrestricted, +165C max for a 15-minute transient. Max fuel tank temperature: +54C."],["What oil pressure requires engine shutdown, and what is the minimum oil pressure for takeoff?","Engine shutdown required below 25 psi. Minimum for takeoff: 35 psi (<72.3% HP) or 45 psi (>90.0% HP)."],["What are the engine takeoff thrust time limits (AEO vs OEI) and the TGT limit at takeoff?","Takeoff thrust: 5 minutes All Engines Operating, 10 minutes One Engine Inoperative. Max TGT at takeoff: 900C."],["What are the starter duty-cycle limits for engine ground start?","3 start cycles max, 3 minutes per cycle, 15-second delay between cycles; after 3 cycles, wait at least 15 minutes before further use."],["At what speed must reverse thrust be cancelled to idle, and above what speed may max reverse be selected?","Cancel reverse thrust to reach idle by 60 KCAS. Max reverse thrust must be selected only above 60 kt airplane speed."]],"cold-weather-ops":[["Below what ambient temperature is airplane start-up prohibited, and after how many hours without power is the airplane considered cold-soaked?","Start-up is prohibited after extended exposure below -40C. The airplane is cold-soaked if power-off time exceeds 5 hours."],["What is the minimum operational static air temperature, and what must be applied down to -80C?","-70C is the minimum operational temperature; a minimum Mach schedule applies from -70C down to -80C (e.g., -76C requires Mach 0.80, -80C requires Mach 0.87)."],["What are the fuel tank temperature caution and warning CAS ranges?","Caution (amber): -35C to -36C. Warning (red): less than -37C. If <= -30C in flight with < 5,000 lb fuel remaining, descend to SAT -60C or warmer and hold minimum Mach 0.80."],["What are the recommended minimum maneuvering speeds in icing by flap setting?","Flaps 0 (UP): 200 KCAS. Flaps 10: 180 KCAS. Flaps 20: 150 KCAS. Flaps 39 (DOWN): Vref + 5 kt."],["At what temperature/moisture condition do icing conditions exist, and when is a visual/tactile wing check required?","Icing conditions exist when SAT is +10C or below with visible moisture. A wing check is required when OAT < 6C or wing fuel temp above 0C can't be confirmed, combined with visible moisture, water on wing, or a dew point/OAT spread of 3C or less."],["Above what altitude is automatic wing/cowl anti-ice inhibited, and what must the crew do?","Automatic anti-ice is inhibited above 35,000 ft; it must be manually selected if protection is required above that altitude."],["What is the ice-shedding technique for engine icing?","Retard one engine at a time to idle for 5 seconds, then accelerate to 90% LP before returning the power lever to its original position."],["After a cold soak colder than -20C, what must be done before attempting APU start?","Warm the batteries externally before attempting APU start (external AC cart if available)."],["After a cold soak at -15C or colder, what APU indication issue may occur, and what corrective action is recommended?","APU EGT/RPM displays may be unreadable; preheat the cockpit (especially the overhead panel) if capable, then start normally while monitoring EGT/RPM."],["What is the maximum APU EGT allowed during a cold-weather start?","1,050C."],["When is engine start required to follow cold-weather procedures based on oil temperature, and what AFM reference applies?","Required any time engine oil temperature is -10C or colder (minimum oil temp -30C), per AFM Alternate Normals 02-08-120, Cold Weather Start and Operation."],["What generator switch procedure is used before cold-weather engine start, and what should be done if GEN FAIL messages appear at idle?","Manually select L and R generator switches OFF prior to start, then ON once engines stabilize at idle. If GEN FAIL/AC POWER FAIL messages appear, select the affected GEN switch OFF for 15 seconds, then back ON."],["What is the minimum oil temperature to accelerate engines for taxi power, and what brake temperature should be built during taxi?","Minimum oil temperature for taxi power: -30C. Use brakes to build brake temperature to a minimum of 100C to aid moisture dissipation."],["What is the minimum engine oil temperature required for takeoff power?","20C (+20C)."],["What are the RwyCC values for Ice (RwyCC 1) and for Dry (RwyCC 6) on the RCAM?","RwyCC 6 = Dry, braking action N/A. RwyCC 1 = Ice, braking action Poor."],["What are the maximum standing-water depth and flap setting for takeoff on a contaminated runway?","Maximum standing-water depth: 15 mm, no credit for clearways; Flaps 20, anti-skid operative, auto ground spoilers operative."],["What are the threshold speed limits for landing on a contaminated runway, and what flap setting is required?","Flaps 39; threshold speed minimum Vref, maximum Vref + 10; maximum standing-water depth 15 mm."],["At ramp temperatures below -15C with APU bleed air ON, what is the maximum time before a water/waste system purge is required?","1 hour 15 minutes (1:15), provided cabin temperature is maintained at or above 10C (50F)."],["At what temperature should life rafts and main batteries be removed due to cold exposure?","Life rafts should be removed below -28C; main batteries must be removed below -20C."],["Is a pre-takeoff contamination check required if takeoff occurs before the holdover table time expires?","Yes for most operators - only operators covered by 14 CFR 121.629 may use holdover tables in lieu of a pre-takeoff contamination check."]],"efvs-ops":[["Qual a altura mínima (HAT) para continuar uma aproximação CAT I com EFVS abaixo da DA/DH?","100 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira (RBAC 91 / 14 CFR 91.176(b))."],["Qual a altura mínima (HAT) para continuar uma aproximação APV ou NPA com EFVS abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH?","200 ft acima da elevação da cabeceira, usando a técnica CDFA."],["Abaixo de 100/200 ft, como o piloto pode continuar a aproximação?","Somente se conseguir identificar as referências visuais exigidas a olho nu, sem o auxílio da imagem EFVS."],["Qual o RVR reduzido mínimo que exige dois pilotos (ou mais) em aproximações APV/NPA com EFVS?","RVR reduzido menor que 550 m exige dois ou mais pilotos."],["Qual o deslocamento angular máximo permitido entre o rumo do segmento final e o rumo da pista em aproximação APV/NPA com EFVS?","2 graus."],["Qual o ângulo de planeio máximo publicado permitido para aproximação APV/NPA com EFVS?","3,7 graus."],["As luzes do VGSI contam como referência visual válida em Operação EFVS?","Não. Diferença chave: as luzes do VGSI NÃO contam como referência visual em Operação EFVS; é preciso ver a cabeceira e a zona de toque."],["Quais os quatro tipos de procedimento em que uma Operação EFVS NÃO é autorizada?","Aproximação por instrumentos para circular (exceto pouso direto); aproximação visual; aproximação CAT II; aproximação CAT III."],["Qual a experiência recente mínima exigida para manter a prerrogativa de Operação EFVS?","Mínimo de 6 aproximações por instrumento usando EFVS nos últimos 6 meses, como único piloto nos controles, no mesmo conjunto aeronave/equipamento EFVS; podem ser diurnas ou noturnas, em qualquer condição climática, terminando em pouso completo."],["Na tabela RVR/CMV, qual o RVR/CMV reduzido correspondente a um mínimo publicado de 700 m?","450 m (exemplo clássico: ILS CAT I com RVR de 700 m -> RVR reduzido de 450 m)."],["Como se calcula o CMV a partir da visibilidade reportada?","CMV = visibilidade reportada x fator (conforme tipo de iluminação da pista e se é aproximação diurna ou noturna). Ex.: 1.000 m com luzes de alta intensidade, à noite -> fator 2,0 -> CMV = 2.000 m."],["Quais as restrições de uso da tabela de conversão de visibilidade para CMV?","Não pode ser usada quando o RVR relatado já está disponível; não serve para mínimos de decolagem; não se aplica a qualquer RVR mínimo inferior a 800 m."],["Para que serve exclusivamente a tabela RVR/CMV do RBAC 91?","Apenas para planejamento de voo e início de uma aproximação IFR em Operação EFVS - não serve para continuar a aproximação abaixo da DA/DH ou MDA/MDH, nem para calcular mínimos de decolagem."],["Quando o piloto deve iniciar uma arremetida durante uma Operação EFVS?","Se qualquer componente do EFVS falhar; se as referências visuais requeridas deixarem de ser identificáveis (com EFVS acima de 100/200 ft, ou a olho nu abaixo desses limites); ou se a aeronave não estiver em posição de pouso normal."],["Qual a responsabilidade do PIC em relação ao ATC ao realizar uma aproximação EFVS?","Informar ao órgão de controle a intenção de realizar o procedimento com EFVS, e notificar imediatamente qualquer anomalia ou deficiência dos equipamentos do sistema."],["No Approach Ban do Reino Unido (EASA), até que altura a aproximação pode continuar mesmo com RVR/visibilidade abaixo do mínimo?","A aproximação não pode continuar abaixo de 1.000 ft acima do aeródromo se o RVR/visibilidade estiver abaixo do mínimo para pouso; após passar 1.000 ft, pode continuar até a DA/H ou MDA/H mesmo que o RVR caia."],["No Approach Ban da França, quais os RVR mínimos controladores de ponto médio e final de pista?","RVR mínimo do ponto médio: 125 m (ou o RVR de zona de toque, se menor; 75 m com sistema de orientação/controle de rollout). RVR mínimo do final de pista: 75 m."],["Segundo o AFM, quando a EVS Landing System pode ser usada para descer até 100 ft acima da zona de toque?","Quando a performance demonstrada atende aos critérios do AC 20-167A, em conformidade com 14 CFR Part 91.176(b); o sistema não fornece vantagem visual ('Visual Advantage') ao visualizar iluminação LED."],["Em que faixa de FPA (ângulo de trajetória de voo) o 'flare cue' é apresentado no HUD?","Somente quando o FPA configurado no menu HUD do DC está entre -2,49 e -5,00 graus."],["A que altura o flare cue começa a subir, e quando o piloto desconecta o piloto automático?","O flare cue começa a subir a partir de 100 ft HAT; o piloto desconecta o piloto automático antes ou em 60 ft de altitude rádio."]],"high-hot-heavy":[["What are the three types of Equal Time Points (ETPs)?","1) Loss of Engine ETP (1E INOP) - uses the Engine Out Driftdown Charts. 2) Loss of Level ETP - Pressurization (DEPRESS) - uses the Emergency Descent Procedure. 3) Maintain Level ETP - Medical (MEDICAL) - level flight maintained, no descent."],["What is the ETP formula, and what do D, GSa, and GSb represent?","Ground distance to ETP = (D x GSb) / (GSa + GSb), in NM. D = total distance airport behind to airport ahead; GSa = ground speed continuing to airport ahead; GSb = ground speed returning to airport behind."],["Using the worked example (D=2,500 NM, GSa=520 kt, GSb=440 kt), what is the ETP distance from the airport behind?","1,146 NM from the airport behind: (2,500 x 440)/(520+440) = 1,146 NM."],["Why should ETPs never be entered into the FMS as waypoints unless they are actual route points?","Because ADS-C would send position reports of non-existing waypoints to ATC."],["What are the NAT standard vertical, lateral (PBCS), and lateral (non-PBCS) separation minima?","Vertical: 1,000 ft. Lateral PBCS track: 1/2 degree or 25 NM (FL350-FL390, PBCS authorization required). Lateral non-PBCS track: 1 degree or 60 NM."],["What are the longitudinal (Mach number technique) separation standards for PBCS versus non-PBCS tracks?","Non-PBCS: 10 minutes. PBCS: 5 minutes."],["When do westbound and eastbound NAT traffic cross 030W, and who publishes each set of OTS tracks and when?","Westbound crosses 030W 1130-1930Z, published by Shanwick at 2200Z. Eastbound crosses 030W 0100-0800Z, published by Gander at 1400Z."],["In a NAT contingency (loss of ATC clearance) where a revised clearance cannot be obtained but the assigned flight level CAN be maintained, what offset climb/descend increments apply below FL410, at FL410, and above FL410?","500 ft increments below FL410, 500 ft at FL410, and 1,000 ft increments above FL410, relative to normal cruising levels, once established on a same-direction 5 NM offset."],["If UNABLE to maintain the assigned flight level during a NAT contingency, what is the required sequence of actions?","Minimize rate of descent to what's operationally feasible; acquire a same-direction 5 NM offset track; descend to FL290 or lower; once below FL290, establish and maintain a 500 ft vertical offset from normal levels until an ATC clearance is received."],["What is the SAND mnemonic for severe weather deviation actions, and what triggers each action?","SAND = South Ascend, North Descend. Turning SOUTH: CLIMB 300 ft. Turning NORTH: DESCEND 300 ft (for deviations greater than 5 NM off track)."],["What are the allowed SLOP (Strategic Lateral Offset) positions, and is offsetting left of centerline ever permitted?","Centerline, 1.0 NM right, or 2.0 NM right of centerline (micro-SLOP in 1/10 NM increments up to 2.0 NM right). NEVER slop left of centerline. ATC approval is not required."],["In the worked driftdown scenario, what are the initial conditions (altitude, weight, OAT, speed)?","FL410, M0.85, 80,000 lb initial driftdown weight, ISA+10 (-56C), left engine flames out after ETP2."],["In the worked driftdown scenario, what is the descent Mach/KCAS, time, fuel used, and distance covered?","Descent Mach/KCAS: 0.80/251 KCAS. Time: 39 minutes. Fuel used: 1,800 lb. Distance: 264 NM."],["What is the final \"start cruise\" speed and altitude reached at the end of the driftdown, and what climb capability exists there?","218 KCAS at 26,500 ft (final driftdown/cruise altitude), with a 200 fpm rate of climb capability at MCT at that LRC speed."],["At the failure of one engine, what thrust setting is set and held on the operating engine, and when should deceleration to the entry descent Mach occur?","Maximum Continuous Thrust (MCT). Deceleration should be performed at the initial cruise altitude, before the start of descent."],["In the 13-step driftdown checklist, what is Step 1 (Fly the Aircraft) and what happens automatically to the autopilot and autothrottle?","Regain and maintain directional control, set MCT on the operating engine. The autopilot remains engaged; the autothrottle disconnects automatically."],["What offset is created early in the driftdown checklist (Step 3), and via what MCDU keystrokes?","A right 5.0 NM offset: PROG -> NEXT, NEXT -> scratchpad: R5 -> LSK 1R (Offset) -> select LNAV on the guidance panel -> confirm FMS is captured/annunciated."],["What transponder code and ADS-C setting are used once proceeding to the alternate airport (Step 11)?","Squawk transponder code 7700; set ADS-C to EMERGENCY."],["Following a left-engine failure, what closes to tie the L Main AC bus to the R Main AC bus, and what two sources power all AC/DC buses?","L Bus Tie (AUTO) closes. The APU GEN and the R IDG (remaining engine's Integrated Drive Generator) power all AC and DC buses."],["What hydraulic result occurs from a left-engine failure even with the PTU providing backup pressure, and at what LH system pressure does the PTU auto-activate?","Loss of the LEFT thrust reverser (the left EDP-fed system can no longer support reverser deployment). PTU auto-activates when L Hydraulic system pressure drops below 1,500 psi."],["How does a single-engine failure affect fuel and pneumatic systems?","Fuel: all components operate normally, but a fuel imbalance develops since only the operating engine draws from its onside tank. Pneumatic: the operating engine can supply bleed air to both L and R packs by opening the isolation valve, so both packs remain available."]]}};